// AUTO-GENERATED by scripts/merge.mjs. CompTIA Security+ SY0-701 twin-term drill bank.
// Do not edit by hand; edit the chunk sources in data/gen/ and re-run merge.
export const QUESTIONS = [
  {"id":"q001","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"A newly hired CISO's first two initiatives are establishing a quarterly risk assessment cycle and publishing a formal data classification policy that every department must follow. Which of the following control categories BEST describes these initiatives?","options":["technical","managerial","operational","physical"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Managerial. Risk assessments and written policies are administrative decisions made by leadership, not anything enforced by technology or performed day to day by staff. The tell: technical = enforced in hardware/software, managerial = policy and risk decisions, operational = day-to-day human processes, physical = tangible barriers."},
  {"id":"q002","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"A security manager schedules monthly phishing-awareness training for all staff and requires SOC analysts to follow a documented incident-handling checklist on every shift. Which of the following control categories do these activities fall under?","options":["managerial","physical","operational","technical"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Operational. Recurring training and analysts working a checklist are day-to-day processes carried out by people. Writing the policy would be managerial; these activities are its human execution. The tell: operational = human day-to-day processes, managerial = policy/risk decisions, physical = tangible barriers, technical = implemented in tech."},
  {"id":"q003","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"To reduce its attack surface, an engineering team deploys a next-generation firewall at the perimeter and enables full-disk encryption on every laptop. Which of the following control categories do these two measures fall under?","options":["managerial","operational","technical","physical"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Technical. Firewalls and encryption enforce protection through hardware and software mechanisms rather than policy, people, or barriers. The tell: technical = implemented in tech/hardware/software, managerial = policy and risk decisions, operational = day-to-day human processes, physical = tangible barriers."},
  {"id":"q004","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"A colocation facility protects its server hall with cipher locks on every door, steel perimeter fencing, and a guard posted at the single entrance. Which of the following control categories do these protections belong to?","options":["physical","technical","operational","managerial"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Physical. Locks, fences, and guards are tangible barriers that control real-world access to the space. The tell: physical = tangible barriers (locks, fences, guards), technical = enforced in hardware/software, operational = day-to-day human processes, managerial = policy and risk decisions."},
  {"id":"q005","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"An IT team uses group policy to push password-complexity and session-timeout settings to every endpoint, while the written password standard is maintained separately by the governance office. Which control category BEST describes the enforced settings?","options":["Managerial","Technical","Operational","Physical"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Technical. The settings are enforced by software on the endpoints themselves, which is what defines the technical category; managerial loses because it covers the written standard behind the settings, not the enforcement pushed by the system. The tell: technical = implemented by systems and software, managerial = the policy and decision layer, operational = people performing day-to-day procedures, physical = tangible barriers."},
  {"id":"q006","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"A CISO commissions and approves a new incident response plan. During the next incident, SOC analysts work through the plan's documented steps. Which of the following correctly categorizes the plan and the analysts' activity?","options":["The plan is managerial; executing the steps is operational","The plan is operational; executing the steps is managerial","The plan is technical; executing the steps is operational","The plan is managerial; executing the steps is technical"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The plan is managerial; executing the steps is operational. Commissioning and approving the plan is governance and decision-making, while analysts carrying out documented procedures is people-executed day-to-day work; reversing the pair fails because the document is the decision layer, not the hands-on activity. The tell: managerial = the decision and policy layer, operational = people performing the procedures, technical = enforced by systems, physical = tangible barriers."},
  {"id":"q007","domain":"D1","family":"control-categories","question":"After several unauthorized entries, a warehouse security manager is directed to add a control from the physical category. Which of the following BEST meets that requirement?","options":["A next-generation firewall","An acceptable use policy","Perimeter fencing at the site boundary","A daily review of access logs"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Perimeter fencing at the site boundary. A fence is a tangible barrier that physically limits entry, which defines the physical category; the firewall is a real control but it is technical, implemented in software. The tell: physical = tangible barriers, technical = implemented by systems, managerial = policies such as an AUP, operational = people-run processes such as log review."},
  {"id":"q008","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"After several after-hours break-in attempts, a facilities team installs high-intensity lighting around a parking garage and posts signs stating the area is patrolled and recorded. No new barriers or alarm systems are added. Which of the following control types BEST describes these measures?","options":["preventive","deterrent","detective","corrective"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Deterrent. Lighting and warning signs discourage an intruder from trying but cannot physically stop or even record anyone, so nothing is prevented or detected. The tell: deterrent = discourages the attempt, preventive = actually stops it, detective = finds it after, corrective = restores after."},
  {"id":"q009","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"A hospital runs a legacy imaging server whose vendor no longer releases patches. Because the known vulnerabilities cannot be fixed, the security team places the server behind stricter firewall rules and adds continuous network monitoring until it is replaced. Which of the following control types do the added measures represent?","options":["deterrent","corrective","directive","compensating"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Compensating. The primary control, patching, is not feasible, so alternate safeguards stand in to reduce the same risk. The tell: compensating = alternate when the primary control is not possible, corrective = restores after an event, directive = mandates behavior, deterrent = discourages."},
  {"id":"q010","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"A SOC receives an IDS alert about a completed intrusion, and a guard reviewing last night's camera footage spots the same event. Neither tool stopped the attack. Which of the following control types do the IDS and the footage review represent?","options":["corrective","preventive","detective","directive"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Detective. Both the IDS alert and the footage review identify and record an incident only after it happened. The tell: detective = finds it after, corrective = fixes/restores after, preventive = stops it before, directive = mandates behavior."},
  {"id":"q011","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"Before receiving network credentials, every new employee must read and sign an acceptable use policy spelling out exactly how company systems may and may not be used. Which of the following control types BEST describes the policy itself?","options":["directive","preventive","deterrent","compensating"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Directive. An acceptable use policy mandates required behavior; it instructs rather than blocks, discourages, or substitutes. The tell: directive = mandates behavior, preventive = stops it before it happens, deterrent = discourages, compensating = alternate when the primary control is not feasible."},
  {"id":"q012","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"A security architect points out that a door lock and a firewall access rule share the same purpose: each stops an unauthorized party from getting in before any damage can occur. Which of the following control types describes both?","options":["detective","preventive","corrective","deterrent"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Preventive. Both controls physically or logically block the attempt before it succeeds, regardless of one being physical and the other technical. The tell: preventive = stops it before it happens, deterrent = only discourages, detective = finds it after, corrective = restores after."},
  {"id":"q013","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"Ransomware encrypts a file server overnight. The next day the team restores the data from the previous night's backup and applies the patch that closed the exploited vulnerability. Which of the following control types does the backup restore represent?","options":["corrective","preventive","compensating","detective"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Corrective. Restoring from backup fixes the damage and returns operations to normal after the incident already happened. The tell: corrective = fixes/restores after the event, preventive = stops it before, compensating = alternate control when the primary is not feasible, detective = finds it after."},
  {"id":"q014","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"Each morning a SIEM correlates the previous night's collected logs and surfaces a suspicious 2:00 a.m. admin login that has already taken place. Which of the following control types is the SIEM acting as?","options":["deterrent","directive","detective","corrective"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Detective. The SIEM identifies an event after the fact; it neither blocked it nor fixed anything. The tell: detective = finds it after (logs, IDS, reviewed footage), deterrent = discourages, directive = mandates behavior, corrective = restores after."},
  {"id":"q015","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"A company enforces endpoint settings that block all removable media outright and adds a login banner warning that unauthorized access will be prosecuted. Which of the following correctly pairs each measure with its control type?","options":["Blocking is deterrent; the banner is preventive","Blocking is preventive; the banner is directive","Blocking is preventive; the banner is deterrent","Blocking is corrective; the banner is deterrent"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Blocking is preventive; the banner is deterrent. The device control stops the action outright before it can occur, while the banner only discourages an intruder who could still proceed; directive loses for the banner because it targets would-be attackers, not the behavior of authorized users. The tell: preventive = blocks the attempt, deterrent = discourages without stopping, directive = mandates authorized users' behavior, corrective = fixes after the incident."},
  {"id":"q016","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"A web server is exploited on Friday through an unpatched flaw. Over the weekend the team re-images the host and applies the vendor fix that closes the exploited vulnerability. Which control type BEST describes these post-incident actions?","options":["Preventive","Compensating","Detective","Corrective"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Corrective. The re-image and patch restore the system and fix the flaw after a successful exploit, and that timing is what classifies them; preventive loses because the same patch applied before any incident would be preventive, but here the damage already happened. The tell: corrective = fixes and restores after the incident, preventive = stops it beforehand, compensating = alternative when the primary control is not feasible, detective = finds it but does not fix it."},
  {"id":"q017","domain":"D1","family":"control-types","question":"A new requirement states that all staff must complete secure-data-handling training and follow the posted media-handling procedure before working with customer records. Which control type does this requirement represent?","options":["Directive","Deterrent","Preventive","Compensating"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Directive. The requirement mandates how authorized employees must behave, which is the defining trait of a directive control; deterrent loses because it aims to discourage would-be attackers, not to instruct legitimate staff. The tell: directive = mandates authorized users' behavior, deterrent = discourages attackers, preventive = blocks the action outright, compensating = substitute when the primary control is infeasible."},
  {"id":"q018","domain":"D1","family":"cia-nonrepudiation","question":"A legal department requires that every outbound contract be digitally signed so that an executive cannot later claim they never approved the document. Which of the following security principles does the signature requirement provide?","options":["integrity","availability","non-repudiation","confidentiality"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Non-repudiation. The goal stated is preventing denial of an action, which is exactly what a digital signature provides. It also gives integrity, but the scenario's requirement is the inability to deny approval. The tell: non-repudiation = cannot deny you did it, integrity = unaltered, confidentiality = secrecy, availability = uptime."},
  {"id":"q019","domain":"D1","family":"cia-nonrepudiation","question":"A backup administrator configures the backup platform to store a SHA-256 hash of every file it writes. During restore tests, hashes are recomputed and compared to detect any alteration. Which of the following principles is being protected?","options":["integrity","confidentiality","availability","non-repudiation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Integrity. Hashing detects whether data changed; it does not hide data or keep systems up. The tell: integrity = unaltered (hashing, checksums), confidentiality = secrecy (encryption), availability = uptime (redundancy), non-repudiation = cannot deny you did it (signatures)."},
  {"id":"q020","domain":"D1","family":"cia-nonrepudiation","question":"An e-commerce company deploys clustered web servers, a standby database replica, and regularly tested backups so the storefront stays reachable even when a component fails. Which of the following CIA triad principles is this design serving?","options":["confidentiality","availability","integrity","non-repudiation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Availability. Redundancy, replicas, and tested backups all exist to keep the service up. The tell: availability = uptime (redundancy, backups), confidentiality = secrecy, integrity = unaltered, non-repudiation = cannot deny you did it."},
  {"id":"q021","domain":"D1","family":"cia-nonrepudiation","question":"A hospital encrypts patient records at rest and in transit so that only authorized clinicians can read them and an intercepted copy is useless to outsiders. Which of the following CIA triad principles is this protecting?","options":["confidentiality","integrity","availability","non-repudiation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Confidentiality. Encryption for secrecy keeps unauthorized parties from reading the data. The tell: confidentiality = secrecy (encryption), integrity = unaltered (hashing), availability = uptime (redundancy), non-repudiation = cannot deny you did it (signatures)."},
  {"id":"q022","domain":"D1","family":"cia-nonrepudiation","question":"A database platform writes a detailed audit log tying every schema change to the specific administrator account that made it, so no admin can credibly deny an action later. Which of the following principles does this support?","options":["integrity","availability","non-repudiation","confidentiality"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Non-repudiation. Binding actions to identities so they cannot be denied is non-repudiation; the logs may help integrity too, but the stated goal is preventing denial. The tell: non-repudiation = cannot deny you did it (audit logs, signatures), integrity = data unaltered, confidentiality = secrecy, availability = uptime."},
  {"id":"q023","domain":"D1","family":"aaa","question":"A network engineer configures the RADIUS server so that every VPN user's login time, session duration, and data transferred are logged for a later audit and for per-department billing. Which of the following parts of the AAA framework is being implemented?","options":["identification","authentication","accounting","authorization"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Accounting. Recording who did what, when, and for how long is the accounting leg of AAA. The tell: authentication = prove who you are, authorization = what you are allowed to do, accounting = record what you did, identification = merely claiming an identity."},
  {"id":"q024","domain":"D1","family":"aaa","question":"After an engineer's smart card and PIN are accepted, the file server checks her group memberships to decide whether she can open the payroll share. Which of the following AAA components performs this second step?","options":["authentication","authorization","accounting","identification"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Authorization. Identity is already proven; deciding what she may access is authorization. The tell: authentication = prove who you are, authorization = what you are allowed to do once proven, accounting = record what you did, identification = the claim itself."},
  {"id":"q025","domain":"D1","family":"aaa","question":"Before allowing a session, a remote-access system verifies a user's claimed identity by checking both a password and a fingerprint scan. Which of the following AAA components is performing this check?","options":["accounting","authentication","authorization","identification"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Authentication. Verifying the claimed identity with factors (password, fingerprint) is authentication; stating the username was merely identification. The tell: authentication = prove who you are, identification = claim who you are, authorization = what you may access, accounting = record what you did."},
  {"id":"q026","domain":"D1","family":"aaa","question":"An API gateway validates a developer's access token successfully but rejects the request because the token lacks the scope required to call the DELETE endpoint. Which of the following AAA components made this determination?","options":["identification","authentication","accounting","authorization"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authorization. The caller's identity was already proven when the token validated; deciding whether that token's scope permits the DELETE action is an access decision. Authentication is tempting because a credential is involved, but the check that failed was about permission, not identity. The tell: authorization = what you are allowed to do, authentication = proving who you are, accounting = recording what you did, identification = the identity claim itself."},
  {"id":"q027","domain":"D1","family":"aaa","question":"Hundreds of new IoT sensors must prove their identity to the network switch before they are allowed to send any data. Which of the following BEST accomplishes this?","options":["Multifactor authentication for the operations team","Device certificates validated through 802.1X","A single pre-shared password reused across all sensors","Biometric readers at the network closet door"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Device certificates validated through 802.1X. Systems authenticate with certificates issued to each device, and 802.1X keeps the switch port closed until that proof succeeds; a shared pre-configured password loses because a credential reused across every sensor cannot uniquely prove any one device's identity. The tell: authenticating systems = device certificates (802.1X), authenticating people = passwords, biometrics, and MFA; a shared static secret proves neither."},
  {"id":"q028","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"In a zero trust deployment, an access request arrives carrying the user's identity, device health report, and location. One component weighs all of this against policy and renders the actual grant-or-deny decision. Which of the following components is it?","options":["Policy Engine","Policy Administrator","Policy Enforcement Point","control plane"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Policy Engine. It is the brain that evaluates the request against policy and decides. The tell: Policy Engine = makes the grant/deny decision, Policy Administrator = generates/relays the access token, Policy Enforcement Point = the gate that opens or blocks the session, control plane = the whole decision-making layer they live in."},
  {"id":"q029","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"A zero trust gateway sits directly in the connection path and either opens or drops each user session depending on the token it receives from the control plane. Which of the following zero trust components is this gateway?","options":["Policy Administrator","Policy Engine","Policy Enforcement Point","data plane"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Policy Enforcement Point. It is the gate in the traffic path that actually allows or blocks the session; it does not make the decision itself. The tell: Policy Enforcement Point = enforces, Policy Engine = decides, Policy Administrator = relays the token, data plane = where the traffic flows."},
  {"id":"q030","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"In a zero trust architecture, one component takes the Policy Engine's decision, then generates and relays the session token that instructs the enforcement point to permit the connection. Which of the following components performs this role?","options":["Policy Administrator","Policy Enforcement Point","data plane","Policy Engine"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Policy Administrator. It is the messenger between decision and enforcement: it issues the token or credential the PEP acts on. The Policy Enforcement Point is tempting, but it only obeys the token; it never generates one. The tell: Policy Administrator = generates/relays the token, Policy Engine = makes the decision, Policy Enforcement Point = enforces it, data plane = where the subject's traffic flows, not a decision component."},
  {"id":"q031","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"A zero trust architect is labeling a network diagram. One area contains the components that make and manage access decisions; a separate area carries the subjects' actual application traffic. Which of the following terms describes the decision-making area?","options":["data plane","adaptive identity","implicit trust zones","control plane"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Control plane. Policy decisions live in the control plane, kept separate from the data plane where subject traffic flows. The tell: control plane = where policy decisions live, data plane = where traffic flows, adaptive identity = risk-based identity validation, implicit trust zones = the legacy assumption zero trust removes."},
  {"id":"q032","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"In zero trust vocabulary, which of the following terms names the layer where the subject's actual application traffic and sessions flow, as opposed to the separate layer where access decisions are made and managed?","options":["control plane","Policy Engine","Policy Administrator","data plane"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Data plane. Traffic flows in the data plane; decisions happen in the control plane. The tell: data plane = where the subject's traffic actually flows, control plane = where policy decisions live, Policy Engine = the decision maker inside the control plane, Policy Administrator = relays the token."},
  {"id":"q033","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"When a user connects from an unfamiliar country on an unmanaged device, the zero trust system automatically demands stronger authentication than it does for the same user at their usual desk. Which of the following zero trust concepts is this?","options":["threat scope reduction","implicit trust zones","adaptive identity","policy-driven access control"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Adaptive identity. Identity validation that tightens or relaxes based on context and risk signals is adaptive identity. The tell: adaptive identity = risk-based, context-aware identity checks, threat scope reduction = shrinking what any subject can reach, policy-driven access control = rules deciding access, implicit trust zones = the legacy trust-by-location model zero trust eliminates."},
  {"id":"q034","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"A zero trust program limits every user to the minimal set of resources their job requires, so a compromised account exposes as little of the environment as possible. Which of the following zero trust concepts does this describe?","options":["threat scope reduction","adaptive identity","control plane","Policy Enforcement Point"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Threat scope reduction. Minimizing what any one subject can reach shrinks the blast radius of a compromise. The tell: threat scope reduction = shrink what an account can reach, adaptive identity = context-aware authentication, control plane = where decisions live, Policy Enforcement Point = the gate enforcing them."},
  {"id":"q035","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"A zero trust architect asks the team to map three duties: render the grant-or-deny decision, issue and relay the session token, and open or drop the connection. Which of the following mappings is correct?","options":["Policy Engine decides; Policy Administrator relays the token; the PEP opens or drops","Policy Administrator decides; Policy Engine relays the token; the PEP opens or drops","Policy Engine decides; the PEP relays the token; Policy Administrator opens or drops","The PEP decides; Policy Administrator relays the token; Policy Engine opens or drops"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Policy Engine decides; Policy Administrator relays the token; the PEP opens or drops. The PE evaluates policy and signals to render the decision, the PA turns that decision into a session token it passes to the enforcement point, and the PEP is the gate in the traffic path; swapping PE and PA fails because the Administrator executes decisions rather than making them. The tell: Policy Engine = decides, Policy Administrator = generates and relays the session token, Policy Enforcement Point = allows or blocks the actual connection."},
  {"id":"q036","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"While labeling a zero trust architecture diagram, an engineer must place the Policy Engine, Policy Administrator, and Policy Enforcement Point on the correct planes. Which of the following placements is correct?","options":["PE in the control plane; PA and PEP in the data plane","PE, PA, and PEP all in the control plane","PE and PA in the control plane; PEP in the data plane","PE and PA in the data plane; PEP in the control plane"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"PE and PA in the control plane; PEP in the data plane. Decision making and session management live in the control plane, while the PEP sits in the data plane with the subject's traffic even though it takes orders from the control plane; placing all three in the control plane fails because enforcement happens where the traffic actually flows. The tell: control plane = where access decisions are made and managed, data plane = where the subject's traffic flows and is enforced."},
  {"id":"q037","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"A security analyst reviews two access-log entries: user=jsmith geo=new country device=unmanaged action=step-up MFA required, then user=jsmith geo=home office device=managed action=password only. Which zero trust concept do these entries demonstrate?","options":["Threat scope reduction","Policy-driven access control","Implicit trust zones","Adaptive identity"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Adaptive identity. The authentication requirement changes with risk signals: a new country on an unmanaged device forces step-up MFA while the usual managed device needs only a password; policy-driven access control loses because it names the general rule evaluation for authorization, not varying authentication strength with risk. The tell: adaptive identity = authentication strength varies with risk, policy-driven access control = identity-plus-context rules decide access, threat scope reduction = limit blast radius, implicit trust zones = the minimized area behind the PEP."},
  {"id":"q038","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"A company retires a VPN that gave every remote user full internal network reach, replacing it with per-application grants so a stolen credential exposes almost nothing. Which zero trust principle does this change apply?","options":["Adaptive identity","Threat scope reduction","Policy-driven access control","Implicit trust zones"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Threat scope reduction. Shrinking each subject's reach from the whole network to specific applications limits the blast radius of any compromise; policy-driven access control loses because the stated goal is minimizing what a stolen credential can touch, not the rule-evaluation mechanism itself. The tell: threat scope reduction = shrink what any one subject can reach, adaptive identity = vary authentication strength with risk, policy-driven access control = identity-plus-context rules decide access, implicit trust zones = the minimized trusted area behind the PEP."},
  {"id":"q039","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"In a new zero trust deployment, every access decision is produced by evaluating rules that combine the requester's identity, device posture, and current context. Which zero trust concept does this describe?","options":["Policy-driven access control","Adaptive identity","Implicit trust zones","Threat scope reduction"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Policy-driven access control. Granting or denying access by evaluating rules over identity, device posture, and context is the general zero trust authorization mechanism; adaptive identity loses because it specifically means varying the authentication strength with risk, not the overall rule evaluation. The tell: policy-driven access control = rule evaluation over identity and context decides access, adaptive identity = authentication strength varies with risk, implicit trust zones = minimized trusted area behind the PEP, threat scope reduction = limit blast radius."},
  {"id":"q040","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"Once a zero trust gateway admits a session, traffic is allowed to flow freely only within one small segment behind the enforcement point. Which zero trust term names that minimized trusted area?","options":["Data plane","Threat scope reduction","Policy Enforcement Point","Implicit trust zone"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Implicit trust zone. It is the area behind the PEP where communication flows without further checks once access is granted, and zero trust keeps these zones as small as possible; data plane loses because it names the layer where all subject traffic flows, not the minimized trusted segment. The tell: implicit trust zone = the small trusted area behind the PEP, data plane = where the traffic flows, Policy Enforcement Point = the gate itself, threat scope reduction = the principle of shrinking those zones."},
  {"id":"q041","domain":"D1","family":"zero-trust","question":"In NIST zero trust terminology, a contractor's laptop requests access to an internal application, and the enforcement point carries its session. Which term names the requesting entity?","options":["Policy Engine","Subject/system","Policy Administrator","Implicit trust zone"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Subject/system. NIST zero trust calls the user, device, or application requesting access the subject (or system for a device), and it sits in the data plane alongside the PEP; Policy Engine loses because it makes access decisions rather than requesting access. The tell: subject/system = the requester in the data plane, Policy Engine = decides, Policy Administrator = relays the session token, Policy Enforcement Point = enforces the connection."},
  {"id":"q042","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"After a smash-and-grab at a neighboring office, a data center wants to stop a vehicle from being driven through its glass lobby entrance while keeping the walkway fully open to pedestrians. Which of the following physical controls BEST meets both requirements?","options":["bollards","fencing","access control vestibule","video surveillance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bollards. Short heavy posts stop vehicles but let people walk between them, which is exactly the stated constraint. Fencing blocks pedestrians too, a vestibule controls a doorway not a vehicle, and cameras only record the crash. The tell: bollards = stop vehicles, fencing = perimeter barrier for people, access control vestibule = one-person-at-a-time entry, video surveillance = detects/deters but stops nothing."},
  {"id":"q043","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"A badge audit shows more people entering the server room than badge swipes recorded, because staff hold the door open for whoever is behind them. The facility installs a double-door enclosure that authenticates and admits exactly one person at a time. Which of the following controls is this?","options":["security guard","access badge","access control vestibule","bollards"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Access control vestibule. The double-door, one-person-per-authentication design exists specifically to stop piggybacking through a held door. The tell: access control vestibule = one-person-at-a-time double door, security guard = human verification, access badge = the credential itself, bollards = stop vehicles."},
  {"id":"q044","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"A warehouse alarm triggers at night when a sensor detects the body heat of an intruder moving through an unlit aisle. Which of the following sensor types detected the intruder?","options":["pressure sensor","infrared sensor","microwave sensor","ultrasonic sensor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Infrared sensor. Detecting emitted body heat is the infrared signature. The tell: infrared = detects heat/body, pressure = detects weight/step, microwave and ultrasonic = detect motion via reflected waves, not heat."},
  {"id":"q045","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"After a theft at the loading dock, investigators pull last week's recorded footage to establish who entered and when. Which of the following physical security controls made this investigation possible?","options":["lighting","security guard","video surveillance","access badge"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Video surveillance. Recorded cameras provide the after-the-fact evidence trail investigators reviewed, a classic detective use of a physical control. The tell: video surveillance = record and review events, lighting = deters and aids visibility, security guard = live human control, access badge = the entry credential."},
  {"id":"q046","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"An electrical substation operator needs a barrier around the entire yard to keep people out, not to stop vehicles at a single entry point. Which of the following BEST meets this requirement?","options":["Bollards","Access control vestibule","Video surveillance","Fencing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fencing. A fence forms a continuous perimeter barrier that keeps people from crossing the boundary anywhere along it; bollards lose because they stop vehicles at specific points while letting people walk right past. The tell: fencing = perimeter barrier against people, bollards = stop vehicles, access control vestibule = one-person-at-a-time interlocked doors, video surveillance = observes and records but blocks nothing."},
  {"id":"q047","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"A facility wants a control at its reception area that can verify photo IDs, question visitors who seem out of place, and physically respond to problems as they happen. Which of the following provides this?","options":["Video surveillance","Access badge readers","A security guard","An access control vestibule"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A security guard. Only a human control can exercise judgment: checking IDs, challenging anomalies, and adapting a response in real time; video surveillance loses because it records and deters but cannot question anyone or intervene. The tell: security guard = judgment and real-time response, video surveillance = watch and record, access badge = automated credential check with a log, vestibule = admits one person per authentication."},
  {"id":"q048","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"After an incident, an investigation team needs an attributable record of exactly who entered the server room and when, generated automatically at each door event. Which physical control produces this?","options":["Video surveillance","Access badges with readers","A security guard","An access control vestibule"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Access badges with readers. Each badge swipe writes a per-person, time-stamped entry record, giving attributable accounting for every door event; video surveillance loses because footage must be reviewed manually and does not automatically tie each entry to a credentialed identity. The tell: access badge = logged per-person entry record, video surveillance = recorded footage to review, security guard = human judgment and response, vestibule = enforces one person per authentication."},
  {"id":"q049","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"A distribution yard needs one control at its vehicle gate that visibly discourages theft and also captures license plates and motion events for later review. Which of the following BEST fits?","options":["Security lighting","Bollards","Video surveillance","Pressure sensors"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Video surveillance. Modern camera systems offer motion recognition and license plate capture, deter when visibly installed, and produce reviewable evidence; lighting loses because it discourages intruders and helps other controls see, but it records nothing. The tell: video surveillance = deters when visible and records evidence, lighting = discourages and improves visibility only, bollards = stop vehicles, pressure sensor = detects weight on a surface."},
  {"id":"q050","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"A security consultant recommends one addition to a dark parking area that will both discourage intruders, who avoid being seen, and make the existing cameras and patrols more effective at night. Which control is recommended?","options":["Additional cameras","Exterior lighting","An access control vestibule","Infrared sensors"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Exterior lighting. Lighting deters intruders who avoid lit areas and simultaneously improves what cameras and guards can see at night; additional cameras lose because the stated need is to make the existing cameras effective, which darkness currently prevents. The tell: lighting = deters and multiplies the effectiveness of cameras and guards, cameras = record and deter but need visibility, infrared sensor = detects body heat, vestibule = controls entry one person at a time."},
  {"id":"q051","domain":"D1","family":"physical-security","question":"A data center entrance must guarantee that the outer door fully closes before the inner door can open, and that each person authenticates alone in the space between them. Which of the following meets this requirement?","options":["A turnstile","Double swing doors","An access control vestibule","A badge reader on the inner door"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An access control vestibule. Its two interlocked doors never open at the same time, holding a single person between them to authenticate alone; a turnstile loses because it admits one person per rotation but has no interlocked second door or holding space. The tell: vestibule = interlocked double doors admitting one authenticated person at a time, turnstile = single rotating gate, badge reader alone = checks credentials but cannot stop someone slipping through behind an employee."},
  {"id":"q052","domain":"D1","family":"deception","question":"A security engineer plants a fake set of database credentials in a configuration file on a web server. The credentials grant no real access, but any attempt to use them anywhere fires a high-priority alert. Which of the following deception techniques is this?","options":["honeypot","honeyfile","honeytoken","honeynet"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Honeytoken. Bait credentials or records that alert when used are honeytokens; the config file is just the delivery spot. A honeyfile would be the bait file itself being opened, not a credential being replayed. The tell: honeytoken = bait credential/record that fires on use, honeyfile = bait file, honeypot = one decoy system, honeynet = a whole decoy network."},
  {"id":"q053","domain":"D1","family":"deception","question":"Instead of a single decoy host, a threat research team builds an entire simulated environment - fake servers, workstations, and generated traffic - to attract intruders and study their tools over weeks. Which of the following deception technologies is this?","options":["honeynet","honeypot","honeyfile","honeytoken"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Honeynet. A whole network of decoys is a honeynet; one decoy system alone would be a honeypot. The tell: honeynet = a whole decoy network, honeypot = one decoy system, honeyfile = a bait file, honeytoken = a bait credential/record."},
  {"id":"q054","domain":"D1","family":"deception","question":"Security engineers stand up a single intentionally vulnerable decoy server on an internet-facing segment purely to attract attackers and record their techniques. Which of the following deception technologies is this?","options":["honeypot","honeynet","honeyfile","honeytoken"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Honeypot. One decoy system built to lure and observe attackers is a honeypot; it would take a whole decoy network to be a honeynet. The tell: honeypot = one decoy system, honeynet = a whole decoy network, honeyfile = a bait file, honeytoken = a bait credential/record."},
  {"id":"q055","domain":"D1","family":"deception","question":"A team plants a spreadsheet named passwords.xlsx on a file share. It contains nothing real, but any attempt to open or copy it triggers an alert because no legitimate user has a reason to touch it. Which of the following deception techniques is this?","options":["honeynet","honeytoken","honeyfile","honeypot"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Honeyfile. A bait file whose access fires an alert is a honeyfile. If the bait were a fake credential being used elsewhere, that would be a honeytoken. The tell: honeyfile = bait file, honeytoken = bait credential/record, honeypot = one decoy system, honeynet = a decoy network."},
  {"id":"q056","domain":"D1","family":"deception","question":"A security engineer commits a fake cloud API key to an internal code repository. Months later an alert fires the instant the key is used from an unknown IP address. Which deception technique is this?","options":["Honeyfile","Honeypot","Honeynet","Honeytoken"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Honeytoken. The bait is a piece of data, a credential with no legitimate use, so any attempt to use it proves theft and traces the leak; honeyfile loses because a honeyfile alerts when a bait file is opened or copied, while this alert fired on the credential being used. The tell: honeytoken = bait data or credential that alerts on use, honeyfile = bait file that alerts on access, honeypot = one decoy system, honeynet = a whole decoy network."},
  {"id":"q057","domain":"D1","family":"deception","question":"A SOC training slide lists three deception tools: a single decoy server, an entire simulated environment of fake hosts and traffic, and a planted bait document. Which of the following names them in order?","options":["Honeypot, honeynet, honeyfile","Honeynet, honeypot, honeytoken","Honeypot, honeyfile, honeynet","Honeyfile, honeynet, honeypot"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Honeypot, honeynet, honeyfile. One decoy system is a honeypot, a whole simulated network of decoys is a honeynet, and a planted bait document is a honeyfile; honeytoken loses for the third item because it is bait data or a credential that alerts on use, not a document. The tell: honeypot = one decoy system, honeynet = a decoy network, honeyfile = a bait file, honeytoken = bait credential or record."},
  {"id":"q058","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A change ticket for a core-switch firmware upgrade includes a section listing the exact commands to reload the previous firmware image if post-upgrade validation fails. Which of the following change management elements is this section?","options":["impact analysis","backout plan","maintenance window","test results"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Backout plan. Documented steps to undo a change and return to the last known-good state are the backout plan. The tell: backout plan = how to undo, impact analysis = what could break, maintenance window = the approved time slot, test results = evidence it worked in staging."},
  {"id":"q059","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A change advisory board approves a disruptive database migration but requires it to run between 1:00 a.m. and 5:00 a.m. Sunday, when the fewest users are connected. Which of the following change management elements is this time slot?","options":["impact analysis","approval process","backout plan","maintenance window"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Maintenance window. The pre-approved low-impact period in which the change may be executed is the maintenance window. The tell: maintenance window = approved time to make the change, approval process = the sign-off itself, impact analysis = what could break, backout plan = how to undo."},
  {"id":"q060","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"Before approving a database schema change, the review board requires a written assessment of every downstream system that could break, including the reporting jobs and the mobile app. Which of the following change management elements is this assessment?","options":["backout plan","impact analysis","test results","maintenance window"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Impact analysis. Documenting what could break and how badly is the impact analysis, done before approval. The tell: impact analysis = what could break, backout plan = how to undo, test results = evidence from staging, maintenance window = the approved time slot."},
  {"id":"q061","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A network team stores every revision of its firewall configuration in a repository, so any change can be attributed, compared, and rolled back to an earlier version. Which of the following change management practices is this?","options":["version control","standard operating procedure","approval process","impact analysis"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Version control. Tracking revisions of configs or code with history and rollback is version control. The tell: version control = track revisions over time, standard operating procedure = the documented repeatable process, approval process = the sign-off gate, impact analysis = what could break."},
  {"id":"q062","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A security analyst reviewing production change records finds this entry: 14:02 firewall rule 88 modified by jdoe; change ticket: none; CAB record: none. Which change management process was bypassed?","options":["Approval process","Backout plan","Impact analysis","Maintenance window"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Approval process. A production rule changed with no ticket and no change advisory board record means the change was never authorized, and formal approval is the first gate every change must pass; maintenance window loses because the log shows missing authorization, not work performed outside an approved time slot. The tell: approval process = authorization before the change, maintenance window = the approved time to make it, impact analysis = predicting what could break, backout plan = how to undo it."},
  {"id":"q063","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A change ticket names one engineer accountable for driving the change through to completion and separately lists the business units the change could affect. Which of the following pairs the two entries with the correct change management elements?","options":["The engineer is a stakeholder; the business units are owners","The engineer is the approver; the business units are owners","The engineer is the owner; the business units are stakeholders","The engineer is the owner; the business units are approvers"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The engineer is the owner; the business units are stakeholders. Ownership names the individual accountable end to end for the change being completed and documented, while stakeholders are the affected parties who must be identified and consulted; reversing the pair fails because affected departments are consulted, not accountable. The tell: ownership = the accountable driver of the change, stakeholders = the affected parties, approval = the change advisory board's authorization to proceed."},
  {"id":"q064","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A change advisory board reviews two sections of a ticket: a written prediction of which downstream systems could break, and the captured output of a successful staging run. Which of the following pairings names these two elements?","options":["Test results; impact analysis","Impact analysis; backout plan","Impact analysis; test results","Backout plan; test results"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Impact analysis, then test results. The written prediction of what could break is the impact analysis, and the captured staging output is the test results section; the reversed pairing loses because a prediction is written before any testing, not produced by it. The tell: impact analysis = predicting what could break BEFORE the change; test results = evidence from staging or a sandbox; backout plan = the procedure to undo the change if it fails."},
  {"id":"q065","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"At 02:30, midway through an approved window, a firmware upgrade leaves the application down and post-change validation fails. Which of the following should the change team do NEXT?","options":["Restore the latest full data backup","Execute the documented backout plan","Submit a new change request for approval","Revert the repository in version control"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Backout plan. Validation failed mid-window, so the team executes the procedure written before the change to reverse it and restore the prior working state; restoring a data backup loses because the failure is the firmware change itself, not lost data, and any restore step would already live inside the plan. The tell: backout plan = the pre-documented procedure to undo a failed change; backup = a copy of data; version control = the revision record that can support a rollback, not the emergency procedure."},
  {"id":"q066","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"A manager wants monthly certificate renewals performed identically by every administrator, so new hires follow one documented step-by-step method for this routine change. Which of the following change management elements does this describe?","options":["Standard operating procedure","Security policy","Backout plan","Approval process"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Standard operating procedure. A documented, repeatable step-by-step method keeps a routine change consistent and reduces human error, even when new staff perform it; a security policy loses because it is a high-level mandate, not step-level instructions. The tell: SOP = how to do the routine change step by step; policy = the high-level requirement; backout plan = how to undo the change; approval process = the authorization gate before it runs."},
  {"id":"q067","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"Weeks after a subnet migration, an on-call engineer troubleshoots from a network map that shows the old addressing, and the renewal procedure still lists retired IP addresses. Which of the following change closure tasks were skipped?","options":["Performing impact analysis; attaching test results","Updating diagrams; updating policies and procedures","Obtaining approval; identifying stakeholders","Writing a backout plan; scheduling a maintenance window"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Updating diagrams and updating policies/procedures. Documentation is part of the change itself, so closure requires revising the network map and every affected procedure; the impact analysis pairing loses because those are inputs prepared BEFORE approval, and this failure appears weeks after implementation. The tell: diagram and procedure updates = mandatory closure tasks after the change; impact analysis and test results = pre-approval inputs; approval, stakeholders, backout plan, and window = gates set before the change runs."},
  {"id":"q068","domain":"D1","family":"change-management","question":"Investigating a routing issue, an engineer compares stored router configurations: Tue 14:02 jsmith: gateway 10.0.0.1; Thu 09:15 mlopez: gateway 10.0.0.254. Which of the following practices makes this attributed comparison and a rollback to Tuesday's version possible?","options":["A backout plan","A full data backup","Version control","An impact analysis"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Version control. Storing every revision with author, timestamp, and content lets the engineer diff two points in time and revert to a known-good configuration; a backout plan loses because it is the undo procedure written for one specific change, not an ongoing attributed history. The tell: version control = the tracked revision history enabling diff, attribution, and revert; backout plan = the undo procedure for a single change; backup = a copy of data with no change-by-change attribution."},
  {"id":"q069","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"A fleet of corporate laptops uses full-disk encryption. Each laptop seals its disk-encryption key inside a dedicated cryptographic chip soldered to its own motherboard, which also validates boot integrity before releasing the key. Which of the following components is being used?","options":["TPM","HSM","secure enclave","key management system"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"TPM. A per-device crypto chip on the motherboard handling boot integrity and the disk key is the Trusted Platform Module. The tell: TPM = crypto chip built into ONE endpoint's motherboard, HSM = dedicated appliance serving keys to many systems, secure enclave = isolated region on the CPU itself, key management system = software key-lifecycle service."},
  {"id":"q070","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"A payment processor must generate, store, and use thousands of signing keys inside tamper-resistant dedicated hardware that serves an entire rack of application servers at high transaction rates. Which of the following solutions BEST fits the requirement?","options":["TPM","key escrow","HSM","secure enclave"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"HSM. Scale (thousands of keys, many servers) plus tamper-resistant dedicated hardware points to a hardware security module appliance. A TPM serves only its own device. The tell: HSM = appliance generating/storing keys for many systems, TPM = one endpoint's chip, key escrow = third party holds key copies, secure enclave = isolated CPU region."},
  {"id":"q071","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"To satisfy a regulatory requirement, a company deposits copies of its data-encryption keys with a bonded third party so the data can still be recovered if the keys are lost or a court compels access. Which of the following arrangements is this?","options":["key escrow","key management system","HSM","TPM"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Key escrow. A third party holding key copies for recovery or lawful access is the defining trait of escrow; no hardware is implied at all. The tell: key escrow = third party holds a copy, key management system = software lifecycle service you run, HSM = your own hardware appliance, TPM = one endpoint's chip."},
  {"id":"q072","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"A smartphone maker stores fingerprint templates and payment keys in an isolated coprocessor region that the main operating system cannot read, so the secrets survive even if the phone is rooted. Which of the following components is this?","options":["TPM","secure enclave","HSM","key escrow"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Secure enclave. An isolated region on the device's own processor protecting keys and biometrics is a secure enclave, typical of phones. The tell: secure enclave = isolated coprocessor region on the CPU/phone, TPM = separate chip on a PC motherboard, HSM = external appliance for many systems, key escrow = third party holds keys."},
  {"id":"q073","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"A DevOps team needs a single software service that creates, distributes, rotates, and revokes the encryption keys used by dozens of microservices, with the full lifecycle automated through an API. Which of the following solutions BEST fits?","options":["HSM","TPM","secure enclave","key management system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Key management system. Lifecycle operations (create, rotate, revoke) delivered as a software service define a KMS; it may use an HSM underneath, but the requirement here is the management layer. The tell: key management system = software key-lifecycle service, HSM = the hardware that stores keys, TPM = one endpoint's chip, secure enclave = isolated CPU region."},
  {"id":"q074","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"An architect comparing two options notes that the first is built into each individual endpoint and protects only that device's own disk key, while the second is a networked appliance serving keys to many servers. Which of the following terms matches the first, per-endpoint option?","options":["HSM","TPM","key management system","key escrow"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"TPM. Built into one endpoint for that device's keys = TPM; the networked appliance described second is the HSM. This one-versus-many split is the classic TPM/HSM discriminator. The tell: TPM = one device's motherboard chip, HSM = shared appliance at scale, key management system = software lifecycle layer, key escrow = third party holds copies."},
  {"id":"q075","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"An employee is terminated, and the legal team must lawfully decrypt project files that were protected with the employee's own encryption key. The organization retrieves a copy of the key that was stored for exactly this situation. Which of the following arrangements made that possible?","options":["HSM","secure enclave","key escrow","TPM"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Key escrow. Recovering a departed user's key from a held copy is the escrow use case: a trusted party keeps key copies for recovery or legal access. The tell: key escrow = held copy for recovery/legal access, HSM = hardware appliance serving live keys, secure enclave = isolated CPU region, TPM = one endpoint's chip."},
  {"id":"q076","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"Which of the following terms describes a dedicated, tamper-resistant hardware appliance that generates and stores cryptographic keys and offloads crypto operations for many systems at once?","options":["TPM","secure enclave","key escrow","HSM"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"HSM. Dedicated appliance, tamper-resistant, many systems: that is the hardware security module. The tell: HSM = appliance for keys at scale, TPM = chip on ONE endpoint's motherboard, secure enclave = isolated region inside a CPU, key escrow = a third party holding key copies."},
  {"id":"q077","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"Which of the following components is an isolated region inside a device's main processor that keeps keys and biometric data protected even when the operating system itself is fully compromised?","options":["key management system","secure enclave","TPM","HSM"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Secure enclave. It lives inside the main CPU and stays isolated from the OS, unlike a TPM, which is its own separate chip on the board. The tell: secure enclave = isolated region on the CPU, TPM = discrete motherboard chip, HSM = external appliance, key management system = software lifecycle service."},
  {"id":"q078","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"An encryption key used by dozens of microservices is found exposed in a public repository. The security team must revoke it and issue a replacement to every service from a single control point within minutes. Which of the following provides this capability?","options":["key escrow","HSM","TPM","key management system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Key management system. Revoking a compromised key and issuing a replacement across many services is active lifecycle management, the KMS's job. Key escrow is tempting after a key incident, but a held recovery copy cannot revoke or distribute anything. The tell: key management system = active create/rotate/revoke lifecycle, key escrow = stored third-party copy for recovery/legal access, HSM = hardware vault that stores keys, TPM = one endpoint's chip."},
  {"id":"q079","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"Before a workstation may join the network, a network access control check requires the machine to remotely attest its measured boot state, which is recorded and signed by a dedicated chip on its own motherboard. Which of the following components provides this attestation?","options":["HSM","secure enclave","key management system","TPM"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"TPM. Storing boot measurements and attesting a single machine's boot integrity is the Trusted Platform Module's job; an HSM is tempting hardware but is a shared appliance that never sees an endpoint's boot process. The tell: TPM = one endpoint's motherboard chip (boot measurements, disk keys), HSM = external appliance serving many systems, secure enclave = isolated CPU region, key management system = software lifecycle service."},
  {"id":"q080","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"A certificate authority keeps its root signing key inside a FIPS-validated hardware appliance; certificates are signed inside the device and the key never leaves it. Which of the following components is this?","options":["HSM","TPM","key escrow","secure enclave"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"HSM. A dedicated, certified appliance performing signing operations internally so the key never leaves is a hardware security module, the standard way CAs guard root keys. The tell: HSM = dedicated appliance, keys used inside it, TPM = one endpoint's chip, key escrow = third party holds copies, secure enclave = isolated CPU region."},
  {"id":"q081","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"Which of the following terms specifically means a third party holds a copy of your keys for later recovery or lawful access, rather than a hardware appliance that generates and guards keys in active production use?","options":["HSM","key escrow","secure enclave","TPM"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Key escrow. The discriminator is who holds it and why: a copy held elsewhere for recovery = escrow; hardware guarding live keys = HSM. The tell: key escrow = held copy, recovery/legal access, HSM = active hardware vault at scale, secure enclave = isolated CPU region, TPM = one endpoint's chip."},
  {"id":"q082","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"In a bank's architecture review, one option orchestrates create, rotate, and revoke operations across many applications in software, while a separate hardware device actually performs the cryptographic operations. Which of the following terms names the orchestration option?","options":["HSM","TPM","key management system","secure enclave"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Key management system. The KMS is the software brain managing the key lifecycle; the hardware doing the crypto is the HSM. The tell: key management system = software orchestration of the key lifecycle, HSM = hardware performing the crypto, TPM = one endpoint's chip, secure enclave = isolated CPU region."},
  {"id":"q083","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-key-handling","question":"A defense contractor requires a FIPS-validated hardware module that generates and stores signing keys for multiple systems and zeroizes every stored key the instant its enclosure is opened. Which of the following components meets this requirement?","options":["TPM","key escrow","secure enclave","HSM"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"HSM. A certified, tamper-responsive appliance that serves keys to multiple systems and erases them on physical intrusion is a hardware security module; a TPM is tempting hardware but protects only its own single endpoint. The tell: HSM = tamper-resistant appliance for keys at scale, TPM = one endpoint's chip, secure enclave = isolated CPU region, key escrow = a third party holding copies."},
  {"id":"q084","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"During a password audit, an engineer notices that two users who chose the identical password have completely different stored hashes, because a unique random value is combined with each password before hashing. Which of the following techniques causes this?","options":["hashing","salting","key stretching","symmetric encryption"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Salting. A unique per-password random value makes identical passwords hash differently and defeats rainbow tables. The tell: salting = random per-password value before hashing, key stretching = many iterations to slow guessing, hashing = the one-way function itself, symmetric encryption = reversible with a shared key."},
  {"id":"q085","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"A developer configures bcrypt with a high work factor so that verifying one password takes a fraction of a second but brute forcing millions of guesses becomes computationally impractical. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["salting","hashing","key exchange","key stretching"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Key stretching. Deliberately expensive, iterated hashing (bcrypt, PBKDF2) exists to make each guess costly. Salting defeats precomputation but adds no compute cost. The tell: key stretching = make each guess expensive, salting = unique random value per password, hashing = one-way fingerprint, key exchange = agreeing on a shared secret."},
  {"id":"q086","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"Two servers need to agree on a shared session secret across an untrusted network without the secret ever being transmitted, so they perform a Diffie-Hellman negotiation. Which of the following terms describes this process?","options":["key exchange","asymmetric encryption","symmetric encryption","hashing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Key exchange. Diffie-Hellman lets both sides derive the same secret without sending it, which is the definition of key exchange. The tell: key exchange = safely agree on a shared key, asymmetric = public/private pair operations, symmetric = using the one shared key for bulk data, hashing = one-way integrity fingerprint."},
  {"id":"q087","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"A site-to-site VPN encrypts a multi-gigabyte nightly transfer quickly using one shared secret key that both endpoints already hold. Which of the following cryptographic methods is doing the bulk encryption?","options":["asymmetric encryption","symmetric encryption","key exchange","hashing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Symmetric encryption. One shared key and high speed for bulk data are the symmetric signature; asymmetric is too slow for the payload and is used to set up the key instead. The tell: symmetric = one shared key, fast, bulk data, asymmetric = key pair, slower, exchange and signatures, key exchange = agreeing on the key, hashing = one-way fingerprint."},
  {"id":"q088","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"A secure email system gives each user a key pair: anyone can encrypt a message to the user with one key, but only the user's other, secret key can decrypt it. Which of the following cryptographic methods is this?","options":["asymmetric encryption","symmetric encryption","salting","key stretching"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Asymmetric encryption. A public/private key pair where the public key encrypts and only the private key decrypts is asymmetric cryptography. The tell: asymmetric = public/private pair, symmetric = one shared key for both directions, salting = random value added to passwords, key stretching = making password guesses expensive."},
  {"id":"q089","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"Reviewing an HTTPS handshake, an engineer notes that public key operations run only at the start of the session to establish a shared secret before fast symmetric encryption takes over. Which of the following processes occurs at the start?","options":["Key stretching","Key exchange","Salting","Hashing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Key exchange. Asymmetric operations run once at session start to securely agree on the symmetric session key that then encrypts the bulk traffic - the hybrid model TLS uses; key stretching loses because it hardens stored passwords, not live sessions. The tell: key exchange = securely agreeing on a shared session key; key stretching = thousands of hash iterations to slow password guessing; salting = per-password randomness; hashing = a one-way integrity digest."},
  {"id":"q090","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"A password storage review lists two protections: a random per-password value that defeats precomputed tables, and thousands of hash iterations that make each guess expensive. Which of the following pairings names them in order?","options":["Key stretching; salting","Salting; hashing","Salting; key stretching","Hashing; key stretching"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Salting, then key stretching. The random per-password value adds uniqueness so rainbow tables cannot be precomputed, and the iterations add cost so each offline guess is slow; the reversed pairing loses because iterations alone do nothing against tables precomputed for unsalted hashes. The tell: salting = uniqueness, kills rainbow tables; key stretching = cost per guess, slows brute force; hashing = the one-way function both protections build on."},
  {"id":"q091","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-methods","question":"A vendor's download page publishes SHA-256: 3f7a9c21 next to an installer. A technician downloads the file, recomputes the digest, and gets the identical value. Which of the following functions and properties makes this integrity check work?","options":["Encryption; only the key holder can read the file","Digital signature; the vendor signs each file with its private key","Salting; a random value makes every digest unique","Hashing; any input change produces a different digest"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Hashing. A one-way function produces a fixed-length digest, and any alteration to the installer would yield a completely different value, so a matching digest confirms the file is unmodified; digital signature loses because nothing here is signed - the technician simply recomputes and compares a published hash. The tell: hashing = a one-way integrity fingerprint; digital signature = a hash encrypted with the sender's private key; encryption = reversible confidentiality; salting = per-password uniqueness for stored credentials."},
  {"id":"q092","domain":"D1","family":"pki-signatures","question":"An analyst using public key cryptography must email a confidential report so that only the intended recipient can open it. Which of the following keys should the analyst use to encrypt the file?","options":["the recipient's public key","the recipient's private key","the sender's public key","the sender's private key"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The recipient's public key. Only the matching private key, held by the recipient alone, can decrypt it, which delivers confidentiality. The tell: encrypt for secrecy = recipient's PUBLIC key, sign for proof of origin = sender's PRIVATE key. Sender keys prove identity, recipient keys protect delivery."},
  {"id":"q093","domain":"D1","family":"pki-signatures","question":"Maria must prove to business partners that a price list really came from her and was not altered in transit, so she digitally signs it before sending. Which of the following keys creates the signature?","options":["the sender's public key","the sender's private key","the recipient's public key","the recipient's private key"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The sender's private key. Signing means encrypting the document hash with her own private key so anyone can verify with her public key, giving integrity plus non-repudiation. The tell: sign = sender's PRIVATE key, verify = sender's public key, encrypt for secrecy = recipient's public key."},
  {"id":"q094","domain":"D1","family":"pki-signatures","question":"Which of the following technologies stores transactions in blocks, where each block contains a hash of the previous one, so that altering any historical record invalidates every block that follows?","options":["open public ledger","digital signature","blockchain","hashing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Blockchain. Chained, hashed blocks are the structure itself; the open public ledger is the visible record built on top of it, and hashing is just the primitive used. The tell: blockchain = chained hashed blocks, open public ledger = the publicly viewable transaction record, digital signature = signed hash proving origin, hashing = one-way fingerprint."},
  {"id":"q095","domain":"D1","family":"pki-signatures","question":"A hash of a message is computed and then encrypted with the sender's private key, letting any recipient verify both that the message is unaltered and who sent it. Which of the following terms describes this construct?","options":["blockchain","hashing","open public ledger","digital signature"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Digital signature. Hash plus sender's-private-key encryption is the definition, and it yields integrity plus non-repudiation. The tell: digital signature = encrypted hash proving origin and integrity, hashing = the fingerprint alone, blockchain = chained hashed blocks, open public ledger = the public transaction record."},
  {"id":"q096","domain":"D1","family":"pki-signatures","question":"A recipient wants to verify that a digitally signed purchase order really came from Raj and was not modified. Which of the following keys does the recipient use to check the signature?","options":["Raj's private key","Raj's public key","the recipient's private key","the recipient's public key"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Raj's public key. Raj signed with his private key, so anyone verifies with his public key; if it validates, only Raj's private key could have made it. The tell: sign = sender's private key, verify = sender's public key, encrypt for secrecy = recipient's public key, decrypt = recipient's private key."},
  {"id":"q097","domain":"D1","family":"pki-signatures","question":"A logistics consortium wants every member and outside auditor able to read and independently verify the full history of shipment transactions, which no single party can alter. Which of the following BEST names that publicly verifiable record?","options":["A blockchain","A digital signature","A hash digest","An open public ledger"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Open public ledger. The publicly readable, independently verifiable record of all transactions that no single party can quietly alter is the open public ledger; blockchain loses because it names the underlying chained-hash technology that implements the ledger, not the publicly viewable record itself. The tell: open public ledger = the record anyone can read and verify; blockchain = the tamper-evident chained-block structure behind it; digital signature = per-message authenticity; hash digest = a single integrity fingerprint."},
  {"id":"q098","domain":"D1","family":"obfuscation","question":"A DLP investigation finds that an insider hid customer records inside the pixel data of ordinary-looking vacation photos before emailing them out; every image opens and displays normally. Which of the following techniques did the insider use?","options":["tokenization","data masking","encryption","steganography"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Steganography. Hiding data inside innocent-looking media so no one suspects a payload exists is steganography; encryption would scramble the data but not disguise its presence. The tell: steganography = hide data inside other media, tokenization = swap value for a vault-mapped token, data masking = show only partial values, encryption = scrambled but visibly protected."},
  {"id":"q099","domain":"D1","family":"obfuscation","question":"A retailer's payment platform replaces each stored card number with a random surrogate value; the real number can be recovered only by querying a hardened vault service. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["data masking","steganography","tokenization","encryption"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Tokenization. A substitute value with a vault lookup back to the original is tokenization; masking is irreversible display hiding, and encryption transforms the value mathematically rather than replacing it. The tell: tokenization = token maps back via a vault, data masking = XXXX-1234 style partial display, steganography = hidden inside media, encryption = reversible with a key."},
  {"id":"q100","domain":"D1","family":"obfuscation","question":"A support portal shows an agent a customer's card number as XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-1234. The hidden digits are permanently obscured in the display layer and cannot be recovered from what the agent sees. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["tokenization","data masking","steganography","encryption"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data masking. Showing only a partial value with the rest irrecoverably hidden is masking; tokenization would substitute a reversible vault-mapped value, and encryption is mathematically reversible with the key. The tell: data masking = partial/scrambled display, not reversible, tokenization = token maps back via a vault, steganography = hidden inside media, encryption = reversible with a key."},
  {"id":"q101","domain":"D1","family":"obfuscation","question":"A retailer must ensure that a breach of its order database yields values with no mathematical relationship to real card numbers, while recurring billing jobs can still retrieve the originals. Which of the following techniques BEST meets both requirements?","options":["encryption","data masking","steganography","tokenization"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tokenization. Random surrogate values have no mathematical link to the originals, yet a vault lookup still lets billing recover the real numbers. Encryption is tempting because it also protects stored data, but ciphertext is mathematically derived from the plaintext and falls with the key. The tell: tokenization = random substitute that maps back via a vault, encryption = reversible math with a key, data masking = partial/scrambled display only, steganography = data hidden inside media."},
  {"id":"q102","domain":"D1","family":"obfuscation","question":"Before production customer data is copied into a developer test environment, an engineer irreversibly replaces names and card digits with scrambled values that no key or vault can ever recover. Which of the following techniques is being used?","options":["Tokenization","Data masking","Encryption","Steganography"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data masking. The values are permanently obscured at the dataset level so developers can work with realistic-looking records while nothing can recover the originals; tokenization loses because tokens map back to the real values through a vault, which is exactly what this requirement forbids. The tell: masking = irreversible display or dataset obscuring; tokenization = reversible via the token vault; encryption = reversible with a key; steganography = hiding data inside other media."},
  {"id":"q103","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"A database administrator must encrypt only the individual rows holding VIP customers' data, so each of those entries is protected separately while the rest of the table remains readable by reporting jobs. Which of the following encryption levels is this?","options":["database","volume","file","record"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Record. Encrypting individual rows/fields inside a table is record-level encryption, the finest granularity. Database-level would cover everything and break the readable-reporting requirement. The tell: record = single row/field, database = the whole database, file = individual files, volume = a logical volume."},
  {"id":"q104","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"Which of the following encryption levels protects an entire physical drive, including the operating system, swap space, and every file, and unlocks once at boot time?","options":["partition","volume","full-disk","file"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Full-disk. The entire drive including OS and swap, unlocked at boot, is full-disk encryption. The tell: full-disk = the whole physical drive, partition = one partition of it, volume = a logical volume, file = individual files only."},
  {"id":"q105","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"A consultant encrypts three client contracts stored in a project folder but leaves the operating system and every other file on the laptop unencrypted. Which of the following encryption levels is the consultant using?","options":["full-disk encryption","volume encryption","database encryption","file-level encryption"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"File-level. Protecting individually chosen files while the rest of the disk stays readable is file-level encryption. The tell: file = individual files, full-disk = the entire drive including the OS, volume = a logical volume, database = an entire data store."},
  {"id":"q106","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"A company applies transparent encryption to its entire customer database, protecting the whole data store as one unit rather than selecting individual tables or rows. Which of the following encryption levels is this?","options":["record-level encryption","file-level encryption","database encryption","partition encryption"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Database. Encrypting the whole database as a unit is database-level encryption; row-by-row protection would be record-level. The tell: database = the whole data store, record = a single row/field, file = individual files, partition = one disk partition."},
  {"id":"q107","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"A security engineer describes three encryption options: the whole physical drive including the OS, unlocked once at boot; a logical storage container; and one slice of a single disk. Which of the following pairings matches the options in order?","options":["Full-disk; volume; partition","Full-disk; partition; volume","Volume; full-disk; partition","Partition; volume; full-disk"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Full-disk, volume, partition. The entire physical drive with the OS unlocked at boot is full-disk encryption, a logical container is a volume, and one slice of a single disk is a partition; the second pairing loses by swapping the logical container with the physical slice. The tell: full-disk = whole physical drive including the OS; volume = logical storage unit that can span disks; partition = a physical slice of one disk."},
  {"id":"q108","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"An administrator encrypts the logical unit holding project data, which spans two physical disks, while the operating system disk remains unencrypted. Which of the following encryption levels is in use?","options":["Partition","Full-disk","File","Volume"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Volume encryption. Only the logical storage unit holding the data is protected, and a volume can span multiple physical disks; partition loses because a partition is a slice of ONE disk and cannot span two. The tell: volume = a logical unit that may span disks; partition = a physical slice of a single disk; full-disk = the whole drive including the OS; file = individual documents."},
  {"id":"q109","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"A database administrator compares two designs: transparent encryption of the entire customer data store at rest, and encrypting only the national ID column so analyst queries return unreadable values. Which of the following pairings of encryption levels is correct?","options":["Record; database","Database; record","Database; file","Volume; record"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Database, then record. Transparent encryption of the whole data store is database-level protection at rest, while keeping one column unreadable even in query results is record-level; database-level alone loses for the column goal because a running database decrypts everything a query touches. The tell: database = the whole store protected at rest; record = individual rows or columns stay unreadable even to queries; file and volume = filesystem granularities outside the database."},
  {"id":"q110","domain":"D1","family":"encryption-levels","question":"A paralegal copies a protected contract from a laptop to a USB drive, and it remains unreadable at the destination because protection is applied to the document itself. Which of the following encryption levels is in use?","options":["Full-disk","Volume","File","Record"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"File encryption. Protection applied to the document itself travels with the file wherever it is copied; full-disk loses because it protects data only while it sits on the encrypted drive, so a copy to USB would land in plaintext. The tell: file = protection travels with each document; full-disk = the whole drive, transparent once booted; volume = a logical container; record = a field or row inside a database."},
  {"id":"q111","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"A browser must confirm in real time whether one specific server certificate has been revoked, without downloading the certificate authority's entire revocation list. Which of the following mechanisms provides this?","options":["CRL","OCSP","CSR","wildcard"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"OCSP. The Online Certificate Status Protocol queries the status of a single certificate in real time. The tell: OCSP = live per-certificate revocation check, CRL = downloaded batch list that can be stale, CSR = the request sent to get a cert, wildcard = one cert covering all subdomains."},
  {"id":"q112","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"An administrator wants a single certificate that secures mail.example.com, shop.example.com, and any subdomain the company adds later. Which of the following certificate types fits this requirement?","options":["wildcard","self-signed","root of trust","CRL"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Wildcard. A *.example.com certificate covers every current and future subdomain at that level. The tell: wildcard = covers *.domain subdomains, self-signed = no external CA vouches for it, root of trust = the anchor CA of the chain, CRL = revocation list."},
  {"id":"q113","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"Which of the following artifacts does an administrator generate on a server - containing its public key and identifying details - and then submit to a certificate authority in order to receive a signed certificate?","options":["CRL","OCSP","CSR","root of trust"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"CSR. The certificate signing request packages the public key and identity information for the CA to sign. The tell: CSR = the request you send to a CA, CRL = list of revoked certs, OCSP = live revocation lookup, root of trust = the anchor the chain terminates at."},
  {"id":"q114","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"A client downloads the certificate authority's published list of revoked certificates once every 24 hours, so a certificate revoked this morning may still be trusted this afternoon. Which of the following mechanisms is described?","options":["OCSP","CSR","wildcard","CRL"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"CRL. A periodically downloaded batch list of revoked certificates is the certificate revocation list, and its staleness window is its classic weakness versus OCSP. The tell: CRL = downloaded list, possibly stale, OCSP = real-time single-cert check, CSR = request for a new cert, wildcard = covers subdomains."},
  {"id":"q115","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"A developer generates a certificate for an internal test server and signs it with the server's own key. Browsers connecting to it warn users because no external authority vouches for the certificate. Which of the following certificate types is this?","options":["self-signed certificate","wildcard certificate","third-party certificate","root of trust"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Self-signed. Signed by its own subject with no CA in the chain, so clients have no reason to trust it and warn. The tell: self-signed = you vouch for yourself, third-party = a trusted CA vouches, wildcard = covers *.domain subdomains, root of trust = the anchor CA in a chain."},
  {"id":"q116","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"In a certificate chain presented by a web server, which of the following is the anchor certificate authority that the chain ultimately terminates at and that client devices inherently trust out of the box?","options":["certificate signing request","root of trust","OCSP","wildcard certificate"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Root of trust. Chains validate upward until they reach a pre-trusted anchor CA, the root of trust. The tell: root of trust = the anchor the chain ends at, certificate signing request = the request for a new cert, OCSP = live revocation check, wildcard = covers subdomains."},
  {"id":"q117","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"Which of the following entities is responsible for validating a requester's identity and then issuing the signed digital certificate that browsers and other clients will trust?","options":["certificate signing request","certificate revocation list","root of trust","certificate authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Certificate authority. The CA verifies identity and issues signed certificates; the CSR is what it receives, and the CRL is what it publishes when certs are revoked. The tell: certificate authority = verifies and issues, certificate signing request = the application for a cert, certificate revocation list = the published revocation list, root of trust = the anchor CA position in a chain."},
  {"id":"q118","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"A PKI training slide contrasts two revocation mechanisms: a list downloaded on a 24-hour cycle that can be stale between publishes, and a real-time query about one certificate during each handshake. Which of the following pairings is correct?","options":["CRL; OCSP","OCSP; CRL","CRL; CSR","OCSP; CSR"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CRL, then OCSP. The periodically downloaded list of revoked serial numbers can be stale until the next publish, while OCSP asks about one specific certificate in real time at handshake; the reversed pairing loses because OCSP never involves downloading the full list. The tell: CRL = batch downloaded revocation list with a staleness window; OCSP = real-time single-certificate status check; CSR = the request sent to obtain a certificate, unrelated to revocation."},
  {"id":"q119","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"An internal tool triggers the TLS error 'certificate verify failed: self signed certificate', while the company's public site presents a certificate issued by a publicly trusted authority. Which of the following pairings names the internal and public certificate types?","options":["Third-party; self-signed","Self-signed; third-party","Wildcard; third-party","Self-signed; wildcard"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Self-signed, then third-party. The internal tool's certificate is signed by its own key with no external authority vouching for it, which is exactly what the verify error reports, while the public site relies on an external, publicly trusted CA; the wildcard pairings lose because wildcard describes subdomain coverage, not who signed the certificate. The tell: self-signed = no third-party trust, browser warnings, acceptable internally; third-party = an external CA vouches, required for public services; wildcard = name coverage, orthogonal to the issuer."},
  {"id":"q120","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"An administrator generates a key pair on a web server and submits a request containing the public key and identity details to a certificate authority. Which of the following must remain on the server and NEVER be sent to the CA?","options":["The certificate signing request","The public key","The private key","The signed certificate"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The private key. The CSR carries only the public key and identity details, and the CA signs the certificate without ever needing the private key, which stays on the server it was generated on; the public key loses because distributing it is exactly the point of the request. The tell: CSR = public key plus identity sent to the CA; private key = never leaves the server; signed certificate = what the CA returns after validation."},
  {"id":"q121","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"A security review of *.example.com notes that one compromised private key would expose mail, shop, and portal simultaneously, since all three subdomains are secured by a single certificate. Which of the following certificate types carries this trade-off?","options":["A self-signed certificate","A root CA certificate","A third-party certificate","A wildcard certificate"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Wildcard certificate. One *.example.com certificate covers every subdomain at that level, which simplifies management but means a single stolen private key exposes mail, shop, and portal together; a root CA certificate loses because the risk described sits on a leaf certificate's subdomain coverage, not the trust anchor. The tell: wildcard = one certificate for all subdomains at one level, shared-key blast radius; self-signed vs third-party = who vouches for the certificate; root CA = the anchor of the chain."},
  {"id":"q122","domain":"D1","family":"certificates","question":"A client fails validation with 'unable to get local issuer certificate' for this chain: leaf CN=app.example.com, Issuer=Example Issuing CA; intermediate CN=Example Issuing CA, Issuer=Example Root CA. Which of the following must the client's trust store contain as the trust anchor?","options":["The Example Root CA certificate","The Example Issuing CA certificate","The app.example.com leaf certificate","The server's certificate signing request"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The root CA certificate. Chain validation must terminate at a trust anchor already installed in the client's store, and this chain terminates at Example Root CA; the intermediate loses because issuing CAs are delivered in the presented chain and are vouched for by the root rather than preinstalled as anchors. The tell: root of trust/root CA = the preinstalled anchor the chain terminates at; intermediate CA = the issuing layer sent with the chain; leaf = the server's own certificate; CSR = the request, never part of validation."},
  {"id":"q123","domain":"D1","family":"physical-sensors","question":"An alarm vendor asks a technician to match three motion sensors to their detection principles: body heat, reflected radio waves, and reflected sound above human hearing. Which of the following matches is correct?","options":["Infrared = sound; microwave = heat; ultrasonic = radio waves","Infrared = heat; microwave = radio waves; ultrasonic = sound","Infrared = radio waves; microwave = sound; ultrasonic = heat","Infrared = heat; microwave = sound; ultrasonic = radio waves"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Infrared = heat; microwave = radio waves; ultrasonic = sound. Infrared sensors detect body heat and work in darkness, microwave sensors emit radio waves and detect changes in the reflections (covering large areas and penetrating some walls), and ultrasonic sensors emit sound above human hearing and detect its reflection; swapping microwave and ultrasonic is the classic error since both work by reflected emissions. The tell: infrared = body heat, microwave = reflected radio waves, ultrasonic = reflected sound waves, pressure = weight or contact."},
  {"id":"q124","domain":"D1","family":"physical-sensors","question":"A raised-floor pad beneath a restricted server aisle must alert the moment anyone steps on it, including a slow-moving intruder who emits very little detectable heat. Which sensor type fits this requirement?","options":["Infrared","Microwave","Ultrasonic","Pressure"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Pressure. A pressure sensor triggers on weight or contact with the pad, so it catches anyone who steps there regardless of heat or motion signature; infrared loses because it depends on detecting body heat, which the stem rules out. The tell: pressure = weight or contact, infrared = body heat, microwave = reflected radio waves, ultrasonic = reflected sound waves."},
  {"id":"q125","domain":"D1","family":"gap-analysis","question":"An organization adopting the NIST Cybersecurity Framework documents which required controls it lacks today and builds a prioritized remediation roadmap toward the framework. Which of the following BEST describes this activity?","options":["Risk assessment","Vulnerability scan","Penetration test","Gap analysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Gap analysis. It compares the current security posture against a defined target state, here the NIST CSF, and outputs a prioritized plan to close the shortfalls; risk assessment loses because it identifies and rates threats and impacts rather than measuring distance from a chosen framework. The tell: gap analysis = current state vs defined target state, risk assessment = identify and rate threats and impacts, vulnerability scan = find technical flaws, penetration test = exploit flaws to prove impact."},
  {"id":"q126","domain":"D1","family":"gap-analysis","question":"After acquiring a subsidiary, a security team maps the subsidiary's existing controls against the corporate security baseline to list every shortfall for internal planning. Which of the following BEST describes this exercise?","options":["Gap analysis","External audit","Vulnerability scan","Risk assessment"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Gap analysis. Comparing the subsidiary's current controls against the corporate baseline to produce a list of shortfalls is a control-level, current-versus-target comparison used for internal planning; an external audit loses because it is a formal conformance examination by an outside party, not an internal planning tool. The tell: gap analysis = internal comparison against a target state with a remediation roadmap, audit = formal conformance examination, vulnerability scan = technical flaw discovery, risk assessment = rate threats and impacts."},
  {"id":"q127","domain":"D1","family":"change-implications","question":"The morning after a new reporting tool rollout, a technician reviews an endpoint log: 09:02 process start reportgen.exe; 09:02 blocked by application execution policy. Which of the following change steps was MOST likely missed?","options":["Documenting a backout plan","Scheduling a maintenance window","Restarting the application service","Updating the application allow list"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Updating the application allow list. The endpoint only executes approved software, so deploying a new binary requires adding it to the allow list as part of the change; restarting the service loses because the block is a policy decision, not a hung process. The tell: a newly deployed app blocked by execution policy = allow list not updated; downtime, restarts, and dependency problems produce outages or ordering failures, not policy blocks."},
  {"id":"q128","domain":"D1","family":"change-implications","question":"During an approved patching window for one database server, a technician also quickly reconfigures a storage switch that is not listed in the change ticket. Which of the following change management concepts was violated?","options":["Restricted activities","Maintenance window","Impact analysis","Ownership"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Restricted activities. The approval defines the scope of what may be touched, and work on a device outside the ticket is itself a violation even when performed inside the window; maintenance window loses because the timing was respected - the scope was not. The tell: restricted activities = staying within the approved scope; maintenance window = staying within the approved time; ownership = who is accountable for the change; impact analysis = predicting what could break."},
  {"id":"q129","domain":"D1","family":"change-implications","question":"A ticket notes that a daemon configuration change is applied by bouncing only that daemon, while a code upgrade requires relaunching the whole application. Which of the following pairings names these two implications in order?","options":["Application restart; service restart","Service restart; application restart","Service restart; downtime","Dependency; application restart"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Service restart, then application restart. A daemon configuration change takes effect by restarting just that one service, the smallest disruption that applies it, while new code requires relaunching the entire application; the reversed pairing loses because it assigns the larger outage to the smaller change. The tell: service restart = bounce one daemon; application restart = relaunch the whole program; downtime = the planned unavailability itself; dependency = one component requiring another to change first."},
  {"id":"q130","domain":"D1","family":"change-implications","question":"A cluster upgrade will make the customer portal unreachable for 40 minutes, so the change owner schedules the outage, announces it to users in advance, and works to minimize it. Which of the following technical implications is being managed?","options":["Dependencies","Restricted activities","Downtime","Legacy applications"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Downtime. Planned unavailability is a technical implication of the change that must be scheduled, communicated ahead of time, and kept as short as possible; dependencies loses because nothing here requires one component to change before another - the portal is simply offline while the upgrade runs. The tell: downtime = the planned unavailability itself; dependencies = required ordering between components; restricted activities = staying inside the approved scope; legacy applications = fragile unsupported software raising change risk."},
  {"id":"q131","domain":"D1","family":"change-implications","question":"An operating system upgrade is repeatedly postponed because a 12-year-old billing application with no vendor support fails on anything newer than the current build. Which of the following technical implications of change does this illustrate?","options":["Dependencies","Downtime","Service restarts","Legacy applications"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Legacy applications. Old, unsupported, fragile software makes every change riskier and can pin the entire environment, blocking the OS upgrade; dependencies loses because that describes a required update ordering between supportable components, while here the blocker is the application's unsupported age itself. The tell: legacy application = unsupported and fragile, pins the environment; dependency = component A must change before component B; downtime = planned unavailability; service restart = the minimal action that applies a change."},
  {"id":"q132","domain":"D1","family":"change-implications","question":"A web application fails after a database server patch. Investigation shows the middleware required its own update before the database patch, an ordering no one had documented. Which of the following technical implications caused the failure?","options":["Dependencies","Legacy applications","Downtime","Restricted activities"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Dependencies. One component required another to be updated first, and the undocumented ordering broke the change; legacy applications loses because the middleware is supported and updatable - the problem is sequence, not age. The tell: dependency = required ordering between components, a top cause of failed changes; legacy application = unsupported fragile software; downtime = planned unavailability; restricted activities = working outside the approved scope."},
  {"id":"q133","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-algorithms","question":"A security architect checks a junior engineer's cheat sheet that maps common algorithms to their uses. Which of the following mappings is correct?","options":["AES: asymmetric key operations; RSA: symmetric bulk encryption; SHA-256: one-way hashing","AES: one-way hashing; RSA: asymmetric key operations; SHA-256: symmetric bulk encryption","AES: symmetric bulk encryption; RSA: one-way hashing; SHA-256: asymmetric key operations","AES: symmetric bulk encryption; RSA: asymmetric key operations; SHA-256: one-way hashing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"AES symmetric, RSA asymmetric, SHA-256 hashing. AES is the fast shared-key standard for bulk data, RSA uses a public/private pair for key exchange and signatures, and SHA-256 produces a one-way digest; the first option loses by swapping AES and RSA, the most common name-to-type mistake. The tell: AES = symmetric bulk cipher; RSA = asymmetric key pair operations; SHA-256 = a one-way hash, not encryption at all."},
  {"id":"q134","domain":"D1","family":"crypto-algorithms","question":"During a cryptography review, an auditor flags RSA-1024 as too weak while accepting AES-128 as strong. Which of the following BEST explains the finding?","options":["Symmetric and asymmetric key lengths are not directly comparable","A longer key always provides more protection regardless of algorithm","AES-128 applies key stretching to multiply its effective length","RSA-1024 is weak only when used for bulk data encryption"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Key lengths are not comparable across algorithm types. A 128-bit AES key resists brute force beyond any practical attack, while 1024-bit RSA falls to factoring advances and current guidance requires 2048 bits or more; the always-longer-is-stronger claim loses because it holds only within one algorithm family. The tell: compare key lengths only within the same algorithm type - AES-128 is strong, RSA-1024 is weak, RSA needs 2048+, and longer keys always cost performance."},
  {"id":"q135","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A loosely organized group defaces a city government's website and posts manifestos protesting a new surveillance ordinance. They demand no money and publicize the breach themselves. Which of the following threat actors BEST fits this profile?","options":["hacktivist","nation-state actor","organized crime","insider threat"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hacktivist. A political or philosophical message with publicity as the goal, and no profit motive, is the hacktivist profile. The tell: hacktivist = philosophical/political message, nation-state = espionage with deep resources, organized crime = financial gain, insider threat = trusted internal person."},
  {"id":"q136","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A security briefing describes an adversary with deep funding, custom tooling, multi-year persistence inside targets, and an espionage mission carried out on behalf of a foreign government. Which of the following threat actors is being described?","options":["organized crime","nation-state actor","hacktivist","unskilled attacker"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Nation-state actor. Resources, patience, and espionage on a government's behalf define the nation-state or APT profile. The tell: nation-state = highly resourced espionage/war, organized crime = financial gain, hacktivist = political message, unskilled attacker = low skill using others' tools."},
  {"id":"q137","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"An audit discovers the marketing team has been running an unapproved cloud database full of customer data for a year. They set it up to move faster and never told IT, though they meant no harm. Which of the following categories BEST fits this activity?","options":["insider threat","unskilled attacker","shadow IT","organized crime"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Shadow IT. Unsanctioned internal technology deployed without IT's knowledge, but without malicious intent, is shadow IT. A malicious trusted employee would be an insider threat. The tell: shadow IT = unsanctioned internal tech, no malice, insider threat = trusted person acting against the org, unskilled attacker = script-using outsider, organized crime = profit-driven group."},
  {"id":"q138","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A well-funded criminal syndicate runs ransomware campaigns against hospital networks, negotiates payouts through professional \"support\" channels, and reinvests profits in better tooling. Which of the following threat actors does this describe?","options":["hacktivist","unskilled attacker","nation-state","organized crime"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Organized crime. Professionalized operations aimed purely at financial gain define the organized-crime actor. The tell: organized crime = financial gain, nation-state = espionage/war with government backing, hacktivist = political message, unskilled attacker = low-skill script user."},
  {"id":"q139","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A systems administrator passed over for promotion begins quietly copying customer databases to personal storage using the legitimate access his role grants him. Which of the following threat actor categories BEST fits?","options":["shadow IT","unskilled attacker","insider threat","organized crime"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Insider threat. A trusted internal person abusing legitimate access, whether for revenge, profit, or ideology, is the insider threat. Shadow IT is unsanctioned tech without malicious intent. The tell: insider threat = trusted person acting against the org, shadow IT = unapproved tech, no malice, unskilled attacker = outsider with borrowed tools, organized crime = external profit-driven group."},
  {"id":"q140","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A teenager downloads an attack tool from a hacking forum and points it at a school's website without understanding how the tool works, defacing the homepage for bragging rights. Which of the following threat actors does this describe?","options":["nation-state actor","organized crime","insider threat","unskilled attacker"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Unskilled attacker. Using someone else's tools with little understanding, for low-stakes motives, is the unskilled attacker (script kiddie) profile. The tell: unskilled attacker = borrowed tools, low skill, nation-state = elite resources and espionage, organized crime = professional profit operations, insider threat = trusted internal person."},
  {"id":"q141","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"On his last day after being fired, a database administrator deletes the backup catalogs he once managed, purely to hurt the company that let him go. Which of the following motivations MOST likely drove this attack?","options":["espionage","revenge","financial gain","philosophical beliefs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Revenge. Sabotage by a wronged (ex-)insider to punish the organization is revenge-motivated. The tell: revenge = payback for a grievance, espionage = stealing secrets for an interested party, financial gain = money, philosophical = a cause or message."},
  {"id":"q142","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A group floods an oil company's website with traffic and defaces its investor page with climate slogans during the annual shareholder meeting. It asks for no payment. Which of the following motivations BEST fits?","options":["blackmail","data exfiltration","philosophical/political beliefs","financial gain"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Philosophical/political beliefs. Disruption paired with a cause message and no profit demand is ideological - the hacktivist motivation. The tell: philosophical/political = the message is the point, blackmail = pay or else, data exfiltration = stealing the data itself, financial gain = money as the goal."},
  {"id":"q143","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"An auditor must classify two findings: engineers running an unapproved personal cloud drive for convenience, and a payroll clerk secretly selling employee records. Which of the following classifies them correctly?","options":["Both are insider threats","The cloud drive is shadow IT; the clerk is an insider threat","The cloud drive is an insider threat; the clerk is shadow IT","Both are shadow IT"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Shadow IT versus insider threat turns on intent. Unsanctioned tech for convenience is shadow IT; a trusted person deliberately acting against the organization is an insider threat. The tell: shadow IT = unapproved but not malicious, insider threat = trusted access used to harm."},
  {"id":"q144","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A semiconductor firm uncovers a 14-month intrusion that used implant malware no security vendor has ever cataloged. The intruders copied proprietary chip designs but ignored payment systems and customer records entirely. Which of the following threat actors is MOST likely responsible?","options":["Organized crime","Hacktivist","Nation-state","Unskilled attacker"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Nation-state. A 14-month covert campaign using never-cataloged custom implants to steal strategic chip designs points to state-sponsored espionage; organized crime loses because nothing monetizable was touched. The tell: nation-state = deep funding, custom tooling, long persistence, espionage; organized crime = financial gain; hacktivist = public message for a cause; unskilled attacker = borrowed, noisy tools."},
  {"id":"q145","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A SOC analyst traces a burst of alerts to someone running an exploit script, downloaded from a public forum and unmodified, against random internet address ranges. The noisy traffic trips every IDS signature. Which of the following threat actors does this MOST likely indicate?","options":["Unskilled attacker","Nation-state","Organized crime","Insider threat"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Unskilled attacker. Running an unmodified public script against random ranges with zero stealth shows low skill and pure opportunism; organized crime loses because professionals customize tooling and pick profitable targets rather than spraying random addresses. The tell: unskilled attacker = others' tools, noisy and untargeted; nation-state = custom stealthy tooling; organized crime = professional profit-driven operations; insider threat = trusted internal access."},
  {"id":"q146","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"A group breaches an agrochemical company, publishes its internal safety memos, and posts a manifesto demanding a product recall, announcing the breach itself on social media. No payment is demanded. Which of the following threat actors BEST fits this profile?","options":["Organized crime","Hacktivist","Nation-state","Insider threat"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hacktivist. Leaking documents to force a policy change and self-publicizing the breach are ideology-driven acts seeking attention for a cause; organized crime loses because no money is sought anywhere. The tell: hacktivist = publicity for a philosophical or political cause; organized crime = financial gain; nation-state = covert espionage, never self-announced; insider threat = a trusted person inside the organization."},
  {"id":"q147","domain":"D2","family":"threat-actors","question":"Investigators map a ransomware operation in which one group sells initial network access, another develops the malware, a third staffs the payment negotiation portal, and money mules launder the proceeds. Which of the following threat actors does this structure indicate?","options":["Nation-state","Hacktivist","Unskilled attacker","Organized crime"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Organized crime. Access brokers, malware developers, negotiators, and money mules form a professional division of labor built entirely around ransom profits; nation-state loses because the whole structure exists to collect payments, not intelligence. The tell: organized crime = businesslike operations for financial gain; nation-state = espionage or war aims; hacktivist = cause and publicity; unskilled attacker = a lone actor with borrowed tools."},
  {"id":"q148","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-phish","question":"An employee receives a text message claiming to be from their bank: \"Unusual activity detected. Tap this link within 24 hours or your account will be locked.\" Which of the following techniques is being used?","options":["phishing","vishing","spear phishing","smishing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Smishing. The delivery channel decides the name, and this arrived by SMS text. The tell: smishing = SMS text, phishing = email, vishing = voice call, spear phishing = targeted at a specific person regardless of polish."},
  {"id":"q149","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-phish","question":"An attacker phones the help desk after hours, claims to be an executive boarding a flight, and pressures the agent into resetting the executive's password on the spot. Which of the following techniques is being used?","options":["vishing","smishing","phishing","spear phishing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Vishing. Social engineering delivered over a voice call is vishing, whatever story the caller uses. The tell: vishing = voice call, smishing = SMS, phishing = email, spear phishing = aimed at one specific target."},
  {"id":"q150","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-phish","question":"A security team traces a credential theft campaign to a fraudulent email blast sent to roughly 40,000 random recipients, hoping a small percentage click and sign in to a fake portal. Which of the following terms describes this campaign?","options":["smishing","phishing","vishing","spear phishing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Phishing. Broad, untargeted fraudulent email is plain phishing; had it been tailored to specific individuals it would be spear phishing. The tell: phishing = mass email, spear phishing = targeted email, vishing = voice, smishing = SMS."},
  {"id":"q151","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-phish","question":"A finance clerk receives a phone call from someone claiming to be the company's bank fraud department, who walks her through \"verifying\" her online banking credentials over the call. Which of the following social engineering techniques is being used?","options":["smishing","vishing","phishing","spear phishing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Vishing. The attack is delivered by voice call, which is the whole discriminator in this family. The tell: vishing = voice call, smishing = SMS text, phishing = email, spear phishing = tailored to one specific target."},
  {"id":"q152","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-phish","question":"A company's finance staff receive an email warning of a mailbox security issue, linking to a page that harvests their login credentials. No payment or transfer is requested. Which of the following BEST describes this technique?","options":["business email compromise (BEC)","smishing","pretexting","phishing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Phishing. A fraudulent email luring the target to a credential-harvesting page is phishing; BEC loses because the message neither comes from a spoofed or compromised executive mailbox nor requests a payment. The tell: phishing = email lure to steal credentials, BEC = executive mailbox angle plus a wire or gift-card request, pretexting = an invented backstory justifying an ask, smishing = SMS channel."},
  {"id":"q153","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-phish","question":"A new SOC analyst is memorizing the phishing family. Which of the following pairings of technique to delivery channel is correct?","options":["vishing = SMS, smishing = voice","phishing = email, vishing = voice, smishing = SMS","phishing = voice, vishing = email","smishing = email, phishing = SMS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Phishing = email, vishing = voice, smishing = SMS. The channel is the entire distinction in this family. The tell: v for voice, sm for SMS, and plain phishing keeps the original email channel."},
  {"id":"q154","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"A well-meaning employee shares a two-year-old article about a product recall in the company chat, believing it is current news. The facts have long since changed, but the employee has no intent to mislead anyone. Which of the following BEST describes this?","options":["disinformation","malinformation","misinformation","influence campaign"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Misinformation. False information passed along in honest error, with no intent to deceive, is misinformation. The tell: misinformation = false, no intent to deceive, disinformation = false and deliberately spread to deceive, influence campaign = coordinated effort to sway opinion at scale."},
  {"id":"q155","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"A fired executive knowingly fabricates stories about his former employer's finances and seeds them across investor forums under fake names, hoping to tank the stock before earnings. Which of the following BEST describes this campaign?","options":["misinformation","malinformation","influence campaign","disinformation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Disinformation. The content is false AND deliberately created to deceive, which is the defining difference from misinformation. The tell: disinformation = deliberate deception, misinformation = honest error, influence campaign = organized perception-shaping operation, malinformation = true info weaponized out of context."},
  {"id":"q156","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"Which of the following terms specifically means false information circulated without any intent to deceive - an honest mistake repeated in good faith?","options":["misinformation","disinformation","malinformation","influence campaign"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Misinformation. Intent is the discriminator: none here. The tell: misinformation = false, spread innocently, disinformation = false, spread deliberately to manipulate, malinformation = genuine info used to harm, influence campaign = the coordinated umbrella operation."},
  {"id":"q157","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"Which of the following terms means false information created and spread deliberately to deceive or manipulate an audience?","options":["misinformation","malinformation","disinformation","influence campaign"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Disinformation. Deliberate intent to deceive is the defining ingredient. The tell: disinformation = false and intentional, misinformation = false but innocent, malinformation = true info used maliciously, influence campaign = the coordinated operation that may use all three."},
  {"id":"q158","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"A company pays a click farm to plant fabricated one-star reviews, which it knows are untrue, to damage a competitor's product launch. Which of the following BEST describes the fabricated reviews?","options":["misinformation","malinformation","brand impersonation","disinformation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Disinformation. The reviews are known to be false and are planted deliberately to deceive buyers, which is the defining intent. Misinformation loses because the company knows the claims are untrue - there is no honest mistake. The tell: disinformation = false content spread deliberately to deceive, misinformation = false content shared without intent to deceive, malinformation = true information used maliciously, brand impersonation = faking a company's look."},
  {"id":"q159","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"A security awareness trainer asks the class to distinguish misinformation from disinformation. Which of the following statements is correct?","options":["Misinformation is true information used maliciously, while disinformation is false information.","Misinformation is spread deliberately to deceive, while disinformation is an honest mistake.","Misinformation is any error, while disinformation must come from a state-sponsored campaign.","Misinformation is shared without intent to deceive, while disinformation is spread deliberately."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Misinformation is innocent, disinformation is intentional - intent to deceive is the only line between them. The reversal option swaps the two, the classic trap, and no state sponsor is required. The tell: mis = mistake, dis = dishonest; same false content, opposite intent."},
  {"id":"q160","domain":"D2","family":"misinformation","question":"A presenter at a threat briefing quizzes the audience on disinformation. Which of the following statements is accurate?","options":["It is always spread by people who believe it is true","It is false information created and spread deliberately to deceive","It only exists on social media","It becomes misinformation once enough people share it"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Disinformation is deliberate deception. Intent at creation defines it; the same false story innocently reshared by believers becomes misinformation in their hands, but the original deliberate act is disinformation. The tell: dis = deliberate, mis = mistaken."},
  {"id":"q161","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"The finance team receives an email from the CEO's real mailbox, which attackers quietly compromised, urgently requesting a wire transfer to a new vendor account before end of day. Which of the following techniques is being used?","options":["impersonation","business email compromise (BEC)","pretexting","brand impersonation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Business email compromise. A compromised or spoofed internal executive mailbox used to request payments is BEC, the costliest form of this family. The tell: BEC = executive email account abused for wire/gift-card requests, impersonation = pretending to be someone in person or by phone, pretexting = the invented backstory, brand impersonation = faking a company's look to its customers."},
  {"id":"q162","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"A caller introduces herself as a newly assigned external auditor, cites a plausible compliance deadline, names the CFO, and uses this invented backstory to justify asking for access to financial records. Which of the following techniques is being used?","options":["impersonation","brand impersonation","pretexting","business email compromise (BEC)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Pretexting. The engineered, believable scenario constructed to justify the request is the pretext; that fabricated context is what distinguishes it from simple impersonation. The tell: pretexting = invented backstory legitimizing the ask, impersonation = claiming to be someone, BEC = compromised executive mailbox, brand impersonation = faking a company's identity."},
  {"id":"q163","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"Customers of a national bank receive emails using the bank's exact logo, colors, and footer, linking to a pixel-perfect copy of its login page that harvests credentials. Which of the following techniques is being used?","options":["impersonation","pretexting","business email compromise (BEC)","brand impersonation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Brand impersonation. Copying a trusted company's visual identity to fool that company's customers is brand impersonation. The tell: brand impersonation = fake the company's look to outsiders, BEC = a compromised or spoofed internal executive mailbox requesting payment, impersonation = pose as an individual, pretexting = the cover story."},
  {"id":"q164","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"An attacker emails staff claiming to be \"Mike, the new IT technician,\" and asks them to install a remote-support tool. There is no elaborate backstory, no spoofed executive mailbox, and no faked corporate branding - just the false identity. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["brand impersonation","pretexting","impersonation","business email compromise (BEC)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Impersonation. Simply posing as someone with a right to ask is impersonation; adding an engineered scenario would make it pretexting, and abusing a real executive mailbox would be BEC. The tell: impersonation = the false identity alone, pretexting = identity plus invented backstory, BEC = compromised/spoofed internal executive email, brand impersonation = faking a company's look."},
  {"id":"q165","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"An analyst is writing a detection playbook for business email compromise. Which of the following descriptions should the playbook use?","options":["a spoofed or compromised internal executive email requesting urgent payments","a fake lookalike of a consumer brand's website","an invented backstory used to justify a request","casually posing as a coworker in a hallway"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"BEC = executive mailbox abuse for money. The compromised or spoofed internal email plus an urgent financial request is the pattern that makes BEC the costliest scam in this family. The tell: BEC = internal executive email and payments, brand impersonation = external customer-facing fakery, pretexting = the cover story, impersonation = posing as a person."},
  {"id":"q166","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"Finance receives an urgent gift-card request from ceo@cornpany.com - a registered lookalike of the real company.com domain, complete with the CEO's real signature block scraped from LinkedIn. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["pretexting","business email compromise (BEC)","watering hole","brand impersonation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Business email compromise. A spoofed or lookalike executive mailbox pressuring staff for payments is BEC - compromise of the real mailbox is not required, spoofing counts. The tell: BEC = executive email angle plus a money request, pretexting = the invented backstory, brand impersonation = faking a company to its customers, watering hole = poisoning a site targets visit."},
  {"id":"q167","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"A caller says he is from the company's HVAC vendor doing \"scheduled emergency maintenance,\" quotes a fake ticket number, and asks the receptionist for the server room door code to \"finish the job.\" Which of the following BEST describes the fabricated maintenance story?","options":["brand impersonation","business email compromise (BEC)","pretexting","typosquatting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Pretexting. The engineered scenario - fake ticket, scheduled maintenance, urgency - is the pretext built to make the request seem legitimate. The tell: pretexting = the believable invented backstory, impersonation = just the false identity, BEC = executive email fraud, typosquatting = lookalike domains."},
  {"id":"q168","domain":"D2","family":"impersonation","question":"An analyst reviews a reported email. Header excerpt: From: \"IT Support\" <helpdesk@deskhelp-mail.com>, Reply-To: itteam@freemail.example. Body: \"Your password expires today. Reply with your current one to keep access.\" No branding, no backstory. Which of the following BEST describes this technique?","options":["Pretexting","Business email compromise","Impersonation","Brand impersonation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Impersonation. The sender simply claims a trusted identity (IT support) from an external mailbox with nothing else built around it; pretexting loses because no fabricated scenario justifies the request - the false identity is the whole play. The tell: impersonation = the assumed identity alone; pretexting = an invented backstory that justifies the ask; brand impersonation = faked corporate look and branding; BEC = a compromised or spoofed internal executive mailbox."},
  {"id":"q169","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-other","question":"A threat analyst traces malware on several clinical workstations to a nursing certification forum the hospital's staff browse daily. Attackers had compromised the forum and planted drive-by exploit code on its pages. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["typosquatting","phishing","brand impersonation","watering hole"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Watering hole. Rather than attacking the hospital directly, the attackers poisoned a legitimate site the target group already visits and waited for staff to arrive. Typosquatting loses because staff reached the real forum at its real address, not a lookalike domain. The tell: watering hole = compromise a site the targets already visit, typosquatting = register a misspelled lookalike domain, phishing = bait sent by email, brand impersonation = faking a company's identity."},
  {"id":"q170","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-other","question":"A user typing quickly lands on a malicious site because an attacker registered a domain matching a common misspelling of a popular address, complete with a cloned login page. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["watering hole","typosquatting","phishing","impersonation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Typosquatting. Registering the misspelled lookalike domain and waiting for fat-fingered visitors is typosquatting; no email lure or compromised legitimate site is involved. The tell: typosquatting = misspelled lookalike domain, watering hole = compromising a site targets already visit, phishing = fraudulent email, impersonation = posing as a person."},
  {"id":"q171","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-other","question":"In a watering hole attack, which of the following describes how the attacker reaches the intended victims?","options":["by compromising a legitimate site the target group routinely visits","by registering a misspelled lookalike domain","by sending fraudulent email to each victim directly","by placing a phone call to each victim"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"By poisoning a site the targets already trust and visit, then waiting. The victims come to the attacker, not the reverse. The tell: watering hole = infect their regular watering spot, typosquatting = catch their typos, phishing = push email at them, vishing = call them."},
  {"id":"q172","domain":"D2","family":"social-eng-other","question":"A security trainer asks the class to contrast a watering hole attack with typosquatting. Which of the following statements is correct?","options":["A watering hole registers a lookalike domain; typosquatting compromises a legitimate site targets visit","Both attacks compromise legitimate sites and differ only in the exploit delivered","A watering hole compromises a legitimate site targets already visit; typosquatting registers a lookalike domain","Both attacks register lookalike domains and differ only in the victims targeted"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Watering hole poisons a legitimate site the targets already visit; typosquatting registers a misspelled lookalike domain and waits for mistakes. The inverted pairing loses because it swaps the two mechanics exactly. The tell: watering hole = a real, trusted site turns hostile, typosquatting = a fake domain pretends to be the address the user meant."},
  {"id":"q173","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A penetration tester scatters branded USB drives around the client's parking lot and lobby. Within two days, three had been plugged into corporate workstations. Which of the following attack vectors does this exercise test?","options":["open service ports","default credentials","removable device","message-based vector"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Removable device. USB baiting exploits the removable-media vector and human curiosity. The tell: removable device = USB media carrying the payload, message-based = email/SMS/IM delivery, open service ports = exposed listening services, default credentials = unchanged factory passwords."},
  {"id":"q174","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"Attackers gain admin access to a newly racked network switch on its first weekend in production because nobody changed the factory admin password printed in the vendor manual. Which of the following vectors was exploited?","options":["open service ports","unsecure networks","removable device","default credentials"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Default credentials. The out-of-the-box password, documented publicly by the vendor, was the way in. The tell: default credentials = unchanged factory passwords, open service ports = exposed services an attacker probes, unsecure networks = unencrypted or open Wi-Fi/LAN, removable device = USB media."},
  {"id":"q175","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A security architect evaluating monitoring products notes one option requires no software installed on the endpoints it watches: a smaller attack surface and nothing for malware to tamper with, but shallower visibility and control. Which of the following models is described?","options":["client-based agent","agentless","agent-based with tamper protection","inline network appliance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Agentless. No installed endpoint software is the agentless trade-off: smaller attack surface and nothing on the endpoint to subvert, at the cost of shallower visibility and control. Tamper protection is tempting because it hardens the agent, but any installed agent is exactly what the stem rules out. The tell: agentless = no installed software, less control, client-based = installed agent that can itself be attacked, inline network appliance = sits in the traffic path, not on the endpoint."},
  {"id":"q176","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"An external scan of a company's address space finds a database service listening on its default port, directly reachable from the internet with no filtering. Which of the following attack vectors does this exposure represent?","options":["default credentials","open service ports","removable device","unsecure networks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Open service ports. An exposed, listening service reachable by attackers is the open-port vector, regardless of what credentials it has. The tell: open service ports = exposed listening service, default credentials = unchanged factory passwords, unsecure networks = unencrypted/open networks, removable device = USB media."},
  {"id":"q177","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A traveling employee joins the free open Wi-Fi at a cafe, which uses no encryption. Another patron passively captures the employee's traffic, including an unencrypted login. Which of the following attack vectors was exploited?","options":["open service ports","removable device","unsecure networks","default credentials"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unsecure networks. An open, unencrypted network lets anyone nearby read the traffic; the weakness is the network itself. The tell: unsecure networks = open/unencrypted Wi-Fi or LAN, open service ports = an exposed listening service, default credentials = factory passwords, removable device = USB media."},
  {"id":"q178","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A user opens an SVG file from a chat message; script embedded inside the image executes in the browser and steals the session. Which of the following attack vectors did the payload travel through?","options":["image-based","voice call","removable device","default credentials"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Image-based. Payload embedded in an image format (SVG can carry script) is the image-based vector - files that look like harmless pictures. The tell: image-based = the image is the carrier, file-based = documents/executables generally, removable device = USB media, voice call = the phone channel."},
  {"id":"q179","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"Attackers blast employees' phones with SMS messages containing a link to a fake benefits-enrollment portal during open-enrollment week. Which of the following attack vector categories is this?","options":["open service ports","message-based","client-based","removable device"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Message-based. Delivery through email, SMS, or instant messaging is the message-based vector; this one rides SMS. The tell: message-based = the communication channel carries the lure, open service ports = exposed listeners, client-based = an installed agent as the way in, removable device = USB."},
  {"id":"q180","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A vulnerability advisory reveals that a company's endpoint monitoring AGENT itself can be exploited for SYSTEM-level code execution, turning the security software into the entry point. Which of the following vector categories does this illustrate?","options":["agentless","unsecure networks","client-based","message-based"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Client-based. Installed software - including security agents - expands the attack surface and can itself be compromised, the core client-based risk that agentless designs avoid. The tell: client-based = the installed agent is attackable, agentless = nothing installed to attack, unsecure networks = open/unencrypted transit, message-based = email/SMS/IM lures."},
  {"id":"q181","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"An accounts-payable clerk receives a call that sounds exactly like the company's controller - an AI-cloned voice - instructing her to release a held payment. Which of the following attack vectors is being used?","options":["removable device","file-based","image-based","voice call"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Voice call. The attack arrives over the phone, cloned voice or not - the voice channel is the vector, and deepfakes just make it more convincing. The tell: voice call = the phone is the path in, message-based = text channels, file-based = a malicious file, image-based = a malicious image."},
  {"id":"q182","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"After an email gateway blocks their phishing lures, attackers compromise a business partner's account on the corporate team messaging platform and deliver the same malicious link through chat, where employees trust it. Which of the following attack vectors are they now using?","options":["Email","Image-based","Voice call","Instant messaging"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Instant messaging. The lure now rides the corporate chat platform, where a known partner's account carries far more implicit trust than external mail; email loses because that channel is blocked and the delivery path has moved to chat. The tell: instant messaging = lure delivered through a chat platform; email = the classic gateway-filtered channel; image-based = payload inside a graphic file; voice call = spoken phone deception."},
  {"id":"q183","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"Employees receive WhatsApp messages on their phones from an unknown number posing as the IT service desk, linking to a fake password reset page. Which of the following threat vector categories does this campaign use?","options":["Instant messaging","Short message service (SMS)","Email","Voice call"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Instant messaging. The lure arrives through a chat application, so the IM channel defines the vector; SMS loses because a WhatsApp message is app-based chat, not a carrier text, even though both appear on phones. The tell: message-based vectors map to their channel - email = mailbox, SMS = carrier text message, instant messaging = chat application, voice call = phone conversation."},
  {"id":"q184","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A network diagram image uploaded to a company's internal wiki contains an embedded script that executes whenever a browser renders the page preview, hijacking viewers' sessions. Which of the following MOST specifically describes the attack vector?","options":["File-based","Message-based","Image-based","Removable device"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Image-based. The payload is embedded in a graphic that executes when the browser renders it, and image-based names that carrier precisely; file-based loses because the ask demands the MOST specific vector and the carrier here is specifically an image. The tell: image-based = payload inside a picture or graphic format; file-based = malicious files generally (documents, archives, executables); message-based = lure carried by email, SMS, or chat; removable device = USB media."},
  {"id":"q185","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"An email filter passes a message because its invoice.zip attachment cannot be scanned. Inside is a macro-enabled spreadsheet that installs a trojan when opened. Which of the following BEST identifies the specific vector that carried the payload?","options":["Message-based (email)","File-based","Image-based","Removable device"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"File-based. The payload is the macro-enabled document itself, smuggled past filtering inside an archive; message-based loses because the ask is the most specific vector and email was only the transport for the malicious file. The tell: file-based = the malicious file (macro doc, PDF, archive) is the carrier; message-based = the lure arrives by email/SMS/IM; image-based = payload hidden inside an image; removable device = USB media carries it in."},
  {"id":"q186","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"Several machines are infected after users double-click quarterly-report.pdf.exe, a disguised executable that a compromised account uploaded to a department file share. Which of the following attack vectors did the malware use?","options":["Removable device","Client-based software","File-based","Image-based"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"File-based. A disguised executable planted on a share is a malicious file waiting to be run; client-based loses because no installed agent was exploited - users executed a rogue file. The tell: file-based = a malicious file is the carrier; client-based = vulnerable installed software is the way in; image-based = script hidden in an image file; removable device = plugged-in USB media."},
  {"id":"q187","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"After an off-site meeting, an employee plugs in a phone charging cable found in the conference room. The cable registers as a keyboard and rapidly types commands that download malware. Which of the following attack vectors does this describe?","options":["Unsecure wired network","File-based","Bluetooth","Removable device"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Removable device. The malicious cable is plugged-in hardware that injects keystrokes the moment it connects, the same vector class as a dropped USB drive; unsecure wired network loses because the laptop's network connection was never the path. The tell: removable device = plugged-in hardware (USB drive, rogue cable) carries the attack; unsecure wired network = an exposed live Ethernet jack; file-based = a malicious file is opened."},
  {"id":"q188","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"Threat intelligence shows an attacker scanning a retailer's branch offices specifically for self-service kiosks running an operating system whose vendor stopped issuing security patches years ago. Which of the following attack vectors is being targeted?","options":["Open service ports","Default credentials","Unsupported systems","Unsecure wireless networks"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unsupported systems. The kiosks will never receive patches, so every new flaw stays exploitable forever, which is exactly why the attacker hunts for them; open service ports loses because the scan targets the retired platform itself, not one exposed service. The tell: unsupported systems = patches ended, exposure is permanent; open service ports = a reachable listening service; default credentials = factory logins never changed."},
  {"id":"q189","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A hospital's imaging console is certified only for an operating system release that no longer receives security updates, and it must remain connected to the clinical network. Which of the following BEST describes the attack vector this device presents?","options":["Removable device","Client-based software","Open service ports","Unsupported system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Unsupported system. Because the certified OS gets no updates, any newly disclosed flaw remains exploitable for the device's whole networked life; client-based loses because the risk is the unpatchable platform, not a vulnerable installed agent. The tell: unsupported systems = no patches will ever come; client-based = installed software that is exploitable but also patchable; open service ports = one specific listening service is exposed."},
  {"id":"q190","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A visitor waiting in a lobby plugs a laptop into an unused wall jack and immediately reaches internal file servers. The switch port required no authentication of any kind. Which of the following attack vectors does this demonstrate?","options":["Unsecure wireless network","Unsecure wired network","Open service ports","Default credentials"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unsecure wired network. A live Ethernet jack that grants inside access to any plugged-in device is the exposed path; open service ports loses because the finding is the unauthenticated network port, not a particular listening service on a host. The tell: unsecure wired = exposed live jacks with no port authentication; unsecure wireless = open or weakly encrypted Wi-Fi; open service ports = a reachable listening service on a system."},
  {"id":"q191","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"During a walkthrough, an assessor finds a live network drop behind a hallway printer that connects any device straight to the internal VLAN, noting an implanted rogue device could sit there unnoticed. Which of the following vectors should the report cite?","options":["Removable device","Open service ports","Unsecure wired network","Bluetooth"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unsecure wired network. The unprotected live drop lets any rogue device join the internal VLAN with no authentication; removable device loses because nothing is plugged into a computer's USB port - the exposure is the network jack itself. The tell: unsecure wired = exposed Ethernet access with no port control; removable device = hardware plugged into a host; open service ports = a listening service reachable on a system."},
  {"id":"q192","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A sales manager leaves a phone's Bluetooth in discoverable mode at a cafe. A nearby attacker silently pairs with the phone and copies its contact list and messages. Which of the following attack vectors was exploited?","options":["Bluetooth","Unsecure wireless (Wi-Fi) network","Unsecure wired network","Open service ports"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bluetooth. Discoverable mode let a nearby attacker pair and pull data over the short-range radio link; Wi-Fi loses because the phone's wireless LAN connection was never involved - the pairing channel was. The tell: Bluetooth = short-range pairing exposure on the device itself; unsecure Wi-Fi = open or weak wireless LAN traffic interception; unsecure wired = exposed Ethernet jacks."},
  {"id":"q193","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A security administrator reviews an external scan of an internet-facing web server: 22/tcp open ssh, 23/tcp open telnet, 80/tcp open http, 443/tcp open https. Management is performed over SSH. Which of the following services should be disabled FIRST?","options":["22/tcp ssh","23/tcp telnet","80/tcp http","443/tcp https"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Telnet on 23/tcp. It carries credentials and sessions in cleartext and is redundant because SSH already provides encrypted management, so it is pure unneeded attack surface; HTTP on 80 loses because web servers routinely keep it just to redirect users to HTTPS. The tell: open service ports = every listening service is attack surface; disable the unneeded cleartext ones first and keep the encrypted equivalents (SSH, HTTPS) that serve a purpose."},
  {"id":"q194","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"An IP camera joins a botnet on the day it goes online. The investigation pulls its configuration, which reads: username admin, password admin (factory default). Which of the following attack vectors did the botnet exploit?","options":["Open service ports","Unsupported systems","Unsecure wireless network","Default credentials"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Default credentials. The camera still used its out-of-box factory login, which botnets try automatically from published default lists; open service ports loses because the config excerpt shows the way in was the unchanged password, not merely a reachable service. The tell: default credentials = factory logins never changed at deployment; open service ports = an exposed listening service; unsupported systems = a platform that no longer receives patches."},
  {"id":"q195","domain":"D2","family":"attack-vectors","question":"A post-incident report states that attackers entered through one phished email account, and it separately inventories every open port, stale account, and exposed application the company presents. Which of the following correctly labels the two concepts?","options":["The phished account is the threat vector, and the full inventory of exposed points is the attack surface","The phished account is the attack surface, and the full inventory of exposed points is the threat vector","Both the account and the inventory are threat vectors, differing only in size","Both the account and the inventory are attack surfaces, differing only in size"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Threat vector vs attack surface. The vector is the specific path the attacker actually used (the phished account), while the attack surface is the total set of points that could be attacked (every port, account, and application). The tell: vector = the path taken in; attack surface = everything attackable; shrinking the surface (closing ports, removing software) eliminates potential vectors."},
  {"id":"q196","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A 60-person firm outsources its entire IT operation - helpdesk, server administration, and patching - to a third party that runs everything remotely under contract. Which of the following BEST describes this third party?","options":["vendor","managed service provider (MSP)","supplier","hardware provider"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Managed service provider. A third party operating your IT on an ongoing basis is an MSP, and its privileged access makes it a major supply-chain risk. The tell: MSP = runs your IT operations, vendor = sells you a product/service, supplier = provides upstream parts/materials, hardware provider = supplies the devices."},
  {"id":"q197","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A risk assessment maps the upstream chain for a laptop line: one firm provides the raw chips and components that the manufacturer builds into the finished machines. Which of the following BEST describes the component firm?","options":["managed service provider (MSP)","software provider","supplier","vendor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Supplier. Providing parts and materials upstream that get built into finished goods is the supplier role. The tell: supplier = upstream parts/materials, vendor = sells you the finished product/service, MSP = operates your IT, software provider = supplies the applications."},
  {"id":"q198","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A company purchases a commercial endpoint-protection suite from the firm that develops and sells it, under a standard license agreement. In supply-chain terms, which of the following BEST describes that firm's role?","options":["managed service provider (MSP)","vendor","supplier","hardware provider"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Vendor. Selling a finished product or service makes the firm a vendor; it does not operate the buyer's IT (MSP) or provide upstream components (supplier). The tell: vendor = sells you the product/service, MSP = runs your IT for you, supplier = upstream parts/materials, hardware provider = supplies the physical devices."},
  {"id":"q199","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A firm assembles and ships the finished laptops, switches, and servers an organization deploys, building them from chips it buys from a third party. In supply-chain terms, which of the following is the laptop firm to the organization?","options":["hardware provider","supplier","managed service provider (MSP)","software provider"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hardware provider. The firm delivers the finished physical devices the organization deploys, which is the hardware provider's role. Supplier is the tempting miss: the third party selling the chips is the supplier, sitting upstream of the finished device. The tell: hardware provider = the finished devices, supplier = upstream parts/materials, software provider = the applications, MSP = runs your IT operations."},
  {"id":"q200","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"Attackers breach a managed service provider and use its legitimate remote-management tool to push ransomware simultaneously to dozens of the MSP's client networks. Which of the following supply-chain parties turned one breach into many?","options":["managed service provider (MSP)","supplier","hardware provider","software provider"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MSP. Its privileged, tooled access into every client makes the MSP a force multiplier: compromise one, reach all. The tell: MSP = operates inside many customers with standing access, vendor/supplier = sell things, hardware/software provider = supply products rather than run your systems."},
  {"id":"q201","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A batch of network appliances arrives from the factory with modified firmware that beacons to an attacker - implanted before the devices ever reached the customer. Which of the following supply-chain parties was the compromise point?","options":["managed service provider (MSP)","software provider","hardware provider","vendor's customer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Hardware provider. Tampering introduced in manufacturing or shipping compromises the hardware supply chain before deployment. The tell: hardware provider = the physical devices and their factory path, software provider = the applications and updates, MSP = operates your IT remotely."},
  {"id":"q202","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A company's own code is clean, but a compromised commercial library it licenses ships a backdoored update that flows straight into the company's product build. Which of the following supply-chain parties introduced the risk?","options":["software provider","hardware provider","managed service provider (MSP)","supplier of raw materials"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Software provider. Licensed code and its update channel are the software supply chain; their compromise becomes yours at build time. The tell: software provider = the code you consume, hardware provider = the devices, MSP = your outsourced operations, supplier = upstream parts and materials."},
  {"id":"q203","domain":"D2","family":"supply-chain","question":"A procurement training slide contrasts two roles: one sells the company its finished firewall product; the other provides the chips that go INTO that firewall upstream. Which of the following pairings is correct?","options":["Both are MSPs","The firewall seller is the supplier; the chip firm is the vendor","The firewall seller is the vendor; the chip firm is the supplier","Both are hardware providers with identical risk"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Vendor sells you the product; supplier feeds the chain upstream. The chip firm never deals with you directly, which is exactly why its risk hides. The tell: vendor = your direct seller, supplier = the parts behind your seller, MSP = a third party operating your IT."},
  {"id":"q204","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"A tester types ' OR '1'='1 into a login form's username field, and the application responds by returning every row of the user table. Which of the following vulnerabilities was exploited?","options":["cross-site scripting (XSS)","buffer overflow","memory injection","SQL injection (SQLi)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SQL injection. Input altered the database query itself, dumping table contents; the target was the database, not other users' browsers. The tell: SQLi = inject SQL into a query to hit the database, XSS = inject script that runs in another user's browser, buffer overflow = write past a memory boundary, memory injection = insert code into a running process."},
  {"id":"q205","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"An attacker posts a product review containing a hidden script tag. Every shopper who later views that page unknowingly runs the script, which ships their session cookies to the attacker. Which of the following vulnerabilities is this?","options":["cross-site scripting (XSS)","SQL injection (SQLi)","malicious update","buffer overflow"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Cross-site scripting. The injected script executes in other users' browsers, stealing their sessions; the victims are the visitors, not the database. The tell: XSS = script runs in another user's browser, SQLi = query manipulation against the database, malicious update = trojanized software update, buffer overflow = overrun a memory boundary."},
  {"id":"q206","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"A vulnerability report explains that a network service copies attacker-supplied data into a fixed-length memory region without checking size, letting the attacker overwrite adjacent memory and hijack execution. Which of the following vulnerabilities is described?","options":["memory injection","buffer overflow","SQL injection (SQLi)","malicious update"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Buffer overflow. Writing past a fixed buffer's boundary to clobber adjacent memory is the classic overflow. The tell: buffer overflow = write past a buffer boundary, memory injection = insert code into a running process's memory space, SQLi = database query manipulation, malicious update = poisoned software update."},
  {"id":"q207","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"Attackers breach a software vendor's build server and slip a backdoor into the next signed update. Thousands of customers install it automatically because it comes from the trusted update channel. Which of the following vulnerabilities is this?","options":["malicious update","memory injection","buffer overflow","SQL injection (SQLi)"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Malicious update. A trojanized update delivered through the vendor's own trusted channel is the malicious-update vulnerability, the pattern behind major supply-chain breaches. The tell: malicious update = poisoned software update, memory injection = code inserted into a running process, buffer overflow = overrun a memory boundary, SQLi = database query manipulation."},
  {"id":"q208","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"An EDR alert shows attack code being written directly into the address space of a running, legitimate process and executed there, leaving no new file on disk. Which of the following vulnerability classes is this?","options":["buffer overflow","memory injection","cross-site scripting (XSS)","malicious update"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Memory injection. Inserting and running code inside a live process's memory, common in fileless malware, is memory injection. A buffer overflow is one way in, but the described technique is the injection itself. The tell: memory injection = code planted in a running process's memory, buffer overflow = writing past a buffer to corrupt memory, XSS = script in another user's browser, malicious update = poisoned update channel."},
  {"id":"q209","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"An attacker sends users a link to a legitimate shopping site with a script tag in the search parameter. The site echoes the text back unencoded, and the script runs in each clicker's browser. Which of the following vulnerabilities is being exploited?","options":["SQL injection (SQLi)","memory injection","malicious update","cross-site scripting (XSS)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cross-site scripting - the reflected variety. The payload travels in the crafted link and is echoed back by the server to execute in the victim's browser, hitting only users who click; SQLi loses because the input executes in the browser and never alters a database query. The tell: XSS = script runs in another user's browser, SQLi = injected SQL hits the database, memory injection = code into a running process, malicious update = trojanized update channel."},
  {"id":"q210","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"A tester enters 1' UNION SELECT username, password FROM users-- into a product search box, and the results page renders the credential table alongside the products. Which of the following vulnerabilities was exploited?","options":["cross-site scripting (XSS)","malicious update","buffer overflow","SQL injection (SQLi)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SQL injection. The UNION SELECT reshaped the database query itself, pulling another table into the results - the payload targeted the database, not other users. The tell: SQLi = the query is the victim, XSS = the viewer's browser is the victim, buffer overflow = memory bounds, malicious update = poisoned update channel."},
  {"id":"q211","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"A report explains that sending an oversized crafted string to an IoT camera's login service overwrites the return address on the stack, letting the attacker redirect execution into their payload. Which of the following vulnerability classes is this?","options":["buffer overflow","SQL injection (SQLi)","malicious update","cross-site scripting (XSS)"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Buffer overflow. Oversized input overwriting adjacent memory - here the stack return address - is the classic overflow leading to code execution. The tell: buffer overflow = past the buffer's boundary into control data, memory injection = planting code in a live process by other means, SQLi/XSS = interpreter and browser attacks."},
  {"id":"q212","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"A security review flags that a desktop application fetches updates over plain HTTP and never verifies signatures, meaning anyone on the path could substitute a poisoned installer that clients would trust and run. Which of the following vulnerabilities does this expose the product to?","options":["memory injection","malicious update","race condition","SQL injection (SQLi)"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Malicious update. An unauthenticated update channel lets attackers deliver trojanized updates that install with full user trust. Signed, verified updates are the defense. The tell: malicious update = the update path is the weapon, memory injection = code into a running process, SQLi = database queries, race condition = timing gaps."},
  {"id":"q213","domain":"D2","family":"app-vulns","question":"A threat hunter investigates a signed process making unusual outbound connections. Memory analysis reveals an executable region in the process that does not exist in its on-disk image, and no new files were written. Which of the following vulnerabilities was exploited?","options":["Buffer overflow","Memory injection","Malicious update","SQL injection"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Memory injection. Attack code was placed directly into the running process's address space, which is why memory no longer matches the signed binary and nothing touched disk; buffer overflow loses because nothing describes input overrunning a fixed-length buffer - overflow is one technique that can enable injection, not this finding. The tell: memory injection = code inserted into a live process's memory; buffer overflow = writing past a buffer boundary to corrupt adjacent memory; malicious update = a trojanized update channel."},
  {"id":"q214","domain":"D2","family":"race-conditions","question":"A program checks that a user may access a file, then opens it a few milliseconds later. An attacker swaps in a symlink to /etc/shadow during that tiny gap. Which of the following terms names the exploited gap between the check and the access?","options":["time-of-check (TOC)","time-of-use (TOU)","time-of-check to time-of-use (TOCTOU)","race condition"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"TOCTOU. The exploit window between checking and using the resource is the time-of-check to time-of-use gap. The tell: TOC = the moment permission is checked, TOU = the moment the resource is used, TOCTOU = the exploitable gap between them, race condition = the general timing-dependent bug class."},
  {"id":"q215","domain":"D2","family":"race-conditions","question":"In a TOCTOU vulnerability writeup, the report references the exact instant at which the application validated the resource's permissions. Which of the following terms names that instant?","options":["time-of-use (TOU)","race condition","TOCTOU window","time-of-check (TOC)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Time-of-check. The validation moment is the TOC; the later access moment is the TOU. The tell: TOC = when permission is checked, TOU = when the resource is actually used, TOCTOU window = the gap between them, race condition = the broader timing-bug category."},
  {"id":"q216","domain":"D2","family":"race-conditions","question":"An online ticketing platform oversells the last seat: two checkout threads read it as available at the same instant and both complete the purchase. Whether the oversell happens depends purely on which thread finishes first. Which of the following BEST classifies this bug?","options":["race condition","time-of-check (TOC)","time-of-use (TOU)","TOCTOU"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Race condition. Any flaw whose outcome depends on the unpredictable timing or ordering of concurrent events is a race condition; TOCTOU loses because it names the specific check-then-use gap exploit, not this general concurrency flaw. The tell: race condition = outcome depends on timing, TOC = the check moment, TOU = the use moment, TOCTOU = the check-to-use gap exploit."},
  {"id":"q217","domain":"D2","family":"race-conditions","question":"In a TOCTOU vulnerability report, which of the following terms names the exact moment the resource is actually accessed or consumed, after its permissions were validated earlier?","options":["race condition","time-of-check (TOC)","time-of-use (TOU)","TOCTOU window"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Time-of-use. The access moment is the TOU; the earlier validation was the TOC, and the exploitable gap between them is the TOCTOU window. The tell: TOU = when the resource is used, TOC = when it is checked, TOCTOU window = the gap, race condition = the general class."},
  {"id":"q218","domain":"D2","family":"race-conditions","question":"Two order-processing threads each read an inventory count showing one unit left at the same instant, and each sells it. The store oversells because the operations interleaved unpredictably. Which of the following BEST classifies this flaw?","options":["time-of-check (TOC)","time-of-use (TOU)","TOCTOU window","race condition"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Race condition. The wrong outcome depends purely on the timing and interleaving of concurrent operations, the definition of a race. It is not specifically TOCTOU because no separate permission check followed by a later use is described. The tell: race condition = timing-dependent outcome, TOC = the moment a resource is checked, TOU = the moment it is used, TOCTOU window = the exploitable gap between check and use."},
  {"id":"q219","domain":"D2","family":"race-conditions","question":"A developer training deck covers TOCTOU flaws. Which of the following correctly pairs the two moments that define one?","options":["Time-of-use is when permission is validated; time-of-check is when the resource is accessed.","Time-of-check is when permission is validated; time-of-use is when the resource is accessed.","The TOCTOU window is the time that passes before the initial permission check occurs.","Time-of-check and time-of-use must happen at the same instant for the flaw to exist."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Time-of-check is the validation instant and time-of-use is the access instant; the attack lives in the gap between them. The reversal option simply swaps the two labels. The tell: TOC = check, TOU = use, TOCTOU window = the gap after the check and before the use - exploitable precisely because the two are NOT the same instant."},
  {"id":"q220","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A cloud tenant exploits a hypervisor flaw to break out of their guest virtual machine and execute code on the underlying host, exposing every other tenant's VM on that box. Which of the following vulnerabilities is this?","options":["resource reuse","VM escape","side loading","jailbreaking"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"VM escape. Breaking out of a guest to reach the hypervisor or host is VM escape, the nightmare scenario for shared infrastructure. The tell: VM escape = guest to host breakout, resource reuse = leftover data in reallocated memory/storage, side loading = installing apps outside the official store, jailbreaking = removing OS restrictions for root."},
  {"id":"q221","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A security advisory warns of active exploitation of a flaw the software vendor does not yet know exists, meaning no patch or signature is available to anyone. Which of the following terms describes this vulnerability?","options":["end-of-life","legacy","zero-day","misconfiguration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Zero-day. Unknown to the vendor and unpatched, giving defenders zero days of warning. The tell: zero-day = no patch exists yet, end-of-life = vendor stopped supporting it, legacy = outdated but still running, misconfiguration = insecure settings, not a code flaw."},
  {"id":"q222","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"An employee removes the manufacturer's operating system restrictions on their personal phone to get root access and install apps the platform forbids, then connects the phone to corporate email. Which of the following terms describes what they did to the phone?","options":["side loading","resource reuse","firmware vulnerability","jailbreaking"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Jailbreaking. Removing OS restrictions to gain root is jailbreaking; merely installing an app from outside the official store, without rooting, would be side loading. The tell: jailbreaking = strip OS restrictions for root access, side loading = install apps outside the official store, resource reuse = leftover data in reallocated resources, firmware vulnerability = flaw in the low-level device code."},
  {"id":"q223","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"An operations team discovers a production firewall model that the vendor stopped selling years ago and no longer writes security patches for. Which of the following terms describes the device's status?","options":["legacy","end-of-life","misconfiguration","firmware vulnerability"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"End-of-life. The vendor has formally ended sales and support, so no patches will ever come. Legacy describes outdated technology still in use, which may or may not still be supported. The tell: end-of-life = vendor ended support, legacy = outdated but still running, misconfiguration = insecure settings, firmware vulnerability = flaw in the device's low-level code."},
  {"id":"q224","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A cloud tenant spins up a new virtual disk and finds fragments of another company's database in the unwiped blocks, left over from the volume's previous owner. Which of the following vulnerabilities is this?","options":["misconfiguration","VM escape","side loading","resource reuse"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Resource reuse. Leftover data surviving in reallocated memory or storage handed to a new tenant is the resource-reuse vulnerability. The tell: resource reuse = residual data in reallocated resources, VM escape = guest-to-host breakout, misconfiguration = insecure settings, side loading = apps installed outside the official store."},
  {"id":"q225","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"Researchers disclose a flaw in a router's low-level bootstrap code - beneath the operating system - that survives OS reinstalls and lets attackers persist invisibly. Which of the following vulnerability categories is this?","options":["firmware","misconfiguration","side loading","VM escape"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Firmware. Flaws in the device's embedded low-level code sit below the OS, survive reinstalls, and are patched only by firmware updates. The tell: firmware = beneath the OS, persists through rebuilds, misconfiguration = wrong settings, side loading = out-of-store app installs, VM escape = guest-to-host breakout."},
  {"id":"q226","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A plant still runs a 15-year-old control workstation because the machinery requires it. The OS is long outdated, though the vendor still sells extended security patches the plant purchases. Which of the following terms BEST describes this system?","options":["zero-day","end-of-life","legacy","resource reuse"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Legacy. Outdated technology still in production is legacy; because the vendor still provides (paid) support, it is not yet end-of-life. The tell: legacy = old but still running and possibly still supported, end-of-life = vendor support has ended, zero-day = an unknown unpatched flaw, resource reuse = leftover data in reallocated resources."},
  {"id":"q227","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"Without rooting or jailbreaking anything, a user downloads an APK file from a website and installs it on her Android phone, bypassing the official store's vetting entirely. Which of the following terms describes this?","options":["jailbreaking","side loading","VM escape","firmware vulnerability"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Side loading. Installing apps from outside the official store, without modifying the OS, is side loading - the store's malware vetting is what gets skipped. The tell: side loading = out-of-store install on a stock device, jailbreaking = removing OS restrictions for root, VM escape = hypervisor breakout, firmware = below-OS code flaws."},
  {"id":"q228","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A breach postmortem finds the exposed cloud storage bucket had no flaw in its code at all - an engineer simply set its permissions to public during testing and never reverted them. Which of the following vulnerability categories is this?","options":["zero-day","end-of-life","VM escape","misconfiguration"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Misconfiguration. The software worked exactly as configured; the configuration was the vulnerability. Human settings errors rival code flaws as breach causes. The tell: misconfiguration = insecure settings, not broken code, zero-day = unknown code flaw, end-of-life = unsupported product, VM escape = guest breakout."},
  {"id":"q229","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A worm moves between servers by exploiting an unpatched flaw in the operating system's built-in file-sharing service. No third-party application is involved anywhere in the chain. Which of the following vulnerability categories does the flaw belong to?","options":["Application vulnerability","Web-based vulnerability","Firmware vulnerability","Operating system-based vulnerability"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Operating system-based vulnerability. The flaw sits in a service the platform itself ships, so the OS, not any application, is what needs patching; application loses because the stem rules out third-party software entirely. The tell: OS-based = the flaw is in the platform and its built-in services; application = a program running on top; web-based = flaws reached through a web app (SQLi, XSS); firmware = code below the OS in the hardware."},
  {"id":"q230","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A vulnerability bulletin describes a kernel flaw that lets any local user on the affected platform elevate to administrator rights, regardless of which applications are installed. Which of the following BEST categorizes this vulnerability?","options":["Operating system-based","Application-based","Cloud-specific","Web application-based"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Operating system-based. A kernel privilege escalation flaw lives in the platform itself, which is why it applies no matter what software sits on top; application loses because the bulletin explicitly makes installed programs irrelevant. The tell: OS-based = kernel and platform service flaws; application = flaws in individual programs; web-based = exploited through a web application; cloud-specific = weaknesses of the cloud management plane and shared tenancy."},
  {"id":"q231","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A developer commits the company's cloud management API key to a public repository. Anyone holding the key can create, modify, or delete resources across the entire tenant. Which of the following vulnerability categories MOST specifically describes this exposure?","options":["Misconfiguration","Cloud-specific","Zero-day","Resource reuse"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cloud-specific. Exposed management-plane keys are a signature cloud weakness because one credential controls the provider's API for the whole tenant; misconfiguration loses because the ask is the MOST specific category and the exposure lives in the cloud management interface, not a general settings error. The tell: cloud-specific = management API/key exposure, weak IAM, public buckets, shared tenancy; misconfiguration = correct software with wrong settings anywhere; resource reuse = leftover tenant data in reallocated resources."},
  {"id":"q232","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"After moving workloads to an IaaS provider, a company assumes the provider patches the guest operating systems, so its instances receive no updates for six months. Which of the following vulnerability categories is the root cause of this exposure?","options":["Cloud-specific","Operating system-based","End-of-life hardware","Zero-day"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Cloud-specific. Misunderstanding the shared responsibility model is a cloud-specific weakness: the provider secures the cloud, but the customer must patch what runs in it; OS-based loses because the missing OS patches are the symptom and the stem asks for the root cause. The tell: cloud-specific = shared responsibility confusion, exposed keys, weak IAM; OS-based = a flaw in the platform code itself; zero-day = no patch exists yet for anyone."},
  {"id":"q233","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A penetration test reports that the company VPN still accepts an export-grade legacy cipher suite and 1024-bit RSA keys. No exploitation was attempted or observed. Which of the following BEST describes the finding?","options":["Cryptographic downgrade attack","Hash collision attack","Cryptographic vulnerability","On-path attack"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cryptographic vulnerability. Weak ciphers and short keys are a weakness present in the configuration, reportable even though nothing was exploited; downgrade attack loses because no one forced the endpoints onto the weaker suite - it is merely accepted. The tell: cryptographic vulnerability = a weak algorithm, key, or implementation exists; downgrade attack = actively forcing negotiation onto the weaker option; collision = two inputs producing the same hash."},
  {"id":"q234","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A code review finds a desktop client encrypts stored credentials with a single hardcoded key embedded in the binary and shared by every installation, so extracting it once decrypts them all. Which of the following vulnerability categories is this?","options":["Misconfiguration","Cryptographic downgrade attack","Malicious update","Cryptographic vulnerability"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cryptographic vulnerability. A hardcoded, reused key is a flawed crypto implementation baked into the code; misconfiguration loses because no setting is wrong - the weakness ships inside the software and no configuration change can remove it. The tell: cryptographic vulnerability = weak algorithms, short keys, or flawed implementations (hardcoded or reused keys); misconfiguration = correct software with wrong settings; downgrade attack = forcing a weaker negotiation at runtime."},
  {"id":"q235","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"Returned loaner laptops keep reinfecting users even after full disk wipes and operating system reinstalls. Forensics locates an implant in the UEFI code that executes before the OS loads. Which of the following vulnerability categories enabled this persistence?","options":["Operating system-based","Firmware","Zero-day","Misconfiguration"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firmware. The implant lives below the operating system in the board's UEFI, which is why reimaging the disk never removes it; OS-based loses because everything the reinstall replaces is clean - the compromise sits underneath it. The tell: firmware = code embedded in the hardware below the OS that survives reimaging; OS-based = flaws in platform software a reinstall would replace; zero-day = describes patch availability, not persistence depth."},
  {"id":"q236","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A cloud provider confirms that code running inside one customer's guest virtual machine abused a hypervisor flaw to read live memory belonging to another customer's VM on the same host. Which of the following vulnerabilities does this describe?","options":["Resource reuse","Memory injection","VM escape","Misconfiguration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"VM escape. The guest actively broke through the hypervisor boundary to reach another tenant's running VM; resource reuse loses because the data was taken from live memory by exploitation, not recovered from unwiped resources handed to a new tenant. The tell: VM escape = active breakout from a guest through the hypervisor; resource reuse = passive leftovers in reallocated memory or storage; memory injection = code placed into a process on a system already under control."},
  {"id":"q237","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A company deletes its cloud volumes while decommissioning a workload. Weeks later, another tenant assigned the same underlying storage recovers intact fragments of the company's files because the blocks were reallocated without being wiped. Which of the following vulnerabilities does this demonstrate?","options":["Resource reuse","VM escape","Cryptographic vulnerability","Side loading"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Resource reuse. The provider reallocated storage that still held the previous tenant's data, so the new tenant inherited the remnants; VM escape loses because nothing broke out of a guest - the data leaked passively through unwiped blocks. The tell: resource reuse = leftover data in reallocated memory or storage, fixed by sanitizing before reassignment; VM escape = an active breakout through the hypervisor."},
  {"id":"q238","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A breach review finds a production database accepted connections with no authentication because the deployment template shipped with authentication disabled. The database software itself contains no code flaw. Which of the following vulnerability categories applies?","options":["Misconfiguration","Zero-day","Operating system-based","Malicious update"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Misconfiguration. Correct software was deployed with the wrong settings - authentication off - a human-error class fixed by configuration enforcement, not a patch; zero-day loses because there is no flaw in the code for any vendor to fix. The tell: misconfiguration = right software, wrong settings (auth disabled, open permissions, debug left on); zero-day = an unknown code flaw with no patch; malicious update = a trojanized update channel."},
  {"id":"q239","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A corporate Android phone joins a botnet. The investigation shows the user enabled the install-unknown-apps setting and installed a game downloaded from a forum; the device was never rooted. Which of the following vulnerabilities enabled the installation?","options":["Jailbreaking","Malicious update","Zero-day","Side loading"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Side loading. The app came from outside the official store, bypassing its vetting and signing review, which on Android needs only a settings toggle; jailbreaking loses because no OS restrictions were removed - the phone was never rooted. The tell: side loading = installing apps from outside the official store; jailbreaking/rooting = removing OS restrictions to gain root control; malicious update = a legitimate update channel delivering trojanized code."},
  {"id":"q240","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A fully patched server is compromised through a flaw that has no CVE entry, no vendor advisory, and no available fix from anyone. Which of the following BEST describes the vulnerability that was exploited?","options":["A missed patch for a known vulnerability","A zero-day vulnerability","An end-of-life platform flaw","A misconfiguration"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Zero-day vulnerability. The vendor does not yet know the flaw exists, so no patch or signature can exist anywhere; a missed patch loses because the server is fully patched - there was no fix to miss. The tell: zero-day = no patch exists YET because the flaw is unknown to the vendor; missed patch = a fix exists but was not applied (patch management failure); end-of-life = no patch will EVER come again."},
  {"id":"q241","domain":"D2","family":"system-vulns","question":"A CISO asks which control still provides protection against exploitation of zero-day vulnerabilities, for which no patch or detection signature can exist yet. Which of the following BEST meets the requirement?","options":["Signature-based antivirus","Accelerated patch deployment","Application allow list","Subscribing to vendor advisories"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Application allow list. Default-deny execution blocks unapproved payloads regardless of whether the flaw they exploit is known, so it still works when no patch or signature exists; accelerated patching loses because there is nothing to deploy for an unknown flaw. The tell: zero-day defenses are compensating by nature - allow lists, behavior detection, segmentation, least privilege; signatures and patches both require the vulnerability to be known first."},
  {"id":"q242","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"Overnight, malware sweeps through a flat corporate network by exploiting an unpatched SMB service on each machine it finds. No user opened, clicked, or ran anything. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["worm","virus","trojan","logic bomb"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Worm. Self-replication across the network with zero user action is the worm signature; a virus needs a host file and a user to trigger it. The tell: worm = self-spreads over the network, virus = needs a host file and user action, trojan = disguised as legitimate software, logic bomb = payload waiting on a trigger condition."},
  {"id":"q243","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"Staff arrive to find every shared drive encrypted and a note demanding cryptocurrency within 72 hours for the decryption key. Which of the following malware types is responsible?","options":["spyware","ransomware","rootkit","keylogger"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Ransomware. Encrypting data and extorting payment for its return defines ransomware. The tell: ransomware = encrypts and extorts, spyware = secretly collects activity, rootkit = hides at the OS/kernel level, keylogger = records keystrokes."},
  {"id":"q244","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"During incident response, analysts discover malware that patched kernel structures to hide its own processes and files from every tool on the box, maintaining privileged persistence for months. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["trojan","bloatware","rootkit","virus"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Rootkit. Kernel-level concealment and persistent privileged access are rootkit behavior, which is why detection from inside the infected OS is unreliable. The tell: rootkit = hides at OS/kernel level, trojan = disguised as legitimate, bloatware = unwanted preinstalled software, virus = infects host files, needs user action."},
  {"id":"q245","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"A database administrator facing layoffs plants a script that will wipe the production databases automatically if his account ever disappears from the payroll system. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["worm","spyware","keylogger","logic bomb"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Logic bomb. A dormant payload triggered by a specific condition, here his own termination, is a logic bomb. The tell: logic bomb = payload fires on a condition/date, worm = self-spreads, spyware = covert data collection, keylogger = records keystrokes."},
  {"id":"q246","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"A user downloads what appears to be a free PDF converter. It converts PDFs as advertised, while quietly installing a remote-access backdoor in the background. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["trojan","virus","worm","bloatware"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Trojan. Software posing as something legitimate and useful that the user willingly installs, hiding a malicious payload, is a trojan. The tell: trojan = disguised as legitimate, virus = attaches to host files and spreads with user action, worm = spreads itself over the network, bloatware = unwanted but not malicious preinstalls."},
  {"id":"q247","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"Forensics on a compromised laptop finds software that silently recorded every keystroke - passwords, messages, card numbers - and uploaded the capture files to an attacker's server nightly. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["spyware","trojan","rootkit","keylogger"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Keylogger. Recording keystrokes specifically is the keylogger's defining behavior; broader covert activity monitoring would be spyware. The tell: keylogger = records keystrokes, spyware = collects activity/browsing generally, trojan = disguised delivery vehicle, rootkit = kernel-level concealment."},
  {"id":"q248","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"A malicious payload rides inside a spreadsheet macro. It runs only when a user opens the file and clicks Enable Content, after which it infects other documents on the machine. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["virus","worm","trojan","spyware"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Virus. It needs a host file and a user action to execute and spread, the classic virus requirements; a worm would spread by itself over the network. The tell: virus = host file plus user action, worm = self-replicates with no user, trojan = poses as legitimate software, spyware = covert monitoring."},
  {"id":"q249","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"An investigation finds software covertly logging a user's browsing history, search terms, and app usage and selling the profile to a third party. It captures no keystrokes. Which of the following malware types is this?","options":["keylogger","bloatware","spyware","rootkit"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Spyware. Covert collection of activity data without consent is spyware; the stem explicitly rules out keystroke capture. The tell: spyware = secretly collects activity, keylogger = specifically records keystrokes, bloatware = unwanted preinstalled software, rootkit = hides at the kernel."},
  {"id":"q250","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"A brand-new consumer laptop arrives crammed with preinstalled trialware and vendor utilities that slow boot times and nag the user. Nothing is stealing data - it is just unwanted. Which of the following categories is this?","options":["spyware","trojan","bloatware","rootkit"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Bloatware. Unwanted preinstalled software that degrades performance without malicious behavior is bloatware - remove it to shrink the attack surface anyway. The tell: bloatware = unwanted but not hostile, spyware = covert data collection, trojan = disguised malice, rootkit = kernel-level hiding."},
  {"id":"q251","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"During a sweep, a technician finds a small device plugged inline between a workstation's keyboard cable and its USB port, storing every keystroke typed at the machine. Which of the following threats is this?","options":["keylogger","rootkit","worm","logic bomb"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Keylogger - the hardware variety. Keystroke capture defines the keylogger whether implemented in software or as an inline dongle no antivirus can see. The tell: keylogger = records keystrokes, rootkit = software hiding in the kernel, worm = self-spreading malware, logic bomb = condition-triggered payload."},
  {"id":"q252","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"An instructor asks what a worm does NOT require that a virus does. Which of the following is correct?","options":["A network connection","A host file and user action to spread","An operating system","A payload"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A worm needs no host file and no user action - it spreads itself across the network by exploiting services. A virus must attach to a file and wait for a user to run it. The tell: worm = autonomous spread, virus = hitchhikes on files and users; both may carry any payload."},
  {"id":"q253","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"Attackers encrypt a firm's files AND exfiltrate copies first, threatening to publish the data publicly if the payment demand is refused - extortion on two fronts. Which of the following malware categories is at work?","options":["spyware","logic bomb","trojan","ransomware"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Ransomware. Encryption plus payment demand is ransomware; the added leak threat is the double-extortion evolution of the same category. The tell: ransomware = extortion over your data's availability or secrecy, spyware = quiet collection, trojan = the disguise it may arrive in, logic bomb = waits for a trigger."},
  {"id":"q254","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"An analyst investigates a marketing workstation and finds a hidden process that captures a screenshot every five minutes and uploads the images and microphone recordings nightly. No files are encrypted and no keystrokes are stored. Which of the following malware types is MOST likely present?","options":["Keylogger","Ransomware","Spyware","Trojan"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Spyware. Covert, broad surveillance of user activity - screenshots, audio, browsing - quietly collected and exfiltrated is spyware's signature; the keylogger option loses because no keystrokes are being recorded. The tell: spyware = broad covert collection of activity, keylogger = records keystrokes specifically, ransomware = encrypts and extorts, trojan = malware disguised as a legitimate program."},
  {"id":"q255","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"A desktop team images ten new laptops, and the first vulnerability scan flags outdated preinstalled trial utilities and vendor toolbars that no employee uses. Nothing behaves maliciously. Which of the following BEST categorizes this software?","options":["Bloatware","Spyware","Trojan","Worm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Bloatware. Unwanted preinstalled software is not malicious, but its unpatched code widens the attack surface, which is why the scan flags it and hardening removes it; spyware loses because nothing is collecting or exfiltrating data. The tell: bloatware = unwanted preinstalled software adding patchable attack surface, spyware = covert data collection, trojan = malware disguised as a legitimate app, worm = self-replicates across the network."},
  {"id":"q256","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"Two weeks after a terminated administrator's account was disabled, an unrecognized script deletes project directories on the 1st of the month. No payment demand or note appears anywhere. Which of the following malware types is MOST likely responsible?","options":["Ransomware","Worm","Rootkit","Logic bomb"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Logic bomb. Dormant code detonating on a condition or date - classically planted by an insider before departure - fits the delayed, triggered destruction; ransomware loses because a destructive payload with no demand means no extortion scheme. The tell: logic bomb = payload triggered by a condition or date, ransomware = encrypts and demands payment, rootkit = hides at kernel level for persistence, worm = self-spreads with no trigger condition."},
  {"id":"q257","domain":"D2","family":"malware","question":"External NetFlow records show a database server beaconing to an unknown host every 10 minutes, yet netstat, the process list, and antivirus on the server all report nothing unusual. Which of the following malware types BEST explains the discrepancy?","options":["Trojan","Rootkit","Logic bomb","Keylogger"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rootkit. Kernel-level hooks intercept system calls, so on-host tools report a clean system while outside network evidence proves activity - detection typically requires booting from trusted external media; a trojan loses because it does not make the OS conceal its connections from local tools. The tell: rootkit = the OS itself lies (on-host tools disagree with external evidence), trojan = disguised as legitimate software, logic bomb = condition-triggered payload, keylogger = records keystrokes."},
  {"id":"q258","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"An attacker sends 60-byte queries to open DNS resolvers, each of which emits a 3,000-byte response aimed at the victim. It is this response-to-request size multiplication that crushes the victim's link. Which of the following DDoS characteristics does this describe?","options":["reflected DDoS","amplified DDoS","on-path attack","credential replay"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Amplified DDoS. The defining feature named here is the size multiplier: tiny request, huge response. Reflection (spoofing the victim's address) is usually combined with it, but the multiplication is amplification. The tell: amplified = small request yields a huge response, reflected = third parties reply to the spoofed victim, on-path = attacker sits between two parties, credential replay = resending captured credentials."},
  {"id":"q259","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"An attacker spoofs the victim's IP address as the source on thousands of requests to innocent third-party servers, so every server's reply lands on the victim instead of the attacker. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["amplified DDoS","on-path attack","reflected DDoS","DNS attack"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Reflected DDoS. Bouncing traffic off third parties by spoofing the victim's source address is reflection; if the replies were also much larger than the requests, amplification would be stacked on top. The tell: reflected = spoofed source makes third parties reply to the victim, amplified = the size multiplier, on-path = interception between two parties, DNS attack = poisoning or hijacking name resolution."},
  {"id":"q260","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"On a hotel network, an attacker inserts themselves between a guest's laptop and the gateway, silently relaying and modifying the traffic flowing in both directions. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["credential replay","reflected DDoS","DNS attack","on-path attack"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"On-path attack. Sitting between two parties to read or alter their traffic in transit is the on-path attack. The tell: on-path = attacker between two parties reading/altering traffic, credential replay = resending captured credentials, reflected DDoS = third parties reply to a spoofed victim, DNS attack = corrupting name resolution."},
  {"id":"q261","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"An attacker sniffs a valid session token from unencrypted traffic, then resends it an hour later to authenticate to the application as the legitimate user. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["credential replay","on-path attack","amplified DDoS","DNS attack"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Credential replay. Capturing valid credentials or a session token and resending them later is replay; the attacker never cracked anything. The tell: credential replay = resend captured credentials/session, on-path = live interception between parties, amplified DDoS = small request/huge response flooding, DNS attack = poisoned name resolution."},
  {"id":"q262","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"Users typing their bank's correct web address are silently routed to a fraudulent server because an attacker poisoned the cached answers on their DNS resolver. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["reflected DDoS","DNS attack","credential replay","on-path attack"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DNS attack. Corrupting name resolution so a correct domain resolves to the attacker's server is DNS poisoning/hijacking. The tell: DNS attack = poisoned or hijacked name resolution, on-path = attacker relaying between two parties, credential replay = resending captured credentials, reflected DDoS = third parties flooding a spoofed victim."},
  {"id":"q263","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"In an office lobby, an attacker's device broadcasts the same SSID as the corporate wireless network at higher power, luring laptops into associating with it instead. Which of the following attack categories is this?","options":["DNS attack","credential replay","amplified DDoS","wireless attack"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Wireless attack. A rogue access point (evil twin) impersonating the legitimate SSID is a wireless-layer attack. The tell: wireless attack = rogue AP/evil twin at the radio layer, DNS attack = poisoned name resolution, credential replay = resent captured credentials, amplified DDoS = small-request/huge-response flooding."},
  {"id":"q264","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"A network engineer is explaining DDoS mechanics to a junior analyst. Which of the following primarily distinguishes an amplified DDoS from a reflected DDoS?","options":["Amplification exploits a large response-to-request size ratio; reflection spoofs the victim's source so third parties reply to it.","Reflection exploits a large response-to-request size ratio; amplification spoofs the victim's source so third parties reply to it.","Amplification requires a large botnet of compromised hosts; reflection can be launched from a single attacking machine.","Amplification works only against DNS resolvers; reflection works against any service that answers spoofed requests."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Amplification is the size multiplier and reflection is the redirection trick: spoofing the victim's source address makes third parties answer the victim. The reversal option swaps the two definitions, and real attacks usually combine both against any suitable protocol, not just DNS. The tell: amplified = response much bigger than the request, reflected = spoofed source makes third parties reply to the victim."},
  {"id":"q265","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"Attackers social-engineer a domain registrar into transferring control of a company's domain, then point its name records at their own servers - every user typing the correct address now lands on attacker infrastructure. Which of the following attack categories is this?","options":["DNS attack","amplified DDoS","credential replay","on-path attack"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"DNS attack - domain hijacking. Controlling the domain's records corrupts name resolution at the source, no cache poisoning needed. The tell: DNS attack = name resolution is the target (poisoning, hijacking), on-path = interception between parties, amplified DDoS = volumetric flooding, credential replay = resent captured credentials."},
  {"id":"q266","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"On a corporate LAN, an attacker sends forged ARP replies so the victim's traffic to the gateway routes through the attacker's machine first, where it is read and forwarded along. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["reflected DDoS","on-path attack","DNS attack","amplified DDoS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"On-path attack. ARP spoofing inserts the attacker between victim and gateway to read or alter traffic in transit - a textbook on-path technique at Layer 2. The tell: on-path = positioned between two parties, DNS attack = poisoned name lookups, reflected/amplified DDoS = flooding via third parties."},
  {"id":"q267","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"An attacker captures an authentication hash off the network and, without ever cracking it, submits the hash itself to authenticate to other servers as that user. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["on-path attack","DNS attack","credential replay","wireless attack"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Credential replay. Reusing captured authentication material as-is (pass-the-hash) is replaying credentials - no cracking required; on-path loses because sniffing the hash was only the capture step, and the attack itself is the resubmission. The tell: credential replay = captured credentials resent to authenticate, on-path = sitting between two parties to read or alter live traffic, DNS attack = poisoned name resolution, wireless = radio-layer attacks."},
  {"id":"q268","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"A wireless controller logs the following just before users report drops: 10:02:14 4,812 deauthentication frames received, source spoofed as corporate BSSID; 10:03:20 all clients disconnected from SSID CORP-WIFI. Which of the following attacks is forcing the clients off the network?","options":["Evil twin","Jamming","Deauthentication attack","Rogue access point"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Deauthentication attack. Spoofed 802.11 management frames order clients to disconnect - a wireless denial of service that often sets up an evil twin to catch the reconnections; evil twin loses because the log shows forged disconnect frames, not an imitation SSID accepting clients. The tell: deauthentication = spoofed management frames force disconnects, evil twin = attacker AP imitating your SSID, rogue AP = unauthorized AP attached to your network, jamming = raw RF interference with no forged frames."},
  {"id":"q269","domain":"D2","family":"ddos-network","question":"During a wireless audit, an analyst finds a consumer access point an employee cabled into a conference room jack for convenience. It broadcasts the SSID HomeNet2 and bridges directly onto the corporate LAN. Which of the following BEST describes this device?","options":["Rogue access point","Evil twin","On-path attack","Deauthentication attack"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rogue access point. Any unauthorized AP attached to your network is rogue, even with innocent intent, because it opens an unmanaged wireless door into the LAN; evil twin loses because the device broadcasts its own SSID rather than imitating the corporate one. The tell: rogue AP = unauthorized AP ON your network, evil twin = attacker AP IMITATING your SSID to lure clients, deauthentication = forged disconnect frames, on-path = an attacker relaying traffic between two parties."},
  {"id":"q270","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"A web server log shows requests like GET /view?file=../../../../etc/passwd, and the response body contains the server's account file from outside the web root. Which of the following attacks is being attempted?","options":["injection","directory traversal","request forgery (CSRF)","replay attack"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directory traversal. Using ../ sequences to walk out of the web root and read arbitrary server files is traversal. The tell: directory traversal = ../ path tricks to escape the web root, injection = malicious input fed to an interpreter, forgery = tricking a user's browser into a state-changing request, replay = resending captured traffic."},
  {"id":"q271","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"While logged in to her bank, a user clicks a link in a forum post. Her browser silently submits a hidden transfer request to the bank, which processes it because her session cookie rides along automatically. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["privilege escalation","replay attack","request forgery (CSRF)","directory traversal"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Request forgery. The attacker tricked the victim's own authenticated browser into sending a state-changing request, the essence of CSRF. The tell: forgery = victim's browser unknowingly sends the request, replay = attacker resends captured traffic, privilege escalation = gaining higher rights, directory traversal = ../ file access."},
  {"id":"q272","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"A help-desk user exploits an unquoted service path flaw on a server and ends up running commands as SYSTEM, far beyond the rights their account was ever granted. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["injection","request forgery (CSRF)","replay attack","privilege escalation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Privilege escalation. Leveraging a flaw to move from limited rights to administrator/SYSTEM is escalation, the follow-on step after most footholds. The tell: privilege escalation = gain higher rights than granted, injection = malicious input to an interpreter, forgery = tricking a browser into a request, replay = resending captured traffic."},
  {"id":"q273","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"An attacker captures a legitimate signed API request that unlocks a facility door, then resends the identical request at will, opening the door repeatedly without ever crafting anything new. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["injection","request forgery (CSRF)","replay attack","directory traversal"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Replay attack. Recording valid traffic and resending it unchanged to repeat the action is replay; nothing was forged or injected. Request forgery loses because it tricks the victim's own browser into sending a new request, while here the attacker resends captured traffic. The tell: replay = attacker resends captured valid traffic, forgery = victim's browser is tricked into sending the request, injection = malicious input to an interpreter, directory traversal = ../ path escape."},
  {"id":"q274","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"A code review guideline warns about the attack category where untrusted input is passed to an interpreter - SQL, LDAP, OS shell - and executed as part of the command. Which of the following categories is this?","options":["directory traversal","request forgery (CSRF)","injection","replay attack"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Injection. Any attack where crafted input becomes executable interpreter syntax - SQLi, command injection, LDAP injection - belongs to the injection category. The tell: injection = input executed by an interpreter, directory traversal = path tricks to read files, forgery = tricking a browser into a request, replay = resending captured traffic."},
  {"id":"q275","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"A web form pings a user-supplied IP for diagnostics. A tester enters 8.8.8.8; rm -rf /tmp/test and the server executes both commands, proving user input reaches the OS shell. Which of the following attack categories is this?","options":["directory traversal","replay attack","request forgery (CSRF)","injection"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Injection - command injection. Input flowing into the OS shell and executing is injection against the command interpreter, the same family as SQLi. The tell: injection = input becomes interpreter commands, directory traversal = path escape to read files, forgery = a victim's browser tricked into requests, replay = resent captured traffic."},
  {"id":"q276","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"A WAF log shows requests containing %2e%2e%2f%2e%2e%2fetc%2fpasswd - URL-encoded to slip past naive filters, but decoding to a familiar pattern. Which of the following attacks is being attempted?","options":["injection","directory traversal","replay attack","privilege escalation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directory traversal. The encoding decodes to ../../etc/passwd - the classic path escape, dressed up to evade filters that only match literal dots and slashes. The tell: directory traversal = ../ escapes (encoded or not), injection = commands into an interpreter, replay = captured traffic resent, privilege escalation = rights beyond those granted."},
  {"id":"q277","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"A compromised ad network serves an invisible image tag whose source URL is a router admin panel's \"disable firewall\" action. Any logged-in administrator whose browser renders the ad silently fires that request. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["replay attack","injection","directory traversal","request forgery (CSRF)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Request forgery. The victim's own authenticated browser is tricked into issuing a state-changing request - no click needed when an image tag fires it; replay loses because nothing was captured, the victim's browser generates the request fresh. The tell: forgery = the victim's browser sends the attacker's request, replay = the attacker resends captured traffic, injection = malicious input into an interpreter, directory traversal = ../ path escapes."},
  {"id":"q278","domain":"D2","family":"app-attacks","question":"From a compromised low-privilege web account, an attacker exploits an unpatched kernel flaw on the server and lands a root shell - rights the web account never had. Which of the following attack categories is the kernel step?","options":["replay attack","request forgery (CSRF)","directory traversal","privilege escalation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Privilege escalation. Moving from limited rights to root via a flaw is vertical escalation, the standard second step after an initial foothold. The tell: privilege escalation = higher rights than granted, injection/traversal = ways to get the foothold, replay/forgery = request-level attacks."},
  {"id":"q279","domain":"D2","family":"crypto-attacks","question":"Reviewing a packet capture from a public hotspot, an analyst sees an attacker rewriting a victim's connections so a banking session stays on unencrypted HTTP even though the bank's server supports and prefers TLS. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["collision attack","birthday attack","downgrade attack","rainbow table attack"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Downgrade attack. Forcing the session onto a weaker protocol - plain HTTP instead of the TLS both ends support - is a downgrade, leaving the traffic readable. The tell: downgrade = force a weaker protocol/cipher, collision = two inputs with the same hash, birthday = probability shortcut to find collisions, rainbow table = precomputed hash lookups."},
  {"id":"q280","domain":"D2","family":"crypto-attacks","question":"A legal team approves contracts by recording each file's MD5 digest. An attacker gets a benign contract approved, then swaps in a malicious version crafted in advance to produce the identical digest. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["collision attack","birthday attack","downgrade attack","brute-force attack"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Collision attack. Two different files engineered to share one hash break the integrity check, letting the malicious contract inherit the benign one's approval; the birthday attack loses because it names the probabilistic method for finding collisions, not the swap itself. The tell: collision = two inputs, same hash, birthday = the probabilistic method for finding collisions faster, downgrade = force weaker crypto, brute force = exhaustively trying inputs."},
  {"id":"q281","domain":"D2","family":"crypto-attacks","question":"A security architect proposes moving from a 128-bit hash to a 256-bit hash because attackers can find a matching pair of inputs after roughly the square root of the expected number of tries. Which of the following attacks does the longer hash defend against?","options":["collision attack","downgrade attack","rainbow table attack","birthday attack"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Birthday attack. The square-root shortcut for finding a matching pair is the birthday attack, so doubling the hash length restores the safety margin. Collision attack is tempting but names the two-inputs-one-hash outcome, while the birthday attack is the math that finds it. The tell: birthday = probability shortcut for collisions, collision = the two-inputs-one-hash outcome, downgrade = force weaker crypto, rainbow table = precomputed lookups."},
  {"id":"q282","domain":"D2","family":"crypto-attacks","question":"An exam-prep flashcard asks how a birthday attack relates to a collision attack. Which of the following is correct?","options":["A birthday attack is a probability technique that finds hash collisions faster than brute force.","A birthday attack downgrades ciphers to weaker versions.","A collision attack only works against encryption keys, not hashes.","They are unrelated to one another."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The birthday attack is the method, the collision is the result. Birthday-paradox math means matches appear after roughly the square root of the brute-force effort. The tell: birthday = the probabilistic technique, collision = the two-inputs-one-hash outcome it produces."},
  {"id":"q283","domain":"D2","family":"crypto-attacks","question":"A rogue access point advertises itself as WPA2-only, forcing nearby WPA3-capable clients to associate using the older protocol's weaker handshake, which the attacker then attacks offline. Which of the following crypto attack patterns is this?","options":["downgrade attack","birthday attack","collision attack","spraying"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Downgrade attack. Forcing capable parties onto an older, weaker protocol - WPA3 down to WPA2, TLS down to SSL - is the downgrade, exploited because compatibility fallbacks exist. The tell: downgrade = weaker protocol forced, collision = same hash from different inputs, birthday = the probability method for collisions, spraying = a password attack."},
  {"id":"q284","domain":"D2","family":"crypto-attacks","question":"An exam-prep drill asks students to separate two attacks: one manipulates protocol negotiation to weaken the crypto in use; the other finds two inputs producing the same hash. Which of the following pairings is correct?","options":["Negotiation = collision; matching hashes = downgrade","Negotiation = downgrade; matching hashes = collision","Both describe birthday attacks","Both describe downgrade attacks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Downgrade attacks weaken the negotiation; collision attacks defeat the hash. One attacks the handshake before data flows, the other attacks integrity math. The tell: downgrade = force the weaker cipher, collision = same digest from different inputs, birthday = the fast way to find collisions."},
  {"id":"q285","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"Reviewing sign-in logs for the company's cloud single sign-on portal, an analyst finds one external IP tried the single password Summer2026! exactly once against every account in the tenant overnight. Which of the following attacks is MOST likely occurring?","options":["credential stuffing","password spraying","brute force","dictionary attack"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Password spraying. One common password tried across every account stays under lockout thresholds while betting that someone chose it. Credential stuffing loses because the attempts reuse one guessed password, not username-password pairs leaked from another breach. The tell: spraying = few passwords, many accounts, brute force = many passwords, one account, dictionary = wordlist-driven guessing, credential stuffing = replaying leaked credential pairs."},
  {"id":"q286","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"An attacker fixates on a single domain-admin account and hammers it with every possible character combination, generating tens of millions of failed logins over a weekend. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["brute force","password spraying","dictionary attack","credential stuffing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Brute force. Exhaustive guessing of every combination against ONE account is brute force, noisy and lockout-prone. The tell: brute force = everything against one account, spraying = a few passwords across many accounts, dictionary = curated wordlist, credential stuffing = reusing leaked credential pairs."},
  {"id":"q287","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"A cracking tool works through a curated wordlist - sports teams, seasons, keyboard walks, and common substitutions like P@ssw0rd - against a stolen hash file. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["password spraying","dictionary attack","credential stuffing","brute force"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dictionary attack. Guessing from a wordlist of likely passwords rather than every possible string is the dictionary approach. The tell: dictionary = curated wordlist of likely passwords, brute force = every possible combination, spraying = few passwords across many accounts, credential stuffing = replaying leaked pairs."},
  {"id":"q288","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"A trainee asks what actually separates a brute-force attack from password spraying. Which of the following is correct?","options":["Brute force hammers one account with many guesses; spraying tries a few passwords across many accounts.","Brute force tries a few passwords across many accounts; spraying hammers one account with many guesses.","Brute force works only offline against stolen hashes; spraying works only against live login portals.","Brute force generates its own candidate guesses; spraying replays previously breached credential pairs."],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"One-versus-many is the whole distinction: brute force is depth against a single account, while spraying is breadth across the userbase, tuned to dodge lockouts. The reversal option swaps the two patterns, and neither attack is limited to online or offline use. The tell: brute force = many passwords, one account, spraying = few passwords, many accounts, credential stuffing = replaying leaked pairs."},
  {"id":"q289","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"An attacker points an automated tool at one executive's webmail login page, submitting each entry from a list of the 10,000 most common passwords until the portal accepts one. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["password spraying","credential stuffing","brute force","dictionary attack"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Dictionary attack. Guessing from a curated list of likely passwords is the dictionary method, whether online or against a stolen hash. Spraying loses because it flips the pattern: a few passwords across MANY accounts, while this attack aims a whole wordlist at ONE account. The tell: dictionary = wordlist of likely passwords, brute force = every possible combination, spraying = few passwords across many accounts, credential stuffing = replaying leaked username/password pairs."},
  {"id":"q290","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"An attacker exfiltrates the password hash database, loads it onto a GPU rig, and grinds through every possible candidate offline - no login attempts ever touch the victim's servers. Which of the following attacks is this?","options":["brute force","password spraying","credential stuffing","phishing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Brute force - the offline variety. Exhaustively attacking captured hashes on the attacker's own hardware is brute force with no lockouts or rate limits to slow it, which is why hash strength and stretching matter. The tell: brute force = try everything, offline = against stolen hashes, spraying = live logins across many accounts, credential stuffing = replayed leaked pairs."},
  {"id":"q291","domain":"D2","family":"password-attacks","question":"A defender asks why password spraying so often evades account lockout policies entirely. Which of the following BEST explains why?","options":["It only attacks accounts that have no lockout policy","Each account receives just one or two attempts, staying under every lockout threshold","It disables the lockout mechanism first","Lockout policies do not apply to password attacks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"One or two guesses per account never trips a threshold set at five or ten failures. Spraying trades depth for breadth precisely to stay invisible to lockouts - detection requires correlating failures ACROSS accounts. The tell: spraying = wide and shallow by design, brute force = deep and loud on one account."},
  {"id":"q292","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A SIEM alert fires: the same account logged in successfully from New York at 9:02 a.m. and from Singapore at 9:10 a.m. No VPN explains it. Which of the following indicators of compromise is this?","options":["concurrent session usage","account lockout","impossible travel","out-of-cycle logging"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Impossible travel. Two logins from locations no human could cover in the elapsed time is the impossible-travel indicator. If both sessions were active simultaneously, concurrent session usage would also fire. The tell: impossible travel = distant logins too close in time, concurrent session usage = same account live in multiple sessions at once, account lockout = repeated failures tripping the lock, out-of-cycle logging = events at abnormal times."},
  {"id":"q293","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"Reconstructing a breach timeline, analysts find the security event log simply has no entries for the two hours surrounding the suspected intrusion, though logging ran normally before and after. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["out-of-cycle logging","blocked content","resource inaccessibility","missing logs"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Missing logs. A gap in otherwise continuous logging suggests an attacker cleared or suppressed entries to hide activity. The tell: missing logs = gaps suggesting tampering, out-of-cycle logging = entries at unusual times, resource inaccessibility = services suddenly unreachable, blocked content = filtered access attempts."},
  {"id":"q294","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A nightly batch platform never runs jobs outside its 10 p.m. to midnight window, yet fresh job-log entries appear timestamped 3:14 a.m. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["out-of-cycle logging","missing logs","impossible travel","concurrent session usage"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Out-of-cycle logging. Log entries appearing at times when the activity should never occur point to unauthorized activity, the out-of-cycle indicator. The tell: out-of-cycle logging = entries at abnormal times, missing logs = suspicious gaps, impossible travel = geographically impossible logins, concurrent session usage = simultaneous sessions on one account."},
  {"id":"q295","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"At 2 p.m., every critical file share in the company becomes unreachable for all staff simultaneously, and the storage system shows the volumes intact but access failing. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["resource consumption","resource inaccessibility","account lockout","blocked content"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Resource inaccessibility. Services or data suddenly unavailable across the board is the inaccessibility indicator, a classic early sign of ransomware or denial of service. The tell: resource inaccessibility = resources suddenly unreachable, resource consumption = CPU/disk/bandwidth spiking abnormally, account lockout = authentication lockouts, blocked content = filtered requests."},
  {"id":"q296","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A SIEM shows the same user account actively working from three IP addresses in three different cities at the same moment, all sessions live simultaneously. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["impossible travel","concurrent session usage","account lockout","out-of-cycle logging"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Concurrent session usage. The defining detail is simultaneous active sessions on one account. Impossible travel is about sequential logins too far apart geographically for the time elapsed. The tell: concurrent session usage = one account live in multiple places at once, impossible travel = back-to-back logins from impossible distances, account lockout = failed-attempt lockouts, out-of-cycle logging = events at abnormal times."},
  {"id":"q297","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A server's CPU and outbound bandwidth sit pinned near 100 percent for hours, far above any recorded baseline, with no scheduled job or legitimate workload to explain it. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["resource inaccessibility","blocked content","missing logs","resource consumption"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Resource consumption. Abnormal spikes in CPU, disk, or bandwidth suggest hidden activity like cryptomining or bulk exfiltration. Inaccessibility would mean the resources stopped responding. The tell: resource consumption = usage spiking abnormally, resource inaccessibility = services suddenly unreachable, missing logs = gaps in the record, blocked content = filtered access attempts."},
  {"id":"q298","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"Every morning around 6 a.m., dozens of employees find their accounts locked, and logs show overnight bursts of failed logins against each account from an unknown external host. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["account lockout","concurrent session usage","impossible travel","blocked content"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Account lockout. Repeated failed attempts tripping the lockout policy across many users is the lockout indicator, and the overnight pattern suggests automated password guessing. The tell: account lockout = failed attempts hitting the lock threshold, concurrent session usage = simultaneous live sessions, impossible travel = geographically impossible logins, blocked content = filtered requests."},
  {"id":"q299","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"Proxy logs show one workstation attempting to reach a known malware command-and-control domain forty times an hour, each attempt blocked by the filter. Nothing has gotten through - yet. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["blocked content","impossible travel","account lockout","missing logs"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Blocked content. Repeated filtered attempts to reach known-bad destinations indicate something on that host is trying to phone home - the block worked, but the host needs investigating. The tell: blocked content = denied attempts as the signal, missing logs = suspicious gaps, impossible travel = geographic login anomalies, account lockout = failed-auth lockouts."},
  {"id":"q300","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A threat intel service alerts a company that a database matching its customer records is being offered for sale on a leak forum - the first sign anything was ever taken. Which of the following indicators of compromise is this?","options":["resource consumption","out-of-cycle logging","published/documented data","concurrent session usage"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Published/documented data. Your data appearing for sale or posted externally is direct evidence a breach already happened, however silent it was internally. The tell: published/documented = the stolen data surfaces outside, resource consumption = abnormal usage spikes, out-of-cycle logging = odd-hours events, concurrent sessions = simultaneous logins."},
  {"id":"q301","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"An analyst distinguishes two server symptoms: CPU and bandwidth pinned far above baseline versus file shares that suddenly stop responding entirely. Which of the following pairings of indicator names is correct?","options":["Both are resource inaccessibility","Pinned usage = resource inaccessibility; dead shares = resource consumption","Pinned usage = resource consumption; dead shares = resource inaccessibility","Both are out-of-cycle logging"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Consumption is usage spiking; inaccessibility is resources going dark. Cryptomining and exfiltration drive consumption; ransomware and DoS cause inaccessibility. The tell: resource consumption = too much activity, resource inaccessibility = no availability at all."},
  {"id":"q302","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"An EDR alert shows the following process chain on a finance workstation: winword.exe -> powershell.exe -EncodedCommand JABzAD0A... followed by schtasks /create /tn Updater /sc onlogon. Which of the following indicator categories does this activity represent?","options":["Out-of-cycle logging","Malicious code","Resource consumption","Concurrent session usage"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Malicious code. An Office process spawning an obfuscated Base64 PowerShell one-liner that installs a persistence task is hostile script execution on the host; out-of-cycle logging loses because nothing here concerns when entries were recorded. The tell: malicious code = hostile scripts, macros, or injected code observed executing, out-of-cycle logging = entries at times they should not occur, resource consumption = unexplained CPU or bandwidth spikes, concurrent sessions = one account live in multiple sessions at once."},
  {"id":"q303","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A web administrator reviewing the application log finds this stored comment submission: POST /comments body: user=guest&text=<script src=//cdn-stat.example/x.js>. Which of the following indicator categories does the submitted fragment represent?","options":["Blocked content","Resource inaccessibility","Missing logs","Malicious code"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Malicious code. A script tag injected into a comment field is hostile code planted to execute in other visitors' browsers, an application-layer malicious code indicator; blocked content loses because nothing was filtered or denied - the payload was accepted. The tell: malicious code = hostile script or injected code present in the environment, blocked content = security filters denying access attempts, resource inaccessibility = services suddenly unavailable, missing logs = gaps where entries should exist."},
  {"id":"q304","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A security analyst reviews the overnight authentication log: 23:10-04:36 svc-backup: 214 failed logons from 198.51.100.7; 04:37 svc-backup: account locked out. Which of the following BEST describes the indicator and its likely cause?","options":["Account lockout caused by a brute-force attack","Account lockout caused by password spraying","Impossible travel caused by credential replay","Concurrent session usage caused by token theft"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Account lockout caused by a brute-force attack. Hundreds of failures hammering ONE account until the lockout threshold trips is the brute-force signature; password spraying loses because spraying spreads a few passwords across MANY accounts precisely to avoid triggering lockouts. The tell: brute force = many passwords against one account (lockouts fire), spraying = few passwords against many accounts (lockouts avoided), impossible travel = distant logins too close in time, concurrent sessions = simultaneous active sessions."},
  {"id":"q305","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"An application's session table shows an account authenticated at 08:04 from the corporate LAN and again at 15:52 from a residential ISP in another state. At 16:10 both sessions are still issuing requests. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["Impossible travel","Out-of-cycle logging","Concurrent session usage","Account lockout"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Concurrent session usage. One account actively working from two places at the same moment points to stolen credentials or a hijacked session token; impossible travel loses because the logins were nearly eight hours apart - the anomaly is the simultaneous overlap, not the login sequence. The tell: concurrent sessions = the same account live in multiple sessions at once, impossible travel = sequential logins from distant places too close in time, out-of-cycle logging = oddly timed entries, account lockout = failed-attempt threshold tripped."},
  {"id":"q306","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A help desk ticket reports a laptop that browses normally but times out on every connection to its EDR update server and several antivirus vendor sites. All other endpoints reach those sites fine. Which of the following indicators does this MOST likely represent?","options":["Resource inaccessibility","Blocked content","Missing logs","Resource consumption"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Blocked content. Malware commonly blocks an endpoint's access to security updates and antivirus sites so defenses cannot refresh, and only security destinations failing on one host is that pattern; resource inaccessibility loses because business files and services remain fully available. The tell: blocked content = access to specific content deliberately blocked, especially security sites, resource inaccessibility = files or services broadly unavailable, missing logs = gaps in the record, resource consumption = unexplained utilization spikes."},
  {"id":"q307","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A SIEM shows the following for one user: 08:55 login success, source 81.2.69.142 (London); 09:05 logout; 09:20 login success, source 114.4.78.11 (Jakarta). Which of the following indicators should the analyst flag?","options":["Concurrent session usage","Account lockout","Out-of-cycle logging","Impossible travel"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Impossible travel. Sequential logins from London and Jakarta 25 minutes apart are physically impossible for one person, indicating compromised credentials; concurrent session usage loses because the London session ended before the Jakarta login - the sessions never overlap. The tell: impossible travel = sequential logins from distant locations too close in time, concurrent sessions = simultaneous overlapping sessions, account lockout = failed attempts tripping a threshold, out-of-cycle logging = entries at abnormal times."},
  {"id":"q308","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"A SOC's dark-web monitoring service finds 300 current employee usernames and passwords in a new paste-site dump. No internal alert, ticket, or log entry has suggested any breach. Which of the following indicators is this?","options":["Published/documented data","Missing logs","Blocked content","Concurrent session usage"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Published/documented. The organization's data appearing publicly - paste sites, leak forums, dark-web markets - is often the first evidence of a quiet breach that internal tooling never caught; missing logs loses because no gap in logging has been observed, only silence. The tell: published/documented = your data or credentials surfacing publicly, missing logs = expected entries absent from the record, blocked content = filtered or denied access to content, concurrent sessions = one account live in several sessions at once."},
  {"id":"q309","domain":"D2","family":"indicators","question":"Reviewing an intrusion, an analyst sees the SIEM received no firewall events from 01:00 to 07:00 on the night of the compromise, although the firewall stayed online and entries resume normally afterward. Which of the following indicators should the analyst suspect?","options":["Out-of-cycle logging","Resource inaccessibility","Missing logs","Blocked content"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Missing logs. A clean gap exactly spanning the intrusion window, with normal logging on either side, indicates the attacker deleted or suppressed entries to erase the trail; out-of-cycle logging loses because that means entries appearing at odd times, and here the entries are absent. The tell: missing logs = expected entries gone (gaps or cleared logs), out-of-cycle logging = entries timestamped when no activity should occur, resource inaccessibility = services unavailable, blocked content = access to content denied."},
  {"id":"q310","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"After a single phished workstation led to domain-wide compromise, the network team divides the flat LAN into separate zones for finance, HR, servers, and guests, with ACLs controlling traffic between zones. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["decommissioning","isolation","segmentation","configuration enforcement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Segmentation. Splitting the network into controlled zones limits lateral movement while everything keeps functioning. Isolation would mean fully cutting something off. The tell: segmentation = split into zones with controlled paths, isolation = completely cut off, decommissioning = retiring an asset, configuration enforcement = pushing and holding a secure config."},
  {"id":"q311","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"To stop unauthorized tools and malware from ever executing, a security team configures endpoints so that ONLY the applications on an approved list will run and everything else is blocked by default. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["least privilege","configuration enforcement","patching","application allow list"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Application allow list. Default-deny execution where only approved applications run is allow listing, the inverse of blocking known-bad. The tell: application allow list = only approved apps execute, least privilege = minimum rights for accounts, configuration enforcement = maintain the secure baseline, patching = fixing known vulnerabilities."},
  {"id":"q312","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"An EDR alert confirms a workstation is compromised. The responder immediately cuts it off from all network connectivity - it can reach nothing and nothing can reach it - while forensics proceeds. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["isolation","segmentation","decommissioning","least privilege"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Isolation. Completely severing a host's connectivity during response is isolation; segmentation would still allow controlled paths. The tell: isolation = fully cut off, segmentation = zones with controlled traffic, decommissioning = permanently retiring the asset, least privilege = minimal rights, not a containment move."},
  {"id":"q313","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"A new junior analyst receives read access to exactly the two log repositories her tasks require - no admin rights, no broad shares - so a compromise of her account exposes as little as possible. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["configuration enforcement","least privilege","segmentation","patching"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Least privilege. Granting only the minimum rights the job requires limits the blast radius of any account compromise. The tell: least privilege = minimum rights needed, configuration enforcement = maintaining the secure baseline, segmentation = splitting the network into zones, patching = fixing known flaws."},
  {"id":"q314","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"An aging file server is powered down, its drives are securely wiped, its certificates and accounts are revoked, and it is formally removed from the asset inventory. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["isolation","patching","decommissioning","hardening"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Decommissioning. Securely retiring an asset - wipe, revoke, remove from inventory - is decommissioning, which prevents forgotten systems from becoming backdoors. The tell: decommissioning = securely retire the asset, isolation = cut it off but keep it, patching = fix and keep running, hardening = tighten a live system's configuration."},
  {"id":"q315","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"A vendor releases an emergency out-of-band fix for a flaw being actively exploited in the wild, and the security team pushes it to every affected server within 24 hours, ahead of the normal monthly cycle. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["segmentation","application allow list","decommissioning","patching"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Patching. Applying the vendor's fix removes the vulnerability itself; for actively exploited flaws, speed beats the routine cycle. The tell: patching = eliminate the flaw, segmentation = limit where attackers can go, allow list = control what runs, decommissioning = retire the system."},
  {"id":"q316","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"Building a new web server, an engineer disables every unneeded service, closes unused ports, removes sample applications, changes all default passwords, and installs a host-based firewall before go-live. Which of the following mitigation categories covers this whole checklist?","options":["hardening","isolation","decommissioning","least privilege"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hardening. Systematically shrinking a system's attack surface - services, ports, defaults, extra software - is hardening, done before exposure. The tell: hardening = reduce the attack surface of a live system, isolation = cut it off, least privilege = minimum account rights, decommissioning = retire it."},
  {"id":"q317","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"After a scan reveals the switch management interface is reachable from every desk, the network team applies an entry permitting management access ONLY from the two administrator subnets and denying all else. Which of the following mitigations did they apply?","options":["access control list (ACL)","application allow list","patching","isolation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"ACL. Permit/deny entries restricting which sources may reach the interface are an access control list - network-level access restriction. The tell: ACL = who may connect over the network, application allow list = which programs may run, isolation = cut off entirely, patching = fix code flaws."},
  {"id":"q318","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"Whenever any server's settings drift from the approved secure baseline - a service re-enabled, a registry value changed - the management platform automatically reverts it within the hour, no ticket required. Which of the following mitigations is this?","options":["decommissioning","configuration enforcement","segmentation","application allow list"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Configuration enforcement. Continuously detecting drift and forcing systems back to the secure baseline is enforcement - hardening made permanent. The tell: configuration enforcement = the baseline reasserts itself, hardening = establishing that baseline, allow list = execution control, decommissioning = retirement."},
  {"id":"q319","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"An audit finds the payroll share readable by every domain user. The administrator removes the broad grant and assigns read and modify rights to only the two HR security groups. Which of the following mitigations was applied?","options":["Network access control list","Permissions","Segmentation","Application allow list"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Permissions. The fix reassigns the rights granted on the file share itself - who may read or modify the resource; a network access control list loses because that is an ordered permit/deny rule list on network devices, not rights on a file. The tell: permissions = rights assigned on files, folders, and shares, network ACL = ordered traffic rules by source, destination, and port, segmentation = dividing the network into zones, application allow list = only approved apps may run."},
  {"id":"q320","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"A security team closes two findings: read rights on a finance folder are trimmed to one group, and a router's rule list is updated to deny external SSH. Which of the following pairs names these two mitigations in order?","options":["Access control list, then permissions","Segmentation, then isolation","Application allow list, then hardening","Permissions, then access control list"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Permissions, then access control list. Rights granted on the folder are permissions, while the router's ordered permit/deny rule list is an access control list; the reversed pairing loses because it assigns the network rule list to the file and the file rights to the router. The tell: permissions = the rights themselves on a file or share, ACL = the ordered rule list a device evaluates by source, destination, and port, segmentation = network zones, isolation = complete cutoff."},
  {"id":"q321","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"Retail point-of-sale terminals must run only the payment application, and unknown malware must be unable to execute no matter what a user attempts to launch. Which of the following mitigations BEST meets this requirement?","options":["Application allow list","Application deny list","Least privilege","Endpoint antivirus"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Application allow list. Default-deny execution permits only the approved payment app, so anything else - including never-before-seen malware - simply cannot run; a deny list loses because it blocks only KNOWN bad software, and unknown malware sails past it. The tell: allow list = default deny, only approved apps execute, deny list = default allow, blocks only listed bad apps, least privilege = minimum rights, not execution control, antivirus = detects known malicious code rather than restricting what may run."},
  {"id":"q322","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"Ransomware is actively encrypting a database server. The incident commander requires that the server exchange no traffic with any system until forensics completes; an engineer instead proposes moving it to a restricted VLAN. Which of the following meets the stated requirement?","options":["Segmentation","Decommissioning","Isolation","Network access control list"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Isolation. The requirement is a complete cutoff - zero communication in either direction - which only full isolation provides; segmentation loses because a restricted VLAN still permits some allowed paths, and the requirement is NO traffic. The tell: isolation = totally cut off from everything, segmentation = restricted but still-connected zones, network ACL = rule-filtered traffic still flows, decommissioning = permanently retiring the asset, which would destroy forensic value here."},
  {"id":"q323","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"A vendor releases a fix for a flaw being actively exploited on a company's fully supported web servers, which can be rebooted in tonight's maintenance window. Which of the following is the BEST mitigation?","options":["Move the servers to a restricted segment","Apply the vendor patch tonight","Increase monitoring of the servers","Purchase cyber insurance for the exposure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Patching. Applying the vendor fix removes the flaw itself, and nothing in the scenario blocks it - the fix exists, the systems are supported, and a window is available; segmentation loses because compensating controls are for when patching is NOT possible. The tell: patch available and applicable = patch, cannot patch (end-of-life, uptime constraint) = compensate with segmentation and monitoring, actively infected host = isolate, insurance = transfers financial risk without fixing anything."},
  {"id":"q324","domain":"D2","family":"mitigations","question":"A plant's end-of-life process controller no longer receives vendor updates but must stay in service for three more years. A scanner flags a critical remotely exploitable flaw. Which of the following is the BEST mitigation?","options":["Apply the latest vendor patch","Decommission the controller immediately","Install endpoint protection on the controller","Segment the controller and add monitoring"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Segmentation with added monitoring. When patching is impossible and the asset must stay in service, a compensating control that limits reachability and watches for exploitation is the correct move; applying the vendor patch loses because an end-of-life product has no patch to apply. The tell: can patch = patch, cannot patch but must keep running = segment and monitor (compensating control), decommissioning fails the stay-in-service constraint, and endpoint agents rarely install on closed end-of-life controllers."},
  {"id":"q325","domain":"D2","family":"actor-attributes","question":"A badge-holding maintenance contractor and a remote criminal group both copy the same payroll database using stolen credentials. An analyst notes only one of them already operated inside the company's trust boundary. Which of the following threat actor attributes separates the two?","options":["Internal versus external","Level of sophistication and capability","Resources and funding","Motivation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Internal versus external. The contractor operates inside the trust boundary with a badge while the crime group attacks from outside, so position relative to the organization is what separates them; sophistication loses because the stem says both ran the identical attack. The tell: internal/external = position relative to the organization; resources/funding = the money behind the actor; sophistication/capability = the skill of the tooling; motivation = what the actor wants."},
  {"id":"q326","domain":"D2","family":"actor-attributes","question":"A threat modeling workshop notes that an insider threat bypasses perimeter defenses entirely because of where the actor sits relative to the organization, not because of superior skill or budget. Which of the following actor attributes is being described?","options":["Resources and funding","Motivation","Internal versus external","Level of sophistication and capability"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Internal versus external. The workshop is describing the actor's position relative to the organization, which is exactly why an insider never has to cross the perimeter; sophistication loses because the stem explicitly rules out skill as the explanation. The tell: internal/external = where the actor sits relative to the org; resources/funding = money behind them; sophistication/capability = skill of their tooling; motivation = what they want."},
  {"id":"q327","domain":"D2","family":"actor-attributes","question":"Two adversary groups run identical phishing campaigns, but an intelligence report notes only one can afford to purchase zero-day exploits and keep attack infrastructure running for years at a time. Which of the following actor attributes explains the difference?","options":["Level of sophistication and capability","Internal versus external","Motivation","Resources and funding"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Resources and funding. Affording zero-day purchases and years of infrastructure is a question of money, not skill - the stem says the campaigns are identical, which is why sophistication loses. The tell: resources/funding = the money behind the actor; sophistication/capability = the skill of the tooling and tradecraft; internal/external = position relative to the organization; motivation = what the actor wants."},
  {"id":"q328","domain":"D2","family":"actor-attributes","question":"One adversary abandons its campaign after two weeks, while another sustains an identical campaign for three years, renting fresh infrastructure and buying new exploits whenever defenders adapt. Which of the following attributes BEST explains the difference?","options":["Motivation","Resources and funding","Level of sophistication and capability","Internal versus external"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Resources and funding. Sustaining a campaign for years by continually renting infrastructure and buying exploits takes deep pockets; sophistication loses because both groups ran the identical campaign and the difference was staying power, not skill. The tell: resources/funding = money behind the actor; sophistication/capability = skill of the tooling; internal/external = position relative to the org; motivation = the actor's goal."},
  {"id":"q329","domain":"D2","family":"actor-attributes","question":"An incident review contrasts one intrusion that used custom implants and living-off-the-land techniques to evade every tool with another that reused a copy-pasted forum script and tripped alarms instantly. Which of the following actor attributes is being contrasted?","options":["Resources and funding","Internal versus external","Level of sophistication and capability","Motivation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Level of sophistication and capability. Custom implants and living-off-the-land tradecraft versus a copy-pasted script is a contrast in skill, not budget - funding loses because the stem never mentions money, only the quality of execution. The tell: sophistication/capability = skill of the tooling and tradecraft; resources/funding = money behind the actor; internal/external = position relative to the organization; motivation = what the actor wants."},
  {"id":"q330","domain":"D2","family":"actor-attributes","question":"A threat intelligence report rates an adversary group as high on the attribute reflecting its ability to develop original exploit code and adapt its tradecraft when detected, regardless of its budget or location. Which of the following attributes is being rated?","options":["Level of sophistication and capability","Resources and funding","Motivation","Internal versus external"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Level of sophistication and capability. Developing original exploits and adapting tradecraft under pressure measures skill; resources and funding loses because the rating is explicitly independent of budget. The tell: sophistication/capability = skill of the tooling and tradecraft; resources/funding = money behind the actor; internal/external = position relative to the org; motivation = the actor's goal."},
  {"id":"q331","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"Analysts attribute a three-year covert intrusion at a foreign ministry to a state-backed group that quietly collected diplomatic cables on upcoming trade negotiations and never sold, leaked, or ransomed anything. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Data exfiltration","Espionage","Financial gain","Blackmail"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Espionage. Covert, long-term collection of a government's negotiating positions is spying for strategic advantage; data exfiltration loses because taking the cables was only the means - the purpose was intelligence, and nothing was sold or leaked. The tell: espionage = intelligence for a strategic or competitive sponsor; data exfiltration = the stolen data itself is the goal; financial gain = money; blackmail = a coercive threat."},
  {"id":"q332","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"A manufacturer secretly pays an intrusion crew to monitor a competitor's research servers for two years, quietly relaying prototype specifications to guide its own product roadmap. Nothing is leaked or sold. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Philosophical or political beliefs","Data exfiltration","Revenge","Espionage"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Espionage. This is corporate spying: covert monitoring feeds a competitor's strategy, so the intelligence purpose defines the motivation; data exfiltration loses because the specifications are collected to inform a rival's plans, not as an end in themselves. The tell: espionage = spying for strategic or competitive advantage; data exfiltration = obtaining the data is the goal; philosophical/political = a public cause; revenge = punishing a perceived wrong."},
  {"id":"q333","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"An intrusion at a retailer touches nothing but the customer database, a full copy of which is later recovered intact from attacker-controlled storage. No ransom, fraud, sale, destruction, or nation-state sponsor is ever tied to the incident. Which of the following MOST specifically describes the attacker's motivation?","options":["Data exfiltration","Financial gain","Espionage","Service disruption"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Data exfiltration. The operation touched only the database and existed solely to copy it out to attacker storage, with no sale, fraud, sponsor, or outage, so removing the data was itself the objective, which is exactly what the SY0-701 data exfiltration motivation names. The tell: data exfiltration = the stolen data is the objective; financial gain = money by any method such as ransom, fraud, or sale; espionage = intelligence for a sponsor; service disruption = taking services down."},
  {"id":"q334","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"Hospital analysts discover gigabytes of patient records were compressed and moved to an external server over several nights. The attacker never contacts the hospital, encrypts nothing, and disrupts no services. Which of the following motivations does the activity MOST likely indicate?","options":["Blackmail","Service disruption","Data exfiltration","Espionage"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Data exfiltration. The sustained, quiet copying of records to an external server with no contact, encryption, or outage shows the data itself was the goal; espionage loses because no strategic sponsor or intelligence purpose is indicated. The tell: data exfiltration = the stolen data is the goal; espionage = covert intelligence for a rival or state; blackmail = coercive threat; service disruption = the outage is the objective."},
  {"id":"q335","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"On the morning of its product launch, an online retailer is hit by wiper malware and a traffic flood that keep its storefront down all day. There is no demand or manifesto, only evidence of a crew hired by a rival. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Blackmail","Disruption or chaos","Philosophical or political beliefs","Service disruption"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Service disruption. The attack is engineered to take one victim's services down at the most damaging moment; disruption or chaos loses because timing the outage to the launch and hiring a crew against one target shows deliberate target logic, not random havoc. The tell: service disruption = a specific target's services taken down on purpose; disruption/chaos = havoc with no target logic; philosophical/political = cause plus manifesto; blackmail = pay-or-else threat."},
  {"id":"q336","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"Which of the following scenarios BEST illustrates service disruption as a threat actor's motivation?","options":["Quietly gathering a rival's strategic plans over many months","Taking a target's online services down is itself the end goal","Defacing random, unrelated websites purely for amusement and attention","Threatening to publish stolen files unless the victim pays a demand"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Service disruption is the motivation when knocking a chosen target's services offline is itself the objective; the amusement option loses because its victims are random and attention is the point, which is disruption or chaos. The tell: service disruption = a chosen target's services down as the goal; disruption/chaos = untargeted havoc for attention; blackmail = threat plus demand; espionage = long-term covert collection."},
  {"id":"q337","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"After stealing a law firm's case files, an attacker emails the managing partner: unless payment arrives within 72 hours, the files go to the press. Nothing was encrypted or damaged. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Financial gain","Data exfiltration","Blackmail","Service disruption"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Blackmail. The pay-or-we-publish threat coerces the victim under a deadline, and that coercive threat is the defining feature; financial gain loses because the motivation named for extortion by threat is blackmail, even though money changes hands. The tell: blackmail = a coercive threat unless the victim complies; financial gain = money through theft, fraud, or sale without coercion; data exfiltration = the data itself is the goal; service disruption = outage as the objective."},
  {"id":"q338","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"A ransomware crew encrypts a logistics company's servers and separately threatens to publish customer data copied beforehand unless payment arrives. An analyst must classify the motivation behind the publication threat specifically. Which of the following applies?","options":["Blackmail","Financial gain","Data exfiltration","Disruption or chaos"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Blackmail. The leak threat coerces payment by holding the stolen data over the victim, which classifies that component of double extortion; financial gain loses because the analyst is asked about the publication threat specifically, and its mechanism is coercion. The tell: blackmail = a threat held over the victim to force compliance; financial gain = profit without a coercive threat; data exfiltration = taking the data is the goal; disruption/chaos = havoc for its own sake."},
  {"id":"q339","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"A fraudster spoofs the CFO's mailbox and persuades an accounts payable clerk to wire 240,000 dollars to an attacker-controlled account. No threat is made and no data is taken. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Blackmail","Financial gain","Data exfiltration","Espionage"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Financial gain. Wire fraud through a spoofed executive mailbox is theft where money is the entire point; blackmail loses because the clerk was deceived, not threatened - no coercive demand was ever made. The tell: financial gain = money obtained by theft, fraud, or sale; blackmail = coercion via a threat; data exfiltration = the data itself is the goal; espionage = intelligence collection."},
  {"id":"q340","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"An intrusion group compromises corporate VPN accounts at dozens of companies and sells the working access to ransomware operators for cryptocurrency, never using it themselves. Which of the following BEST describes the group's motivation?","options":["Data exfiltration","Blackmail","Philosophical or political beliefs","Financial gain"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Financial gain. The group is an access broker converting compromised accounts into cryptocurrency, so money is the end goal; data exfiltration loses because no dataset is taken - working access, not data, is the product being sold. The tell: financial gain = money is the point; data exfiltration = stealing the data itself; blackmail = coercive threat; philosophical/political = advancing a cause."},
  {"id":"q341","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"An independent researcher discovers an authentication bypass in a vendor's mobile application and privately reports it through the vendor's coordinated disclosure program, asking only that it be fixed. Which of the following BEST describes the researcher's motivation?","options":["Ethical","Financial gain","Philosophical or political beliefs","Espionage"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ethical. Privately reporting the flaw through coordinated disclosure so it gets fixed is the motivation of improving security; financial gain loses because the researcher asks for nothing but a fix. The tell: ethical = probing and disclosing responsibly to improve security; financial gain = money; philosophical/political = a public cause; espionage = covert intelligence collection."},
  {"id":"q342","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"A hobbyist hacker stumbles on an exposed database belonging to a charity, downloads nothing, and immediately notifies the charity with steps to secure it, declining the reward the charity offers. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Financial gain","Revenge","Ethical","Data exfiltration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ethical. Notifying the victim, taking nothing, and turning down a reward shows the goal was making the charity safer; financial gain loses because the offered reward was refused outright. The tell: ethical = responsible disclosure to improve security; financial gain = money; revenge = punishing a perceived wrong; data exfiltration = taking the data is the goal."},
  {"id":"q343","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"Over a single weekend, an attacker defaces dozens of unrelated small-business websites with joke images and brags about it on social media. The victims share no industry or cause, and no demands are made. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Philosophical or political beliefs","Service disruption","Financial gain","Disruption or chaos"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Disruption or chaos. Random unrelated victims, joke content, social media bragging, and zero demands mean havoc and attention are the point; philosophical or political loses because no cause or message accompanies the defacements. The tell: disruption/chaos = untargeted havoc for its own sake; philosophical/political = a cause and a manifesto; service disruption = one chosen target's services taken down deliberately; financial gain = money."},
  {"id":"q344","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"An intruder with no known tie to a company roams its network deleting random files, changing desktop wallpapers, and crashing print servers. Nothing is stolen, no demand arrives, and no statement is published. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Revenge","Disruption or chaos","Service disruption","Blackmail"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Disruption or chaos. Random destruction by an outsider with no theft, no demand, and no statement is havoc for its own sake; revenge loses because the intruder has no known grievance or connection to the company to punish. The tell: disruption/chaos = havoc with no target logic or cause; revenge = punishing a perceived wrong by someone with a grievance; service disruption = the outage of a chosen target is the objective; blackmail = coercive demand."},
  {"id":"q345","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"In the opening hours of a ground offensive, coordinated cyberattacks disable the defending nation's power grid operators and military communications networks. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation behind the cyberattacks?","options":["Espionage","Service disruption","War","Philosophical or political beliefs"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"War. Cyberattacks synchronized with a military offensive against a defender's grid and communications support combat objectives, which is cyberwarfare; service disruption loses because the outages are the method serving a military campaign, not the end in itself. The tell: war = cyber operations supporting military objectives; service disruption = the outage itself is the goal; espionage = covert intelligence; philosophical/political = activism for a cause."},
  {"id":"q346","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"After a county board approves a pipeline, a group doxxes the board members, leaks the planning documents, and publishes a manifesto demanding the approval be reversed. No payment is requested. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Philosophical or political beliefs","Blackmail","Financial gain","Disruption or chaos"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Philosophical or political beliefs. The campaign publicly pressures a policy reversal and explains itself in a manifesto, so the cause is the motivation; blackmail loses because nothing is held back in exchange for a demand - the leak already happened in service of the cause, and no payment is sought. The tell: philosophical/political = a cause plus a public message; blackmail = a threat withheld until the victim complies; financial gain = money; disruption/chaos = havoc with no cause."},
  {"id":"q347","domain":"D2","family":"threat-motivations","question":"An engineer demoted in a reorganization uses his still-active credentials to corrupt the code repository of the project that replaced his former role. He gains nothing financially and tells no one. Which of the following BEST describes the motivation?","options":["Financial gain","Revenge","Disruption or chaos","Blackmail"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Revenge. Sabotaging exactly the project that replaced his role punishes the perceived wrong of the demotion; disruption or chaos loses because the precise target choice reveals a personal grievance, not random havoc. The tell: revenge = punishing a perceived wrong; disruption/chaos = havoc with no personal target logic; financial gain = money; blackmail = a coercive threat with a demand."},
  {"id":"q348","domain":"D2","family":"physical-attacks","question":"Overnight, an intruder crowbars open a data closet door and smashes the lock off a server rack, tripping the forced-entry alarm. Nothing was cloned, badged, or logged in. Which of the following attacks occurred?","options":["Physical brute force","Brute-force password attack","RFID cloning","Environmental attack"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Physical brute force. Forced entry with visible damage - pried doors, smashed locks - is the physical version of brute force; a brute-force password attack loses because no credentials were guessed anywhere, the same words just live in a different domain. The tell: physical brute force = forced-entry damage; RFID cloning = duplicated badge credentials open doors cleanly; environmental attack = sabotage of the facility's power or HVAC systems."},
  {"id":"q349","domain":"D2","family":"physical-attacks","question":"A security analyst reviews badge logs: 07:58 badge 4417 enters Building A; 08:09 badge 4417 enters Building C, 30 km away. The badge's owner is abroad on vacation. Which of the following attacks MOST likely occurred?","options":["Physical brute force","Environmental attack","RFID cloning","Credential replay"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"RFID cloning. One badge appearing at two distant readers minutes apart while its owner is away means the credential was duplicated - the physical twin of impossible travel; credential replay loses because it describes resending captured network authentication, not presenting a copied physical badge at doors. The tell: RFID cloning = duplicated badge credentials; physical brute force = forced-entry damage; environmental attack = sabotage of the facility's power or HVAC systems."},
  {"id":"q350","domain":"D2","family":"physical-attacks","question":"A stranger repeatedly rides an office elevator standing unusually close to employees while holding a small device near their badge pockets. Days later, an unissued fob opens the lab door. Which of the following attacks does this indicate?","options":["Environmental attack","RFID cloning","Physical brute force","On-path attack"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"RFID cloning. The handheld reader skimmed badge data at close range, and the copy written to a blank fob now opens doors as if legitimate; on-path loses because nothing intercepted network traffic between two parties - a physical credential was duplicated. The tell: RFID cloning = duplicated badge credentials; physical brute force = forced-entry damage; environmental attack = sabotage of the facility's power or HVAC systems."},
  {"id":"q351","domain":"D2","family":"physical-attacks","question":"An intruder enters a mechanical room and manually shuts off the data center's cooling loop. No systems are touched and no data alarms fire, but the server hall overheats until hardware fails. Which of the following attack types is this?","options":["Distributed denial-of-service","Physical brute force","RFID cloning","Environmental attack"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Environmental attack. Sabotaging the facility's HVAC takes systems down by attacking their environment rather than the systems themselves; DDoS loses because availability was destroyed through heat, not traffic floods. The tell: environmental attack = sabotage of the facility's power or HVAC systems; physical brute force = forced-entry damage; RFID cloning = duplicated badge credentials."},
  {"id":"q352","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"Sales laptops spend weeks on hotel and airport networks where the corporate perimeter cannot filter traffic reaching them. The security team wants each laptop to block unsolicited inbound connections wherever it is. Which of the following should be deployed?","options":["A host-based firewall","A host-based intrusion prevention system","Full-disk encryption","Disabling unused ports and protocols"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Host-based firewall. Per-host inbound and outbound filtering by port, address, and application travels with the laptop, protecting it when no network firewall stands in front; HIPS loses because the stated need is blocking unsolicited connections by rule, not inspecting allowed traffic for exploit patterns. The tell: host-based firewall = filters connections by attributes (port, IP, app), HIPS = inspects traffic and drops known attack patterns, disabling ports/protocols = shrinks listening services, full-disk encryption = protects data at rest, not connections."},
  {"id":"q353","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A security engineer describes two host protections: one rejects inbound traffic that is not from the management subnet based purely on source address and port; the other inspects permitted traffic and drops packets matching exploit signatures. Which of the following pairs is correct?","options":["HIPS, then host-based firewall","Host-based firewall, then HIDS","Host-based firewall, then HIPS","HIDS, then HIPS"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Host-based firewall, then HIPS. Filtering by connection attributes such as source and port is firewall logic, while inspecting already-allowed traffic for exploit patterns and dropping it is prevention logic; the HIDS pairing loses because a HIDS only alerts and never drops packets. The tell: host-based firewall = \"that port is closed\", HIPS = \"this packet matches an exploit, dropped\", HIDS = detects and alerts only, with no blocking."},
  {"id":"q354","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A public web server must keep port 443 open, but the team wants traffic that already passed the network firewall to be inspected on the host and dropped when it matches known exploit patterns. Which of the following should be installed?","options":["A host-based intrusion detection system","A host-based intrusion prevention system","A host-based firewall","An application allow list"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Host-based intrusion prevention system. A HIPS sits in the host's traffic path, matches signatures and behavior, and actively drops malicious packets that a port-based filter must let through; a HIDS loses because it only detects and alerts, never blocks. The tell: HIPS = inspects and blocks on the host, HIDS = inspects and alerts only, host-based firewall = filters by port and address, which cannot help when the port must stay open, allow list = controls which applications execute, not network traffic."},
  {"id":"q355","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"During a review, a security analyst notes that a database server's host firewall correctly allowed traffic on port 1433, yet an exploit inside that allowed session compromised the service. Which of the following host protections would have blocked the exploit?","options":["Stricter host firewall rules","Disabling unused ports and protocols","Changing default passwords","A host-based intrusion prevention system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Host-based intrusion prevention system. The attack rode a legitimately allowed connection, so only on-host inspection that recognizes and drops exploit patterns inside permitted traffic could stop it; stricter firewall rules lose because the port must stay open for the database and the connection's attributes were legitimate. The tell: HIPS = blocks malicious content inside allowed connections, host firewall = permits or denies by attributes and cannot judge payloads, disabling ports = removes unneeded listeners, default password changes = stops factory-credential logins."},
  {"id":"q356","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A scan of a newly built server returns: 23/tcp open telnet; 445/tcp open microsoft-ds (SMBv1 enabled); 443/tcp open https. Only HTTPS is required for the server's role. Which of the following hardening steps directly remediates the findings?","options":["Changing default passwords","Disabling unused ports and protocols","Installing a host-based intrusion prevention system","Removing unnecessary software"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Disabling unused ports and protocols. Turning off Telnet and SMBv1 removes the unneeded listening services entirely, so the exposures disappear from the next scan; removing unnecessary software loses because these findings are enabled services and protocols, not installed application packages. The tell: disabling ports/protocols = shrink the set of listening services, software removal = uninstall unneeded packages, default password changes = kill factory logins, HIPS = drop exploit-pattern traffic while the service keeps listening."},
  {"id":"q357","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A hardening baseline for new Linux servers includes the requirement: reduce the number of services accepting network connections to the minimum the role needs. Which of the following steps satisfies this line item?","options":["Removal of unnecessary software","Installing endpoint protection","Disabling unused ports and protocols","Changing default passwords"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Disabling unused ports and protocols. Stopping unneeded services and closing their listeners is the step that directly shrinks how many services accept connections; removal of unnecessary software loses because it targets installed packages, and a package can be present without listening on the network. The tell: disabling ports/protocols = fewer listening services, software removal = fewer installed (patchable) packages, endpoint protection = detect and block malware locally, default password changes = eliminate factory credentials."},
  {"id":"q358","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A technician is finishing the deployment checklist for 60 new IP cameras. Attackers routinely try the factory logins printed in the vendor's public manual. Which of the following checklist steps stops those attempts from succeeding?","options":["Changing the default passwords","Disabling unused ports and protocols","Installing a host-based firewall","Removing unnecessary software"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Default password changes. Replacing factory credentials at deployment means the publicly documented logins no longer work, which directly defeats attackers who try them; disabling ports and protocols loses because the management interface must remain reachable - the flaw is the known credential, not the open service. The tell: default password changes = kill factory logins, disabling ports/protocols = remove unneeded listeners, host-based firewall = filter connections by rule, software removal = shrink patchable surface."},
  {"id":"q359","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"After several network printers were reconfigured by an outsider, an investigation finds each web console still accepted the administrator credentials from the publicly downloadable setup guide. Which of the following hardening steps would have prevented the compromise?","options":["Installing a host-based intrusion prevention system","Removing unnecessary software","Disabling unused ports and protocols","Changing default passwords during deployment"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Default password changes. The outsider simply logged in with documented factory credentials, so replacing them during deployment removes the exact access path used; disabling ports and protocols loses because the web console is a needed management service - the problem was the credential, not the listener. The tell: default password changes = factory logins stop working, disabling ports/protocols = unneeded services stop listening, HIPS = drops exploit-pattern traffic (no exploit was used here), software removal = fewer installed packages."},
  {"id":"q360","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A vulnerability scan of a new application server flags vendor sample applications and an unused database administration console left over from installation. Which of the following hardening steps remediates these findings?","options":["Disabling unused ports and protocols","Removal of unnecessary software","Changing default passwords","Installing a host-based firewall"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Removal of unnecessary software. Uninstalling the sample apps and unused console deletes the vulnerable code outright, so there is nothing left to patch or exploit; disabling ports and protocols loses because these findings are installed packages, and stopping a listener still leaves the vulnerable software on disk. The tell: software removal = uninstall unneeded (patchable) packages, disabling ports/protocols = stop unneeded listening services, default password changes = replace factory logins, host-based firewall = rule-based connection filtering."},
  {"id":"q361","domain":"D2","family":"host-hardening","question":"A desktop engineering team wants to shrink each workstation's patch workload and attack surface by eliminating applications that telemetry shows no employee has launched in a year. Which of the following hardening steps is this?","options":["Configuration enforcement","Application allow list","Removal of unnecessary software","Disabling unused ports and protocols"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Removal of unnecessary software. Every installed package is attack surface that must be patched, so uninstalling unused applications reduces both exposure and maintenance effort; an application allow list loses because blocking execution still leaves the vulnerable packages installed and needing patches. The tell: software removal = the package is gone, allow list = the package cannot run but remains installed, disabling ports/protocols = fewer listening services, configuration enforcement = automatically holding settings at the baseline."},
  {"id":"q362","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"A DevOps team packages an application with just its libraries and dependencies, no guest operating system. Instances share the host's kernel, start in seconds, and a hundred of them fit where a dozen VMs used to. Which of the following technologies is being used?","options":["containerization","virtualization","serverless","microservices"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Containerization. Sharing the host kernel and packaging only the app plus dependencies is the container model, which is why it is lighter and faster than full VMs. The tell: containerization = shared kernel, app + deps only, virtualization = full guest OS per VM on a hypervisor, serverless = provider-run functions, microservices = an app architecture of small services."},
  {"id":"q363","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"A team deploys code that runs only when an upload event fires. The cloud provider manages every server, patches everything underneath, and bills per execution; the team never sees an instance. Which of the following cloud models is being used?","options":["virtualization","containerization","serverless","microservices"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Serverless. Event-triggered functions on fully provider-managed infrastructure, billed per execution, define serverless computing. The tell: serverless = no server to manage, pay per run, containerization = you still run the container hosts, virtualization = full VMs you administer, microservices = the design pattern, not the hosting model."},
  {"id":"q364","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"After an outage, an operations team rebuilds its entire cloud environment in an hour by re-running declarative template files stored in version control. Every subnet, server, and policy comes back identically. Which of the following practices made this possible?","options":["virtualization","containerization","microservices","infrastructure as code (IaC)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Infrastructure as code. Provisioning infrastructure from versioned template files makes environments repeatable, auditable, and quick to rebuild. The tell: IaC = infrastructure defined in code/templates, virtualization = VMs on a hypervisor, containerization = kernel-sharing app packages, microservices = small independent services."},
  {"id":"q365","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"A hosting provider runs twelve isolated customer servers on one physical machine. Each carries its own complete guest operating system, and a hypervisor allocates the hardware among them. Which of the following technologies is this?","options":["Containerization","Serverless","Microservices","Virtualization"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Virtualization. A full guest OS per instance managed by a hypervisor is classic virtualization; containers would share one kernel instead. The tell: virtualization = full guest OS per VM on a hypervisor, containerization = shared kernel, app + deps only, serverless = provider-run functions, microservices = an application design pattern."},
  {"id":"q366","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"An e-commerce platform is rebuilt from one monolith into small, independently deployable services - cart, payment, catalog - that communicate over APIs and scale separately when load demands. Which of the following designs is this?","options":["Microservices","Containerization","Virtualization","Serverless"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Microservices. Splitting an application into small independent services with API communication is the microservices architecture; how each service is hosted (containers, VMs, serverless) is a separate choice. The tell: microservices = the app split into independent services, containerization = the packaging technology, virtualization = full VMs, serverless = provider-managed functions."},
  {"id":"q367","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"A developer builds an application image bundling her code with its libraries, then runs that identical image unchanged on her laptop, the test cluster, and the production hosts. Which of the following technologies provides this portability?","options":["Virtualization","Serverless","Containerization","IaC"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Containerization. A portable image carrying the app plus its dependencies runs identically anywhere a container runtime exists; virtualization loses because moving a VM between environments means hauling an entire guest OS along. The tell: containerization = portable app + deps image on a shared host kernel, virtualization = full guest OS per VM, serverless = provider-run functions with no image of a server to carry, IaC = provisioning infrastructure from code, not a runtime."},
  {"id":"q368","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"A deployment template containing a hardcoded database password is used to provision 200 cloud instances, giving every one of them the identical weakness. Which of the following infrastructure as code implications does this demonstrate?","options":["Configuration drift accumulating between instances","A single template flaw replicating into every deployment","Virtual machine sprawl escaping the asset inventory","A container escape exposing the shared host kernel"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A single template flaw replicating into every deployment. IaC builds every instance from the same definition, so a hardcoded secret or misconfiguration is stamped out identically at scale - the risk side of its consistency benefit; configuration drift is tempting but it is the problem IaC eliminates, not the one it creates. The tell: IaC = infrastructure provisioned from code/templates, so flaws propagate everywhere instantly; sprawl belongs to unmanaged VMs and escape belongs to containers sharing a kernel."},
  {"id":"q369","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"After migrating a batch job to event-driven serverless functions, a developer asks which operating system patching duties the team retains. Which of the following BEST describes the team's remaining security surface?","options":["The guest operating system and its runtime patches","The hypervisor and the underlying host firmware","The function code, its permissions, and its dependencies","The physical servers that execute each function"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The function code, its permissions, and its dependencies. Serverless removes every OS-level patching task because the provider owns and patches the entire platform beneath the function; the guest operating system option is tempting because that duty exists in IaaS, but in serverless there is no server the customer manages at all. The tell: serverless = the provider runs the functions and everything below them; the customer secures only code, function permissions, and third-party dependencies."},
  {"id":"q370","domain":"D3","family":"containerization","question":"A retailer splits its monolithic application into dozens of independently deployed services that communicate over internal APIs. Which of the following is the security team's MOST significant new concern?","options":["Patching one large shared application runtime","A single code flaw exposing the entire application","Longer deployment cycles for every release","Authenticating and securing service-to-service API traffic"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authenticating and securing service-to-service API traffic. Microservices turn internal function calls into network communication, so every API path between services must now be authenticated and encrypted (often with mutual TLS); a single flaw exposing the entire application is tempting but that is the monolith's problem the split just reduced. The tell: microservices = the app decomposed into small independent services, shrinking blast radius but multiplying network paths; the shared runtime and big-bang releases are monolith traits."},
  {"id":"q371","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A defense contractor's classified analysis workstation has no network cabling, its radios physically removed, and data arrives only on inspected removable media signed in through a checkpoint. Which of the following isolation models is in use?","options":["logical segmentation","SDN","on-premises","air-gapped"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Air-gapped. Physical isolation with literally no network connection is the air gap, the strongest and least convenient isolation model. The tell: air-gapped = physically disconnected, logical segmentation = separated by VLANs/subnets on shared hardware, SDN = software-controlled networking, on-premises = merely hosted in your own building."},
  {"id":"q372","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A network engineer separates finance, engineering, and guest traffic into different VLANs and subnets that all ride the same physical switches. Nothing is physically separate. Which of the following isolation models is being used?","options":["air-gapped","SDN","logical segmentation","decentralized"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Logical segmentation. Separation enforced in configuration (VLANs, subnets) over shared physical hardware is logical, not physical, isolation. The tell: logical segmentation = VLANs/subnets on shared gear, air-gapped = physically disconnected, SDN = control plane software-driven, decentralized = distributed control, not a separation method."},
  {"id":"q373","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A data center team stops configuring switches box by box. Instead, a central software controller programs all forwarding behavior through APIs, separating the decision-making layer from the packet-moving layer. Which of the following technologies is being described?","options":["SDN","air-gapped","logical segmentation","centralized"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SDN. Software-defined networking splits the control plane (the controller's decisions) from the data plane (the switches forwarding packets) and manages the network programmatically. The tell: SDN = software controller programs the network, logical segmentation = VLAN/subnet separation, air-gapped = no connection at all, centralized = a governance style, not the networking technology."},
  {"id":"q374","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A global retailer routes every network configuration decision and policy change through one headquarters control point that every site depends on: consistent, but a single point of failure. Which of the following models does this describe?","options":["Decentralized","Centralized","Air-gapped","Logical segmentation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Centralized. One control point making decisions for everything is centralization, with consistency as the upside and a single point of failure as the risk. The tell: centralized = one control point, decentralized = authority spread across independent nodes, air-gapped = physically disconnected, logical segmentation = VLAN/subnet separation."},
  {"id":"q375","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A blockchain network spreads decision authority across thousands of independent nodes so that no single node controls the system and no single failure stops it. Which of the following models is this?","options":["Centralized","Air-gapped","Decentralized","SDN"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Decentralized. Authority distributed across independent nodes with no single controller is decentralization, trading consistency guarantees for resilience against single-point failure. The tell: decentralized = distributed control, centralized = one control point, air-gapped = no connectivity at all, SDN = software-driven network control (which is typically centralized)."},
  {"id":"q376","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A security analyst reviews endpoint events from a classified air-gapped workstation: 09:12 new device connected - USB mass storage; 09:14 file written from drive E: report_tool.exe; 09:17 malware detected: report_tool.exe. Which of the following is the MOST likely infection vector?","options":["Removable media carried to the workstation","A phishing email attachment","A drive-by download from a compromised website","Lateral movement from an infected network peer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Removable media carried to the workstation. The USB insertion minutes before detection matches the one vector that can cross an air gap, since files reach the machine only by hand-carried media; a phishing attachment is tempting as the most common vector elsewhere, but this host cannot receive email. The tell: air-gapped = no network connection at all, so email, web, and lateral movement are impossible; removable media becomes THE attack vector for physically isolated systems."},
  {"id":"q377","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"A penetration test report states that compromising a single management component let testers rewrite forwarding behavior on every switch in the software-defined network. Which of the following components did the testers MOST likely capture?","options":["An access-layer switch forwarding packets","The centralized SDN controller","A passive network tap","An edge router at the perimeter"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The centralized SDN controller. SDN concentrates all forwarding decisions in the controller's control plane, so capturing it grants command of every device it programs - the architecture's single high-value target; an access-layer switch is tempting because switches move the packets, but one switch yields only its own traffic. The tell: SDN = control plane (the centralized controller deciding) split from the data plane (switches forwarding); own the controller and you own the network."},
  {"id":"q378","domain":"D3","family":"isolation-models","question":"An OT engineer reviews three proposals for the turbine control network: a VLAN reachable only through a firewall, a network with no routed path to any other network, and a network with no physical connection at all. Which of the following correctly names the three designs in order?","options":["Isolation, segmentation, air gap","Air gap, isolation, segmentation","Segmentation, isolation, air gap","Segmentation, air gap, isolation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Segmentation, isolation, air gap. A VLAN with a firewalled path keeps controlled connectivity (segmentation), removing every routed path cuts the network off logically (isolation), and removing the physical connection is the strongest form (air gap); the orderings that put air gap on a design that still has cabling lose because an air gap tolerates no connection whatsoever. The tell: segmentation = divided but controlled paths exist; isolation = fully cut off logically; air gap = physically disconnected, data moves only by removable media."},
  {"id":"q379","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"A water utility's control room monitors pumping stations spread across three counties, collecting telemetry and sending commands to remote sites over wide-area links. Which of the following BEST names this supervisory system?","options":["IoT","RTOS","ICS","SCADA"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SCADA. Supervisory control and data acquisition is the wide-area monitoring and command layer sitting above industrial processes, classic for utilities. The tell: SCADA = supervisory layer over wide areas, ICS = the industrial control systems running the physical processes themselves, IoT = internet-connected smart devices, RTOS = the timing-guaranteed operating system inside devices."},
  {"id":"q380","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"An engineer is designing an airbag controller that must respond within strict deadlines every single time, with no best-effort scheduling allowed. Which of the following BEST meets this requirement?","options":["SCADA","RTOS","IoT","ICS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"RTOS. A real-time operating system guarantees deterministic response within fixed deadlines, exactly what a safety device like an airbag controller demands; ICS is tempting because the controller sits inside a machine, but ICS names industrial process control systems, not the timing-guaranteed software. The tell: RTOS = guaranteed timing for real-time tasks, ICS = industrial control systems running physical processes, SCADA = the wide-area supervisory layer above ICS, IoT = internet-connected smart devices."},
  {"id":"q381","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"A homeowner installs smart thermostats, cameras, and video doorbells that all connect to home Wi-Fi and report to a phone app through the vendor's cloud. Which of the following categories do these devices fall under?","options":["ICS","IoT","SCADA","RTOS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"IoT. Internet-connected consumer smart devices are the Internet of Things, notorious for weak default credentials and rare patches. The tell: IoT = internet-connected consumer/smart devices, ICS = industrial systems running physical processes, SCADA = the wide-area supervisory layer, RTOS = the timing-guaranteed OS inside embedded gear."},
  {"id":"q382","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"On a factory floor, networked controllers and sensors directly operate the assembly-line machinery - starting motors, regulating temperature, halting lines on faults. Which of the following BEST describes these systems?","options":["IoT","RTOS","ICS","SCADA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"ICS. Industrial control systems directly run physical processes on-site; SCADA would be the supervisory layer watching many such sites over a wide area. The tell: ICS = runs the physical process, SCADA = supervises ICS across distances, IoT = consumer smart devices, RTOS = the real-time OS inside controllers."},
  {"id":"q383","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"A medical device maker documents the small purpose-built computer permanently installed inside each pacemaker to run its firmware and control pacing functions. Which of the following BEST classifies this component?","options":["Embedded system","IoT device","SCADA system","Real-time operating system"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Embedded system. A special-purpose computer built into a larger device to perform a fixed function is the definition of embedded, regardless of whether it ever touches a network; real-time operating system is tempting because a pacemaker likely runs one, but the question asks what the computer itself is, not which OS type it runs. The tell: embedded = purpose-built computer inside a device; IoT = internet-connected smart device; SCADA = supervisory layer over industrial control; RTOS = the timing-deterministic OS an embedded device may run."},
  {"id":"q384","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"Dozens of smart cameras on a corporate LAN, still using factory default credentials, are discovered participating in a botnet. Which of the following would BEST secure this class of device going forward?","options":["Install endpoint detection and response agents on each camera","Rely on the vendor's automatic firmware update service","Block all outbound traffic at the perimeter firewall","Move the cameras to a dedicated VLAN and change default credentials"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Move the cameras to a dedicated VLAN and change default credentials. IoT devices cannot be hardened like servers, so the canonical fix is removing the default passwords that let the botnet in and segmenting the devices away from corporate assets; installing EDR agents is tempting but IoT hardware lacks the compute and vendor support to host them. The tell: IoT = weak by design (default credentials, rare patches, no room for agents), so the mitigation is segmentation plus credential and firmware hygiene, not endpoint software."},
  {"id":"q385","domain":"D3","family":"embedded-systems","question":"A biomedical engineer explains why the hospital's standard endpoint protection agent cannot be installed on its infusion pumps. Which of the following BEST explains the constraint?","options":["The pumps' firmware storage is too small to retain security logs","The pumps' real-time operating system cannot tolerate added processing latency","The pumps' network segment drops the agent's management traffic","The pumps' warranty requires vendor approval for configuration changes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The pumps' real-time operating system cannot tolerate added processing latency. An RTOS guarantees deterministic response within strict deadlines, and security software that steals cycles or delays scheduling can jeopardize dosing accuracy, so agents are unacceptable by design; the warranty answer is tempting because vendor restrictions are real, but the stated constraint is architectural, not contractual. The tell: RTOS = deterministic timing guarantees come first, so latency-adding security software is excluded and protection moves to the network (segmentation, monitoring)."},
  {"id":"q386","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"An architecture review praises a design that keeps serving users while individual components fail, then heals itself back to full capacity automatically. Which of the following architectural properties is being described?","options":["availability","resilience","scalability","responsiveness"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Resilience. Absorbing failure, continuing to operate, and recovering is resilience; availability is the resulting uptime, but the property described is the ability to take a punch. The tell: resilience = recover and keep serving under failure, availability = uptime now, scalability = grow with load, responsiveness = latency of responses."},
  {"id":"q387","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"Unable to reduce a breach risk any further with controls it can afford, a company buys a cyber insurance policy so the financial impact would land on the insurer. Which of the following considerations does this reflect?","options":["risk transference","resilience","scalability","cost"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Risk transference. Shifting the financial consequences of a risk to a third party, classically via insurance, is transference. The tell: risk transference = a third party absorbs the impact, resilience = withstand and recover, cost = the budget factor itself, scalability = growth capacity."},
  {"id":"q388","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"A streaming platform architect designs for a live sports final: the system must smoothly add capacity to absorb a tenfold jump in concurrent viewers, then shrink back afterward. Which of the following architecture considerations is this?","options":["Scalability","Resilience","Responsiveness","Availability"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Scalability. Growing (and shrinking) to handle changing load is scalability. The tell: scalability = grow to meet load, resilience = withstand failures and recover, responsiveness = how fast each request returns, availability = the uptime itself."},
  {"id":"q389","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"An SLA workshop distinguishes uptime from speed: which of the following architecture considerations specifically concerns how quickly the system returns an answer to each user request, measured as latency?","options":["Availability","Scalability","Responsiveness","Resilience"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Responsiveness. Latency of responses is responsiveness; a system can be up (available) yet still painfully slow. The tell: responsiveness = latency of the response, availability = whether it responds at all, scalability = capacity growth, resilience = surviving failures."},
  {"id":"q390","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"An online brokerage selects a dual data center design with redundant components at every layer specifically to meet a contractual 99.99% uptime commitment. Which of the following architecture considerations drives this design?","options":["Resilience","Scalability","Availability","Responsiveness"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Availability. A design chosen to satisfy an uptime percentage targets the fraction of time the service is reachable, which is availability by definition; resilience is tempting because redundancy also helps systems recover, but the stated goal is the uptime number itself, not how gracefully failures are absorbed. The tell: availability = uptime now; resilience = absorbing failure and bouncing back; responsiveness = how fast each request is answered; scalability = growing to meet load."},
  {"id":"q391","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"Presenting a cloud migration to the board, a CFO emphasizes that the company will stop purchasing servers as capital assets and instead pay a monthly operating expense. Which of the following architecture considerations is being weighed?","options":["Cost","Risk transference","Ease of deployment","Scalability"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Cost. Trading capital expense for operating expense is the classic financial framing of a cloud migration, and it is purely about how the architecture is paid for; risk transference is tempting because moving to a provider does shift some risk, but the CFO's argument is about spending structure, not shifting liability. The tell: cost = the capex/opex expense of the architecture; risk transference = shifting risk to a third party; ease of deployment = effort to stand it up; scalability = growing with load."},
  {"id":"q392","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"During a flash sale, an e-commerce site never goes down, but every page takes 40 seconds to load and customers abandon their carts. Which of the following architecture considerations failed?","options":["Availability","Resilience","Ease of recovery","Responsiveness"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Responsiveness. The site stayed up, so the failure is in how quickly it answers each request - latency, the heart of responsiveness; availability is tempting because slow feels like broken, but by definition the service remained reachable the entire time. The tell: availability = up and reachable now; responsiveness = answers fast; resilience = bounces back from failure; ease of recovery = how simply the environment is restored afterward."},
  {"id":"q393","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"A tax preparation service must expand its infrastructure to absorb filing-deadline traffic many times its normal volume, then contract afterward to control spending. Which of the following architecture considerations does this requirement describe?","options":["Responsiveness","Scalability","Availability","Compute"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Scalability. Growing to meet a load surge and shrinking back afterward is the ability to scale, which cloud elasticity automates; responsiveness is tempting because the surge threatens page speed, but the requirement is about adding and removing capacity, not the latency of each answer. The tell: scalability = grow (and shrink) with load; responsiveness = how fast requests are answered; availability = uptime; compute = the raw processing capacity a platform can offer."},
  {"id":"q394","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"A startup must launch a new customer portal within five days and cannot wait for hardware procurement or rack installation. Which of the following architecture considerations favors a cloud-based design in this scenario?","options":["Patch availability","Ease of recovery","Ease of deployment","Power"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ease of deployment. The deciding factor is how quickly the solution can be stood up, and cloud with infrastructure as code deploys in hours while on-premises hardware takes weeks; ease of recovery is tempting because it also favors cloud, but the stem's constraint is launch speed, not restoring after a failure. The tell: ease of deployment = effort to stand the solution up initially; ease of recovery = effort to restore it after failure; patch availability = whether the vendor still ships fixes; power = electrical supply constraints."},
  {"id":"q395","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"An architect designs the environment so nightly snapshots plus infrastructure as code templates can rebuild every server within one hour of a total failure. Which of the following considerations does this design optimize?","options":["Ease of recovery","Ease of deployment","Resilience","Availability"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ease of recovery. Snapshots plus redeployable templates make restoring the whole environment fast and simple after a failure, which is exactly what this consideration measures; resilience is tempting because recovery is part of bouncing back, but resilience describes the system continuing to serve through failure, while this design addresses how easily it is rebuilt afterward. The tell: ease of recovery = how quickly/simply you restore after failure; ease of deployment = initial stand-up effort; resilience = absorbing failure while serving; availability = the uptime outcome."},
  {"id":"q396","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"An engineer comparing two firewall appliance lines notes that one vendor still releases regular security fixes while the other line is end-of-sale with no further updates promised. Which of the following architecture considerations differentiates the two options?","options":["Inability to patch","Compute","Resilience","Patch availability"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Patch availability. Whether the vendor still supplies security fixes is a vendor-side supply question, and it separates a supported product line from one heading to end-of-life; inability to patch is tempting but that describes a system that cannot practically be updated at all, while both appliances here can apply patches if the vendor ships them. The tell: patch availability = does the vendor still supply fixes (vendor-side); inability to patch = the system itself cannot be updated (system-side), which drives compensating controls."},
  {"id":"q397","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"An industrial controller on the production line has no mechanism to receive software updates of any kind, so the security team surrounds it with firewalled segmentation and monitoring. Which of the following considerations forced the compensating controls?","options":["Patch availability","Inability to patch","Ease of recovery","Compute"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Inability to patch. A device with no update mechanism can never be fixed directly, so protection must be wrapped around it with segmentation and monitoring instead; patch availability is tempting but that concerns whether a vendor still ships fixes, and no fix could be applied to this controller even if one existed. The tell: inability to patch = the system itself cannot be updated (system-side constraint driving compensating controls); patch availability = whether the vendor supplies fixes (vendor-side)."},
  {"id":"q398","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"Battery-operated field sensors along a pipeline cannot sustain always-on encrypted radio links without exhausting their batteries within days. Which of the following architecture considerations constrains the security design?","options":["Compute","Cost","Power","Resilience"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Power. The limiting factor is the energy budget of battery devices, which decides whether always-on encrypted communication is even feasible; compute is tempting because small sensors are also processor-limited, but the stem's stated failure is battery drain, not processing capacity. The tell: power = the electrical supply/consumption constraint of the architecture; compute = available processing capacity; cost = the expense trade-off; resilience = bouncing back from failure."},
  {"id":"q399","domain":"D3","family":"arch-considerations","question":"A security architect finds that the low-cost microcontroller inside each smart meter cannot perform TLS handshakes or host any monitoring agent. Which of the following architecture considerations limits the controls that are even possible?","options":["Power","Responsiveness","Patch availability","Compute"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Compute. The microcontroller lacks the processing capacity for cryptographic handshakes or agent software, so the available controls are dictated by what the hardware can execute; power is tempting because constrained devices often face both limits, but the stem describes processing shortfalls, not battery drain. The tell: compute = processing capacity shapes which controls can run at all; power = the energy budget; patch availability = vendor-supplied fixes; responsiveness = how fast the system answers."},
  {"id":"q400","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"During an outage, a perimeter firewall loses contact with its management platform and, per its configuration, passes ALL traffic uninspected so revenue systems stay reachable. Which of the following failure behaviors is configured?","options":["fail-open","fail-closed","fail-secure","fail-safe"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Fail-open. On failure the network device permits traffic, trading security for availability. The tell: fail-open/fail-closed = the network-device pair (permit vs block on failure), fail-safe/fail-secure = the door-lock pair (unlock for people vs stay locked for assets). This is a network device that permits, so fail-open."},
  {"id":"q401","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"When a data center loses power, the electronic maglocks on its exit doors release automatically so occupants can evacuate. The design deliberately puts human life above securing the room. Which of the following terms describes this failure mode?","options":["fail-open","fail-closed","fail-secure","fail-safe"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fail-safe. For physical locks, failing to the unlocked state to protect people is fail-safe; staying locked to protect assets would be fail-secure. The tell: fail-safe = door unlocks, life safety first, fail-secure = door stays locked, assets first, fail-open/fail-closed = the equivalent pair for network devices, not doors."},
  {"id":"q402","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"A network access control gateway is configured so that if it cannot reach its policy server, it denies all new connections rather than risk admitting an unvetted device. Which of the following failure behaviors is this?","options":["fail-open","fail-safe","fail-closed","fail-secure"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Fail-closed. A network device that blocks traffic on failure fails closed, favoring security over availability. The tell: fail-closed = network device blocks on failure, fail-open = it permits, fail-secure = a door staying locked, fail-safe = a door unlocking for evacuation. Network pair = open/closed; door pair = safe/secure."},
  {"id":"q403","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"A bank vault's electronic lock is wired so that if building power is lost, the lock stays engaged and the vault remains sealed, protecting the contents over convenience. Which of the following failure modes is this?","options":["Fail-safe","Fail-secure","Fail-open","Fail-closed"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fail-secure. A physical lock that stays locked on failure to protect assets fails secure; unlocking to let people out would be fail-safe. The tell: fail-secure = door stays locked, assets first, fail-safe = door unlocks, life safety first, fail-open/fail-closed = the network-device pair, not door locks."},
  {"id":"q404","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"For a network security device, which of the following failure modes favors availability by continuing to permit traffic through when the device can no longer enforce its rules?","options":["Fail-closed","Fail-secure","Fail-safe","Fail-open"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fail-open. Permitting traffic on failure keeps the business online at the cost of inspection. The tell: fail-open = network device permits on failure (availability), fail-closed = it blocks (security), fail-safe/fail-secure = the door-lock pair for people versus assets."},
  {"id":"q405","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"An inline content filter is configured so that if its scanning engine crashes, the device stops forwarding packets entirely rather than pass a single unfiltered byte. Which of the following behaviors is configured?","options":["Fail-open","Fail-secure","Fail-safe","Fail-closed"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Fail-closed. A network device blocking all traffic on failure fails closed, choosing security over availability. The tell: fail-closed = block on failure, fail-open = permit on failure, fail-secure/fail-safe = the physical-lock pair. Network devices pair open/closed; doors pair safe/secure."},
  {"id":"q406","domain":"D3","family":"fail-modes","question":"An architect sets failure modes for two inline devices: an IPS on a hospital's clinical network, where downtime could delay patient care, and a firewall guarding a classified research enclave. Which of the following pairings is MOST appropriate?","options":["Fail-closed on the IPS and fail-open on the firewall","Fail-open on the IPS and fail-closed on the firewall","Fail-open on both devices","Fail-closed on both devices"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fail-open for the IPS and fail-closed for the firewall. On the clinical network availability is the overriding requirement, so a failed IPS should pass traffic rather than interrupt patient care; on the classified enclave confidentiality wins, so a failed firewall must block everything. Fail-closed on both loses because it would take clinical systems down for a priority the stem assigns only to the enclave. The tell: fail-open = failure permits traffic (availability first); fail-closed = failure blocks traffic (security first); choose per link based on which loss hurts more."},
  {"id":"q407","domain":"D3","family":"active-passive","question":"A monitoring sensor hangs off a switch SPAN port, receiving a mirrored copy of every packet. Live traffic never actually flows through the sensor itself. Which of the following connection methods is being used?","options":["active","inline","passive","tap/monitor"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tap/monitor. Receiving a mirrored copy via SPAN or a tap, outside the traffic path, is the tap/monitor method, and it makes the device inherently passive. The tell: tap/monitor = gets a copy of traffic, inline = traffic flows through the device, passive = can only observe, active = can block."},
  {"id":"q408","domain":"D3","family":"active-passive","question":"A security engineer deploys a prevention appliance that must drop malicious packets in real time before they reach the server farm. Which of the following connection methods allows the appliance to block traffic?","options":["inline","tap/monitor","passive","active"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Inline. Blocking in real time requires sitting in the traffic path so every packet flows through the device and can be dropped; active is tempting but names the blocking role, not the connection method that enables it. The tell: inline = in the path, can drop packets, tap/monitor = mirrored copy off the path, active = the blocking role inline placement enables, passive = watch and alert only."},
  {"id":"q409","domain":"D3","family":"active-passive","question":"In a design document, a sensor is labeled with the role meaning it can observe and alert on traffic but has no ability whatsoever to block or modify it. Which of the following terms describes this role?","options":["inline","tap/monitor","active","passive"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Passive. Observation without the ability to intervene is the passive role, typical of an IDS on a SPAN port. The tell: passive = watch and alert only, active = can block or alter, inline = the in-path placement, tap/monitor = the mirrored-copy placement."},
  {"id":"q410","domain":"D3","family":"active-passive","question":"A design review labels each security device by role: one can drop or rewrite packets in real time, while another can only watch and report. Which of the following terms describes the device that can act on traffic?","options":["Active","Passive","Inline","Tap"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Active. The ability to block or modify traffic is the active role; placement (inline) is what usually makes it possible. The tell: active = can block/alter, passive = observe only, inline = the in-path placement, tap = the mirrored-copy placement."},
  {"id":"q411","domain":"D3","family":"active-passive","question":"A security appliance is cabled between two network segments so that every production packet physically flows through it on the way to the other side. Which of the following placement terms describes this?","options":["Tap","Inline","Passive","Active"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Inline. In-path cabling where traffic flows through the device is inline placement, the prerequisite for blocking (and a potential bottleneck). The tell: inline = traffic passes through it, tap = it receives a copy, active/passive = what the device can DO, not where it sits."},
  {"id":"q412","domain":"D3","family":"active-passive","question":"A security team deploys a NetFlow collector that receives flow summaries exported by the routers. The device analyzes and reports on traffic patterns but has no ability to drop or alter any packet. Which of the following BEST describes its role?","options":["Active","Passive","Inline","Tap"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Passive. The collector only receives and reports on traffic data and can never block or modify anything, which is the passive role; tap loses because it names a placement that delivers a copy of traffic, not what the device can do. The tell: passive = observe and alert only, active = can block or alter traffic, inline = the in-path placement, tap = the mirrored-copy placement."},
  {"id":"q413","domain":"D3","family":"jump-proxy","question":"A plant's OT management VLAN is isolated from the corporate network. To configure the controllers, engineers must first connect to one dedicated hardened host inside the management VLAN and pivot onward from there. Which of the following BEST describes this host?","options":["reverse proxy","jump server","forward proxy","load balancer"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Jump server. A hardened, audited pivot host that engineers connect THROUGH to reach an isolated management network is a jump server; a proxy is tempting but proxies broker application requests, not administrative sessions. The tell: jump server = admin access pivot into a protected segment, reverse proxy = fronts servers for inbound clients, forward proxy = fronts clients going outbound, load balancer = distributes traffic across servers."},
  {"id":"q414","domain":"D3","family":"jump-proxy","question":"An appliance sits in front of a company's web farm, accepts every inbound client request, filters them, caches popular content, and hides the real servers' internal addresses from the internet. Which of the following systems is being described?","options":["reverse proxy","jump server","forward proxy","load balancer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reverse proxy. Fronting servers to handle inbound client requests, with caching and shielding, is the reverse proxy role. A pure load balancer distributes load but the filtering/caching/shielding described is proxy work. The tell: reverse proxy = represents the SERVERS to inbound clients, forward proxy = represents the CLIENTS going outbound, jump server = admin pivot host, load balancer = distributes traffic across servers."},
  {"id":"q415","domain":"D3","family":"jump-proxy","question":"All employee web browsing is routed through one internal device that enforces the acceptable use policy, filters URLs, and caches common pages on the users' behalf before requests leave for the internet. Which of the following systems is being used?","options":["jump server","reverse proxy","forward proxy","load balancer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Forward proxy. Handling internal clients' outbound requests with filtering and caching is the forward proxy role. The tell: forward proxy = outbound, acts for the clients, reverse proxy = inbound, acts for the servers, jump server = admin gateway host, load balancer = spreads load across servers."},
  {"id":"q416","domain":"D3","family":"jump-proxy","question":"Which of the following systems has the defining purpose of acting as a secure administrative pivot: one controlled, auditable entry point through which operators reach an isolated management network?","options":["Reverse proxy","Proxy server","Forward proxy","Jump server"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Jump server. Admin access consolidation with hardening and logging is the jump server's whole reason to exist. The tell: jump server = the admin choke point, proxy server = brokers/filters application requests, forward proxy = for outbound clients, reverse proxy = for inbound service traffic."},
  {"id":"q417","domain":"D3","family":"jump-proxy","question":"A hardening guide calls for a single fortified intermediary host, sometimes called a bastion host, to serve as the one controlled choke point for administrative access into a segmented network. Which of the following terms names it?","options":["Jump server","Proxy server","Forward proxy","Reverse proxy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Jump server. Bastion host and jump box are the common aliases; the function is a hardened admin gateway. The tell: jump server = hardened admin entry point, proxy server = application request broker, forward proxy = client-side broker, reverse proxy = server-side broker."},
  {"id":"q418","domain":"D3","family":"jump-proxy","question":"A new administrator argues the corporate VPN already secures remote management, so routing SSH and RDP through the hardened, logged pivot host is redundant. Which of the following BEST explains why the host is still required?","options":["The VPN only provides network access; the jump server gates and logs admin sessions","The jump server terminates the VPN tunnel and assigns remote administrators their IP addresses","The VPN encrypts traffic only as far as the jump server, which re-encrypts it","The jump server filters and caches the administrators' web requests"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The jump server provides what the VPN cannot: one hardened, MFA-protected choke point where every management session into the server zone is authenticated and logged, while the VPN merely places the administrator on the network; the tunnel-termination option loses because that describes a VPN concentrator, not a jump server. The tell: VPN = gets you onto the network; jump server = the controlled, audited admin pivot within it; proxy = broker for client application requests, not admin logins."},
  {"id":"q419","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"A security engineer wants the detection capability of the existing IDS but needs malicious packets actually stopped before they reach hosts, so she deploys the same engine inline with blocking enabled. Which of the following controls has she deployed?","options":["IDS","IPS","HIDS","HIPS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"IPS. Same detection, different posture: inline placement plus the ability to drop packets turns detection into prevention. The tell: IPS = inline, detects AND blocks, IDS = out of band, detects and alerts only, HIDS/HIPS = the same pair implemented on a single host."},
  {"id":"q420","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"A web server is compromised even though the network sensor raised timely alerts throughout the attack. The sensor monitors a mirrored copy of traffic from a SPAN port. Which of the following controls is MOST likely deployed?","options":["IPS","HIPS","HIDS","IDS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"IDS. An out-of-band sensor on a SPAN port sees only a copy of traffic, so it can alert but has no path to drop the attack; an IPS is tempting because detection worked, but an IPS sits inline and would have blocked the packets. The tell: IDS = passive, out of band, alerts only, IPS = inline, detects AND blocks, HIDS = detect-only on a single host, HIPS = detect and block on a single host."},
  {"id":"q421","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"A HIPS agent can block attacks locally on its host. Which of the following controls likewise runs on a single host but only monitors and alerts, taking no blocking action?","options":["IPS","IDS","HIDS","HIPS"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"HIDS. Host-based intrusion detection watches one machine and alerts, while HIPS adds local blocking. The tell: HIDS = one host, detect only, HIPS = one host, detect and block, IDS/IPS = the same split at the network level."},
  {"id":"q422","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"Deployed inline at the network edge, a control inspects every passing packet and automatically drops or resets connections matching malicious signatures before they touch internal hosts. Which of the following controls is it?","options":["IPS","IDS","HIDS","HIPS"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"IPS. Inline placement plus automatic blocking is intrusion prevention. The tell: IPS = inline, detects AND blocks, IDS = passive detection and alerts, HIDS/HIPS = the host-based equivalents of each."},
  {"id":"q423","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"An interviewer notes that IDS and IPS share the same detection methods, then asks the candidate to state the core difference. Which of the following statements is correct?","options":["The IDS sits inline and can block traffic, while the IPS is out of band and only alerts","The IPS runs only on individual hosts, while the IDS monitors only network segments","The IPS sits inline and can block traffic, while the IDS is out of band and only alerts","The IDS uses only anomaly detection, while the IPS relies only on signature matching"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The IPS sits inline and can block traffic, while the IDS is out of band and only alerts: same detection engine, different placement and response. The mirror-image option loses because it swaps the two roles exactly. The tell: the P is prevention (active, inline, blocks), the D is detection (passive, copy of traffic, alerts)."},
  {"id":"q424","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"The same detection signature runs on a tap-fed IDS at one office and an inline IPS in front of an e-commerce site. The signature generates a false positive at both locations. Which of the following describes the difference in impact?","options":["The IDS blocks the flagged traffic while the inline IPS merely alerts","Both devices drop the matching connections until the rule is retuned","Neither deployment is affected because legitimate traffic never matches signatures","The IDS costs analyst triage time; the IPS blocks real customer orders"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The IDS wastes triage time while the IPS blocks real orders. A tap-fed IDS is out of band and can only alert, so its false positive costs analyst effort; the inline IPS acts on the same match and drops legitimate customer traffic, turning a tuning error into a business outage. The tell: IDS = passive, detect and alert only; IPS = inline, detect and block - identical detection, so a false positive is an annoyance on one and an outage on the other."},
  {"id":"q425","domain":"D3","family":"ips-ids","question":"An inline IPS with signatures updated hourly fails to block a brand-new exploit on its first day in the wild. Which of the following detection methods would have given the BEST chance of catching the attack?","options":["Increasing the signature update frequency","Deploying the same signatures on a second sensor","Mirroring traffic to an out-of-band IDS","Anomaly detection against a learned traffic baseline"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Anomaly detection against a learned baseline. Signatures can only match patterns someone has already written, so a first-day exploit stays invisible no matter how fresh the update; more frequent updates lose for exactly that reason. The tell: signature = matches known attack patterns, precise but blind to novel attacks; trend/anomaly = flags deviation from learned normal, so new activity can surface at the cost of more false positives; sensor placement changes response, not recognition."},
  {"id":"q426","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A security team needs to protect one public web application specifically: inspecting HTTP requests and blocking SQL injection and cross-site scripting before they reach the app. Which of the following devices BEST fits this need?","options":["NGFW","WAF","UTM","Layer 4 firewall"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"WAF. A web application firewall specializes in HTTP-layer inspection for web attacks like SQLi and XSS in front of a specific application. An NGFW is broader network protection, not app-request-focused. The tell: WAF = protects a web app from HTTP attacks, NGFW = application-aware network firewall, UTM = all-in-one security box, Layer 4 = ports and IPs only."},
  {"id":"q427","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A small business with no dedicated security staff wants one appliance combining firewall, antivirus, intrusion prevention, and content filtering in a single managed box. Which of the following device types meets this need?","options":["UTM","WAF","NGFW","Layer 7 firewall"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"UTM. Unified threat management bundles many security functions into one appliance, trading best-of-breed depth for simplicity, a fit for small shops. The tell: UTM = all-in-one bundle, NGFW = advanced firewall with app awareness, WAF = web-app HTTP protection, Layer 7 firewall = the application-aware capability itself."},
  {"id":"q428","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"An architect notes the current firewall filters only by port and IP address. The replacement must recognize applications regardless of port, do deep packet inspection, and tie rules to user identity. Which of the following firewall types meets these requirements?","options":["WAF","UTM","NGFW","Layer 4 firewall"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"NGFW. Application awareness, deep packet inspection, and identity integration are the next-generation firewall's defining upgrades over Layer 4 filtering. The tell: NGFW = app-aware, DPI, identity rules, Layer 4 = ports and IPs only, WAF = HTTP protection for one web app, UTM = many functions in one box."},
  {"id":"q429","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A legacy firewall makes allow/deny decisions purely on source and destination IP addresses and TCP/UDP port numbers, completely blind to the application data inside the packets. At which of the following layers does it operate?","options":["Layer 2","Layer 4","Layer 3","Layer 7"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Layer 4. Filtering on TCP/UDP port numbers requires transport-layer visibility, so a port/IP firewall operates at Layer 4; Layer 3 loses because network-layer filtering sees IP addresses but not ports. The tell: Layer 4 = ports and IPs, Layer 7 = application payload content, Layer 3 = IP addressing and routing only, Layer 2 = hardware-address switching."},
  {"id":"q430","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A next-generation firewall distinguishes a chat client from a file-sharing tool running on the same port by inspecting the actual payload of the traffic. At which of the following layers does this content-aware filtering happen?","options":["Layer 3","Layer 4","Layer 2","Layer 7"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Layer 7. Recognizing applications by payload content is application-layer inspection, the capability that defines NGFWs; Layer 4 loses because port and IP filtering cannot tell two applications apart on the same port. The tell: Layer 7 = application-content awareness, Layer 4 = ports and IPs, Layer 3 = IP addressing and routing, Layer 2 = hardware-address switching."},
  {"id":"q431","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A security analyst reviewing web server logs finds: GET /login.php?user=admin' OR 1=1 -- HTTP/1.1 200. The request traversed the stateful perimeter firewall on port 443 unchallenged. Which of the following devices would BEST have blocked it?","options":["Unified threat management appliance","Intrusion detection system","Next-generation firewall","Web application firewall"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A web application firewall. The ' OR 1=1 -- payload is SQL injection carried inside a well-formed HTTPS request, and a WAF parses HTTP semantics to block exactly this class of web app attack; the NGFW loses because its strength is identifying applications and users, not inspecting a web form's input for injection. The tell: WAF = Layer 7 defense purpose-built for web apps (SQL injection, XSS); NGFW = general application-aware perimeter; IDS = alerts but cannot block; UTM = bundled functions without the WAF's app-payload focus."},
  {"id":"q432","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A consultant recommends replacing a branch office's UTM appliance with a next-generation firewall. Which of the following capabilities BEST distinguishes the NGFW from the UTM it replaces?","options":["Identifying and controlling applications regardless of port","Bundling antivirus and spam filtering into one appliance","Filtering traffic by source address and destination port","Caching frequently requested web content for users"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Application awareness. An NGFW performs deep packet inspection to identify and control the actual application regardless of port, and can tie rules to user identity; the antivirus-and-spam bundle loses because packaging many functions in one box is precisely what the UTM already is. The tell: UTM = all-in-one appliance for small sites; NGFW = application-aware deep packet inspection; the differentiator is identifying applications, not counting features."},
  {"id":"q433","domain":"D3","family":"firewalls","question":"A firewall administrator must block a messaging application that employees run over TCP port 443. The rule base contains only entries such as: permit tcp 10.0.0.0/8 any eq 443; permit tcp 10.0.0.0/8 any eq 80; deny ip any any. Which of the following capabilities is the firewall missing?","options":["Stateful connection tracking","Network address translation","Layer 7 application content inspection","Layer 4 port and protocol filtering"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Layer 7 application content inspection. Rules keyed to addresses and ports cannot tell the messaging app apart from ordinary HTTPS on 443, so blocking it requires reading the application content itself; stateful tracking loses because the firewall already tracks connections, and state says nothing about which app is inside them. The tell: Layer 4 = filters on IP and port; Layer 7 = filters on application content; an app riding an allowed port is invisible to Layer 4 rules."},
  {"id":"q434","domain":"D3","family":"port-auth","question":"A wireless engineer configures WPA3-Enterprise so each user must authenticate with individual credentials against a RADIUS server before the access point grants any network access. Which of the following standards provides this access control?","options":["802.1X","EAP","PEAP","EAP-TLS"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"802.1X. Enterprise Wi-Fi's per-user authentication gate is 802.1X port-based network access control, with the access point blocking all traffic until the RADIUS server approves; EAP is tempting because the credentials ride inside it, but EAP is the framework 802.1X transports, not the access control standard. The tell: 802.1X = the port-gatekeeping standard on both wired and wireless networks, EAP = the authentication framework it carries, PEAP/EAP-TLS = specific methods inside that framework."},
  {"id":"q435","domain":"D3","family":"port-auth","question":"A security engineer must deploy wireless authentication in which the server and every client device authenticate each other using certificates, with no passwords involved. Which of the following methods meets this requirement?","options":["PEAP","EAP-TLS","802.1X","EAP"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"EAP-TLS. Mutual certificate authentication - client and server each present a certificate - is EAP-TLS, the passwordless method; PEAP is tempting but it uses a server-side certificate only, tunneling user credentials such as passwords inside TLS. The tell: EAP-TLS = certificate authentication on both sides, no passwords, PEAP = server certificate plus tunneled credentials, EAP = the overall framework these methods run in, 802.1X = the port-control standard that transports EAP."},
  {"id":"q436","domain":"D3","family":"port-auth","question":"A network engineer rolls out WPA3-Enterprise Wi-Fi: a connecting laptop gets no network access until it authenticates, and the credential exchange rides inside the gatekeeping standard that keeps the connection closed. Which of the following standards is the gate?","options":["802.1X","EAP","PEAP","EAP-TLS"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"802.1X. Enterprise Wi-Fi uses 802.1X port-based access control to keep the connection unauthorized until authentication succeeds; EAP loses because it is the authentication framework carried inside the gate, not the gate itself. The tell: 802.1X = the port gate, EAP = the framework spoken through it, PEAP/EAP-TLS = specific EAP methods."},
  {"id":"q437","domain":"D3","family":"port-auth","question":"A wireless engineer describes EAP-TLS and PEAP as specific implementations chosen within a broader, pluggable authentication framework. Which of the following is that framework?","options":["802.1X","RADIUS","CHAP","EAP"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"EAP. The Extensible Authentication Protocol is deliberately a framework, and PEAP and EAP-TLS are methods plugged into it; 802.1X loses because it is the port-control standard that transports EAP, not the framework itself. The tell: EAP = the extensible framework, 802.1X = the gate that carries it, RADIUS = the AAA server protocol behind the gate, CHAP = a single challenge-response method, not a framework."},
  {"id":"q438","domain":"D3","family":"port-auth","question":"A wireless engineer must configure enterprise Wi-Fi so that the connecting client and the authentication server each authenticate with a certificate. Which of the following EAP methods satisfies the requirement?","options":["PEAP","EAP-TTLS","EAP-TLS","EAP-FAST"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"EAP-TLS. It is the certificate-on-both-sides method, giving mutual authentication at the cost of deploying client certificates through a PKI; PEAP loses because only the server presents a certificate while the client authenticates with a password inside the protected tunnel. The tell: EAP-TLS = client and server certificates (mutual, strongest); PEAP = server certificate creating a TLS tunnel that shields a password exchange; EAP-TTLS and EAP-FAST likewise tunnel credentials rather than requiring client certificates."},
  {"id":"q439","domain":"D3","family":"port-auth","question":"Contractors plugging into the wired network must first demonstrate patch and antivirus compliance as a condition of admission, and noncompliant machines are confined to a remediation segment. Which of the following BEST enforces this?","options":["802.1X port authentication","A next-generation firewall","A host-based intrusion prevention system","Network access control"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Network access control. NAC checks a device's identity and health posture - patch level and antivirus - before granting admission and confines failing devices to a remediation segment. 802.1X loses because it authenticates the port but does not evaluate patch or antivirus posture. The tell: NAC = admission based on device health; 802.1X = the port-auth gate it often rides on; a firewall filters traffic already on the network."},
  {"id":"q440","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"Remote employees reach internal web applications through their browsers over TCP port 443 with nothing extra installed; the encryption is the same technology that protects HTTPS. Which of the following remote-access technologies is being used?","options":["IPSec","TLS VPN","SD-WAN","SASE"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"TLS VPN. Browser-based access over 443 using TLS, with no thick client, is the TLS (SSL) VPN model, easy to deploy and firewall-friendly. The tell: TLS VPN = browser, port 443, no client install, IPSec = network-layer tunnels typically with a client or between gateways, SD-WAN = multi-link traffic steering, SASE = cloud-delivered network plus security."},
  {"id":"q441","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A company issues laptops with an always-on VPN client that builds a network-layer tunnel using ESP so all traffic is encrypted back to headquarters before it leaves the device. Which of the following technologies is in use?","options":["TLS VPN","IPSec","SASE","SD-WAN"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"IPSec. A client-based tunnel at the network layer using ESP for confidentiality and integrity is IPSec; a TLS VPN is tempting for remote access, but it works at the transport/application layer over port 443, not with ESP at Layer 3. The tell: IPSec = network-layer tunnels with AH/ESP, client-based or site-to-site, TLS VPN = browser-friendly access over 443, SD-WAN = multi-link traffic steering, SASE = cloud-delivered networking plus security."},
  {"id":"q442","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A hospital group's clinics each have MPLS, broadband, and LTE links. An overlay controller steers latency-sensitive telehealth video across whichever link currently performs best while bulk traffic uses the rest. Which of the following technologies is this?","options":["SASE","IPSec","TLS VPN","SD-WAN"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SD-WAN. Software-defined steering of each application's traffic across multiple transport links by performance and cost is SD-WAN's core job; SASE is tempting but adds cloud-delivered security services (ZTNA, SWG, CASB), which nothing here mentions. The tell: SD-WAN = dynamic multi-link traffic steering, SASE = SD-WAN plus cloud-delivered security, IPSec = the encrypted tunnel protocol itself, TLS VPN = browser-based remote access."},
  {"id":"q443","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A company replaces its patchwork of VPNs and web filters with one cloud-delivered service combining SD-WAN connectivity with zero-trust network access, secure web gateway, and CASB functions. Which of the following models has it adopted?","options":["SD-WAN","IPSec","SASE","TLS VPN"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SASE. Secure access service edge merges SD-WAN networking with a stack of cloud-delivered security services into one offering. The tell: SASE = networking plus security from the cloud, SD-WAN = the networking piece alone, IPSec = the tunnel protocol, TLS VPN = browser-based access."},
  {"id":"q444","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A network architect must join two office networks with a permanent, always-on encrypted tunnel connecting the entire subnets at the network layer, not per-user browser sessions. Which of the following technologies BEST fits?","options":["TLS VPN","SASE","IPSec","SD-WAN"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"IPSec. A permanent site-to-site tunnel connecting whole networks at Layer 3 is IPSec's classic role; a TLS VPN loses because it suits per-user, often clientless remote access over 443 rather than an always-on subnet-to-subnet link. The tell: IPSec = network-layer site-to-site tunnels (AH/ESP), TLS VPN = transport-layer user access over 443, SD-WAN = multi-link steering overlay, SASE = cloud-delivered networking plus security."},
  {"id":"q445","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A study guide asks for the general term covering any technology that builds an encrypted tunnel across an untrusted network so remote traffic behaves as if it were on the private LAN. Which of the following terms is it?","options":["SD-WAN","SASE","IPSec","VPN"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"VPN. Virtual private network is the umbrella term; IPSec and TLS are particular ways to build one. The tell: VPN = the general encrypted-tunnel concept, IPSec = one implementation at Layer 3, SD-WAN = traffic steering across links, SASE = the cloud-delivered service bundle."},
  {"id":"q446","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"External contractors working from hotel networks that block most outbound ports must reach an internal web portal using only a browser, with no software installed. Which of the following remote access solutions BEST meets the requirements?","options":["A site-to-site IPSec tunnel between gateways","A TLS VPN portal on TCP port 443","An IPSec remote-access client on each laptop","An SD-WAN overlay across the hotel links"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A TLS VPN. It tunnels over TCP 443, the port guest networks almost never block, and supports clientless access from a browser; the IPSec client loses on both stated constraints, since it requires installed software and its network-layer protocols are commonly blocked on restrictive networks. The tell: TLS VPN = port 443, clientless-friendly remote access; IPSec = network-layer tunneling best suited to site-to-site links; SD-WAN = link optimization between sites, not user remote access."},
  {"id":"q447","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A network engineer compares two packet captures. Between two application servers, ESP protects the payload but the original IP headers remain visible; between two site gateways, each original packet is fully encapsulated inside a new IP header. Which of the following identifies the IPSec modes, in order?","options":["Tunnel mode, then transport mode","AH first, then ESP","Main mode, then aggressive mode","Transport mode, then tunnel mode"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Transport mode, then tunnel mode. Transport mode encrypts only the payload and leaves the original IP header exposed, matching the host-to-host capture; tunnel mode encapsulates the entire original packet inside a new header for the gateway-to-gateway link. The AH-then-ESP option loses because those are IPSec protections (integrity-only vs encryption), not modes. The tell: transport = payload only, original headers visible, host-to-host; tunnel = whole packet wrapped in a new header, gateway-to-gateway."},
  {"id":"q448","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"Remote employees' video conferences are saturating the VPN concentrator. An engineer proposes tunneling only corporate-bound traffic and routing internet traffic directly from each laptop. Which of the following names the proposal and its security trade-off?","options":["Split tunnel; the internet-bound traffic is no longer inspected","Full tunnel; all traffic is inspected at the cost of concentrator bandwidth","Split tunnel; corporate-bound traffic loses its encryption in transit","Full tunnel; internet-bound traffic bypasses the concentrator entirely"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Split tunnel. Only corporate-bound traffic crosses the VPN, relieving the concentrator, but each laptop's direct internet traffic now bypasses corporate inspection and filtering; the full tunnel options lose because sending everything through the concentrator is exactly what is saturating it. The tell: full tunnel = all traffic through the VPN (full inspection, full bandwidth cost); split tunnel = corporate traffic only (efficient, but internet-bound traffic goes uninspected)."},
  {"id":"q449","domain":"D3","family":"secure-tunnels","question":"A network manager who already operates SD-WAN across 40 branches asks what adopting SASE would add. Which of the following is the BEST answer?","options":["Centralized path selection across broadband and LTE links","Encrypted site-to-site tunnels between each pair of branch gateways","A cloud-delivered security stack (SWG, CASB, zero trust network access)","Dedicated firewall hardware appliances installed at every branch location"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The cloud-delivered security stack. SASE converges the SD-WAN connectivity the manager already has with security delivered from the cloud - secure web gateway, CASB, zero trust network access, firewall as a service; centralized path selection loses because that is the SD-WAN capability already in place. The tell: SD-WAN = optimized, policy-driven connectivity only; SASE = SD-WAN plus the cloud-delivered security stack that follows users anywhere."},
  {"id":"q450","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A hospital's data inventory includes patient records governed by HIPAA and cardholder data governed by PCI DSS. External law and industry mandates dictate exactly how both must be stored, transmitted, and retained. Which of the following data types BEST describes these records?","options":["regulated","trade secret","intellectual property","financial"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Regulated. When external law or mandated standards dictate handling, the data is regulated data, whatever else it may also be. The tell: regulated = external rules dictate handling, trade secret = secret business advantage, intellectual property = creations you own rights to, financial = money-related records as a content category."},
  {"id":"q451","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A beverage company's most valuable asset is its unpatented secret formula. It is kept internal precisely because publishing or patenting it would let rivals copy the recipe when protection expires. Which of the following BEST classifies this information?","options":["financial","intellectual property","trade secret","regulated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Trade secret. Competitive-advantage information kept deliberately secret, and unpatented so it never expires into the public domain, is a trade secret. The tell: trade secret = secret sauce guarded internally, intellectual property = the broader owned-creations category including patents that are published, regulated = law dictates handling, financial = monetary records."},
  {"id":"q452","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"During a classification review, a marketing brochure already published on the company website is assessed: its release could cause no harm whatsoever. Which of the following classifications applies?","options":["confidential","public","restricted","critical"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Public. Information whose disclosure causes no harm needs no protection and is classified public. The tell: public = no harm if released, confidential = harm if released, restricted = tightly limited audience, critical = severe damage if lost or disclosed."},
  {"id":"q453","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A software company's legal team inventories the patents on its algorithms and the copyrights on its source code, all legally protecting its developers' creations. Which of the following data types BEST describes these assets?","options":["regulated","intellectual property","trade secret","financial"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Intellectual property. Patents and copyrights are legal protections over creations, the IP category. A trade secret would be kept unregistered and hidden instead. The tell: intellectual property = legally protected creations (patents, copyrights), trade secret = valuable because it stays secret, regulated = external law dictates handling, financial = monetary records."},
  {"id":"q454","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A business impact analysis marks one dataset as so essential that losing it would halt the organization's core functions outright. Which of the following classifications applies?","options":["confidential","public","critical","restricted"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Critical. Data whose loss stops the business cold is critical, a rating about operational dependence, not secrecy. The tell: critical = the business halts without it, confidential = harm if disclosed, restricted = narrowest allowed audience, public = no harm if released."},
  {"id":"q455","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"An internal employee-morale survey is posted where any staff member can read it. Leadership notes a public leak would embarrass the company, though the data is not limited to need-to-know access. Which of the following classifications BEST fits?","options":["Public","Confidential","Restricted","Sensitive"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Sensitive. Disclosure would cause harm or embarrassment, yet the data circulates broadly inside the company rather than on a need-to-know basis, which is exactly the sensitive tier; confidential is tempting but requires need-to-know limits and meaningful business harm. The tell: public = no harm if released; sensitive = harm or embarrassment, broadly internal; confidential = need-to-know business harm; restricted = severe harm, tightest circle."},
  {"id":"q456","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"Merger negotiation documents are shared only with executives on a need-to-know basis. Disclosure would meaningfully damage the deal, but it would endanger no one and break no law. Which of the following labels should the data owner assign?","options":["Confidential","Restricted","Sensitive","Private"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Confidential. Need-to-know access plus meaningful business harm on disclosure places the documents in the confidential tier; restricted is the tempting step up, but the stem rules out the severe or legal harm that tier implies. The tell: confidential = need-to-know, meaningful business harm; restricted = tightest circle, severe or legal harm; sensitive = broadly internal, embarrassment-level harm; private = about a person, not the business."},
  {"id":"q457","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A subcontractor already labels its need-to-know business documents confidential. Weapon-system schematics go further: disclosure would bring severe harm and legal penalties, and only a handful of cleared engineers may open them. Which of the following labels applies to the schematics?","options":["Confidential","Critical","Restricted","Sensitive"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Restricted. The schematics sit above the existing confidential tier: the access circle is tighter and the harm is severe with legal consequences; critical is tempting in a defense context, but that label marks data whose loss halts operations, not data whose secrecy is paramount. The tell: restricted = tightest circle, severe or legal harm; confidential = need-to-know business harm; critical = loss stops operations regardless of secrecy; sensitive = embarrassment-level internal data."},
  {"id":"q458","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A new classification policy must distinguish records about individual employees from records about company operations. An HR system stores each employee's salary figures and medical-leave requests. Which of the following labels fits the HR records?","options":["Confidential","Private","Sensitive","Restricted"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Private. Salary and medical-leave records are information about individual people, which is what the private label marks; confidential is the tempting alternative, but that tier describes need-to-know information about the business itself. The tell: private = about a person; confidential = about the business, need-to-know; sensitive = embarrassment-level internal data; restricted = severe harm, tightest circle."},
  {"id":"q459","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"An intern labels the company's published press releases confidential to be safe. The data owner overrides the label, noting the releases already appear on the investor site and stricter handling would waste money. Which of the following labels is MOST appropriate?","options":["Sensitive","Private","Confidential","Public"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Public. Already-published material can cause zero harm on further disclosure, and labeling it higher imposes costly handling controls it does not need - though public data still needs integrity protection, since an altered press release is still an incident; confidential is the tempting safe default, but overclassification has real cost. The tell: public = no harm if released; sensitive = embarrassment-level harm; confidential = need-to-know business harm; private = about a person."},
  {"id":"q460","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A parcel carrier's route-optimization dataset is compiled entirely from public map sources, so secrecy is irrelevant. Yet losing the dataset for even one hour stops every delivery nationwide. Which of the following classification labels BEST applies?","options":["Critical","Restricted","Public","Confidential"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Critical. This label is availability-centric: it marks data the business cannot operate without, regardless of whether the contents are secret; restricted is the tempting high-tier pick, but nothing here needs a tight secrecy circle - the stem is about loss, not disclosure. The tell: critical = loss halts operations regardless of secrecy; restricted = severe harm from disclosure, tightest access; public = no harm if released; confidential = need-to-know business harm."},
  {"id":"q461","domain":"D3","family":"data-classification","question":"A workshop asks attendees to label three items in order: individual salary records, merger plans limited to executives, and a brochure published on the company website. Which of the following label sets is correct?","options":["Confidential, restricted, sensitive","Sensitive, private, public","Private, confidential, public","Private, public, confidential"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Private, confidential, public. Salary records are information about individual people, merger plans are need-to-know business information whose leak causes meaningful harm, and an already-published brochure can cause no harm; the set ending 'public, confidential' is the tempting scramble, but the published brochure, not the merger plan, is the zero-harm item. The tell: private = about a person; confidential = need-to-know business harm; public = no harm if released."},
  {"id":"q462","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"An analytics engine loads millions of customer records into server RAM and actively computes across them. A security review notes this is the stage where encryption is hardest to maintain, since the CPU must see plaintext. Which of the following data states applies?","options":["at rest","in transit","in use","data sovereignty"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In use. Data being actively processed in memory is in use, the hardest state to protect (secure enclaves exist for exactly this). The tell: in use = being processed in RAM/CPU, at rest = sitting in storage, in transit = crossing a network, data sovereignty = which country's laws apply."},
  {"id":"q463","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"A cloud customer discovers that because its backups are stored in a Frankfurt region, German and EU law govern that data regardless of where the company is headquartered. Which of the following concepts is at work?","options":["in transit","geolocation","data sovereignty","at rest"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Data sovereignty. Data is subject to the laws of the country where it physically resides, a core consideration when picking cloud regions. The tell: data sovereignty = local law follows the storage location, geolocation = determining where data or users physically are, at rest/in transit = protection states, not legal jurisdiction."},
  {"id":"q464","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"Customer records stream across the internet from a branch office to headquarters, wrapped in TLS for the journey. Which of the following data states describes the records while on the wire?","options":["at rest","in transit","in use","data sovereignty"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"In transit. Data crossing a network is in transit, which is exactly what TLS protects. The tell: in transit = moving across a network (TLS/IPSec), at rest = sitting in storage (disk encryption), in use = active in memory, data sovereignty = whose laws govern it."},
  {"id":"q465","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"An inventory notes files sitting encrypted on a server's hard drive: not being read, not being sent anywhere, just stored. Which of the following data states describes them?","options":["at rest","in transit","in use","geolocation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"At rest. Stored and idle is at rest, the state disk and volume encryption protect. The tell: at rest = in storage, in transit = on the network, in use = loaded in memory being processed, geolocation = determining physical location, not a data state."},
  {"id":"q466","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"A sales laptop is stolen from a parked car, but the investigation closes quickly: full-disk encryption kept the thief from reading anything on the drive. Which of the following data states did the control protect?","options":["Data in transit","Data in use","Data sovereignty","Data at rest"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Data at rest. The files were sitting on storage, not moving or being processed, and full-disk encryption is the canonical at-rest control; data in use is the tempting miss for a laptop out in the field, but that state means actively processed in memory. The tell: at rest = stored on media (disk encryption); in transit = moving over a network (TLS); in use = processed in memory; data sovereignty = the laws of wherever data physically resides, not a state."},
  {"id":"q467","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"A retailer's point-of-sale terminals send card authorization requests across the public internet to a payment processor. An architect documents the data state on this path and its canonical control. Which of the following pairs is correct?","options":["Data at rest; full-disk encryption","Data in transit; TLS","Data in use; secure enclave","Data in transit; full-disk encryption"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data in transit; TLS. Authorization requests crossing a network are in transit, and TLS is the canonical control that protects them on the wire; the pair matching in transit with full-disk encryption is the tempting trap - right state, but a storage control cannot protect moving packets. The tell: at rest = stored (full-disk encryption); in transit = moving over a network (TLS or IPSec); in use = processed in memory (secure enclave)."},
  {"id":"q468","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"Breach forensics show card numbers were scraped directly from an application server's memory during transaction processing, even though every disk uses full-disk encryption and every session uses TLS. Which of the following data states was left unprotected?","options":["Data in use","Data at rest","Data in transit","Non-human-readable data"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Data in use. Data must be decrypted to be processed, so the records loaded in memory sat in plaintext where the scraper found them - the hardest state to protect; data at rest is the tempting answer because encryption seemed to be everywhere, but disk and session controls simply do not cover memory. The tell: in use = actively processed in memory; at rest = stored on media; in transit = moving over a network; non-human-readable describes a data format, not a state."},
  {"id":"q469","domain":"D3","family":"data-states","question":"A security architect must map one control to each data state: full-disk encryption, TLS, and secure enclave processing. Which of the following correctly matches the controls to at rest, in transit, and in use, in that order?","options":["TLS, full-disk encryption, secure enclave","Full-disk encryption, secure enclave, TLS","Full-disk encryption, TLS, secure enclave","Secure enclave, TLS, full-disk encryption"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Full-disk encryption, TLS, secure enclave. Stored data is protected on the disk, moving data is protected on the wire, and processed data is protected inside isolated hardware; the set swapping secure enclave and TLS is the tempting scramble, but an enclave protects computation, not network transport. The tell: at rest = stored (full-disk encryption); in transit = moving over a network (TLS); in use = processed in memory (secure enclave)."},
  {"id":"q470","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A hospital's billing system replaces each patient's national ID with a random substitute value before storing it. The real ID can be recovered only by querying a separate, tightly guarded vault. Which of the following data protection methods is in use?","options":["encryption","tokenization","masking","hashing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Tokenization. A substitute value with vault-based lookup to the original is tokenization; no mathematical relationship links token to real ID, unlike encryption. The tell: tokenization = vault maps token back to the value, encryption = mathematically reversible with a key, masking = partial display, not recoverable, hashing = one-way, never recoverable."},
  {"id":"q471","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A call-center application shows agents a customer's card number as XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-1234. The hidden digits are not present in the display layer at all, so no agent action can reveal them. Which of the following data protection techniques is shown?","options":["masking","tokenization","obfuscation","encryption"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Masking. Displaying only a partial value with the remainder irrecoverably hidden is data masking, standard for support screens. The tell: masking = partial display (XXXX-1234), tokenization = substitute value recoverable via a vault, encryption = reversible with a key, obfuscation = obscuring data without a real key (the general, least-specific method), too broad for this display-masking scenario."},
  {"id":"q472","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A streaming service checks each viewer's location and refuses to serve licensed content to IP addresses outside approved countries, allowing or denying purely by where the user is. Which of the following data protection methods is in use?","options":["permission restrictions","geographic restrictions","masking","tokenization"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Geographic restrictions. Access decisions keyed to the user's physical location are geo-restrictions (geofencing). The tell: geographic restrictions = where you are decides access, permission restrictions = who you are and your rights decide, masking = partial display of values, tokenization = vault-mapped substitutes."},
  {"id":"q473","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"To let users confirm a downloaded installer was not tampered with, the vendor publishes a one-way fingerprint of the file; comparing fingerprints proves integrity, and the fingerprint cannot be reversed into the file. Which of the following methods is the vendor using?","options":["hashing","encryption","masking","tokenization"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hashing. A one-way integrity fingerprint that cannot be reversed is a hash; matching digests prove the file is unaltered. The tell: hashing = one-way integrity check, encryption = reversible confidentiality, masking = hiding displayed digits, tokenization = substitute values via a vault."},
  {"id":"q474","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A compliance officer objects when the cloud provider enables cross-region replication that copies EU customers' records into a US region for durability. Which of the following concepts BEST explains the objection?","options":["Geofencing","Geolocation","Geographic restrictions","Data sovereignty"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Data sovereignty. Once the records physically reside in a US region they become subject to US law as well, which is exactly the legal exposure the officer is flagging; geographic restrictions is the tempting pick, but that is the technical control that could pin storage to approved regions - the fix, not the concern. The tell: data sovereignty = data is governed by the laws of wherever it physically resides; geolocation = determining physical position; geofencing = a virtual boundary that triggers policy; geographic restrictions = the allow-or-deny-by-location policy itself."},
  {"id":"q475","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"Before allowing the corporate email app to open, an MDM platform first determines each phone's physical position from GPS and nearby Wi-Fi networks. Which of the following capabilities is the MDM using?","options":["Geolocation","Geofencing","Geographic restrictions","Data sovereignty"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Geolocation. Determining where the device physically is - via GPS, Wi-Fi, or IP - is the geolocation capability that other controls build on; geofencing is the tempting sibling, but that is the virtual boundary that triggers an action once the position is known. The tell: geolocation = determining physical position; geofencing = a virtual boundary that triggers policy; geographic restrictions = the allow-or-deny-by-location policy; data sovereignty = the legal jurisdiction over data where it resides."},
  {"id":"q476","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A SOC observes waves of credential stuffing from IP ranges in countries where the firm has no customers or employees. Which of the following would BEST reduce these attempts without affecting legitimate users?","options":["Permission restrictions","Geographic restrictions","Segmentation","Geolocation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Geographic restrictions. Denying access from locations with zero legitimate users cuts the attack traffic at no cost to real customers; geolocation is the tempting near-miss, but it only determines where a connection comes from - the restriction is the policy that acts on that information. The tell: geographic restrictions = allow or deny by location; geolocation = determining position; permission restrictions = control by identity and access rights; segmentation = control by network reachability."},
  {"id":"q477","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"To shrink its PCI DSS assessment scope, a retailer moves every system storing cardholder data onto a dedicated VLAN reachable only through a firewall, so most corporate systems cannot reach the data at all. Which of the following data protection methods is this?","options":["Tokenization","Permission restrictions","Encryption","Segmentation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Segmentation. Isolating the cardholder data environment on its own firewalled network limits which systems can even reach the data, which is also how PCI scope shrinks; permission restrictions is the tempting sibling, but that controls who may access data through identity rights, not what can reach it over the network. The tell: segmentation = control by network reachability; permission restrictions = control by identity and access rights; encryption = reversible protection with a key; tokenization = swap values for vaulted tokens."},
  {"id":"q478","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"An auditor investigating how a contractor opened HR salary files reviews the share's access control list:\n\\\\HR-FS01\\Salary\nHR-Payroll: Modify\nEveryone: Read\nWhich of the following data protection methods is missing?","options":["Segmentation","Permission restrictions","Encryption","Masking"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Permission restrictions. The 'Everyone: Read' entry grants the whole organization access to salary data, so the contractor needed no exploit - least-privilege access rights were never applied; segmentation is the tempting fix, but the contractor reached the share through a legitimate identity grant, not an open network path. The tell: permission restrictions = control by identity and access rights (ACLs, least privilege); segmentation = control by network reachability; encryption = protection with a key; masking = hide part of a displayed value."},
  {"id":"q479","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A code reviewer finds API keys stored as Base64 strings in a configuration file. The developer reports the keys are encrypted. Which of the following BEST describes the protection actually applied?","options":["Encryption","Tokenization","Obfuscation","Hashing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Obfuscation. Base64 is a reversible encoding with no secret at all - anyone who finds the strings can decode them instantly, so the keys are merely obscured; encryption is the tempting claim, but encryption requires a key, and without one there is no real confidentiality. The tell: obfuscation = making data unclear without any key; encryption = reversible only with a key; hashing = one-way integrity digest; tokenization = substitute values redeemable through a secure vault."},
  {"id":"q480","domain":"D3","family":"data-protection","question":"A records archive must be stored with a third-party service, but the contract requires that the provider can NEVER read the contents. Which of the following approaches meets the requirement?","options":["Encrypt the archive before upload and keep the keys in-house","Have the provider tokenize the records when they arrive","Apply irreversible data masking before uploading the archive","Require the provider to encrypt the archive at rest"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Encrypt the archive before upload and keep the keys in-house. Encryption is reversible only with the key, so retaining it means the provider stores ciphertext it can never open; provider-side encryption at rest is the tempting option, but then the provider holds the keys and can read the data, and provider tokenization fails the same way because its vault holds the real values. The tell: encryption = confidentiality controlled by whoever holds the key; tokenization = the vault owner can recover values; masking = display-time hiding that destroys recoverability."},
  {"id":"q481","domain":"D3","family":"load-balancing","question":"A device in front of a pool of identical web servers assigns each incoming user request to one of them, raising total throughput and keeping any single server from being overwhelmed during peaks. Which of the following techniques is the device performing?","options":["clustering","load balancing","active-passive configuration","active-active configuration"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Load balancing. Distributing incoming requests across a server pool for throughput and headroom is load balancing. The tell: load balancing = spread requests across servers, clustering = servers grouped to act as one system with failover, active-active/active-passive = whether all nodes or only one carry load."},
  {"id":"q482","domain":"D3","family":"load-balancing","question":"Several database nodes are grouped so they present to applications as one system. When one node dies mid-transaction, another instantly assumes its role and clients never notice. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["load balancing","active-active configuration","active-passive configuration","clustering"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Clustering. Nodes grouped to act as a single system with seamless failover form a cluster; the emphasis is unified identity and takeover, not request distribution. The tell: clustering = many nodes, one logical system, load balancing = distributing requests, active-active/active-passive = the load-sharing modes a cluster can run in."},
  {"id":"q483","domain":"D3","family":"load-balancing","question":"In a high-availability firewall pair, the primary node handles 100 percent of traffic while an identical standby sits idle, ready to take over only if the primary fails. Which of the following configurations is this?","options":["active-active configuration","active-passive configuration","load balancing","clustering"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Active-passive. One node works, the twin waits; capacity is idle but failover is simple and predictable. Active-active would have both nodes sharing load. The tell: active-passive = standby waits idle, active-active = all nodes carry traffic simultaneously, load balancing = the distribution function, clustering = the grouping itself."},
  {"id":"q484","domain":"D3","family":"load-balancing","question":"Two identical VPN concentrators both carry live user traffic simultaneously, sharing the load. When one fails, the survivor absorbs the full load until its twin returns. Which of the following configurations is this?","options":["active-passive configuration","active-active configuration","clustering","load balancing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Active-active. Both nodes working at once, with the survivor absorbing everything on a failure, is active-active: full capacity use, but each node must be sized for the whole load. The tell: active-active = all nodes live, active-passive = one works while one waits, clustering = the grouping mechanism, load balancing = the distribution function."},
  {"id":"q485","domain":"D3","family":"load-balancing","question":"A high-availability pair is documented as active-passive. Which of the following describes the second node's behavior during normal operation?","options":["It distributes incoming requests across a server pool.","It groups with the first node to act as one system.","It shares the live traffic load with the first node.","It sits idle on standby and takes over only if the first node fails."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The passive node waits. It carries nothing until the active node fails, then assumes the load: simple and predictable, at the cost of idle capacity. The tell: active-passive = hot standby doing nothing, active-active = both nodes working, load balancing = spreading requests, clustering = acting as one system."},
  {"id":"q486","domain":"D3","family":"load-balancing","question":"An architect asks for the technique whose purpose is failover and high availability: multiple nodes joined to behave as a single system, rather than a device merely spreading requests for throughput. Which of the following techniques meets the request?","options":["load balancing","clustering","active-active configuration","active-passive configuration"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Clustering. Joining nodes into one logical system for failover is clustering; load balancing distributes work but the pool members stay individual systems. The tell: clustering = one logical system, high availability, load balancing = request distribution for throughput, active-active/active-passive = the modes a cluster or pair can run in."},
  {"id":"q487","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"An online retailer's continuity requirement says order processing must resume within 15 minutes of losing a data center. It maintains a second facility with live, fully configured servers and continuously synchronized data, ready for near-immediate cutover. Which of the following recovery sites is this?","options":["hot site","warm site","cold site","geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hot site. Running systems plus continuously current data means failover in minutes, the hot site's defining (and most expensive) promise. The tell: hot = live systems, synced data, minutes, warm = equipment ready but data must be restored, hours, cold = empty powered space, days to weeks, geographic dispersion = distance between facilities."},
  {"id":"q488","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A nonprofit with a tiny budget decides it can tolerate two weeks of downtime after a disaster. It leases an empty facility offering power, cooling, and floor space only - no hardware, no data, nothing installed. Which of the following site types is this?","options":["hot site","warm site","geographic dispersion","cold site"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cold site. A powered empty shell that everything must be brought into is a cold site: cheapest option, slowest recovery. The tell: cold = space and power only, days to weeks, warm = hardware waiting but data restore needed, hot = running and synchronized, geographic dispersion = the distance principle, not a site type."},
  {"id":"q489","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A company's recovery facility is stocked with racked servers, network gear, and live connectivity, but the systems hold no current data; backups must be restored first, making recovery a matter of hours. Which of the following site types is this?","options":["hot site","warm site","cold site","geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Warm site. Equipment ready, data not loaded, recovery in hours: the warm site is the middle option on cost and speed. The tell: warm = hardware ready, restore data first, hours, hot = synchronized and running, minutes, cold = empty shell, weeks, geographic dispersion = spacing sites apart."},
  {"id":"q490","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A continuity planner insists the new backup facility be located hundreds of miles from headquarters specifically so one hurricane, earthquake, or regional grid failure cannot take out both facilities at once. Which of the following recovery concepts is this?","options":["hot site","warm site","geographic dispersion","cold site"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Geographic dispersion. Separating facilities beyond a single disaster's reach is dispersion, a principle that applies whatever the site temperature. The tell: geographic dispersion = distance defeats regional disasters, hot/warm/cold = how ready the site is, not where it sits."},
  {"id":"q491","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"Two firms compare recovery postures. Firm A's facility has racks, connectivity, and hardware waiting, though no current data loaded; Firm B's is only a powered empty shell. Which of the following site types is Firm A's facility?","options":["cold site","hot site","geographic dispersion","warm site"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Warm site. Hardware and connectivity in place with data still to restore is warm; Firm B's empty shell is the cold site. The tell: warm = equipped, awaiting data, cold = empty space, hot = running with synchronized data, geographic dispersion = the distance principle."},
  {"id":"q492","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"An auditor's report labels a facility a hot site. Which of the following BEST describes what the auditor found?","options":["Fully equipped and running with synchronized data, failover in minutes","Hardware and connectivity ready but data must be restored first, recovery in hours","Only powered floor space, everything must be brought in, recovery in days to weeks","Backup facilities spread far apart to survive a regional disaster"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hot = running now. Live systems with continuously synchronized data and failover in minutes define the hot site. The tell: hot = minutes, warm = hours, cold = days to weeks, and dispersion is about distance, not readiness."},
  {"id":"q493","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A trading firm cannot tolerate more than a few minutes of lost transactions. A vendor offers a half-price facility with equipment racked and connected, where data would be restored from backups after an event. Which of the following site types BEST meets the firm's requirement?","options":["warm site","cold site","geographic dispersion","hot site"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Hot site. Only a running facility with continuously synchronized data holds transaction loss to minutes; the discounted offer is a warm site, tempting on price, but its restore-from-backup gap loses hours of transactions the firm cannot accept. The tell: hot = live systems, synced data, minutes, warm = equipment ready but data must be restored, hours, cold = empty powered space, days to weeks, geographic dispersion = distance between facilities, not readiness."},
  {"id":"q494","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A regional insurer rules out a continuously synchronized recovery facility as unaffordable, but also cannot accept weeks of downtime buying equipment after a disaster. Which of the following site types did the insurer select?","options":["cold site","warm site","hot site","geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Warm site. Explicitly the compromise: cheaper than hot, far faster than cold, recovery in hours once backups are restored. The tell: warm = the middle path, hot = expensive and instant, cold = cheap and slow, geographic dispersion = a siting principle, not a readiness tier."},
  {"id":"q495","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"After declaring a disaster, a company arrives at its alternate facility to find bare floor space. Staff must purchase and install servers and network gear, then restore everything from offsite media, before any operations resume. Which of the following site types does it operate?","options":["hot site","warm site","geographic dispersion","cold site"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cold site. Bring-everything-yourself recovery measured in days to weeks is the cold site experience; the low lease cost is paid back in downtime. The tell: cold = empty shell, longest recovery, warm = gear waiting, hot = running and current, geographic dispersion = how far apart sites are."},
  {"id":"q496","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A vendor pitches a cold site disaster recovery contract to a company's IT director. Which of the following expectations should the director set with management before signing?","options":["Racked hardware and connectivity are in place, but data must be restored, making recovery a matter of hours.","Live duplicate systems run with continuously current data, allowing failover within minutes of a disaster.","Bare space with power and cooling only; all hardware and data must be brought in over days to weeks.","Facilities are simply placed far apart so a single regional disaster cannot destroy both of them."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cold site: the contract buys bare space, power, and cooling, so management must expect a days-to-weeks recovery window while hardware is procured and data restored. The warm description loses because racked hardware awaiting only data is a costlier tier than a cold contract delivers. The tell: cold = empty shell, days to weeks, warm = equipped but data must load, hours, hot = running and current, minutes, geographic dispersion = a siting principle, not a facility."},
  {"id":"q497","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"An organization has contracted a warm site, and the recovery team activates it after an outage. Which of the following should the team realistically expect to find?","options":["Fully running duplicate systems with continuously current data, ready for failover within minutes.","Only conditioned space with power and cooling, requiring days to weeks to equip and restore.","Racked servers and connectivity in place, but backups must be restored first, making recovery a matter of hours.","Two facilities placed far enough apart that a single disaster cannot strike both of them."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Warm site: equipment and links are ready, but restoring data from backups puts recovery at a matter of hours. The hot description loses because continuously current data is exactly the premium a warm contract avoids paying for. The tell: warm = equipped, restore data first, hours, hot = already running and current, minutes, cold = empty shell, days to weeks, geographic dispersion = distance, not readiness."},
  {"id":"q498","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A manufacturer wants operations restored within a few hours of a disaster but refuses to pay for a continuously running duplicate facility. It leases a site already stocked with servers and connectivity and plans to restore data after declaring an event. Which of the following site types BEST fits?","options":["warm","hot","cold","geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Warm. Recovery within hours, without hot-site cost, is precisely the warm compromise: gear ready, data restored on activation. The tell: warm = the cost/speed middle ground, hot = premium for minutes, cold = cheap but weeks, dispersion = siting distance."},
  {"id":"q499","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"A hospital determines that losing access to electronic health records for more than a few minutes endangers patients. Which of the following recovery site options provides near-immediate failover?","options":["hot","warm","cold","geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Hot. Only a hot site's live, synchronized systems deliver failover measured in minutes, which the clinical requirement demands regardless of cost; a warm site loses because restoring data takes hours the patients do not have. The tell: hot = running now with current data, minutes, warm = restore data first, hours, cold = build from scratch, weeks, geographic dispersion = distance, not readiness."},
  {"id":"q500","domain":"D3","family":"sites","question":"An auditor flags that a company's primary data center and its recovery site sit eight miles apart in the same river valley, warning that one flood could destroy both. Which of the following resilience principles did the company fail to apply?","options":["hot","warm","cold","geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Geographic dispersion. However capable the recovery site is, co-locating it within the same disaster footprint defeats its purpose; the fix is distance. The tell: geographic dispersion = separate the sites beyond one event's reach, hot/warm/cold = readiness of a site, irrelevant if both drown together."},
  {"id":"q501","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"After a single vendor's firmware flaw took down every firewall in the fleet simultaneously, an architect proposes deliberately mixing vendors and operating systems so one flaw can never again hit everything at once. Which of the following resilience approaches is this?","options":["multi-cloud","platform diversity","capacity planning","continuity of operations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Platform diversity. Mixing vendors, operating systems, and technologies prevents a single defect from being a monoculture-wide event. The tell: platform diversity = different tech so one flaw cannot hit all, multi-cloud = the same idea applied to cloud providers, capacity planning = forecasting people/tech/infrastructure needs, continuity of operations = keeping the mission running through disruption."},
  {"id":"q502","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"Ahead of a major product launch, a firm forecasts the additional support staff, server capacity, and bandwidth it will need to absorb the projected surge, and provisions them in advance. Which of the following resilience activities is this?","options":["capacity planning","platform diversity","multi-cloud","continuity of operations"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Capacity planning. Forecasting and provisioning the people, technology, and infrastructure future demand requires is capacity planning. The tell: capacity planning = will we have enough people/tech/infrastructure, platform diversity = avoid a single-vendor monoculture, multi-cloud = spread across providers, continuity of operations = keep functioning during disruption."},
  {"id":"q503","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"Burned by a region-wide outage at its sole cloud provider, a company re-architects to run workloads across Amazon, Microsoft, and Google simultaneously so no single provider failure takes everything down. Which of the following resilience approaches is this?","options":["platform diversity","multi-cloud","continuity of operations (COOP)","capacity planning"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Multi-cloud. Spreading workloads across multiple cloud providers removes the single-provider dependency. It is platform diversity's cloud-specific cousin. The tell: multi-cloud = multiple cloud providers, platform diversity = mixed vendors/OS/tech generally, COOP = keeping essential functions running through disruption, capacity planning = forecasting needed resources."},
  {"id":"q504","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"A government agency maintains a formal plan describing exactly how its essential functions will keep operating during and after a major disruption, from alternate facilities to succession of leadership. Which of the following resilience approaches does this describe?","options":["capacity planning","multi-cloud","continuity of operations (COOP)","platform diversity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Continuity of operations. COOP is the documented plan for keeping mission-essential functions alive through a disruption. The tell: COOP = keep essential functions running no matter what, capacity planning = forecasting resources for demand, multi-cloud = spreading across providers, platform diversity = avoiding a tech monoculture."},
  {"id":"q505","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"An assessment warns that every server and workstation in the company runs the same operating system, so one exploitable kernel flaw could compromise the entire estate at once. Which of the following resilience strategies BEST addresses this risk?","options":["Defense in depth","Platform diversity","Multi-cloud deployment","Continuity of operations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Platform diversity. Deliberately mixing operating systems and vendors ensures a single flaw cannot compromise everything at once; defense in depth loses because stacking more control layers on one platform still falls to that platform's zero-day. The tell: platform diversity = vary the platforms themselves; defense in depth = layer different controls; multi-cloud = multiple cloud providers specifically; COOP = keep essential functions running during disruption."},
  {"id":"q506","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"Despite deploying across three regions of its cloud provider, a company's application went down during the provider's global control-plane outage. Which of the following changes would BEST protect against a repeat?","options":["Add a fourth region with the current provider","Negotiate a stronger uptime SLA with the provider","Increase instance counts within each existing region","Distribute the workload across a second cloud provider"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Multi-cloud. Regions of one provider share its global control plane and account infrastructure, so only a second provider survives a provider-level failure; adding a fourth region loses because it still sits inside the same failure domain that just collapsed. The tell: multi-region = redundancy within one provider; multi-cloud = redundancy across providers - the only defense when the provider itself is the single point of failure."},
  {"id":"q507","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"During a ransomware outage, a hospital continues admitting and treating patients using predefined paper downtime procedures while IT rebuilds the encrypted systems. Which of the following does the paper process demonstrate?","options":["Continuity of operations","Disaster recovery","Incident response","Capacity planning"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Continuity of operations. COOP keeps essential business functions running through a disruption, including non-IT fallbacks such as paper workflows; disaster recovery loses because it is the parallel technical effort to restore the encrypted IT systems, not the ability to keep treating patients. The tell: COOP = the business keeps functioning during the outage; DR = the IT systems get rebuilt; incident response = handling the security incident itself."},
  {"id":"q508","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"A resilience review finds that only one engineer knows how to restore the payroll mainframe, and that engineer retires in six months. Which of the following BEST addresses the finding?","options":["Upgrading the recovery site's network circuit","Purchasing additional software licenses for the failover systems","Cross-training more engineers on the restore procedure","Adding compute headroom at the recovery site"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cross-training - people capacity planning. Resilience depends on staffing depth, and one engineer holding all recovery knowledge is a key-person risk that hardware cannot fix; the licenses and circuit options lose because they address technology and infrastructure capacity while the gap is human. The tell: capacity planning covers people (staffing, cross-training), technology (licenses, compute), and infrastructure (power, cooling, bandwidth) - match the fix to the scarce resource."},
  {"id":"q509","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"A disaster recovery cutover succeeds, but the recovery environment then fails under load: its database licenses cap concurrent connections and its compute cannot absorb production transaction volume. Which of the following capacity planning areas fell short?","options":["Infrastructure","Technology","People","Geographic dispersion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Technology capacity. License caps and undersized compute mean the recovery software stack cannot carry production volume even though the cutover itself worked; infrastructure loses because power, cooling, and bandwidth were not the constraint here. The tell: technology capacity = licenses and compute; infrastructure capacity = power, cooling, bandwidth, and space; people capacity = staffing depth and cross-training."},
  {"id":"q510","domain":"D3","family":"resilience-planning","question":"A failover test to the warm site fails when the site's network circuit saturates well below production bandwidth, even though its servers, licenses, and staffing were sufficient. Which of the following capacity planning areas was undersized?","options":["Technology","People","Platform diversity","Infrastructure"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Infrastructure capacity. Bandwidth, like power, cooling, and physical space, is physical-plant capacity, and a circuit that cannot carry production load starves an otherwise adequate site; technology loses because the servers and licenses were explicitly sufficient. The tell: infrastructure = power, cooling, bandwidth, space; technology = licenses and compute; people = staffing - the resource that failed names the category."},
  {"id":"q511","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"An incident response team gathers in a conference room and talks through a ransomware scenario step by step - who calls whom, what gets isolated, when legal is engaged - without touching a single live system. Which of the following tests is this?","options":["tabletop exercise","failover test","simulation","parallel processing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Tabletop exercise. A discussion-based walkthrough with no systems touched is the tabletop: cheap, safe, and great for finding plan gaps. The tell: tabletop = talk through it, simulation = act out the scenario with real response steps, failover test = actually cut production over, parallel processing = run the recovery site alongside production."},
  {"id":"q512","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"To prove its DR plan is more than paper, an organization deliberately shuts down the primary system during a scheduled window and cuts production over to the backup site for real. Which of the following tests is the organization performing?","options":["tabletop exercise","simulation","failover test","parallel processing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Failover test. Actually switching live production to the recovery system is the failover test: the most convincing proof and the most disruptive if it goes wrong. The tell: failover = really cut over, parallel processing = recovery runs alongside production untouched, simulation = realistic actions without cutover, tabletop = discussion only."},
  {"id":"q513","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"A bank validates its recovery site by running it alongside live production with the same workload, confirming it keeps up - while production itself is never taken offline. Which of the following continuity tests is this?","options":["failover test","parallel processing","simulation","tabletop exercise"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Parallel processing. Running the recovery environment beside production proves capacity with zero production risk. The tell: parallel processing = both run at once, failover = production actually switches, simulation = acted-out scenario, tabletop = conversation in a conference room."},
  {"id":"q514","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"During a scheduled exercise, responders receive a realistic injected breach scenario and perform their actual response procedures - isolating test hosts, paging on-call, drafting comms - but production is never cut over. Which of the following tests is being conducted?","options":["tabletop exercise","failover test","parallel processing","simulation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Simulation. Acting out the response with real steps in a controlled setting, short of an actual cutover, is a simulation: more realistic than a tabletop, less risky than failover. The tell: simulation = perform the steps, tabletop = discuss the steps, failover = flip production for real, parallel processing = run both sites simultaneously."},
  {"id":"q515","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"A continuity manager wants the cheapest, lowest-impact FIRST test of a brand-new DR plan. Which of the following test types should the manager schedule?","options":["simulation","failover","parallel processing","tabletop"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tabletop. A discussion-based walkthrough costs little and risks nothing, which is why brand-new plans start there; a simulation loses because acting out real steps already carries more cost and impact than a first-pass talk-through. The tell: tabletop = talk it through, no systems touched, simulation = act it out, controlled, failover = actually cut over, parallel processing = run both environments side by side."},
  {"id":"q516","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"An audit committee reviewing the continuity test schedule asks which planned test type is the MOST disruptive to live operations. Which of the following should the continuity manager identify?","options":["failover","tabletop","parallel processing","simulation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Failover. Deliberately cutting live production over to the backup system is the strongest proof and the biggest operational risk; parallel processing loses because the recovery site runs alongside production, which is never taken offline. The tell: failover = production really moves, parallel processing = both run side by side, simulation = real actions without cutover, tabletop = discussion only."},
  {"id":"q517","domain":"D3","family":"continuity-testing","question":"Leadership wants proof that the standby data center can process the full daily transaction load but forbids any test that could interrupt live production service. Which of the following continuity tests should the team schedule?","options":["Parallel processing","Failover test","Simulation","Tabletop exercise"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Parallel processing. The standby site runs the same transactions alongside production and the outputs are compared, proving real capacity with zero interruption risk; a failover test loses because it proves the cutover by actually moving live service, which the stem forbids. The tell: parallel processing = run beside production and compare; failover = actually cut over; simulation = act out procedures in a controlled scenario; tabletop = discussion only."},
  {"id":"q518","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"Minutes before applying a risky hypervisor patch, a virtualization admin captures the exact current state of the VM so one click can return it to this instant if the patch goes wrong. Which of the following backup methods BEST fits?","options":["snapshot","replication","journaling","offsite backup"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Snapshot. A point-in-time image you can revert to instantly is a snapshot, the standard pre-change safety net for VMs. The tell: snapshot = frozen point-in-time image, replication = continuous mirror of writes, journaling = log of changes replayed to a moment, offsite = where a copy lives."},
  {"id":"q519","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A database team's recovery requirement is to restore to any exact transaction - say, the moment before a bad deployment - by replaying a log of every change made since the last full backup. Which of the following techniques provides this?","options":["snapshot","replication","journaling","offsite backup"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Journaling. Recording every change in a log so you can replay to a precise point delivers transaction-level recovery granularity. The tell: journaling = change log replayed to an exact moment, snapshot = discrete point-in-time images only, replication = a continuously current mirror (which faithfully copies mistakes too), offsite = location."},
  {"id":"q520","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A company ships encrypted backup media to a facility in another city each week, so a fire that destroys headquarters cannot also destroy the backups. Which of the following BEST describes this copy?","options":["snapshot","journaling","replication","offsite backup"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Offsite backup. A copy kept at a separate geographic location survives building-level disasters; that placement is its whole point. The tell: offsite = separate facility, onsite = same building, fast but shares the building's fate, snapshot/journaling/replication = how copies are made, not where they live."},
  {"id":"q521","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A storage engineer configures the primary array to copy every write to a second array at a partner site in near real time, keeping the two nearly identical at every moment. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["snapshot","replication","journaling","onsite backup"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Replication. Continuously mirroring writes so the copy stays current is replication, built for minimal data loss on failover. The tell: replication = continuous near-real-time mirror, snapshot = periodic frozen images, journaling = a replayable change log, onsite backup = a local copy."},
  {"id":"q522","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A change-control document requires capturing \"a point-in-time image of the volume that can be reverted to exactly as it existed at that instant.\" Which of the following terms is being defined?","options":["snapshot","replication","journaling","offsite backup"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Snapshot. Point-in-time image plus exact revert is the snapshot definition. The tell: snapshot = frozen instant you can return to, replication = live mirror, journaling = replayable change log, offsite backup = a copy stored elsewhere."},
  {"id":"q523","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"An admin's priority is that the secondary copy stay constantly current with production, so a failover loses almost nothing - not a periodic image from hours ago. Which of the following approaches fits?","options":["snapshot","journaling","onsite backup","replication"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Replication. Constant currency of the copy is replication's promise, minimizing the recovery point. Snapshots by definition age between captures. The tell: replication = always current mirror, snapshot = as fresh as the last capture, journaling = replay log for precision recovery, onsite backup = local copy."},
  {"id":"q524","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A vendor proposal describes its backup strategy as journaling. Which of the following BEST describes what that strategy does?","options":["Captures a single point-in-time image you can revert to","Continuously mirrors writes to a second location in near real time","Records every change in a log so transactions can be replayed to a precise point","Keeps a copy in a separate geographic facility"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Journaling = the change log. Every transaction is recorded so recovery can replay to an exact moment. The tell: journaling = replayable log of changes, snapshot = point-in-time image, replication = live mirroring, offsite = geographic separation."},
  {"id":"q525","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A team captures an image of the file server every night at midnight and formally accepts that a failure at 11 p.m. will lose that entire day's changes. Which of the following backup methods are they relying on?","options":["snapshot","replication","journaling","offsite backup"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Snapshot. Nightly point-in-time captures mean the recovery point is the most recent snapshot, and everything since is lost, exactly the accepted trade-off. The tell: snapshot = periodic frozen images, data since the last one is gone, replication = continuously current, journaling = replay to any point, offsite = storage location."},
  {"id":"q526","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"An architect notes that backups kept on local storage in the same building restore fastest, but a fire or flood that destroys the building destroys them too. Which of the following terms describes this copy?","options":["offsite backup","onsite backup","snapshot","replication"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Onsite backup. Local copies trade disaster survival for restore speed; that is why strategies pair them with offsite copies. The tell: onsite = same building, fast restore, shared fate, offsite = another facility, survives the building, snapshot/replication = techniques, not locations."},
  {"id":"q527","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"During a backup design review, a junior administrator asks what a snapshot actually captures. Which of the following BEST describes it?","options":["A continuous near real-time copy kept identical to the source.","A log of every change that can be replayed to a precise point.","A point-in-time image of the volume, revertible to exactly that instant.","A copy stored in the same building as production for the fastest restore."],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Snapshot. It freezes the volume at an instant, and reverting returns it exactly to that moment; the change log loses because journaling replays individual changes rather than storing one frozen image. The tell: snapshot = point-in-time image, replication = continuous mirror, journaling = replayable log, onsite = a location, not a method."},
  {"id":"q528","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"A compliance officer worries that backup tapes could be stolen in transit to the offsite vault and read by whoever takes them. Which of the following backup safeguards directly renders stolen tape data unreadable without the key?","options":["offsite","frequency","encryption","replication"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Encryption. Encrypting backup media makes physical theft a nuisance instead of a breach; possession without the key yields nothing. The tell: encryption = confidentiality of the media, offsite = where it is stored, frequency = how often backups run, replication = continuous copying."},
  {"id":"q529","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"After a near-miss, a team moves from one backup per day to one every four hours, specifically to shrink the window of data that could be lost between backups and tighten the recovery point objective. Which of the following backup attributes did the team change?","options":["recovery","replication","journaling","frequency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Frequency. The interval between backups bounds the worst-case loss, so raising frequency directly tightens the RPO. The tell: frequency = how often, drives RPO, recovery = the restore act, replication = continuous mirroring, journaling = replayable change log."},
  {"id":"q530","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"Ransomware encrypts the production file servers. The response team wipes the systems and restores clean data from the most recent unaffected backups, returning services to a working state. Which of the following backup activities does this describe?","options":["recovery","replication","snapshots","journaling"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Recovery. Restoring data from backup to return systems to operation is the recovery step, the payoff for every other backup decision. The tell: recovery = restoring from the copies, replication/snapshots/journaling = the ways the copies got made."},
  {"id":"q531","domain":"D3","family":"backups","question":"Ransomware encrypts a firm's primary storage array, and within seconds the near-real-time replica at the recovery site holds the same encrypted data. Which of the following additions would BEST enable recovery from this type of event?","options":["Synchronous replication to a third site","Shorter replication intervals","Relocating the replica farther from the primary","Point-in-time backups kept offline"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Point-in-time backups kept offline. Replication faithfully mirrors whatever happens to production - including the encryption - so recovery requires a copy frozen from before the attack that the malware cannot touch; a third replicated site is the tempting add-on, but it would hold the same encrypted data seconds later. The tell: replication = continuous live mirror that protects against hardware or site failure, not bad data; backups and snapshots = point-in-time copies you can roll back to; journaling = replay logged changes to a precise moment."},
  {"id":"q532","domain":"D3","family":"power","question":"A hospital's server rack cannot tolerate even a momentary power gap. When utility power blips, a battery instantly carries the load for the seconds to minutes needed for longer-term power to come online. Which of the following devices provides this instant ride-through?","options":["generator","UPS","power distribution unit","dual power supply"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"UPS. The uninterruptible power supply's battery bridges outages instantly but only briefly, buying time for the generator. The tell: UPS = instant battery, short duration, generator = seconds to start, runs for days on fuel, PDU = distributes power within the rack, dual power supply = redundancy inside one device."},
  {"id":"q533","domain":"D3","family":"power","question":"During a six-hour utility outage, a facility stays up on a fuel-powered unit that needed several seconds to start after the failure and can keep producing power for as long as fuel is delivered. Which of the following devices is this?","options":["UPS","power distribution unit","generator","dual power supply"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Generator. Fuel-driven, slow to start, effectively unlimited runtime: the generator handles the long haul after the UPS bridges the startup gap. The tell: generator = long-duration fuel power with a startup delay, UPS = instant but brief battery, PDU = power distribution hardware, dual power supply = component redundancy in a single chassis."},
  {"id":"q534","domain":"D3","family":"power","question":"A branch office's server rack reboots several times a month when utility power dips for a few seconds. Extended outages are rare, and management accepts that risk. Which of the following would BEST address the reboots?","options":["generator","power distribution unit","dual power supply","UPS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"UPS. Its battery instantly rides through seconds-long dips with no gap, which is exactly what is rebooting the rack; a generator loses because it takes seconds to start and targets the extended outages management already accepts. The tell: UPS = instant, short battery ride-through, generator = slow-starting, long-duration fuel power, power distribution unit = routes power in the rack, no backup, dual power supply = redundancy within one device."},
  {"id":"q535","domain":"D3","family":"power","question":"A facility relies on a diesel generator to survive extended outages. Which of the following inherent limitations must its power design compensate for?","options":["It only routes power to rack equipment and provides no backup itself.","It offers redundancy inside one device but no outage protection.","It gives instant battery power but only for seconds to minutes.","It takes several seconds to start, so a battery unit must bridge the gap until it runs."],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Generators start slow. Those several seconds of startup are a hard gap that a UPS battery must cover, which is why the two are always paired. The tell: generator = long duration, delayed start, UPS = instant, short duration; each covers the other's weakness."},
  {"id":"q536","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A CIO is weighing a move from company-owned servers to a public cloud provider and asks which security duties the company would keep no matter which service model is chosen. Which of the following responsibilities NEVER transfers to the provider?","options":["Patching the hypervisor layer","Physical security of the data center","Protecting data and managing user access","Maintaining server hardware and firmware"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Protecting data and managing user access. Moving off owned hardware hands physical, hardware, and hypervisor duties to the provider, but who may touch the company's data is a decision only the customer can make; hypervisor patching is tempting because it is a security duty, yet it belongs to the provider the moment the workload leaves owned hardware. The tell: the customer always keeps responsibility for its data and identity/access in every service model; the service model only moves where the rest of the line sits."},
  {"id":"q537","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A breach investigation finds customer records exposed because an IaaS storage bucket was configured for public read access. Which of the following parties is responsible for the misconfiguration?","options":["The cloud service provider","The cloud customer","The managed service provider","The software vendor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The cloud customer. In IaaS the provider secures the facility, hardware, and hypervisor, while everything the customer configures, including storage permissions and the data itself, stays customer-side; the cloud service provider is tempting but it delivered the bucket exactly as the customer configured it. The tell: the customer always keeps responsibility for its data and identity/access in every service model; IaaS additionally leaves OS, application, and configuration duties with the customer."},
  {"id":"q538","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A company runs Linux virtual machines on an IaaS platform. Which of the following security tasks belongs to the customer rather than the cloud service provider?","options":["Patching the guest operating system","Patching the hypervisor","Maintaining the physical host hardware","Securing the data center facility"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Patching the guest operating system. In IaaS the customer manages everything from the guest OS upward, including patches, applications, and data, while the provider owns the hypervisor, hardware, and facility; hypervisor patching is tempting because it also involves patching, but it sits below the line the provider controls. The tell: IaaS splits at the hypervisor - provider secures facility, hardware, and hypervisor; customer secures guest OS, applications, configuration, and data."},
  {"id":"q539","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A team replaces its on-premises help desk software with a SaaS product, and the manager asks what security work is still theirs. Which of the following remains the customer's responsibility?","options":["Patching the application code","Hardening the server operating system","Maintaining the provider's network infrastructure","Classifying data and managing user access"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Classifying data and managing user access. In SaaS the provider runs the application, OS, and infrastructure, but deciding how sensitive the data is and which users get which access can only be done by the organization that owns the data; patching the application is tempting because customers patch in other models, yet in SaaS the vendor ships and maintains the code. The tell: SaaS moves nearly the whole stack to the provider, but data protection and identity/access stay with the customer in every service model."},
  {"id":"q540","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A security architect needs the document that defines which security tasks the cloud provider performs and which stay with the customer under each service model. Which of the following should the architect consult?","options":["Service level agreement","Responsibility matrix","Memorandum of understanding","Statement of work"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Responsibility matrix. It is the table that maps each security duty to provider or customer per service model, which is exactly what the architect is asking for; the service level agreement is tempting because it also governs the provider relationship, but it covers uptime targets and penalties, not who secures what. The tell: responsibility matrix = who secures what per service model; SLA = measurable performance targets with penalties; MOU = non-binding intent; statement of work = deliverables for one specific job."},
  {"id":"q541","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"An organization keeps regulated records on-premises and bursts its web workloads to a public cloud during peak season. Which of the following is the PRIMARY security challenge of this hybrid design?","options":["Enforcing consistent security controls across both environments","Transferring accountability for the regulated data to the provider","Losing physical control of the on-premises servers","Patching the cloud provider's hypervisor on schedule"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Enforcing consistent security controls across both environments. Hybrid cloud means two different toolsets, policy engines, and monitoring stacks, so keeping controls uniform across them is the core challenge; losing physical control of on-premises servers is tempting but false - those servers stay in the organization's own facility. The tell: hybrid = private plus public combined, and its signature implications are inconsistent controls, identity spanning both, and split monitoring; accountability for data never transfers in any model."},
  {"id":"q542","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"During an intrusion investigation, analysts cannot correlate the public cloud's audit logs with events in the on-premises SIEM because formats and timestamps do not align. Which of the following hybrid cloud implications does this illustrate?","options":["Inconsistent identity federation between environments","Unencrypted data in transit between environments","Fragmented monitoring and logging across environments","An expanded physical attack surface"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Fragmented monitoring and logging across environments. Hybrid architectures produce two log pipelines with different formats and toolsets, so visibility gaps appear exactly when correlation matters most; inconsistent identity federation is tempting because it is another hybrid implication, but nothing in the symptoms involves authentication across environments. The tell: hybrid implications each have a distinct symptom - correlation failure = monitoring gap; login sprawl = federation gap; exposed links = transit protection gap."},
  {"id":"q543","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A SaaS vendor that processes a retailer's customer data suffers a breach, and regulators open an inquiry into the exposure. Which of the following BEST describes who is accountable for the data?","options":["The SaaS vendor, because it operated the breached platform","The cloud provider hosting the vendor's infrastructure","Neither party, because the contract divided the liability","The retailer, because accountability for its data never transfers"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The retailer, because accountability for its data never transfers. Outsourcing can transfer the operation of a service and some financial risk, but the data owner still answers to regulators for its customers' data; the SaaS vendor is tempting because it caused the breach, yet it faces contractual consequences, not the owner's regulatory accountability. The tell: with third-party vendors you can transfer operation and share risk, but accountability for the data always stays with the data owner."},
  {"id":"q544","domain":"D3","family":"cloud-models","question":"A defense contractor must guarantee project data never resides on hardware shared with other organizations and must retain full control over physical security. Which of the following architecture models meets both requirements?","options":["On-premises infrastructure","Public cloud with dedicated host instances","Hybrid cloud with private connectivity","Community cloud shared with vetted partners"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"On-premises infrastructure. Owning and operating the facility keeps data off third-party hardware entirely and leaves physical security in the contractor's hands, at the price of carrying every security duty alone; dedicated host instances are tempting because the hardware is not shared, but the provider still controls the building and the racks. The tell: on-premises = full control and full, unshared responsibility; every cloud option places data in a third party's facility under a shared responsibility split."},
  {"id":"q545","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"A network architect is deciding where to place a new customer-facing web server so that a compromise of the server cannot provide a direct path to the internal LAN. Which of the following placements is BEST?","options":["On the internal user VLAN behind the perimeter firewall","Outside the perimeter firewall at the internet-facing edge","In a screened subnet between the internet and the LAN","In the management zone alongside administrative systems"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A screened subnet. Placing the internet-facing web server in a buffer network means a compromise still faces a firewall before the internal LAN; the internal VLAN loses because a breached server there would sit directly beside user workstations. The tell: where a system sits determines what its compromise can reach - a screened subnet buffers internet-facing services from the internal LAN, and nothing internet-reachable belongs inside that LAN."},
  {"id":"q546","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"A security engineer reviews the perimeter firewall rules: 1) permit tcp any -> 172.16.40.20 eq 443; 2) permit tcp any -> 172.16.40.20 eq 80; 3) permit ip 172.16.40.0/24 -> any; 4) deny ip any any. Host 172.16.40.20 is the company web server, and 172.16.40.0/24 is the internal user LAN. Which of the following flaws do the rules reveal?","options":["The public web server sits inside the internal user subnet","Rule 4 blocks all outbound internet access for internal users","The rules are evaluated in the wrong order for a deny-by-default policy","Port 80 should never be permitted inbound through the perimeter firewall"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A public web server on the internal user LAN. Rules 1 and 2 invite the entire internet to a host inside the user subnet, so compromising it puts an attacker beside every workstation; the outbound-blocking option loses because rule 3 explicitly permits the LAN outbound before the final deny. The tell: anything the internet can reach belongs in a screened subnet that buffers the internal LAN - placement determines what a compromise can reach next."},
  {"id":"q547","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"An audit flags that a company's web front end, application tier, and database share one flat network with unrestricted traffic between them. Which of the following design changes BEST addresses the finding?","options":["A single next-generation firewall at the internet perimeter edge","Separate security zones with default-deny rules between tiers","Full-disk encryption on the database server","An IDS sensor attached to the existing flat segment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Separate security zones. Grouping each tier into its own zone forces traffic through enforcement points that deny by default and allow only the flows each tier requires; the perimeter NGFW loses because it never sees tier-to-tier traffic moving inside the flat segment. The tell: zones create trust boundaries enforced between segments - a control can only protect traffic that actually crosses it."},
  {"id":"q548","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"A security analyst scans a file server that needs to offer only SMB and finds 14 listening services, including 21/tcp ftp, 23/tcp telnet, 80/tcp http, and 445/tcp microsoft-ds. Which of the following BEST reduces the server's attack surface?","options":["Move the server to a screened subnet","Deploy an IDS sensor on the file server's network segment","Require TLS on the FTP and web services","Disable all services and close all ports except SMB"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Disable everything except SMB. The attack surface is the sum of exposed entry points, and removing 13 unneeded listeners eliminates those entry points outright; the IDS sensor loses because monitoring detects attacks against the surface without shrinking it. The tell: attack surface = the exposed openings (ports, services, accounts); attack vector = the path an attacker actually uses; hardening reduces the surface, monitoring only watches it."},
  {"id":"q549","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"During a walkthrough, a technician finds an HVAC vendor's cellular support modem wired directly to a building controller, creating a path into the facility network that bypasses the perimeter firewall and appears on no network diagram. Which of the following infrastructure considerations did the design fail to account for?","options":["Connectivity","Device placement","Sensor placement","Responsiveness"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Connectivity. Every link into the environment is part of the security design, and an undocumented vendor modem that bypasses the firewall is an unmanaged path straight into the facility network; device placement loses because the controller itself sits where it belongs - the flaw is the untracked link reaching it. The tell: connectivity = accounting for every path in and out; device placement = where an asset or control sits; sensor placement = where monitoring collects; responsiveness = how quickly the system answers."},
  {"id":"q550","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"A SOC's SIEM shows rich traffic events from headquarters but nothing at all from the branch office segment. Which of the following BEST restores visibility into the branch?","options":["Write additional correlation rules on the SIEM at headquarters","Move the SIEM collector to the perimeter","Deploy a sensor on the branch segment to feed the SIEM","Enable an inline IPS at headquarters"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A sensor on the branch segment. Monitoring only sees traffic where a collection point exists, so the unmonitored branch needs its own sensor forwarding events to the SIEM; extra correlation rules lose because no rule can analyze events that are never collected. The tell: the SIEM is the analysis engine and sensors are its eyes - each segment that must be visible needs a sensor, and placement determines what gets seen."},
  {"id":"q551","domain":"D3","family":"infra-placement","question":"A security engineer must position an IDS sensor to record every attack attempted against the organization, including attempts the perimeter firewall drops. Which of the following placements meets the requirement?","options":["Inside the perimeter firewall","Outside the perimeter firewall","On the internal management VLAN","In front of the database servers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Outside the perimeter firewall. A sensor placed before the enforcement point sees all attempts, including the ones the firewall would drop; the inside placement loses because it records only the traffic that penetrated. The tell: outside the firewall = every attempt; inside = only what got through; a sensor's placement determines what it can see."},
  {"id":"q552","domain":"D3","family":"data-types","question":"A firm provides outside counsel with litigation strategy memos and draft settlement agreements. The security team warns that mishandling could forfeit attorney-client privilege in an active case. Which of the following data types BEST describes this material?","options":["Legal information","Regulated data","Intellectual property","Financial information"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Legal information. Litigation strategy, contracts, and privileged case material are legal information, and careless handling can destroy the privilege that shields them in court; regulated data is tempting, but no external statute dictates handling here - the risk is losing privilege, not a compliance penalty. The tell: legal = privileged case material; regulated = external law mandates handling; intellectual property = registered legal rights; financial = money and payment records."},
  {"id":"q553","domain":"D3","family":"data-types","question":"During a data inventory, an analyst tags a repository holding quarterly earnings statements, accounts payable ledgers, and corporate banking details. Which of the following data types BEST describes the repository's contents?","options":["Legal information","Trade secret","Financial information","Regulated data"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Financial information. Earnings statements, ledgers, and banking details are records about the organization's money, which is exactly what the financial type labels; regulated data is tempting because rules such as SOX touch financial reporting, but that label describes an external handling mandate, not what the content is. The tell: financial = money and payment records; regulated = external law mandates handling; trade secret = value lives in secrecy alone; legal = privileged case material."},
  {"id":"q554","domain":"D3","family":"data-types","question":"A DLP administrator proposes excluding a product's serialized binary telemetry from inspection, reasoning that no person could read it if it leaked. Which of the following BEST explains why the exclusion is a mistake?","options":["Binary encoding acts as encryption while the data is stored","Non-human-readable data still requires the same protection as readable data","DLP policies are only required to cover regulated data types","Telemetry data is classified as public unless labeled otherwise"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Non-human-readable data still requires the same protection as readable data. Attackers' tools can parse serialized binary easily, so a leak discloses the contents regardless of readability; the encoding-as-encryption option is tempting, but a format change involves no key and anyone can decode it. The tell: readability never removes the need for protection - human- vs non-human-readable only describes whether a person can interpret the data directly, not whether it is sensitive."},
  {"id":"q555","domain":"D3","family":"data-types","question":"A retailer's marketing team treats EU customers' personal data like any other contact list. Privacy counsel notes an external privacy law dictates exactly how it must be processed and disclosed, regardless of any internal label. Which of the following data types applies?","options":["Confidential data","Intellectual property","Legal information","Regulated data"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Regulated data. Handling requirements for EU personal data are imposed by external law (GDPR), so the obligations exist no matter what the organization calls the data internally; confidential is tempting, but that is an internal classification the organization chooses, not a mandate it inherits. The tell: regulated = external law mandates handling; confidential = an internal label for business harm; legal = privileged case material; intellectual property = registered legal rights."},
  {"id":"q556","domain":"D3","family":"data-types","question":"A manufacturer patented one production method, publishing its details in the filing, but deliberately never filed its second method, which only five senior engineers know. Which of the following BEST describes the unfiled method?","options":["Patent","Trade secret","Copyright","Trademark"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Trade secret. The unfiled method's protection comes entirely from staying secret - there is no registration, and the moment it leaks the advantage is gone; patent is the tempting contrast, but patents are publicly disclosed in exchange for registered legal rights. The tell: trade secret = value lives in secrecy alone; patent, copyright, and trademark = registered intellectual property rights that survive disclosure."},
  {"id":"q557","domain":"D3","family":"data-types","question":"An awareness module asks employees which of the company's assets counts as intellectual property. Which of the following items belongs in that category?","options":["An unpatented internal pricing process","Quarterly revenue projections","A patented compression algorithm","A signed vendor NDA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A patented compression algorithm. Intellectual property is the umbrella for registered creations of the mind such as patents, copyrights, and trademarks; the unpatented pricing process is the tempting pick, but with no registration its protection rests on secrecy alone, making it a trade secret. The tell: intellectual property = registered legal rights; trade secret = value lives in secrecy alone; financial = money records such as revenue projections; legal = privileged or contractual case material such as an NDA."},
  {"id":"q558","domain":"D4","family":"baselines","question":"A security team writes down the approved hardened configuration - services disabled, password policy, logging settings - that every new server must match before it may enter production. Which of the following baseline steps is this?","options":["Establish","Deploy","Maintain","Decommission"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Establish. Defining and documenting the approved secure configuration is establishing the baseline; rolling it out and keeping systems on it come later. The tell: establish = define the standard, deploy = push it onto systems, maintain = keep systems matching it over time, decommission = retire it."},
  {"id":"q559","domain":"D4","family":"baselines","question":"Months after rollout, a team keeps patching systems, watches for configuration drift, and re-applies settings whenever a server slips out of line with the approved configuration. Which of the following baseline activities is this?","options":["Deploy","Maintain","Establish","Decommission"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Maintain. Drift detection, patching, and re-applying settings keep systems aligned with the baseline over time, the ongoing maintain phase. The tell: maintain = keep systems on the standard, deploy = the initial rollout, establish = writing the standard, decommission = retiring it."},
  {"id":"q560","domain":"D4","family":"baselines","question":"With the golden image approved, the endpoint team uses its configuration management tool to push that exact secure configuration onto all 400 new workstations before they ship to users. Which of the following baseline activities is this?","options":["Establish","Maintain","Configuration enforcement","Deploy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Deploy. Rolling the approved baseline out onto systems is the deploy phase; keeping them compliant afterward is maintain. The tell: deploy = push the standard out, establish = define it, maintain = hold systems to it over time, configuration enforcement = the mechanism maintain uses."},
  {"id":"q561","domain":"D4","family":"baselines","question":"In the three-phase baseline process, which of the following does the maintain phase primarily involve?","options":["Writing the initial secure reference configuration","Pushing the approved configuration out to new systems","Continuously enforcing the configuration and correcting drift over time","Selecting which systems the baseline applies to"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Maintain = fight drift forever. Continuous enforcement, drift detection, and correction keep systems matching the baseline long after rollout. The tell: establish = write it, deploy = push it, maintain = keep it true over time."},
  {"id":"q562","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-install","question":"Before mounting a single access point in a new warehouse, a wireless engineer walks the floor with measurement gear, recording signal propagation, interference sources, and dead zones to decide where each AP belongs. Which of the following describes this activity?","options":["Heat map","WPA3","Site survey","RADIUS"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Site survey. The physical walkthrough measuring coverage and interference is the site survey; the heat map is the color-coded coverage picture the survey produces. The tell: site survey = the measuring activity, heat map = the visual output, WPA3 = the encryption standard, RADIUS = the authentication server."},
  {"id":"q563","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-install","question":"An enterprise requires each Wi-Fi user to sign in with their own domain username and password, checked against a central authentication server, instead of everyone sharing one passphrase. Which of the following components provides this?","options":["RADIUS","WPA3","Heat map","Site survey"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"RADIUS. Per-user enterprise authentication (802.1X/WPA-Enterprise) is brokered by a RADIUS server checking individual credentials. The tell: RADIUS = central per-user authentication, WPA3 = the encryption protocol, site survey = planning walkthrough, heat map = coverage visualization."},
  {"id":"q564","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-install","question":"After finishing the warehouse walkthrough, the wireless engineer delivers a color-coded floor plan showing measured signal strength in every zone, with red patches marking weak coverage. Which of the following is this deliverable?","options":["Site survey","Heat map","WPA3","RADIUS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Heat map. The color-coded coverage visualization is the heat map, produced from site survey measurements. The tell: heat map = the visual output, site survey = the measuring walkthrough that produced it, WPA3 = encryption, RADIUS = per-user authentication."},
  {"id":"q565","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-install","question":"A wireless hardening checklist requires the current-generation standard that encrypts traffic on the enterprise WLAN, replacing its predecessor's weaker handshake. Which of the following meets this requirement?","options":["RADIUS","Site survey","WPA3","Heat map"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"WPA3. The current Wi-Fi protected access standard secures wireless traffic confidentiality, improving on WPA2's handshake weaknesses. The tell: WPA3 = the encryption standard, RADIUS = the authentication server behind enterprise mode, site survey/heat map = planning activity and its output."},
  {"id":"q566","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"Sales staff read company email on their own personal phones. The company never bought the hardware and accepts it has only limited say over what else runs on those devices. Which of the following deployment models is this?","options":["COPE","CYOD","BYOD","MDM"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"BYOD. Bring your own device: employee-owned hardware, minimal organizational control, maximum privacy friction. The tell: BYOD = user owns it, COPE = company owns it, personal use allowed, CYOD = user picks from a company-approved list, MDM = the management platform, not an ownership model."},
  {"id":"q567","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"A firm buys smartphones, issues them to staff, and explicitly allows personal apps and use on these company-owned devices. Which of the following deployment models is this?","options":["COPE","BYOD","CYOD","MDM"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"COPE. Corporate-owned, personally enabled: the company owns and controls the device but sanctions personal use. The tell: COPE = corporate-owned, personal use permitted, BYOD = employee-owned, CYOD = employee chooses from an approved catalog, MDM = the management tooling."},
  {"id":"q568","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"Whatever the ownership model, the security team needs one platform to push configuration policies, enforce device encryption, and remotely wipe any phone reported lost. Which of the following provides this capability?","options":["BYOD","CYOD","COPE","MDM"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"MDM. Mobile device management is the control plane - policy push, encryption enforcement, remote wipe - layered on top of whichever ownership model is chosen. The tell: MDM = the management platform, BYOD/CYOD/COPE = who owns and picks the hardware."},
  {"id":"q569","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"New hires choose their work phone from a short list of approved, company-purchased models - iPhone or one of two Androids - and IT manages whichever they pick. Which of the following deployment models is this?","options":["BYOD","CYOD","COPE","MDM"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CYOD. Choose your own device: the employee picks from a company-approved, company-purchased catalog, giving IT standardization with user choice. The tell: CYOD = pick from the approved list, BYOD = bring your personal phone, COPE = company device with personal use allowed, MDM = the management platform."},
  {"id":"q570","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"Field technicians use phones they purchased themselves, and the company reimburses part of each monthly bill for work use. A manager asks whether the stipend makes the phones corporate assets. Which of the following deployment models still applies?","options":["CYOD","COPE","MDM","BYOD"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"BYOD. The technicians own the hardware, and a partial bill reimbursement does not transfer ownership - the model stays bring your own device. COPE loses because the company never purchased or issued the phones. The tell: BYOD = employee-owned hardware, CYOD = company-bought from an approved list, COPE = company-owned with personal use allowed, MDM = the management platform, not a deployment model."},
  {"id":"q571","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"Executives receive smartphones the organization purchased and fully manages through its device platform, and each signs a policy that expressly permits personal photos, apps, and calls on the device. Which of the following deployment models is in use?","options":["COPE","CYOD","BYOD","MDM"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"COPE. Corporate-owned, personally enabled: the organization buys and fully manages the phones while formally sanctioning personal use. CYOD loses because the executives are issued devices, not choosing from an approved catalog. The tell: COPE = company owns, personal use allowed, CYOD = user picks from the company-bought approved list, BYOD = the user owns the device, MDM = the platform managing any of them."},
  {"id":"q572","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"Staff will enroll personal phones for work email only if the company can never view or erase their personal photos and messages. Which of the following MDM configurations BEST satisfies the staff and the security team?","options":["Full-device remote wipe whenever a phone is reported lost","A work container with selective wipe of corporate data","Blocking installation of all non-approved personal applications","Continuous location tracking of every enrolled device"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A work container with selective wipe. Containerization walls corporate mail and files off from personal content, so the company can erase only its own data; a full-device wipe is exactly the personal-data destruction employees refused to accept. The tell: selective wipe removes the corporate container alone, full wipe factory-resets the whole personal phone, and app blocking or tracking control the device rather than separating the data."},
  {"id":"q573","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-deployment","question":"An organization is ending its employee-owned phone program because support costs are unsustainable, but employees insist on keeping a choice of handset. Which of the following deployment models BEST meets both goals?","options":["CYOD","COPE","BYOD","MDM"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CYOD. Employees pick from a short company-purchased, supported list, preserving choice while the help desk supports only approved models; COPE keeps company ownership but the company picks the device, dropping the choice employees demanded. The tell: BYOD = employee-owned, COPE = company-owned and company-chosen with personal use allowed, CYOD = employee choice from a company-owned approved list, and MDM is the management tool, not a deployment model."},
  {"id":"q574","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"A developer fixes a web form so every submission is checked first: entries containing SQL keywords or script tags are rejected before the application ever processes them. Which of the following secure coding practices is this?","options":["Input validation","Secure cookies","Code signing","Sandboxing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Input validation. Checking and rejecting untrusted input before processing is the front-line defense against injection and XSS. The tell: input validation = vet what comes in, code signing = prove where software came from, sandboxing = run code in a contained space, secure cookies = protect session tokens in transit."},
  {"id":"q575","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"A vendor attaches a cryptographic signature to each installer it ships, letting customers verify the package really came from the vendor and was not modified since signing. Which of the following practices is this?","options":["Sandboxing","Static code analysis","Code signing","Input validation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Code signing. Signing binaries proves origin and integrity, the defense that makes malicious-update attacks detectable. The tell: code signing = authenticity and integrity of shipped software, static analysis = examining source for flaws, sandboxing = contained execution, input validation = filtering untrusted input."},
  {"id":"q576","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"A SOC policy requires suspicious email attachments to be opened only inside a disposable, isolated virtual environment, so anything malicious detonates where it can reach nothing real. Which of the following practices is this?","options":["Static code analysis","Input validation","Secure cookies","Sandboxing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Sandboxing. Running untrusted content in a contained, throwaway environment keeps any malice away from production. The tell: sandboxing = isolated execution space, static analysis = reading code without running it, input validation = filtering user input, secure cookies = protecting session tokens."},
  {"id":"q577","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"A developer sets the HttpOnly and Secure flags on the application's session tokens, so scripts cannot read them and browsers refuse to send them over cleartext connections. Which of the following application security practices is this?","options":["Secure cookies","Code signing","Sandboxing","Static code analysis"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Secure cookies. HttpOnly and Secure flags harden session cookies against script theft (XSS) and cleartext exposure. The tell: secure cookies = protecting session tokens with flags, code signing = proving software origin, sandboxing = contained execution, static analysis = code review by tool."},
  {"id":"q578","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"A web team receives two findings in the same week: the login form is bypassed with the input ' OR 1=1 and the comment field stores script tags that later run in visitors' browsers. Which of the following practices remediates BOTH findings?","options":["Code signing certificates","Secure cookie attributes","Server-side input validation","Application sandboxing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Server-side input validation. Rejecting or sanitizing untrusted input before processing defeats both SQL injection and cross-site scripting, the two findings described; secure cookies protect the session token but stop neither injection. The tell: input validation stops injected input at the door, secure cookies protect tokens in transit, code signing proves who shipped a binary, and sandboxing contains untrusted code you choose to run."},
  {"id":"q579","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"An analyst reviewing a capture of the employee portal sees the following sent over TCP port 80: GET /account HTTP/1.1 with Cookie: session=9d41acb2. Which of the following changes BEST protects the session?","options":["Set the Secure attribute on the session cookie","Set the HttpOnly attribute on the session cookie","Add server-side input validation to the portal forms","Sign the portal's application code before each release"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Secure attribute with HTTPS enforced. The capture shows the session cookie traveling in cleartext on port 80, and Secure makes browsers send it only over encrypted connections; HttpOnly is tempting but stops script access to the cookie, not cleartext transmission. The tell: Secure = transport protection against capture on the wire, HttpOnly = protection from script theft, and this artifact shows a wire exposure, not a script problem."},
  {"id":"q580","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"Field technicians download a diagnostic utility from a vendor portal and must confirm before installing that each copy truly came from the vendor and was not altered in transit. Which of the following provides this assurance?","options":["Running the utility inside an isolated sandbox first","Performing static analysis on the utility","Validating input before the utility processes it","Verifying the code signature on the download"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Code signing verification. A valid signature made with the vendor's private key proves origin and that the file is unmodified since signing; a sandbox would reveal bad behavior but cannot prove who published the file or whether it is authentic. The tell: code signing = verified origin plus integrity, sandboxing = safe observation of behavior, static analysis = examining code without running it, input validation = filtering untrusted user input."},
  {"id":"q581","domain":"D4","family":"app-security","question":"During an investigation, an analyst obtains a suspicious installer and needs to watch exactly what it does when executed, with no chance of it reaching production systems. Which of the following should the analyst use?","options":["A honeypot","A sandbox","Static code analysis","File quarantine"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A sandbox. Detonating the sample in an isolated environment lets the analyst observe its runtime behavior while it can touch nothing real; a honeypot is tempting but is a decoy that lures attackers in, not a place to test your own suspicious file. The tell: sandbox = you run untrusted code safely, honeypot = bait for attackers, static analysis = never executes the code, quarantine = locks the file away without observing it."},
  {"id":"q582","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"A security tool sweeps the network ranges every night, discovering every connected device - including a dozen that appear on no official list. Which of the following asset-management activities is the tool performing?","options":["Inventory","Enumeration","Acquisition","Classification"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Enumeration. Actively discovering what is really on the network, including unknown devices, is enumeration; the inventory is the maintained record it feeds. The tell: enumeration = active discovery of what exists, inventory = the kept list, acquisition = the purchasing process, classification = labeling by sensitivity."},
  {"id":"q583","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"An administrator tags each server according to the sensitivity of the data it stores - public, internal, confidential - so the correct handling and protection rules apply to each. Which of the following asset-management activities is this?","options":["Classification","Assignment","Enumeration","Ownership"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Classification. Labeling assets by data sensitivity drives how each must be protected and handled. The tell: classification = sensitivity labels, assignment/ownership = naming who is accountable for the asset, enumeration = discovering what exists."},
  {"id":"q584","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"An auditor asks for the maintained master record of every hardware and software asset the organization owns, with owner and location for each. Which of the following asset-management elements is being requested?","options":["Enumeration","Acquisition","Assignment","Inventory"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Inventory. The maintained master list of assets is the inventory; enumeration is the discovery activity that keeps it honest. The tell: inventory = the kept record, enumeration = actively finding what exists, acquisition = the buying process, assignment = naming who owns each asset."},
  {"id":"q585","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"Purchasing completes the order for a new database server and the machine formally enters the company's possession, kicking off its lifecycle records. Which of the following asset lifecycle stages is this?","options":["Assignment","Ownership","Acquisition","Enumeration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Acquisition. Procuring the asset and taking possession is acquisition, the lifecycle's first stage. The tell: acquisition = obtaining the asset, assignment/ownership = designating who is accountable for it, enumeration = discovering assets already on the network."},
  {"id":"q586","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"A marketing team subscribes to a cloud file-sharing service on a corporate credit card, and the security team first learns of it when customer data is found there. Which of the following asset management processes MOST likely failed?","options":["Enumeration","Disposal and decommissioning","Acquisition and procurement","Assignment and accounting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Acquisition/procurement. Security review belongs in the purchasing process, and bypassing it with a credit card produced unvetted shadow IT; assignment and accounting would fail later in the lifecycle, but this asset never entered the lifecycle at all. The tell: acquisition = security vetting before purchase, assignment = naming an owner after purchase, enumeration = actively discovering what is on the network, disposal = securely retiring the asset."},
  {"id":"q587","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"During an access review of an aging file server used by several departments, nobody can say who is responsible for approving or removing user access. Which of the following asset management elements is missing?","options":["Classification","An assigned owner","An enumeration scan","A sanitization procedure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An assigned owner. Assignment and accounting give every asset a named, accountable person who approves access and lifecycle decisions, which is exactly the void described; classification is tempting but only labels the data's sensitivity and names no decision-maker. The tell: ownership = who is accountable, classification = how sensitive, enumeration = discovering what exists, sanitization = wiping media at disposal."},
  {"id":"q588","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"After an incident involving an unmanaged host, a security manager wants to actively discover every device currently connected to the network and reconcile the results against the asset database. Which of the following activities accomplishes this?","options":["Inventory","Classification","Procurement","Enumeration"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Enumeration. Active discovery scanning identifies what is really on the network so unknown or rogue devices can be reconciled against the records; inventory is tempting but is the maintained list itself, the very record the manager suspects is incomplete. The tell: inventory = the record you keep, enumeration = the active discovery that tests the record against reality."},
  {"id":"q589","domain":"D4","family":"asset-management","question":"Attackers compromise an internet-facing server that IT staff deployed two years earlier but never added to the asset records, so it was skipped by patching and monitoring. Which of the following practices failed?","options":["Inventory","Enumeration","Certification","Data retention"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Inventory. The organization cannot patch or monitor an asset missing from its authoritative record, and this server was known at deployment yet never recorded; enumeration is the discovery scan that could later catch the gap, but the practice that broke was record-keeping. The tell: inventory = the record of what you own, enumeration = actively discovering what is out there; a machine your own staff deployed and lost track of is a record failure."},
  {"id":"q590","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"Laptops rotating out of the executive team will be reissued to another department, so IT wipes the drives with a verified erasure tool while keeping the hardware in service. Which of the following disposal steps is this?","options":["Destruction","Sanitization","Certification","Data retention"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Sanitization. Removing all data while the asset survives for reuse is sanitization; destruction would end the hardware's life too. The tell: sanitization = data gone, hardware reusable, destruction = the asset is physically destroyed, certification = the signed proof of disposal, data retention = how long data must be kept."},
  {"id":"q591","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"Failed drives that once held classified material are fed through an industrial shredder, reducing them to fragments no lab could ever recover data from. Which of the following disposal methods is this?","options":["Sanitization","Certification","Destruction","Data retention"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Destruction. Physically destroying the media guarantees the data dies with it, the standard for the most sensitive material. The tell: destruction = the media is physically gone, sanitization = wiped but intact for reuse, certification = the paperwork proving it happened, data retention = the keep-it policy."},
  {"id":"q592","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"After destroying a batch of retired drives, the disposal vendor hands over a signed document listing every serial number and attesting each was destroyed. Which of the following describes this document?","options":["Certification","Sanitization","Destruction","Data retention"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Certification. The signed attestation of what was destroyed, and when, is the certificate of destruction that auditors ask to see. The tell: certification = the proof document, destruction = the act itself, sanitization = wiping for reuse, data retention = the policy on keeping data."},
  {"id":"q593","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"A regulation obliges the company to keep financial records for seven full years before they may be disposed of, and legal holds can extend that. Which of the following concepts dictates how long the data must be kept?","options":["Sanitization","Data retention","Destruction","Certification"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data retention. Retention rules define how long data must be preserved before disposal is even permitted. The tell: data retention = the mandatory keep period, sanitization = wiping for reuse, destruction = physically eliminating media, certification = the signed proof of disposal."},
  {"id":"q594","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"At contract end, a colocation provider degausses every tenant drive and then pulverizes the platters, guaranteeing nothing is recoverable by any means. Which of the following disposal methods is this?","options":["Certification","Sanitization","Destruction","Data retention"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Destruction. Degaussing plus physical pulverizing ends the media's existence along with its data. The tell: destruction = the media is physically gone, sanitization = data removed, media survives, certification = the attestation document, data retention = the keep-it-first rule."},
  {"id":"q595","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"An asset-disposal standard lists sanitization as one approved method. Which of the following BEST describes sanitization?","options":["Physically shredding or degaussing media so it is gone","Removing data so the media can be safely reused","A signed document proving data was destroyed","The required period data must be kept before disposal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Sanitization = wipe and reuse. Data is verifiably removed while the hardware lives on for redeployment or resale. The tell: sanitization = data gone, media survives, destruction = media gone too, certification = the paperwork, retention = the schedule."},
  {"id":"q596","domain":"D4","family":"asset-disposal","question":"A compliance auditor asks for proof that hard drives retired last year and collected by a disposal vendor were actually destroyed. Which of the following should the organization produce?","options":["The corporate data retention schedule","The vendor's service level agreement","Certificates of destruction from the vendor","Chain of custody forms from past investigations"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Certificates of destruction. Certification is the documented proof that sanitization or destruction actually occurred, naming each serial number, the method, and the date; the service level agreement is tempting but promises the service without proving anything about any specific drive. The tell: certification = proof disposal happened, retention = how long data must be kept first, chain of custody = evidence handling for legal proceedings, not disposal."},
  {"id":"q597","domain":"D4","family":"analysis-types","question":"A pipeline tool inspects an application's source code for insecure functions, hardcoded secrets, and injection flaws - all without ever executing the program. Which of the following analysis types is this?","options":["Package monitoring","Sandboxing","Dynamic analysis","Static analysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Static analysis. Examining code without running it is static analysis (SAST), typically wired into the build pipeline. The tell: static = read the code, never run it, dynamic = observe it while running, package monitoring = watch dependencies for new CVEs, sandboxing = the contained place you run things."},
  {"id":"q598","domain":"D4","family":"analysis-types","question":"Malware analysts detonate a suspicious binary inside an instrumented, isolated environment, recording every file write, registry change, and network call it makes as it runs. Which of the following analysis types is this?","options":["Package monitoring","Code signing","Static analysis","Dynamic analysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Dynamic analysis. Observing behavior during execution is dynamic analysis; the instrumented sandbox is where it safely happens. The tell: dynamic = run it and watch, static = examine without running, package monitoring = dependency vulnerability alerts, code signing = proving software origin."},
  {"id":"q599","domain":"D4","family":"analysis-types","question":"A DevOps team subscribes to a service that pages them whenever any open-source library in their build acquires a newly disclosed vulnerability, so they can bump versions fast. Which of the following activities is this?","options":["Package monitoring","Static analysis","Dynamic analysis","Input validation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Package monitoring. Watching third-party dependencies for new vulnerabilities is package monitoring, the guard against inheriting someone else's flaw. The tell: package monitoring = track dependency CVEs, static analysis = scan your own code, dynamic analysis = observe running behavior, input validation = filter untrusted input."},
  {"id":"q600","domain":"D4","family":"analysis-types","question":"A security lead explains the difference between the two main testing approaches. Static analysis differs from dynamic analysis in that static analysis does which of the following?","options":["Examines the code or binary without executing it","Runs the program and observes its runtime behavior","Watches third-party dependencies for disclosed vulnerabilities","Feeds malformed inputs to a live running process"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Static = no execution. It reads source or binaries for flaws; anything involving running the program is dynamic. The tell: static = examine at rest, dynamic = observe while running, package monitoring = watch the dependencies, fuzzing = a dynamic technique using malformed input."},
  {"id":"q601","domain":"D4","family":"analysis-types","question":"On every commit, the CI server launches the freshly built application, bombards it with malformed inputs, and watches for crashes, hangs, and unexpected system calls. Which of the following analysis types is running?","options":["Static analysis","Dynamic analysis","Package monitoring","Reverse engineering"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dynamic analysis. The application is executing and being observed - fuzzing malformed inputs at a running build is dynamic testing. The tell: dynamic = the program runs, static = it never runs, package monitoring = dependency CVE alerts, reverse engineering = deconstructing a binary's logic."},
  {"id":"q602","domain":"D4","family":"analysis-types","question":"After a supply chain incident, a development team generates a software bill of materials listing every third-party component in its product and wants ongoing alerts whenever a listed component gains a newly published CVE. Which of the following activities is this?","options":["Static analysis","Dynamic analysis","Fuzzing","Package monitoring"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Package monitoring. Alerting on newly disclosed vulnerabilities in third-party components covers the inherited code the team did not write; the SBOM only lists what to watch. Static analysis loses because it examines the team's own code, not advisories about dependencies. The tell: package monitoring = watch inherited components for known vulns, static analysis = examine code without executing it, dynamic analysis = run it and observe, fuzzing = malformed-input testing of a running build."},
  {"id":"q603","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"A threat analyst builds a daily briefing entirely from free public sources: vendor blogs, security researchers on social media, and government advisories. Which of the following intelligence source types is this?","options":["Proprietary threat feed","Information-sharing organization","Dark web monitoring","OSINT"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"OSINT. Open-source intelligence is anything gathered from freely available public sources. The tell: OSINT = free public sources, proprietary feed = paid vendor intelligence, information-sharing organization = a formal peer community (ISAC), dark web monitoring = watching criminal forums and markets."},
  {"id":"q604","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"Hospitals across a region exchange fresh indicators of compromise through a formal healthcare industry community, warning each other about campaigns targeting their sector. Which of the following threat-intelligence sources is this?","options":["Dark web monitoring","OSINT","Information-sharing organization","Proprietary threat feed"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Information-sharing organization. Sector-based communities (ISACs/ISAOs) formally exchanging indicators among peers are sharing organizations. The tell: information-sharing organization = formal industry peer exchange, OSINT = public sources, proprietary feed = purchased intel, dark web monitoring = watching criminal marketplaces."},
  {"id":"q605","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"A bank pays a security vendor for a curated subscription feed of indicators, malware analysis, and actor profiles that is unavailable to the public. Which of the following threat-intelligence sources is this?","options":["OSINT","Information-sharing organization","Dark web","Proprietary third-party feed"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Proprietary third-party feed. Paid, curated, non-public intelligence from a vendor is the proprietary feed. The tell: proprietary feed = purchased and curated, OSINT = free public sources, information-sharing organization = peer exchange in a sector community, dark web = watching criminal venues directly."},
  {"id":"q606","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"An intel team maintains personas on hidden criminal marketplaces and closed forums, watching for the company's customer credentials being offered for sale. Which of the following threat-intelligence sources is this?","options":["Dark web","OSINT","Information-sharing organization","Proprietary third-party feed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Dark web. Monitoring criminal marketplaces and hidden forums for your own stolen data is dark web intelligence. The tell: dark web = watching the criminal underground, OSINT = open public sources, information-sharing organization = peer community exchange, proprietary feed = vendor subscription."},
  {"id":"q607","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"A regional credit union wants early warning of fraud campaigns already hitting peer financial institutions, with indicators exchanged member to member under formal sharing agreements. Which of the following threat intelligence sources BEST fits this need?","options":["Open-source intelligence","An information-sharing organization","Dark web monitoring","A proprietary third-party feed"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An information-sharing organization. Sector bodies such as ISACs let industry peers exchange indicators under membership agreements, giving exactly the peer-to-peer early warning described; a proprietary feed is tempting but is purchased from one vendor rather than exchanged among peers. The tell: information-sharing organization = peers trading intel within a sector, proprietary = paid vendor product, OSINT = free public sources, dark web = watching criminal marketplaces."},
  {"id":"q608","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"A SOC finds free public advisories too noisy and too slow, and leadership approves budget for a curated subscription that includes vendor analyst context and confidence ratings. Which of the following source types is being acquired?","options":["Open-source intelligence","An information-sharing organization","Dark web monitoring","A proprietary third-party feed"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A proprietary third-party feed. Paid vendor intelligence trades money for curation, context, and fidelity beyond what free sources offer, which is the exact upgrade described; an information-sharing organization involves peer exchange under agreements, not a purchased vendor product. The tell: proprietary = commercial curated feed you pay for, OSINT = the free public sources being outgrown, information-sharing organization = sector peers, dark web = hidden marketplace monitoring."},
  {"id":"q609","domain":"D4","family":"threat-intel","question":"After a competitor's breach, executives ask how the company would find out if its own employees' credentials were being offered for sale to criminals. Which of the following intelligence sources closes this gap?","options":["Dark web monitoring","Open-source intelligence","A proprietary third-party feed","An information-sharing organization"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Dark web monitoring. Watching hidden marketplaces and closed criminal forums reveals stolen company credentials and data offered for sale before they are used in an attack; OSINT is tempting but covers openly published sources, which criminal markets are not. The tell: dark web = criminal marketplaces and hidden forums, OSINT = the free public web, proprietary = paid vendor curation, information-sharing organization = sector peer exchange."},
  {"id":"q610","domain":"D4","family":"security-assessments","question":"A company launches a public program paying external researchers a reward for each valid vulnerability they responsibly disclose through the program's rules. Which of the following assessment approaches is this?","options":["Penetration testing","Bug bounty","Process audit","System audit"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Bug bounty. Crowdsourced, pay-per-finding vulnerability discovery under disclosure rules is a bug bounty, ongoing rather than a scoped engagement. The tell: bug bounty = open crowd, paid per valid bug, penetration test = a hired team on a defined scope and timeframe, audits = reviews against requirements, not exploitation."},
  {"id":"q611","domain":"D4","family":"security-assessments","question":"An organization signs a rules-of-engagement document and hires an authorized team to actively exploit its systems for three weeks, proving which weaknesses are actually reachable and damaging. Which of the following assessments is this?","options":["System audit","Bug bounty","Penetration testing","Process audit"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Penetration testing. A scoped, authorized engagement that exploits weaknesses to demonstrate real impact is a pen test. The tell: penetration test = authorized active exploitation on a defined scope, bug bounty = open-ended crowd program, system/process audit = checking conformance to requirements without attacking."},
  {"id":"q612","domain":"D4","family":"security-assessments","question":"An assessor spends a week comparing the company's access-control procedures and change tickets against a required framework, confirming the documented controls are actually followed in practice. Which of the following assessments is this?","options":["Penetration testing","Bug bounty","System and process audit","Responsible disclosure"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"System and process audit. Verifying conformance of systems and procedures against requirements, via records and interviews rather than attacks, is an audit. The tell: audit = check against requirements, penetration test = actively exploit, bug bounty = crowd-paid findings, responsible disclosure = the reporting etiquette."},
  {"id":"q613","domain":"D4","family":"security-assessments","question":"A security manager explains two assessment budget line items to finance. Which of the following BEST distinguishes a penetration test from a bug bounty program?","options":["A pen test pays researchers per valid finding; a bug bounty is one fixed engagement by a hired team","A pen test runs continuously throughout the year; a bug bounty is limited to a short fixed window","A pen test is a scoped engagement by a hired team; a bug bounty pays any researcher per valid finding","A pen test only reviews documentation and source code; a bug bounty actively exploits production systems"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Penetration test = a bounded, authorized engagement by a specific hired team under rules of engagement, while a bug bounty is a standing invitation paying any external researcher per valid finding. The swapped-payment option loses because it inverts the two models exactly. The tell: pen test = defined team, scope, and window, bug bounty = open crowd, anytime, paid per valid disclosure."},
  {"id":"q614","domain":"D4","family":"security-assessments","question":"A security manager wants the most accurate report of missing patches on the server fleet with the fewest false positives, so the tool must read installed software versions from inside each host. Which of the following scan types should be scheduled?","options":["A credentialed scan","A non-credentialed scan","A penetration test","A system and process audit"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A credentialed scan. Logging in lets the scanner read installed versions and local settings directly, producing the most accurate patch picture with the fewest false positives; a penetration test loses because it proves exploitability rather than enumerating missing patches. The tell: credentialed = authenticated inside view, accurate; non-credentialed = the outside attacker's view; penetration test = actively exploits; audit = reviews process and controls, not patch levels."},
  {"id":"q615","domain":"D4","family":"security-assessments","question":"Before an external review, a security analyst must document exactly which weaknesses on a public web server are visible to an unauthenticated attacker on the internet, without exploiting any of them. Which of the following methods provides this view?","options":["A credentialed vulnerability scan","A penetration test","A system and process audit","A non-credentialed vulnerability scan"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A non-credentialed vulnerability scan. Scanning without any login shows exactly what an unauthenticated outsider can see, and nothing gets exploited; a penetration test loses because the stem forbids exploitation. The tell: non-credentialed = the attacker's outside view; credentialed = authenticated inside view with local detail; penetration test = exploits to prove impact; audit = reviews procedures and controls."},
  {"id":"q616","domain":"D4","family":"false-pos-neg","question":"An IDS raises a data-exfiltration alert overnight. Investigation shows the traffic was a routine, authorized offsite backup job. The alert fired, but nothing malicious happened. Which of the following describes this outcome?","options":["False negative","False positive","True positive","True negative"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"False positive. The system alerted on benign activity: alarm without a real event. Noisy but visible. The tell: false positive = alert, no real attack, false negative = attack, no alert, true positive = alert on a real attack, true negative = silence on benign activity."},
  {"id":"q617","domain":"D4","family":"false-pos-neg","question":"Post-incident review reveals malware ran on a host for six weeks while the antivirus stayed completely silent about it the entire time. Which of the following does this missed detection represent?","options":["False positive","True negative","False negative","True positive"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"False negative. Real malicious activity that produced no alert is the false negative, the most dangerous outcome because nobody knows to respond. The tell: false negative = missed real attack, false positive = false alarm, true positive = caught real attack, true negative = correctly quiet."},
  {"id":"q618","domain":"D4","family":"false-pos-neg","question":"Drowning in noisy alerts, an analyst dials down the sensitivity of the detection rules so they fire far less often. Which of the following outcomes does this over-tuning MOST likely increase?","options":["True positives","False positives","True negatives","False negatives"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"False negatives. Lowering sensitivity trades noise for blindness: fewer false alarms, but real attacks start slipping through unalerted. The tell: sensitivity down = false positives drop, false negatives rise; sensitivity up = the reverse. Tuning is choosing which error you can live with."},
  {"id":"q619","domain":"D4","family":"false-pos-neg","question":"An EDR platform quarantines a digitally signed remote administration tool the IT department deploys to every desktop, flagging it as malware. Investigation confirms the tool is legitimate and approved. Which of the following describes this outcome?","options":["True positive","False negative","False positive","True negative"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"False positive. The alert fired on approved, benign software: a false alarm that costs analyst time but reflects no real threat. True positive loses because the flagged tool was not actually malicious. The tell: false positive = alert without a threat, false negative = threat without an alert, true positive = alert on a real threat, true negative = correct silence."},
  {"id":"q620","domain":"D4","family":"false-pos-neg","question":"A vulnerability scan declares a server clean, but the server actually carries an unpatched, exploitable flaw the scanner simply failed to find. Which of the following BEST describes this result?","options":["True negative","False negative","False positive","True positive"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"False negative. The scan said safe when it was not - a real weakness went unreported. The tell: false negative = missed real problem, true negative = correctly reported clean, false positive = flagged a non-issue, true positive = correctly found a real issue."},
  {"id":"q621","domain":"D4","family":"false-pos-neg","question":"A detection engineering course argues the false negative is more dangerous than the false positive. Which of the following BEST explains why?","options":["A real threat went undetected and unaddressed","An analyst wasted time investigating a harmless alert","A benign event was correctly ignored","A genuine threat was correctly blocked"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A real threat slipped through silently. False positives waste time; false negatives let attacks proceed with no one aware a response is needed. The tell: false positive = costs attention, false negative = costs the breach nobody saw."},
  {"id":"q622","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A vulnerability advisory references one specific, publicly known flaw by its unique catalog identifier so every vendor and tool can point at the same issue. Which of the following elements is the identifier?","options":["CVSS","CVE","Exposure factor","Vulnerability classification"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CVE. Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures entries are unique catalog IDs naming one specific public vulnerability. The tell: CVE = the name/ID of the flaw, CVSS = the 0-10 severity score, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost, vulnerability classification = grouping findings by type."},
  {"id":"q623","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A remediation dashboard sorts findings by a standardized number from 0 to 10 expressing each vulnerability's severity. Which of the following elements supplies that number?","options":["CVSS","CVE","Environmental variables","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CVSS. The Common Vulnerability Scoring System produces the 0-10 severity rating used for prioritization. The tell: CVSS = the score, CVE = the identifier, environmental variables = your customization of the score, risk tolerance = how much deviation you will absorb before acting."},
  {"id":"q624","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"In a war-room briefing an analyst says, \"the flaw is tracked as CVE-2021-44228.\" Which of the following elements is that string functioning as?","options":["CVE","CVSS","Exposure factor","Environmental variables"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CVE. The string is a catalog identifier naming exactly one public vulnerability (Log4Shell); it says nothing about severity. The tell: CVE = the ID being cited, CVSS = the severity number that would accompany it, exposure factor = loss percentage, environmental variables = score adjustments."},
  {"id":"q625","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A vendor advisory lists a new flaw as \"CVE-2024-21412, scored 9.8.\" A patch manager must justify pulling this finding ahead of older tickets based on severity alone. Which of the following elements provides that justification?","options":["CVE","CVSS","Exposure factor","Vulnerability classification"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CVSS. The 9.8 is a Common Vulnerability Scoring System rating, the standardized 0-10 severity number remediation priority runs on; the CVE string beside it is tempting but only names the flaw and carries no severity. The tell: CVSS = the score you prioritize by, CVE = the identifier you cite, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost, vulnerability classification = grouping findings by type."},
  {"id":"q626","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"Building a quantitative model, a risk analyst estimates a successful ransomware hit would wipe out 60 percent of a server's asset value. Which of the following terms describes that 60 percent figure?","options":["Exposure factor","Environmental variables","CVSS","Vulnerability classification"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Exposure factor. The percentage of an asset's value lost in one incident is the EF, which multiplies asset value to give SLE. The tell: exposure factor = percent of value lost, CVSS = vulnerability severity, environmental variables = CVSS customization, vulnerability classification = grouping by type."},
  {"id":"q627","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"Two companies score the identical vulnerability differently: one adjusted the base metrics to reflect that the affected system is internet-facing and business-critical in ITS network. Which of the following CVSS elements permits this adjustment?","options":["Exposure factor","CVE","Risk tolerance","Environmental variables"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Environmental variables. The environmental metric group tailors the base score to your own deployment's exposure and asset criticality, so the same flaw can rate differently in different shops. The tell: environmental variables = your context adjusting the score, CVSS base = the universal number, CVE = the identifier, exposure factor = loss percentage in risk math."},
  {"id":"q628","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A vulnerability manager groups the quarter's findings by type - injection, misconfiguration, missing patch - and by severity band before assigning them to remediation teams. Which of the following terms describes this grouping?","options":["CVE","CVSS","Vulnerability classification","Exposure factor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Vulnerability classification. Sorting findings into categories by type and severity for handling is classification. The tell: vulnerability classification = grouping the findings, CVE = one flaw's identifier, CVSS = one flaw's score, exposure factor = the loss percentage."},
  {"id":"q629","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A junior analyst asks for the \"CVE score\" of a new finding. The senior corrects the phrasing: the CVE entry itself carries no severity number - that comes from a different element entirely. Which of the following elements actually supplies the severity number?","options":["CVE","CVSS","Exposure factor","Environmental variables"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CVSS. The CVE names the flaw; the CVSS scores it. Conflating them is the classic twin-term trap this family exists to kill. The tell: CVE = identity, CVSS = severity. An ID cannot be critical; a score cannot be exploited."},
  {"id":"q630","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"Two scanners report the same flaw under their own product-specific names, confusing the ticket queue. Which of the following lets the team normalize both reports to a single reference?","options":["CVSS","Vulnerability classification","Exposure factor","CVE"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"CVE. The shared catalog identifier collapses vendor-specific finding names into one reference every tool and ticket can agree on; vulnerability classification is tempting but grouping by type never names an individual flaw. The tell: CVE = one flaw's universal identifier, CVSS = its severity score, vulnerability classification = local grouping by type, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost."},
  {"id":"q631","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A bank normally allows 30 days to fix medium-severity findings, but a new flaw on its payment platform pushes the risk further from that baseline than the bank is prepared to absorb, so it patches immediately. Which of the following describes the amount of deviation an organization will absorb on a specific risk before acting?","options":["CVSS","Exposure factor","Environmental variables","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Risk tolerance. Tolerance is the acceptable deviation around the norm for a specific risk; exceed it and action triggers regardless of standard timelines. The tell: risk tolerance = how far past the line you will let one risk drift, CVSS = the flaw's severity, environmental variables = score tailoring, exposure factor = loss percentage."},
  {"id":"q632","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"In a vulnerability management program, which of the following BEST describes what a CVE entry actually functions as?","options":["A 0 to 10 score expressing how severe the flaw is","The unique vendor-neutral identifier naming one specific known vulnerability","The percentage of an asset's value lost when the flaw is exploited","The set of local modifiers that adjust a published score for one environment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"CVE = the identifier. It names exactly one publicly known vulnerability and carries no severity of its own. The tell: CVE = identity, CVSS = the 0-10 severity, exposure factor = the loss percentage, environmental variables = the local score modifiers."},
  {"id":"q633","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A scoring framework combines base, temporal, and environmental metric groups into one number that expresses how severe a vulnerability is. Which of the following is this framework?","options":["Exposure factor","Vulnerability classification","Risk tolerance","CVSS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"CVSS. Base, temporal, and environmental metric groups are the CVSS structure, producing the 0-10 severity score. The tell: CVSS = the scoring framework, exposure factor = risk-math loss percentage, vulnerability classification = grouping findings, risk tolerance = acceptable deviation before acting."},
  {"id":"q634","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A finding in a scan report carries a CVSS rating. Which of the following BEST describes that rating?","options":["A 0 to 10 numeric severity score for the vulnerability","A catalog identifier that uniquely names the vulnerability","A grouping of findings by weakness type and severity band","The share of asset value expected to be lost in one event"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CVSS = the 0-10 severity score. It quantifies how bad the flaw is, not what it is called. The tell: CVSS = severity number, CVE = the name, vulnerability classification = the grouping, exposure factor = the loss share in risk calculations."},
  {"id":"q635","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"In quantitative risk analysis, which of the following terms names the percentage of an asset's value that is lost when a particular threat is actually realized?","options":["Environmental variables","Exposure factor","CVSS","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Exposure factor. The EF is the loss percentage per event, multiplied by asset value to yield single loss expectancy. The tell: exposure factor = percent of value lost, CVSS = vulnerability severity, environmental variables = CVSS tailoring, risk tolerance = acceptable deviation."},
  {"id":"q636","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A team raises a flaw's score above the vendor's published base value because the vulnerable database in THEIR deployment stores regulated health records, raising the confidentiality requirement. Which of the following CVSS elements reflects such local security requirements?","options":["CVE","Vulnerability classification","Environmental variables","Exposure factor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Environmental variables. The environmental metrics adjust the base score to one organization's deployment - asset criticality and security requirements included. The tell: environmental variables = your context reshaping the score, base CVSS = the universal published value, CVE = the name, exposure factor = risk-math loss percentage."},
  {"id":"q637","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"Before publicly disclosing a newly discovered flaw, a researcher asks the numbering authority to reserve a unique identifier in the public catalog so all future advisories reference the same entry. Which of the following elements is being reserved?","options":["CVE","CVSS","Exposure factor","Environmental variables"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"CVE. Researchers reserve a CVE ID pre-disclosure so the flaw has one public name from day one; no score exists yet. The tell: CVE = the reservable identifier, CVSS = the score assigned when analyzed, exposure factor and environmental variables = risk and scoring adjustments, not names."},
  {"id":"q638","domain":"D4","family":"cve-cvss","question":"A hospital's scanner reports the same CVSS 8.1 flaw on the patient-facing scheduling system and on an isolated development box, and the team remediates the scheduling system first. Which of the following prioritization factors BEST justifies the order?","options":["The CVE identifier assigned to the flaw","The CVSS base score","Industry and organizational impact","The vulnerability classification"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Industry and organizational impact. With identical severity scores, what the affected system does for the business - patient-facing hospital care versus an isolated dev box - is the prioritization input that breaks the tie; the CVSS base score loses because the stem states it is the same on both hosts. The tell: CVE = the identifier; CVSS = the severity number; industry and organizational impact = business context that reorders equal scores; classification = grouping findings by type."},
  {"id":"q639","domain":"D4","family":"vuln-response","question":"A legacy application server runs software its vendor stopped patching, and the business cannot retire it this year. Unable to fix the flaws, the team wraps the server in strict network access rules and enhanced monitoring as alternate protection. Which of the following BEST describes this response?","options":["Patching","Compensating control","Exception","Insurance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Compensating control. When the primary fix (patching) is impossible, alternate safeguards that reduce the same risk are compensating controls. An exception would be formally accepting the risk without adding protection. The tell: compensating control = substitute protection, exception = documented acceptance, patching = the unavailable primary fix, insurance = transferring financial impact."},
  {"id":"q640","domain":"D4","family":"vuln-response","question":"A security analyst marks a critical finding remediated after the patch deploys. Before closing the ticket, the analyst schedules a fresh automated assessment of the host to confirm the finding no longer appears. Which of the following validation steps is this?","options":["Audit","Penetration testing","Segmentation","Rescanning"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Rescanning. Re-running the automated assessment after remediation is the standard way to confirm the fix actually removed the finding. Penetration testing loses because it actively exploits to prove impact, not to verify that one patch took effect. The tell: rescanning = re-test to confirm the fix, audit = independent review of the process, penetration testing = exploit to demonstrate impact, segmentation = a mitigation, not a validation."},
  {"id":"q641","domain":"D4","family":"vuln-response","question":"A low-severity flaw sits in a rarely used system that would cost more to fix than it is worth. Management instead purchases a cyber insurance policy to absorb any financial loss if the flaw is ever exploited. Which of the following vulnerability responses is this?","options":["insurance","patching","segmentation","exception"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Insurance. Transferring the financial impact of the risk to an insurer is the insurance response, a form of risk transference. The tell: insurance = someone else pays if it happens, patching = eliminate the flaw, segmentation = contain the exposure, exception = formally accept it as-is."},
  {"id":"q642","domain":"D4","family":"vuln-response","question":"After a remediation ticket closes, an independent reviewer formally examines the system's controls, configurations, and procedures against documented requirements and issues a findings report. Which of the following validation methods does this describe?","options":["rescanning","audit","segmentation","patching"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Audit. A formal, independent human review of controls and procedures against documented requirements is an audit. Rescanning is tempting as the usual post-fix validation, but it is an automated scanner re-test, not a manual review against requirements. The tell: audit = independent review against requirements, rescanning = automated re-scan of the host, patching = the fix itself, segmentation = a containment response, not a validation method."},
  {"id":"q643","domain":"D4","family":"vuln-response","question":"Attackers are actively exploiting a critical flaw in a company's internet-facing VPN appliance, and the vendor released a fix this morning. Which of the following responses should the security team take FIRST?","options":["Purchase cyber insurance to transfer the financial risk","Apply the vendor fix through an emergency change window","Segment the appliance onto an isolated network","Deploy compensating controls until the next patch cycle"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Patching. A vendor fix exists and exploitation is active, so applying it through an emergency out-of-band change is the default response; compensating controls lose because they are the fallback for when the fix cannot be applied. The tell: patching = apply the available fix; segmentation and compensating controls = alternate protection when patching is not feasible; insurance = transfers financial risk and fixes nothing."},
  {"id":"q644","domain":"D4","family":"vuln-response","question":"A CISO asks the vulnerability management team for a monthly view of how many critical findings have stayed open longer than 30 days, plus the current register of approved exceptions. Which of the following is the team being asked to produce?","options":["Vulnerability reporting","Rescanning","Alerting","Vulnerability classification"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Vulnerability reporting. Aging metrics, trend views, and the exceptions register are the stakeholder-facing summaries that communicate remediation posture; alerting loses because it is a real-time push on a triggering condition, not a periodic rollup. The tell: reporting = periodic metrics and registers for stakeholders; rescanning = re-run the scan to confirm a fix; alerting = immediate notification when a condition fires; classification = grouping findings by type and severity."},
  {"id":"q645","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"A SOC overwhelmed by false positives adjusts SIEM rule thresholds and suppresses known-benign patterns, cutting noise while preserving true detections. Which of the following BEST describes this activity?","options":["Log aggregation","Alert tuning","Archiving","Quarantine"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Alert tuning. Adjusting rules and thresholds to reduce false positives without losing real detections is tuning, an ongoing SOC discipline. Log aggregation loses because it only collects logs centrally and does nothing about noisy rules. The tell: alert tuning = refine what fires, log aggregation = collect logs in one place, archiving = long-term retention, quarantine = isolating a suspicious item."},
  {"id":"q646","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"A platform continuously pulls log data from firewalls, domain controllers, servers, and endpoints into one central searchable repository where analysts run correlation queries. Which of the following functions does this describe?","options":["Log aggregation","Alerting","Reporting","Archiving"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Log aggregation. Centralizing logs from many sources into one repository is aggregation, the foundation a SIEM's correlation and alerting build on. The tell: log aggregation = gather it all in one place, alerting = fire on matches, reporting = summarize for humans, archiving = retain long term."},
  {"id":"q647","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"A two-year log retention requirement forces the team to move older log data into compressed long-term storage, where it remains retrievable for audits but no longer burdens the active analysis platform. Which of the following functions is this?","options":["archiving","log aggregation","alerting","quarantine"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Archiving. Long-term retention storage outside the hot analysis path is archiving, driven by compliance retention rules. The tell: archiving = long-term cold retention, log aggregation = central collection for analysis, alerting = firing on matches, quarantine = isolating suspicious items."},
  {"id":"q648","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"When an endpoint tool flags a downloaded file as suspicious, it immediately moves the file into an isolated store where it cannot execute, pending analyst review. Which of the following monitoring actions is this?","options":["alerting","scanning","quarantine","archiving"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Quarantine. Isolating a suspicious item so it cannot run while awaiting review is quarantine. The tell: quarantine = sealed off but preserved, alerting = telling humans, scanning = looking for problems, archiving = long-term storage of old data."},
  {"id":"q649","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"The moment a SIEM correlation rule matches - five failed logins followed by a success from a new country - the platform pages the on-call analyst automatically. Which of the following monitoring functions fired?","options":["archiving","log aggregation","alerting","quarantine"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Alerting. Automatically notifying humans when a detection rule matches is the alerting function - aggregation feeds it, tuning refines it. The tell: alerting = notify on match, log aggregation = collect centrally first, archiving = long-term storage, quarantine = isolate a suspicious item."},
  {"id":"q650","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"Each month the security team compiles dashboards summarizing incident counts, mean response times, and top attack categories for leadership review. Which of the following monitoring functions is this?","options":["reporting","alert tuning","scanning","log aggregation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reporting. Summarizing security activity into digestible metrics for stakeholders is reporting - the human-consumption layer above raw alerts. The tell: reporting = summaries for people, alerting = real-time notification, alert tuning = refining what fires, log aggregation = the underlying collection."},
  {"id":"q651","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"During a post-incident review, a SOC discovers an API abuse attack generated thousands of error entries in the web application's own logs but never registered in host or network telemetry. Which of the following monitoring layers was missing?","options":["Systems monitoring","Infrastructure monitoring","Application monitoring","Log archiving"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Application monitoring. The only evidence lived in the app's error and API logs, a layer the SOC was not watching; systems monitoring loses because host telemetry showed nothing to collect. The tell: systems = host and server events; applications = app errors, transactions, and API activity; infrastructure = network devices and links; archiving = long-term retention, not a visibility layer."},
  {"id":"q652","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"A monitoring engineer is assigning new telemetry feeds to the correct resource bucket: firewall interface state changes, switch health metrics, and WAN link utilization. Which of the following monitoring categories covers these feeds?","options":["Systems monitoring","Application monitoring","Infrastructure monitoring","User behavior monitoring"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Infrastructure monitoring. Firewall interfaces, switch health, and WAN links are the network devices and connectivity that make up infrastructure telemetry; systems monitoring loses because no host or server metrics appear in the list. The tell: infrastructure = network devices and links; systems = hosts and servers; applications = app-level errors and transactions; user behavior monitoring = per-user activity baselines."},
  {"id":"q653","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"A SOC already receives a monthly summary of authentication failures. Management now wants the on-call analyst notified within seconds whenever any service account records 10 failed logins in one minute. Which of the following activities must be configured?","options":["Reporting","Alerting","Scanning","Archiving"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Alerting. A near-real-time notification the moment a defined threshold is crossed is the alerting activity; reporting loses because it is the periodic summary the SOC already gets each month. The tell: alerting = event-driven push the instant a condition fires; reporting = scheduled rollup for review; scanning = proactive recurring checks; archiving = long-term retention."},
  {"id":"q654","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"Every Sunday night an automated job probes all servers for missing patches and configuration drift and feeds the findings into the security dashboard. Which of the following monitoring activities does the weekly job represent?","options":["Alerting","Archiving","Reporting","Scanning"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Scanning. Scheduled automated checks for weaknesses and drift are the scanning activity inside the monitoring lifecycle, feeding dashboards and alerts; reporting loses because the job gathers findings rather than summarizing them for stakeholders. The tell: scanning = recurring proactive checks that feed the pipeline; alerting = condition-triggered notification; reporting = periodic human-consumable summary; archiving = retention."},
  {"id":"q655","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"A junior analyst notices the monitoring platform pages the on-call engineer the instant a rule fires, yet also emails executives a quarterly package of incident trends and compliance status. Which of the following activities produces the quarterly package?","options":["Reporting","Alerting","Scanning","Log aggregation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reporting. The quarterly executive package is a periodic, human-consumable summary that supports decisions and audits; alerting loses because it is the instant page, the other half of the contrast in the stem. The tell: reporting = periodic pull for stakeholders; alerting = real-time push to responders; scanning = proactive checking; log aggregation = collecting logs into one place."},
  {"id":"q656","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"Investigators reconstruct a breach that began eight months ago only because the SIEM moves logs older than 90 days into low-cost long-term storage instead of deleting them. Which of the following monitoring activities made the investigation possible?","options":["Log aggregation","Archiving","Reporting","Alert tuning"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Archiving. Retaining aged logs in long-term storage preserved evidence far past the active analysis window, which is exactly what a months-later investigation needs; log aggregation loses because central collection alone says nothing about keeping data for eight months. The tell: archiving = long-term retention for later investigation and compliance; aggregation = collecting into one place now; reporting = summaries; alert tuning = cutting false positives."},
  {"id":"q657","domain":"D4","family":"monitoring","question":"EDR flags a workstation beaconing to a known command-and-control domain. The analyst must stop any spread immediately while keeping the malware available for later analysis. Which of the following actions should be taken FIRST?","options":["Reimage the workstation from a golden image","Delete the detected files from the disk","Quarantine the host from the network","Archive the host's logs to long-term storage"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Quarantine. Isolating the host cuts the beaconing and any spread immediately while preserving the malware and its artifacts for analysis; deleting the files loses because it destroys the very evidence the analyst must keep. The tell: quarantine = contain and preserve; deletion and reimaging = eradication steps that erase evidence; archiving = retention, not containment."},
  {"id":"q658","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"Investigating suspected exfiltration, an engineer needs records showing which internal hosts talked to which external addresses, when, and how much data moved - but does not need full packet contents. Which of the following tools provides exactly this?","options":["SIEM","NetFlow","SNMP traps","DLP"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"NetFlow. Flow records capture conversation metadata - endpoints, timing, volume - without payloads, ideal for spotting exfiltration patterns. The tell: NetFlow = who talked to whom and how much, SIEM = central log correlation, SNMP traps = device fault alerts, DLP = blocking sensitive data leaving."},
  {"id":"q659","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"Compliance requires that employees be prevented from emailing files containing credit card numbers outside the company, with attempts detected and blocked automatically. Which of the following tools enforces this requirement?","options":["DLP","SIEM","Vulnerability scanner","NetFlow"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"DLP. Data loss prevention inspects outbound content for sensitive patterns like card numbers and blocks the transfer. The tell: DLP = stop sensitive data leaving, SIEM = correlate and alert on logs, vulnerability scanner = find unpatched flaws, NetFlow = traffic metadata."},
  {"id":"q660","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"When a switch's temperature crosses a threshold, the switch itself immediately sends an unsolicited alert message to the network management station without being polled. Which of the following mechanisms is this?","options":["NetFlow","SCAP","SNMP traps","SIEM"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SNMP traps. A trap is the device-initiated, unsolicited notification to the manager, unlike normal SNMP polling where the manager asks. The tell: SNMP trap = device pushes the alert, SNMP polling = manager pulls status, NetFlow = traffic metadata, SIEM = log correlation platform."},
  {"id":"q661","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"An organization runs security tools from three vendors and wants their configuration and vulnerability checks automated and comparable through one common machine-readable format. Which of the following standards enables this?","options":["SIEM","DLP","SNMP","SCAP"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SCAP. The Security Content Automation Protocol standardizes how tools express and exchange configuration and vulnerability checks, making multi-vendor results comparable. SNMP loses because it monitors and manages network devices, not security check content. The tell: SCAP = the common security-check automation format, SNMP = device management protocol, SIEM = log correlation platform, DLP = data-leak blocking."},
  {"id":"q662","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"A SOC needs one platform that ingests logs from firewalls, servers, and applications enterprise-wide, correlates events across them, and raises an alert when the combined pattern looks like an attack no single log would reveal. Which of the following tools meets this need?","options":["SIEM","NetFlow","SNMP traps","vulnerability scanner"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SIEM. Centralized ingestion plus cross-source correlation and alerting is the security information and event management platform. The tell: SIEM = correlate everything, alert on patterns, NetFlow = traffic metadata, SNMP traps = device fault notifications, vulnerability scanner = finds weaknesses, not events."},
  {"id":"q663","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"Which of the following tools actively probes hosts and applications for known weaknesses - missing patches, insecure configurations, default credentials - and produces a prioritized findings report?","options":["antivirus","vulnerability scanner","SIEM","DLP"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Vulnerability scanner. Active probing for known weaknesses with a findings report is the scanner's function. The tell: vulnerability scanner = finds flaws before attackers, antivirus = catches malware on hosts, SIEM = correlates events, DLP = stops data leaving."},
  {"id":"q664","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"A hardening project measures every server against a consensus-developed set of recommended secure configuration settings, such as the CIS guides. Which of the following resources is the project using?","options":["SCAP","NetFlow","SIEM","benchmarks"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Benchmarks. Consensus secure-configuration guides like the CIS Benchmarks are the measuring stick for hardening. The tell: benchmarks = the recommended settings themselves, SCAP = the automation format for checking them, SIEM = event correlation, NetFlow = traffic metadata."},
  {"id":"q665","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"On each workstation, software scans files against known-malware signatures and heuristics, blocking and quarantining anything that matches before it executes. Which of the following tools is this?","options":["SIEM","antivirus","NetFlow","SCAP"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Antivirus. Host-based signature and heuristic malware blocking is the antivirus/anti-malware role - the endpoint's baseline defense. The tell: antivirus = block known malware on the host, SIEM = correlate logs centrally, NetFlow = traffic metadata, SCAP = the automation format for checks."},
  {"id":"q666","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"A defense contractor must verify thousands of hosts against a mandated configuration baseline using automated checks written in a standardized, machine-readable format that any conforming scanner can process. Which of the following should the contractor use?","options":["A CIS benchmark","SIEM","NetFlow","SCAP"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SCAP. The Security Content Automation Protocol defines the standardized machine-readable formats that let any conforming tool run and report the same configuration checks; a CIS benchmark loses because it is the recommended-settings content, not the automation format for checking. The tell: SCAP = the standard for expressing and exchanging checks; benchmark = the published settings to check against; SIEM = log correlation; NetFlow = traffic flow records."},
  {"id":"q667","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"An administrator building the company's first hardened Windows Server image wants an authoritative, publicly published set of recommended secure configuration settings as the starting point. Which of the following BEST fits?","options":["SCAP","Group Policy","The security team's existing baseline","A CIS benchmark"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A CIS benchmark. Consensus-developed, published hardening guides are the authoritative starting point an organization then tailors into its own baseline; the existing internal baseline loses because the stem says this is the first hardened image, so none exists yet. The tell: benchmark = the published industry reference; baseline = the organization's adopted configuration built from it; SCAP = the automation format for checking compliance; Group Policy = the mechanism that pushes settings."},
  {"id":"q668","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"A known trojan copied to a workstation is quarantined within seconds, yet a fileless attack running entirely in PowerShell memory on the same host goes undetected. Which of the following tools MOST likely produced both results?","options":["Antivirus","EDR","File integrity monitoring","DLP"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Antivirus. Signature matching quarantines a known trojan file instantly but has nothing to match against an attack that lives only in PowerShell memory; EDR loses because behavioral endpoint detection is exactly what would be expected to flag the fileless activity, not miss it. The tell: antivirus = signature match on known malware files; EDR = behavioral detection and response, including fileless; FIM = alerts on file changes; DLP = stops sensitive data leaving."},
  {"id":"q669","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"A network engineer explains that the management station normally learns device health by polling every five minutes, yet interface-down notifications arrive within one second of a failure. Which of the following MOST likely delivers the fast notifications?","options":["SNMP traps sent by the devices","More frequent SNMP polling","NetFlow records exported by the devices","A SIEM correlation rule"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SNMP traps. A trap is a device-initiated, unsolicited notification pushed to the management station the moment the event occurs, independent of any polling cycle; faster polling loses because even aggressive pulls cannot beat an immediate push. The tell: trap = device pushes on event; poll/get = manager pulls on schedule; NetFlow = traffic conversation records, not device health; SIEM correlation = analyzes data it already received."},
  {"id":"q670","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"A SOC evaluates a platform that ingests logs from hundreds of sources, correlates related events into a single alert, and pages an analyst; every containment action is then performed manually. Which of the following BEST describes the platform?","options":["SOAR","EDR","SIEM","A NetFlow collector"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SIEM. Enterprise-wide log ingestion, cross-source correlation, and alerting with humans performing the response is the SIEM's role; SOAR loses because the stem says every containment step is manual, and SOAR's defining feature is automated playbook response. The tell: SIEM = collect, correlate, alert; SOAR = orchestrate and execute the response automatically; EDR = endpoint telemetry and response; NetFlow collector = flow records only."},
  {"id":"q671","domain":"D4","family":"security-tools","question":"After integrating its alert platform with playbooks, a SOC handles a phished account automatically: the host is isolated, the account disabled, and a ticket opened with no analyst action, cutting containment from four hours to 90 seconds. Which of the following was implemented?","options":["SIEM","NAC","SCAP","SOAR"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SOAR. Playbook-driven automation that isolates the host, disables the account, and opens the ticket without analyst action is security orchestration, automation, and response; SIEM loses because it correlates and alerts but leaves the response steps to people. The tell: SOAR = machine-speed playbook response; SIEM = correlation and alerting; NAC = posture-checked admission when devices join the network; SCAP = the configuration check automation standard."},
  {"id":"q672","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"A network architect places the public web and mail servers on a separate buffer network sandwiched between the internet and the internal LAN, so internet users can reach those services without ever touching the internal network. Which of the following describes this design element?","options":["Access list","Screened subnet","Firewall rule","Signature"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Screened subnet. The buffer network hosting public-facing services between the internet and the LAN is the screened subnet (the SY0-701 term for the older perimeter-network concept). The tell: screened subnet = the buffer zone itself, access list/firewall rule = the filtering entries protecting it, signature = a detection pattern."},
  {"id":"q673","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"An IPS drops a packet because its contents exactly match a stored pattern for a specific known exploit. Which of the following detection methods is this?","options":["Signature","Trend","Firewall rule","Screened subnet"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Signature. Matching traffic against stored patterns of known attacks is signature-based detection: precise on known threats, blind to novel ones. The tell: signature = known-pattern match, trend = deviation from behavioral baselines, firewall rule = port/address policy, screened subnet = the buffer network."},
  {"id":"q674","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"Instead of matching stored exploit patterns, a sensor learns weeks of normal traffic volume and flags activity that deviates sharply from that baseline. Which of the following detection methods is this?","options":["signature","trend","screened subnet","access list"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Trend. Baseline-and-deviation detection is trend (anomaly) analysis, able to spot novel attacks that signatures miss - at the price of more false positives. The tell: trend = deviation from learned normal, signature = match to a known pattern, access list = static filter rules, screened subnet = the buffer network."},
  {"id":"q675","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"A router's configuration holds an ordered set of permit and deny statements evaluated top-down against each packet's source, destination, and port to decide what passes. Which of the following terms describes this construct?","options":["signature","trend","access list","screened subnet"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Access list. The ordered permit/deny statements filtering packets by address and port are an ACL, evaluated first-match. The tell: access list = the rule list itself, signature = an attack pattern, trend = baseline deviation, screened subnet = the network zone the rules often protect."},
  {"id":"q676","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"A firewall evaluates its access list top-down, first match wins:\n10 PERMIT TCP any -> 10.0.20.5 port 443\n20 DENY IP 172.16.0.0/16 -> any\n30 PERMIT TCP any -> 10.0.20.8 port 25\n40 PERMIT TCP 172.16.5.0/24 -> 10.0.20.9 port 22\nAdministrators in 172.16.5.0/24 added rule 40 but still cannot SSH to 10.0.20.9. Which of the following explains the failure?","options":["The implicit deny at the end of the list drops the sessions","Rule 20 matches the traffic before rule 40 is ever evaluated","Rule 10 limits the administrators to HTTPS destinations only","Rule 40 lacks a matching rule permitting the return traffic"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rule 20 matches first. Processing is top-down with first match, and 172.16.5.0/24 falls inside 172.16.0.0/16, so the broad deny at rule 20 consumes the traffic before rule 40 is ever read; the implicit deny loses because a written rule already matched earlier in the list. The tell: first match wins = an earlier broad rule shadows a later specific one; implicit deny = only drops traffic no written rule matched; the fix is moving the specific permit above the broad deny."},
  {"id":"q677","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"A firewall's entire rule set is shown; an unwritten implicit deny follows rule 30:\n10 PERMIT TCP 10.1.1.0/24 -> any port 443\n20 PERMIT TCP 10.1.1.0/24 -> any port 80\n30 DENY TCP any -> any port 23\nA host at 10.1.2.15 attempts an HTTPS connection to an internet server. Which of the following will happen?","options":["The connection is permitted by rule 10","The connection is denied by rule 30","The connection is dropped by the implicit deny","The connection is permitted because no deny rule matches it"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dropped by the implicit deny. The source 10.1.2.15 is outside 10.1.1.0/24, so rules 10 and 20 never match, and rule 30 covers only port 23; unmatched traffic falls through to the silent default drop. The tell: implicit deny = anything no written rule matches is dropped by default; explicit deny = a written rule that matches; \"no deny matched, so it passes\" is exactly the misconception firewalls do not follow."},
  {"id":"q678","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"Policy requires that SSH management of the core firewall be possible only from the administrator subnets 192.168.10.0/24 and 192.168.20.0/24. Which of the following rule sets BEST enforces the policy?","options":["Permit the two administrator subnets to port 22, then deny all other sources to port 22","Deny all sources to port 22, then permit the two administrator subnets to port 22","Permit the two administrator subnets to all ports, then deny everything else","Permit any source to port 22, then deny the two administrator subnets on other ports"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Permit the two administrator subnets to port 22, then deny everything else to port 22. With top-down first-match processing, the specific permits must come before the broad deny; the deny-first option loses because the deny would match every SSH session, including the administrators', before their permits are read. The tell: order = specific permits above broad denies; permitting all ports grants more than the policy; permitting any source to 22 inverts the requirement."},
  {"id":"q679","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"As part of a hardening change, a firewall administrator adds a rule denying TCP port 23 to all network devices while continuing to permit TCP port 22. Which of the following BEST describes the effect?","options":["Telnet is blocked while SSH management continues","HTTP is blocked while HTTPS browsing continues","FTP is blocked while SFTP transfers continue","SMTP is blocked while IMAP delivery continues"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Telnet is blocked while SSH management continues. TCP 23 is Telnet's cleartext management port and TCP 22 carries SSH; the FTP option loses because FTP control runs on TCP 21, which this change never touches. The tell: 23 = Telnet, 22 = SSH (and SFTP), 21 = FTP, 80/443 = HTTP/HTTPS, 25 = SMTP; blocking a port silences the service bound to it, nothing else."},
  {"id":"q680","domain":"D4","family":"firewall-config","question":"A company must publish a web server to the internet, but a compromise of that server must not give attackers a direct path to the internal file servers. Which of the following placements BEST meets the requirement?","options":["On the internal LAN behind stricter host firewall rules","In a screened subnet between the internet and the internal network","Behind a jump server on the management network","On the same VLAN as the file servers with an IPS inline"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A screened subnet. Hosting the public server in a buffer network bounded by firewall interfaces lets internet users reach it while a compromise stays walled off from the LAN; the internal-LAN option loses because host rules on the same network still leave a direct path to the file servers. The tell: screened subnet = buffer network for public-facing servers; same-VLAN placement = no boundary at all; jump server = an administrative access pivot, not a public hosting zone."},
  {"id":"q681","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"To keep users off malware domains, the security team configures the company resolver to simply refuse to return IP addresses for any domain on a known-bad list; the connection dies before it starts. Which of the following filtering techniques is this?","options":["Centralized proxy","Content categorization","DNS filtering","Reputation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DNS filtering. Blocking at name resolution - no answer, no connection - is DNS filtering, cheap and effective at scale. The tell: DNS filtering = block the lookup, centralized proxy = traffic routes through a filtering middlebox, content categorization = classify sites by topic, reputation = score-based blocking."},
  {"id":"q682","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"Remote laptops need web filtering that works at airports and home offices, far from the corporate network, so the team installs filtering software directly on every device. Which of the following approaches is this?","options":["Agent-based","Centralized proxy","Content categorization","DNS filtering"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Agent-based. Filtering software installed on each endpoint travels with the device, working wherever it connects. The tell: agent-based = filtering lives on the device, centralized proxy = filtering lives on the network path, DNS filtering = blocking lookups, content categorization = the site-classification engine any of them may use."},
  {"id":"q683","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"The web filter blocks any site classified under the gambling or adult topics, no matter the specific URL, because policy bans whole classes of content rather than individual addresses. Which of the following techniques is this?","options":["content categorization","reputation","centralized proxy","DNS filtering"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Content categorization. Classifying sites by topic and blocking whole categories enforces policy by content class. The tell: content categorization = blocked by topic, reputation = blocked by risk score, centralized proxy = the on-path architecture, DNS filtering = blocking the name lookup."},
  {"id":"q684","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"Every employee web request is forced through one on-network appliance that inspects, filters, and logs it before forwarding to the internet; devices cannot bypass it. Which of the following web-filter architectures is this?","options":["reputation","URL scanning","content categorization","centralized proxy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Centralized proxy. Forcing every request through one on-path inspection point is the centralized proxy architecture; the question asks about the architecture, not the checks it runs. The tell: centralized proxy = one on-path filtering point all traffic must traverse, URL scanning = examining the specific requested address, categorization/reputation = the classification engines a proxy can use."},
  {"id":"q685","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"Sales laptops spend most of their time on home and hotel networks without connecting to the VPN, and users keep reaching phishing sites the on-premises filter would have blocked. Which of the following approaches BEST closes the gap?","options":["A centralized proxy","Agent-based web filtering","Content categorization","Block rules on the perimeter firewall"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Agent-based web filtering. Filtering software installed on each laptop enforces policy wherever the device connects, on or off the VPN; a centralized proxy loses because off-network traffic never transits the corporate choke point. The tell: agent-based = policy travels with the device; centralized proxy = enforcement only where traffic passes through; categorization and block rules = decision techniques, not deployment points."},
  {"id":"q686","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"Attackers host phishing pages at specific paths on a popular file-sharing domain that the business must keep reachable. Which of the following web filter techniques can block the malicious pages while leaving the rest of the domain available?","options":["DNS filtering","Content categorization","URL scanning","Reputation-based filtering"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"URL scanning. Examining the full requested URL lets the filter deny the specific malicious paths while permitting everything else on the domain; DNS filtering loses because refusing resolution is all-or-nothing for the whole domain. The tell: URL scanning = full-path awareness; DNS filtering = domain-level only; categorization = the site's topic class; reputation = the site's risk score."},
  {"id":"q687","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"A new acceptable use policy bans access to all social media and streaming video sites from warehouse workstations, and the security team refuses to build or maintain lists of individual sites. Which of the following techniques BEST implements the policy?","options":["Content categorization","Block rules for each known site","Reputation-based filtering","URL scanning"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Content categorization. Blocking the social media and streaming categories enforces the policy across every site in those classes without listing a single address; per-site block rules lose because they require exactly the list maintenance the team rejected. The tell: categorization = allow or block whole topic classes; block rules = explicit entries per address; reputation = risk score, not topic; URL scanning = matching specific requested paths."},
  {"id":"q688","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"Following an incident, a security analyst must immediately stop all users from reaching one specific malicious URL while the rest of that site stays reachable, even though the filter's category and reputation engines still rate it safe. Which of the following should the analyst create?","options":["A recategorization request to the filter vendor","An explicit block rule for the URL","A DNS filtering policy for the site's domain","A lower reputation threshold for all sites"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An explicit block rule. A written deny entry for the URL overrides whatever the category and reputation engines conclude and takes effect immediately; the recategorization request loses because it depends on the vendor and takes time the analyst does not have. The tell: block rule = the explicit policy entry that always wins; categorization and reputation = automated verdicts a rule can override; DNS filtering = would drop the whole domain, not one URL."},
  {"id":"q689","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"A phishing campaign uses a domain registered six hours earlier that no vendor has categorized yet, but the web filter still denies the request based on the domain's age and hosting history. Which of the following techniques blocked the request?","options":["Content categorization","URL scanning","An explicit block rule","Reputation-based filtering"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Reputation-based filtering. A risk score built from domain age, hosting, and history can block brand-new domains no category database has seen; content categorization loses because the stem says the domain has not been categorized yet. The tell: reputation = score-based verdict that covers the never-before-seen; categorization = needs an assigned topic class; block rule = a manually written entry; URL scanning = checks the requested path against known-bad lists."},
  {"id":"q690","domain":"D4","family":"web-filter","question":"A facility has hundreds of IoT sensors and printers that cannot run any client software, yet every device must be prevented from reaching known malicious domains. Which of the following protects all of them with a single network-level change?","options":["Agent-based filtering on each device","URL scanning within each device's browser","DNS filtering at the network resolver","A centralized proxy requiring per-device configuration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DNS filtering. Refusing to resolve known-bad domains at the network resolver protects every device that uses it, with nothing installed or configured on the endpoints; agent-based filtering loses because these devices cannot take agents. The tell: DNS filtering = enforce at the resolver, domain-level, covers agentless devices; agent-based = requires client software; proxy = traffic must be steered through it per device; URL scanning = needs inspection of the full request."},
  {"id":"q691","domain":"D4","family":"os-hardening","question":"A Windows administrator must push a standardized security configuration - password policy, disabled services, audit settings - to thousands of domain-joined workstations from one central point, reapplied automatically. Which of the following mechanisms accomplishes this?","options":["SELinux","Group Policy","AppArmor","Local Security Policy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Group Policy. Centralized, domain-wide Windows configuration enforcement is Group Policy's job. The tell: Group Policy = central control of domain-joined Windows, Local Security Policy = one standalone machine, SELinux/AppArmor = Linux mandatory access control."},
  {"id":"q692","domain":"D4","family":"os-hardening","question":"A Linux hardening standard requires mandatory access control: every process is confined to a security context restricting exactly which files and ports it may touch, constraints even root cannot override. Which of the following mechanisms enforces this using labeled security contexts?","options":["Group Policy","Local Security Policy","SELinux","AppArmor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SELinux. Label-based security contexts enforcing mandatory access control on Linux is SELinux; AppArmor achieves similar confinement using path-based profiles instead. The tell: SELinux = labels/contexts, AppArmor = path-based profiles, Group Policy = Windows domains, Local Security Policy = a standalone Windows box."},
  {"id":"q693","domain":"D4","family":"os-hardening","question":"A kiosk PC runs Windows but is deliberately not joined to any domain, so no domain-level configuration reaches it. Which of the following enforces its password and audit policy?","options":["Group Policy","SELinux","Local Security Policy","AppArmor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Local Security Policy. A standalone Windows machine gets its security settings from Local Security Policy; Group Policy requires domain membership to apply centrally. The tell: Local Security Policy = one non-domain Windows machine, Group Policy = centralized domain enforcement, SELinux/AppArmor = Linux mandatory access control frameworks."},
  {"id":"q694","domain":"D4","family":"os-hardening","question":"On a Red Hat server, a web daemon cannot read a file even though standard permissions allow it. The audit log shows the denial came from the file's security label, not its mode bits. Which of the following mechanisms is blocking access?","options":["AppArmor","Group Policy","Local Security Policy","SELinux"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SELinux. A denial driven by security labels despite permissive standard file permissions is SELinux mandatory access control on a Red Hat system. AppArmor is tempting as the other Linux confinement option, but it uses per-program path profiles, not file labels, and ships with Ubuntu/SUSE. The tell: SELinux = label/context-based mandatory access control (Red Hat family), AppArmor = path-based profiles (Ubuntu/SUSE), Group Policy and Local Security Policy = Windows mechanisms."},
  {"id":"q695","domain":"D4","family":"os-hardening","question":"In Active Directory, an admin links a configuration object to an organizational unit, and every computer in that OU automatically receives the same screen-lock timeout and audit logging settings. Which of the following mechanisms delivers these settings?","options":["SELinux","Group Policy","Local Security Policy","AppArmor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Group Policy. GPOs linked to OUs push centralized settings to every domain-joined machine in scope automatically. Local Security Policy is tempting for the same settings, but it configures only one standalone machine, not an entire OU. The tell: Group Policy = domain-wide central enforcement through Active Directory, Local Security Policy = one standalone Windows machine, SELinux/AppArmor = Linux mandatory access control."},
  {"id":"q696","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A domain owner publishes a DNS record listing the only mail servers authorized to send for the domain. Receiving servers check each arriving message's source IP against that list. Which of the following mechanisms is this?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","Secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SPF. The Sender Policy Framework is the DNS-published list of authorized sending IPs, checked by receivers. The tell: SPF = authorized sender IPs in DNS, DKIM = cryptographic signature on the message, DMARC = the policy tying both together, secure email gateway = the filtering appliance."},
  {"id":"q697","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"Every outbound message from a domain carries a header containing a cryptographic signature generated with the domain's private key. Receivers verify it with a public key from DNS, proving the message body was not altered and genuinely came from the domain. Which of the following mechanisms is this?","options":["DMARC","SPF","DKIM","Secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DKIM. DomainKeys Identified Mail signs messages cryptographically, giving integrity and domain authenticity that travel with the message itself. The tell: DKIM = signature in the headers, SPF = source-IP check against DNS, DMARC = policy and reporting over both, gateway = the filtering box."},
  {"id":"q698","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"Messages relayed through a third-party forwarding service keep failing the recipient's IP-based sender check, because the forwarder's IP is not on the origin domain's list. Yet a second authentication check still passes, since its verification data travels inside the message headers. Which of the following provides the second check?","options":["Secure email gateway","DMARC","SPF","DKIM"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"DKIM. Its signature rides in the headers and survives forwarding, while SPF breaks because the forwarder's IP is not authorized. This survival difference is a classic SPF-vs-DKIM discriminator. The tell: DKIM = travels with the message, SPF = tied to the sending IP, DMARC = the policy layer, gateway = perimeter filtering."},
  {"id":"q699","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"An appliance at the mail perimeter scans every inbound and outbound message for spam, malicious attachments, and sensitive data leaks before anything reaches a mailbox or leaves the company. Which of the following components is this?","options":["Secure email gateway","DMARC","DKIM","SPF"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Secure email gateway. The inline scanning appliance or service at the mail boundary is the gateway; the DNS-based records authenticate senders but scan nothing. The tell: secure email gateway = the filtering box, SPF/DKIM/DMARC = DNS-published authentication and policy records."},
  {"id":"q700","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"One mechanism takes the SPF and DKIM results, applies domain alignment, and tells receiving servers whether to deliver, quarantine, or reject mail that fails - publishing that instruction in DNS. Which of the following mechanisms is this?","options":["DMARC","DKIM","SPF","Secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"DMARC. It is the policy layer over SPF and DKIM: alignment plus a published none/quarantine/reject instruction for failures. The tell: DMARC = the decision policy and reporting, SPF = IP authorization, DKIM = message signature, gateway = the filtering appliance."},
  {"id":"q701","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A security team knows attackers spoof its domain but has zero visibility into the scale. It wants recurring aggregate reports from receiving servers worldwide about all mail claiming to come from the domain. Which of the following delivers those reports?","options":["SPF","DKIM","Secure email gateway","DMARC"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"DMARC. Its reporting function (aggregate RUA reports) is the only mechanism here that sends the domain owner feedback about mail claiming its name. The tell: DMARC = policy plus reporting back to the owner, SPF/DKIM = checks with no feedback loop, gateway = local filtering."},
  {"id":"q702","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A marketing team adopts a cloud email platform that must send campaigns as the corporate domain. An admin edits one DNS text record, adding the vendor's outbound mail servers to the set of hosts permitted to send for the domain. Which of the following mechanisms is being updated?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SPF. The DNS record listing which hosts may send for the domain is the SPF record; authorizing a new sender means adding its addresses there. The tell: SPF = the authorized-senders list in DNS, DKIM = the message signature, DMARC = policy over both, gateway = the filtering appliance."},
  {"id":"q703","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A security team wants spoofed mail that fails authentication to be actively rejected by receivers, and wants to name the mailbox where receivers send their feedback reports. Which of the following DNS records controls both the enforcement action and the reporting destination?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DMARC. The p=reject enforcement instruction and the rua reporting address both live in the DMARC record. The tell: DMARC = failure handling plus reporting, SPF = who may send, DKIM = the signature, gateway = the scanning box."},
  {"id":"q704","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"Every inbound message is routed through an appliance that detonates attachments in a sandbox, rewrites embedded links through a safe redirect, and strips messages flagged as phishing before delivery. Which of the following components performs this?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Secure email gateway. Sandboxing, URL rewriting, and phishing filtering are inline content-security functions of the gateway; DNS records authenticate but never scan. The tell: gateway = active content inspection in the mail path, SPF/DKIM/DMARC = authentication and policy records."},
  {"id":"q705","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A mailing list server appends a footer to every message it relays. Because the message body changed after the origin domain generated its verification data, the receiver's check now fails. Which of the following mechanisms does the body modification break?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DKIM. Its signature covers the message body, so any modification - even an appended footer - invalidates it. SPF does not care about content, only the sending IP. The tell: DKIM = breaks when content changes, SPF = breaks when the sending host changes, DMARC = judges the combined result."},
  {"id":"q706","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A domain already publishes both underlying email authentication methods. Which of the following capabilities does DMARC add that SPF and DKIM do not provide on their own?","options":["A cryptographic signature over the message so tampering is detectable","A failure-handling policy with alignment and receiver reporting","A DNS list of IP addresses permitted to send for the domain","An inline appliance that scans and filters the mail stream"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"DMARC adds the decision layer: alignment with the visible From domain, a quarantine or reject instruction for failures, and reporting back to the owner. The signature option is tempting because DMARC evaluates it, but the signature itself is DKIM's job and already exists. The tell: SPF = the IP list, DKIM = the signature, DMARC = what receivers should DO about the results plus the feedback loop, gateway = the filtering appliance."},
  {"id":"q707","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"The mail team needs somewhere to build rules that quarantine suspicious inbound messages and force TLS on all mail to a specific partner domain. Which of the following components hosts these mail-flow rules?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Secure email gateway. Quarantine rules and per-domain TLS enforcement are mail-flow policies configured on the gateway; DNS authentication records have no rule engine. The tell: gateway = configurable mail-flow filtering and routing, SPF/DKIM/DMARC = published authentication records."},
  {"id":"q708","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"To validate an arriving message, the receiving server queries a public key stored at a named selector under the sending domain's DNS, then checks the signature attached to the message against it. Which of the following mechanisms relies on this selector-based key lookup?","options":["DKIM","SPF","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"DKIM. The selector-based public key in DNS validating a message signature is the DKIM verification flow. The tell: DKIM = selector, public key, signature, SPF = plain list of allowed IPs, DMARC = policy record, gateway = the appliance."},
  {"id":"q709","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A security team begins a phased email authentication rollout that will end with a DMARC reject policy. Outbound message signing is not yet available on its mail servers. Which of the following records should the team publish FIRST?","options":["DKIM","SPF","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SPF. With signing unavailable, the authorized-senders list is the one authentication check the team can publish now, and DMARC needs at least one passing check beneath it. DMARC is tempting as the stated end goal, but a policy over checks that do not yet exist does nothing. The tell: SPF = the foundational who-may-send record, DKIM = the signature layer that requires signing capability, DMARC = the policy added once a check exists, gateway = filtering, not authentication."},
  {"id":"q710","domain":"D4","family":"email-security","question":"A message passes both underlying authentication checks, yet it is still treated as failing because the domain in the visible From header does not match the domain that actually authenticated. Which of the following mechanisms enforces that From-header matching requirement?","options":["SPF","DKIM","DMARC","secure email gateway"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DMARC. Alignment is DMARC's signature requirement: the authenticated domain must match the From header the user sees, closing the loophole where attackers pass checks on a domain they control while displaying yours. The tell: DMARC = alignment with the visible From, SPF/DKIM = raw pass/fail with no alignment concept."},
  {"id":"q711","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"Before any laptop gets a network connection, a control checks that it carries current patches, running antivirus, and disk encryption; failures land the device in a quarantine VLAN for remediation. Which of the following technologies is this?","options":["EDR","FIM","UBA","NAC"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"NAC. Network access control gates admission on device health posture and quarantines the non-compliant. The tell: NAC = health check at the network door, EDR = continuous endpoint activity monitoring and response, FIM = watching critical files for changes, UBA = modeling user behavior for anomalies."},
  {"id":"q712","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"An agent on every workstation continuously records process launches, file activity, and network connections, and when it recognizes ransomware behavior it automatically isolates the host from the network. Which of the following technologies is this?","options":["EDR","XDR","NAC","DLP"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"EDR. Endpoint detection and response continuously monitors endpoint activity and can respond automatically, including host isolation. The tell: EDR = deep endpoint recording and response, XDR = the same correlation extended across endpoint, network, email, and cloud, NAC = admission control, DLP = blocking data leaving."},
  {"id":"q713","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"A vendor platform stitches together detections from endpoints, network sensors, email security, and cloud workloads into one correlated investigation view, so an attack crossing all four shows as one incident. Which of the following technologies is this?","options":["EDR","FIM","XDR","UBA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"XDR. Extended detection and response correlates telemetry across multiple layers beyond the endpoint, unifying the investigation. The tell: XDR = cross-layer correlation, EDR = endpoint-only depth, FIM = file change alerts, UBA = user behavior anomalies."},
  {"id":"q714","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"An incident response team learns attackers replaced system binaries on a server weeks before anyone noticed. Which of the following controls, had it been deployed, would have alerted at the moment the files were modified?","options":["DLP","UBA","NAC","FIM"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FIM. File integrity monitoring baselines critical system files and alerts the instant a monitored file deviates from known-good, exactly the detection this breach lacked. UBA loses because it flags anomalous user activity, not changes to files. The tell: FIM = watch files for unauthorized change, DLP = stop data exfiltration, NAC = gate network admission by device posture, UBA = flag anomalous user behavior."},
  {"id":"q715","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"A platform learns each user's normal working pattern - login times, locations, data touched - then flags a finance user who suddenly downloads gigabytes of records at 3 a.m. from a new country. Which of the following technologies is this?","options":["EDR","NAC","UBA","FIM"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"UBA. User behavior analytics baselines normal per-user activity and flags anomalies, catching compromised accounts and insiders that signature tools miss. The tell: UBA = anomalous user behavior, EDR = endpoint process/file/network monitoring, NAC = admission health checks, FIM = file change detection."},
  {"id":"q716","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"Endpoint software inspects every file being copied to USB storage and blocks any containing data tagged sensitive, logging the attempt for review. Which of the following technologies enforces this?","options":["FIM","EDR","DLP","UBA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DLP. Blocking tagged sensitive data from leaving via removable media is data loss prevention at the endpoint. The tell: DLP = stop sensitive data exfiltration, FIM = alert on file changes, EDR = detect malicious behavior, UBA = flag abnormal user patterns."},
  {"id":"q717","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"A SOC lead complains their endpoint tool sees each host deeply but cannot connect an email lure, the endpoint infection it caused, and the cloud-account abuse that followed into one story. Which of the following technologies BEST closes that gap?","options":["EDR","NAC","DLP","XDR"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"XDR. Extended detection and response correlates endpoint, email, network, and cloud telemetry into one linked incident; more EDR loses because EDR only deepens per-host visibility and never ties the email lure to the cloud abuse. The tell: XDR = correlation across layers, EDR = depth on the endpoint only, NAC = admission control, DLP = data leaving."},
  {"id":"q718","domain":"D4","family":"endpoint-detection","question":"Attackers use phished credentials to log in as a valid employee and operate within that account's normal permissions, evading signature and static-threshold rules. Which of the following BEST detects this compromised account?","options":["Endpoint detection and response","User behavior analytics","Data loss prevention","A signature-based SIEM correlation rule"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"User behavior analytics. UBA builds a per-user baseline and flags deviations from normal behavior, catching a compromised-but-authorized account that signatures and static thresholds miss. EDR loses because it watches endpoint process behavior, not the anomaly in a user's login and access pattern. The tell: UBA = learned per-user baselines; EDR = endpoint process telemetry; DLP = sensitive-data movement; SIEM rules = static thresholds."},
  {"id":"q719","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"Every quarter, managers receive a report of their team members' system access and must confirm each entitlement is still needed, revoking anything no longer justified by the person's role. Which of the following identity processes is this?","options":["Provisioning","Attestation","Identity proofing","Federation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Attestation. Periodic managerial review and confirmation that access remains appropriate is access attestation (recertification). The tell: attestation = recurring confirm-or-revoke review, provisioning = granting access at start, identity proofing = verifying a person is who they claim, federation = trusting another organization's authentication."},
  {"id":"q720","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"The moment HR records a termination, an automated workflow disables the employee's account, revokes every access right, and reclaims licenses - all before the person reaches the parking lot. Which of the following processes is this?","options":["Provisioning","Identity proofing","De-provisioning","Attestation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"De-provisioning. Removing accounts and access at separation is de-provisioning, and speed matters: lingering access is a classic insider risk. The tell: de-provisioning = access removed at exit, provisioning = access granted at entry, attestation = periodic review in between, identity proofing = verifying identity up front."},
  {"id":"q721","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"On a new hire's first morning, an automated workflow has already created her accounts and granted exactly the access her role requires - nothing requested manually. Which of the following identity processes is this?","options":["provisioning","de-provisioning","attestation","identity proofing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Provisioning. Creating accounts and granting role-appropriate access at onboarding is provisioning. The tell: provisioning = access granted at entry, de-provisioning = removed at exit, attestation = periodically re-confirmed, identity proofing = proving the person is who they claim before any of it."},
  {"id":"q722","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"After one sign-in to the corporate identity provider, a user opens the HR app, the expense tool, and email without ever being prompted to log in again. Which of the following capabilities is this?","options":["federation","identity proofing","provisioning","single sign-on"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Single sign-on. One authentication unlocking many applications is SSO. Federation extends the idea across organizational boundaries, but everything here lives under one identity provider. The tell: SSO = one login, many apps, federation = trusting another org's login, provisioning = granting access, identity proofing = verifying the human."},
  {"id":"q723","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"Before issuing credentials to a new remote hire nobody has met in person, HR requires a government ID scan matched against a live selfie video to confirm the applicant is a real person who is who they claim. Which of the following identity processes is this?","options":["attestation","identity proofing","provisioning","de-provisioning"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Identity proofing. Verifying that a claimed identity belongs to a real, present person BEFORE any account exists is proofing - the step that defeats fake-applicant fraud. The tell: identity proofing = prove the human first, provisioning = then grant the access, attestation = periodically re-confirm it, de-provisioning = remove it at exit."},
  {"id":"q724","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"A supplier's engineers open your company's ordering portal using their OWN company's corporate logins - your portal trusts their employer's identity provider to vouch for them, and you never issue them accounts. Which of the following capabilities is this?","options":["single sign-on","identity proofing","federation","provisioning"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Federation. Trusting another organization's identity provider across a company boundary is federation; SSO within one organization is the same convenience without the cross-boundary trust. The tell: federation = trust another org's logins, single sign-on = one login for many apps under one provider, provisioning = creating accounts yourself, identity proofing = verifying the human."},
  {"id":"q725","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"An access review finds a ten-year employee still holds permissions accumulated from four previous roles, far beyond what her current job requires. Which of the following BEST describes this condition?","options":["Privilege escalation","Least privilege","Privilege creep","Separation of duties"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Privilege creep. Rights accumulated across successive role changes and never revoked leave a user with far more access than the current role needs; the fix is periodic access review with role-based assignment. Privilege escalation loses because that is an attacker exploiting a flaw to gain higher rights, not the gradual buildup of legitimate grants. The tell: privilege creep = leftover access from old roles; privilege escalation = an attack raising privileges; least privilege = the principle being violated."},
  {"id":"q726","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"A security architect notes that if the marketing team's shared account were compromised, the attacker could export the entire customer database. Which of the following BEST describes the risk created by the account's permissions?","options":["Privilege creep from stale role grants","Weak identity proofing at enrollment","Insufficient separation of duties","Excessive standing permissions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Excessive permissions. Granting an account more access than it needs enlarges the blast radius - the damage one compromised account can do - so least privilege would shrink what a single breach exposes. Privilege creep is close but describes access accumulated over time from role changes, whereas here the standing permission set is simply too broad. The tell: excessive permissions and blast radius = one account can reach too much now; privilege creep = leftover rights from past roles."},
  {"id":"q727","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"A caller claiming to be a traveling executive pressures the help desk to reset the executive's password immediately. Which of the following controls BEST prevents an attacker from succeeding this way?","options":["Identity proofing","Access attestation","Multifactor authentication","Single sign-on"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Identity proofing. Verifying the caller's identity before issuing or resetting a credential stops social-engineered resets by an impostor. Multifactor authentication loses because the attacker is requesting a new credential, bypassing the victim's existing factors rather than presenting them. The tell: identity proofing = verify the person BEFORE issuing or resetting credentials; attestation = periodic review of EXISTING entitlements."},
  {"id":"q728","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"An employee who transferred from finance to marketing kept her old finance access for a year. Which of the following recurring controls is designed to catch and remove such residual entitlements?","options":["Identity proofing","Attestation","Single sign-on","Provisioning"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Attestation. Periodic attestation has managers or data owners recertify that each user's existing entitlements are still appropriate, catching residual access after a transfer. Identity proofing loses because it verifies who a person is before issuing identity, not whether current access remains justified. The tell: attestation = recurring review of EXISTING access; identity proofing = verifying identity BEFORE issuance; provisioning = granting access."},
  {"id":"q729","domain":"D4","family":"identity-lifecycle","question":"A newly purchased SaaS application cannot integrate with the company's identity provider because it supports no common federation standard, forcing employees to keep a separate password for it. Which of the following capabilities is the application missing?","options":["Federation","Attestation","Interoperability","Identity proofing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Interoperability. It means IAM components support open standards such as SAML, OAuth/OIDC, and SCIM so tools from different vendors work together; without it the app cannot join the identity provider and becomes a silo. Federation is close but is the trust arrangement interoperability enables - the app cannot even federate because it speaks no common standard. The tell: interoperability = standards compatibility that lets systems connect; federation = the cross-organization trust built on that compatibility."},
  {"id":"q730","domain":"D4","family":"federation-protocols","question":"A user clicks \"Log in with corporate SSO\" on a cloud HR application. The browser bounces to the company identity provider, authenticates, and returns carrying a signed XML assertion that the HR app accepts as proof of identity. Which of the following protocols is in use?","options":["LDAP","OAuth","OpenID Connect","SAML"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SAML. The signed XML assertion passed from identity provider to service provider is SAML's fingerprint, the classic enterprise web-SSO flow. The tell: SAML = XML assertions for SSO, OAuth = delegated access tokens, OpenID Connect = JSON/OAuth-based login, LDAP = directory lookups."},
  {"id":"q731","domain":"D4","family":"federation-protocols","question":"A photo-printing app requests access to a user's cloud storage. The user approves the request at the storage provider, which hands the app a scoped token to fetch photos on the user's behalf - the user's password is never shared with the app. Which of the following protocols enables this delegated access?","options":["OAuth","SAML","LDAP","OpenID Connect"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"OAuth. Delegated, scoped access via tokens without sharing credentials is OAuth's exact purpose - authorization, not login. The tell: OAuth = app acts on your behalf with a token, OpenID Connect = proves who you are on top of OAuth, SAML = XML SSO assertions, LDAP = directory queries."},
  {"id":"q732","domain":"D4","family":"federation-protocols","question":"An in-house application needs to look up user accounts, group memberships, and phone attributes from on-premises Active Directory. Which of the following protocols does it use to query the directory?","options":["OAuth","SAML","LDAP","OpenID Connect"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"LDAP. Querying a directory service for accounts, groups, and attributes is LDAP's job. The tell: LDAP = directory lookups, SAML/OpenID Connect = web SSO flows, OAuth = delegated authorization tokens."},
  {"id":"q733","domain":"D4","family":"federation-protocols","question":"An identity architect describes a protocol that exchanges XML-based security assertions between an identity provider and a service provider to deliver browser single sign-on across enterprise web applications. Which of the following protocols is being described?","options":["OAuth","SAML","LDAP","OpenID Connect"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SAML. XML assertions between IdP and SP for browser SSO is the SAML definition. The tell: SAML = XML assertions, enterprise SSO, OpenID Connect = the JSON-era login layer on OAuth, OAuth = delegated authorization, LDAP = directory queries."},
  {"id":"q734","domain":"D4","family":"federation-protocols","question":"A security analyst reviewing packet captures sees an application sending directory binds and user lookups to a domain controller in plaintext on port 389. The analyst wants these queries encrypted in transit. Which of the following should the application use instead?","options":["LDAPS","LDAP","SAML","OAuth"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"LDAPS. LDAP over TLS on port 636 encrypts the directory binds and lookups that cross the wire in plaintext on 389; plain LDAP is what the capture already shows, so switching to it changes nothing. The tell: LDAPS = encrypted directory access on 636, LDAP = plaintext directory queries on 389, SAML = XML browser SSO, OAuth = delegated authorization tokens."},
  {"id":"q735","domain":"D4","family":"federation-protocols","question":"A mobile app lets users sign in with an existing cloud account. During the authorization flow, the provider also returns a JSON ID token proving who the user is, not just a token granting access to resources. Which of the following protocols supplies this sign-in capability?","options":["OAuth","SAML","LDAP","OpenID Connect"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"OpenID Connect. The JSON ID token asserting the user's identity, issued on top of the OAuth flow, is OIDC's authentication layer; OAuth alone only grants scoped access and never proves who the user is. The tell: OpenID Connect = authentication (ID token) built on OAuth, OAuth = delegated authorization only, SAML = XML assertions for enterprise SSO, LDAP = directory lookups."},
  {"id":"q736","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"On a military network, every file and every user carries a classification label. The operating system itself - never the file's owner - grants or denies access by comparing clearance to classification, and no user can override the labels. Which of the following models is in use?","options":["discretionary access control","role-based access control","rule-based access control","mandatory access control"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Mandatory access control. System-enforced labels that owners cannot override are MAC, the military-grade model. The tell: mandatory = system decides via labels, discretionary = the owner decides, role-based = job group decides, rule-based = a fixed rule set (like ACL conditions) decides."},
  {"id":"q737","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"On a shared file server, whoever creates a folder can personally grant or revoke other users' access to it, entirely at their own discretion. Which of the following access control models does this describe?","options":["discretionary access control","mandatory access control","attribute-based access control","role-based access control"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Discretionary access control. The resource owner controlling who gets in is DAC, flexible and typical of standard file systems. The tell: discretionary = owner's choice, mandatory = system labels the owner cannot change, role-based = permissions follow job groups, attribute-based = multi-condition evaluation."},
  {"id":"q738","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"A document opens only when the requester is in the finance department AND on a corporate-managed device AND connecting from an approved country - the system evaluates all three conditions about the user, device, and context together. Which of the following models is being enforced?","options":["role-based access control","rule-based access control","attribute-based access control","mandatory access control"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Attribute-based access control. Decisions combining attributes of the user, device, and environment are ABAC, the most granular model. The tell: attribute-based = many contextual conditions evaluated together, role-based = job group alone, rule-based = fixed system-wide rules, mandatory = classification labels."},
  {"id":"q739","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"A hospital grants permissions to the \"nurse\" group and the \"billing\" group; individual users get access purely by being placed into the job group that matches their position. Which of the following models does this illustrate?","options":["attribute-based access control","role-based access control","discretionary access control","rule-based access control"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Role-based access control. Permissions attach to job roles and users inherit them by membership, simplifying administration at scale. The tell: role-based = access follows the job group, attribute-based = contextual conditions, discretionary = owner grants, rule-based = fixed rules like time-of-day restrictions."},
  {"id":"q740","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"A network policy denies ALL inbound Telnet (TCP port 23) connections across the enterprise. The rule fires on the traffic itself, identically for every user, no matter who they are or what role they hold. Which of the following access control models enforces this?","options":["role-based access control","rule-based access control","attribute-based access control","mandatory access control"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rule-based access control. A fixed system-wide rule applied uniformly regardless of identity - protocol or port conditions, ACL entries - is rule-based. The tell: rule-based = same rule for everyone, role-based = depends on your job group, attribute-based = evaluates multiple contextual attributes, mandatory = classification labels."},
  {"id":"q741","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"On a Linux server, a file's owner runs chmod and setfacl to decide which users and groups may read or write that file, entirely at the owner's own judgment. Which of the following access control models does this reflect?","options":["discretionary access control","role-based access control","mandatory access control","rule-based access control"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Discretionary access control. Standard Unix permissions put access in the owner's hands, the definition of DAC. The tell: discretionary = owner sets permissions, mandatory = the system's labels override owners (SELinux), role-based = job-group driven, rule-based = uniform fixed rules."},
  {"id":"q742","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"On a classified system, even the analyst who authored a document cannot grant a cleared colleague access to it - only the system's labels and clearances decide. Which of the following access control models is enforced?","options":["Discretionary access control","Role-based access control","Rule-based access control","Mandatory access control"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Mandatory access control. The system enforces classification labels and clearances that no user - not even the data's creator - can override or delegate. Discretionary access control loses because that is the model where the owner grants access at their own discretion, the opposite of what the scenario forbids. The tell: mandatory access control = system labels no one can override; discretionary access control = the owner decides."},
  {"id":"q743","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"A bank grants each employee access based purely on their job title - every teller receives the identical teller permission set upon being assigned that position. Which of the following access control models is this?","options":["Rule-based access control","Role-based access control","Discretionary access control","Attribute-based access control"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Role-based access control. Permissions attach to the job role, so a user inherits a standard set by being assigned that role - here, teller. Rule-based access control loses because it applies system-wide if/then conditions to everyone regardless of role, whereas access here is defined by job function. The tell: role-based access control = permissions by job function; rule-based access control = an if/then condition that fires identically for all."},
  {"id":"q744","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"A system enforces one blanket condition on everyone: any connection from outside the corporate IP range is denied, regardless of the user's identity or job. Which of the following access control models does this represent?","options":["Rule-based access control","Role-based access control","Mandatory access control","Discretionary access control"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Rule-based access control. A single system-wide if/then rule - deny anything from outside the corporate IP range - applies uniformly to every user irrespective of identity or role. Role-based access control loses because it would grant access by the user's job function, but this rule ignores who the user is. The tell: rule-based access control = a blanket if/then condition for everyone; role-based access control = permissions tied to job role."},
  {"id":"q745","domain":"D4","family":"access-control-models","question":"A facility's badge system denies every employee entry between 22:00 and 05:00, regardless of who they are or what role they hold. Which of the following access control approaches BEST describes this restriction?","options":["Role-based access control","Mandatory access control","Time-of-day restriction","Discretionary access control"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Time-of-day restriction. Access is allowed or denied purely by the clock - blocked overnight here - independent of identity or role, a rule-based flavor keyed on time. Role-based access control loses because entry does not depend on the person's job function; the hour alone decides. The tell: time-of-day restriction = access gated by the clock for everyone; role-based access control = access by job function."},
  {"id":"q746","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"A banking login requires a password followed by a fingerprint scan on the user's device. Which of the following authentication factors does the fingerprint satisfy?","options":["something you know","something you have","somewhere you are","something you are"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Something you are. Biometrics - fingerprints, face, iris - are inherence factors. Combined with the password (something you know), this makes true two-factor authentication. The tell: are = biometric traits, know = passwords/PINs, have = tokens/phones/cards, somewhere you are = location."},
  {"id":"q747","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"After entering a password, the user must type the rotating six-digit code displayed by an authenticator app on their phone. Which of the following factors does the code represent?","options":["something you have","something you are","something you know","somewhere you are"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Something you have. The code proves possession of the enrolled device generating it; the fact you type it does not make it knowledge - it changes every 30 seconds. The tell: have = possession of a token/device, know = a memorized secret, are = biometrics, somewhere you are = location context."},
  {"id":"q748","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"An engineer authenticates with a small physical USB device: tap or insert, and it completes a cryptographic challenge automatically - there is no code to read or type. Which of the following BEST describes this device?","options":["soft token","hard token","security key","biometric"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Security key. A physical device performing challenge-response authentication (FIDO2/U2F style) with no displayed code is a security key. A hard token traditionally displays a code you type. The tell: security key = cryptographic tap/insert, hard token = physical code display, soft token = app-generated code, biometric = a body trait."},
  {"id":"q749","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"A conditional access policy verifies the user's physical geographic position - GPS coordinates or network region - before permitting the login. Which of the following authentication factors is being verified?","options":["something you know","something you have","something you are","somewhere you are"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Somewhere you are. Location-based verification is its own factor, typically used to supplement rather than replace the classic three. The tell: somewhere you are = location, know = memorized secret, have = possessed device/token, are = biometric trait."},
  {"id":"q750","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"A legacy system requires a password and then the answer to a personal security question. An auditor rules this is NOT multifactor authentication. Which of the following BEST explains the ruling?","options":["Both inputs are the 'something you know' factor","The two inputs are 'something you have' and 'something you are'","A security question counts as 'something you are'","Passwords are never accepted as an authentication factor"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Both are knowledge. A password and a security-question answer are two memorized secrets - the same factor twice. Multifactor requires different factor TYPES, not two prompts. The tell: two of the same factor = single-factor with extra steps, know + have or know + are = true MFA."},
  {"id":"q751","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"An organization issues some staff a dedicated key-fob device that displays a rotating one-time code, while others generate the same code in a smartphone authenticator app. Which of the following BEST describes the key-fob implementation?","options":["Soft token","Hard token","Security key","Biometric factor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Hard token. A dedicated physical device that displays one-time codes is a hard token; generating the same code in a phone app is a soft token. Security key loses because that performs a cryptographic challenge-response rather than displaying a code to type. The tell: hard token = physical code-display device; soft token = authenticator app code; security key = phishing-resistant challenge-response."},
  {"id":"q752","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"A phishing kit tricks users into typing their one-time passcodes into a fake portal, which relays them in real time to the attacker. Which of the following MFA implementations BEST defeats this technique?","options":["A time-based authenticator app","An SMS one-time passcode","A hardware token displaying codes","A FIDO2 security key"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Security key. A FIDO2 key uses origin-bound cryptographic challenge-response, so there is no code for a user to be tricked into relaying and it will not work on a fake site. A time-based authenticator app loses because its displayed code can still be phished and relayed within its validity window. The tell: security key = phishing-resistant challenge-response; soft-token, SMS, and hard-token codes are all relayable one-time codes."},
  {"id":"q753","domain":"D4","family":"mfa","question":"A shared clinical workstation must verify each nurse quickly, but nothing can be carried between rooms and no secret can be memorized for the shared device. Which of the following authentication methods BEST fits?","options":["Biometrics","Hardware token","Soft-token app","SMS passcode"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Biometrics. A something-you-are factor requires nothing to carry and nothing to memorize, fitting a shared workstation where tokens would be passed around and passwords written down. A hardware token loses because it must be physically carried and could be shared or lost between rooms. The tell: biometrics = inherent factor with nothing to hold or recall; hard and soft tokens and SMS all require possessing a device."},
  {"id":"q754","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A directory policy blocks users from changing their password again until the current one has been in place at least three days, defeating the trick of cycling through changes to return to an old favorite. Which of the following password settings does this describe?","options":["expiration","age","complexity","length"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Age. Minimum password age forces a password to live a set time before it can be changed, blocking rapid cycling past history checks. The tell: age = minimum time before change, expiration = maximum time before forced change, complexity = required character mix, length = minimum characters."},
  {"id":"q755","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A security analyst investigating a breach finds that a single password leaked from one third-party website also unlocked the same employee's accounts on five internal systems. Which of the following password weaknesses does this illustrate?","options":["default credentials","password reuse","shared accounts","weak complexity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Password reuse. One credential valid across many accounts turns a single third-party leak into five compromises - exactly what credential stuffing exploits. Shared accounts lose because these are one employee's own accounts, not one login used by many people. The tell: reuse = the same password across accounts, shared accounts = many users on one account, default credentials = vendor-set passwords never changed, complexity = character variety within a single password."},
  {"id":"q756","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"Citing research that the number of characters matters more than symbol requirements, a security team raises the minimum from 8 to 16 characters as the biggest single upgrade against brute forcing. Which of the following password settings did the team strengthen?","options":["complexity","expiration","age","length"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Length. Every added character multiplies the brute-force keyspace, making length the dominant strength driver. The tell: length = how many characters, complexity = which character classes, expiration = how long it lives, age = how soon it may be changed."},
  {"id":"q757","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A company eliminates typed secrets entirely: users authenticate with a fingerprint or a hardware security key, and there is no passphrase to remember, leak, or phish. Which of the following approaches is this?","options":["expiration","complexity","passwordless","password manager"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Passwordless. Authentication via biometrics or security keys with no typed secret removes the phishable password altogether. The tell: passwordless = no secret phrase exists, password manager = a vault for the secrets you still have, complexity/expiration = rules governing traditional passwords."},
  {"id":"q758","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A policy already forces a password change every 60 days. To also stop users from immediately changing a new password several times in a row to loop back to a favorite, which of the following settings must be added?","options":["Maximum password age","Minimum password age","Password history length","Password complexity"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Minimum password age. It sets how long a password must be kept before it can be changed again, blocking the rapid-cycling trick that exhausts password history to return to a favorite; the 60-day rule is the maximum age and does not stop cycling. Password history alone loses because users can still cycle through changes quickly to flush it without a minimum age. The tell: minimum age = enforced wait before changing again; maximum age or expiration = forced change after a period."},
  {"id":"q759","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A security team documents the organization's traditional policy of requiring every user to change their password every 90 days. Which of the following password practices does this describe?","options":["Minimum password age","Password complexity","Password reuse limits","Password expiration"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Password expiration. A forced periodic change - the traditional 90-day rule - is expiration, or maximum password age; current NIST guidance favors changing only on evidence of compromise. Minimum age loses because that is the shortest time before a user may change again, not the forced-change interval. The tell: expiration = forced change after a set period; minimum age = enforced wait before the next change."},
  {"id":"q760","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"After an unrelated retail website is breached, attackers use the leaked email-and-password pairs to log into the same employees' corporate accounts. Which of the following password weaknesses made this possible?","options":["Password reuse","Insufficient password length","Missing password expiration","Weak password complexity"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Password reuse. Using the same password on the breached site and at work let attackers replay the leaked credentials against corporate accounts, a credential-stuffing attack. Insufficient length loses because the password could be long yet still fail once reused on a compromised third-party site. The tell: reuse = same password across sites enabling stuffing; length and complexity address guessing, not replay of a known-good password."},
  {"id":"q761","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"To stop employees from reusing passwords across sites, a security team wants a tool that generates and stores a unique, strong password for every account behind one master secret. Which of the following BEST meets this need?","options":["Single sign-on","A passwordless scheme","A password manager","Multifactor authentication"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Password manager. It generates and vaults a unique strong password per site behind one master secret, directly eliminating reuse. Single sign-on loses because it reduces logins to one identity rather than storing many distinct site passwords. The tell: password manager = many unique vaulted credentials; single sign-on = one credential authenticating to many apps."},
  {"id":"q762","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A team debates whether to require special characters in their 8-character passwords or to raise the minimum length to 16 characters. Which of the following changes MOST increases resistance to brute-force cracking?","options":["Adding special characters at 8 characters","Increasing the length to 16 characters","Requiring mixed-case letters at 8 characters","Forcing a password change every 30 days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Length. Each added character multiplies the search space far more than adding character classes to a short password, so 16 characters beats an 8-character password with symbols. Adding special characters loses because it grows variety only modestly while length grows the keyspace exponentially. The tell: length = dominant strength driver from a larger keyspace; complexity = character variety, a weaker lever; expiration does not affect crack resistance."},
  {"id":"q763","domain":"D4","family":"password-security","question":"A company rolls out passkeys so users sign in with a device biometric and a FIDO2 credential, with no shared secret ever typed or stored on the server. Which of the following BEST describes this authentication approach?","options":["A password manager","Password complexity enforcement","Single sign-on","Passwordless authentication"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Passwordless. Authentication uses a biometric plus a device-bound FIDO2 credential with no shared secret typed or stored, removing the phishing and reuse surface of passwords entirely. A password manager loses because it still stores and submits passwords - the secret persists. The tell: passwordless = no shared secret exists; password manager = secrets still exist, just vaulted."},
  {"id":"q764","domain":"D4","family":"pam","question":"An administrator's account normally carries standard user rights. When a privileged task arises, the PAM system elevates the account to admin for exactly 30 minutes, then strips the elevation automatically. Which of the following PAM techniques is in use?","options":["just-in-time permissions","password vaulting","ephemeral credentials","privileged session management"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Just-in-time permissions. Elevating rights only when needed and auto-revoking them afterward is JIT, eliminating standing privilege. The tell: just-in-time = temporary elevation of an existing account, password vaulting = checking secrets out of a store, ephemeral credentials = short-lived throwaway credentials, session management = monitored privileged sessions."},
  {"id":"q765","domain":"D4","family":"pam","question":"An audit finds every administrator knows the same static root password, it has not rotated in two years, and no record shows who used it. The servers still require a local root account. Which of the following privileged access management capabilities BEST remediates these findings?","options":["just-in-time permissions","ephemeral credentials","privileged session management","password vaulting"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Password vaulting. Putting the root password in a controlled vault adds attributed checkout logging and automatic rotation while the required local account stays in place. Ephemeral credentials lose because the root account must persist - the fix is controlling its standing secret, not issuing throwaway ones. The tell: password vaulting = store and check out privileged passwords from a secured vault, ephemeral credentials = short-lived credentials that self-expire, just-in-time = temporary elevation of an existing account, session management = monitoring the privileged session itself."},
  {"id":"q766","domain":"D4","family":"pam","question":"A cloud PAM design issues access credentials that exist only for the moments they are needed and expire automatically after a single use, leaving no standing secret for an attacker to steal later. Which of the following approaches does this describe?","options":["password vaulting","just-in-time permissions","ephemeral credentials","privileged session management"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ephemeral credentials. Short-lived, single-use, self-expiring credentials mean there is nothing durable to steal. The tell: ephemeral credentials = the credential itself is temporary, just-in-time = the permission on an account is temporary, vaulting = long-lived secrets locked up, session management = oversight of the session."},
  {"id":"q767","domain":"D4","family":"pam","question":"A security engineer is explaining just-in-time permissions to an auditor. Which of the following BEST describes the capability?","options":["Granting elevated rights only while a task requires them, then revoking them automatically","Storing shared privileged passwords in a vault and rotating them after each checkout","Issuing disposable credentials that expire on their own after a single use","Placing all privileged accounts under a single governance and auditing program"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Just-in-time permissions. Rights are elevated on demand and revoked when the task ends, eliminating standing privilege; ephemeral credentials come close, but there the credential itself, not the account's rights, is what expires. The tell: just-in-time = temporary rights on an account, vaulting = stored rotating secrets, ephemeral credentials = the credential itself is disposable, PAM = the whole program."},
  {"id":"q768","domain":"D4","family":"pam","question":"A deployment pipeline requests a credential that lives just minutes - long enough for one deployment - then self-invalidates, so even an intercepted copy is worthless afterward. Which of the following PAM techniques produced it?","options":["password vaulting","ephemeral credentials","just-in-time permissions","privileged access management"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Ephemeral credentials. Machine-issued, minutes-long, single-use credentials are ephemeral by design - nothing standing to steal. The tell: ephemeral credentials = the secret itself is short-lived, just-in-time = an account's rights are short-lived, vaulting = durable secrets under lock, PAM = the umbrella."},
  {"id":"q769","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"An automated provisioning pipeline is hard-constrained: no matter what a request says, it can never expose a storage bucket to the public internet or exceed the monthly spending cap. Which of the following BEST describes these built-in constraints?","options":["ticket creation","guard rails","escalation","single point of failure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Guard rails. Hard limits that keep automation inside policy no matter the input are guard rails, protecting against both mistakes and abuse at machine speed. The tell: guard rails = boundaries on what automation may do, ticket creation = an automation use case, escalation = routing to humans, single point of failure = the concentration risk."},
  {"id":"q770","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"A team automates all onboarding through one orchestration script on one server. The day that server fails, no account can be created anywhere in the company until it is restored. Which of the following automation risks does this illustrate?","options":["guard rails","single point of failure","escalation","ticket creation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Single point of failure. Concentrating a critical process in one automation means its failure halts everything that depended on it. The tell: single point of failure = one broken piece stops the whole process, guard rails = safety limits, escalation = handing off to humans, ticket creation = a task automation handles."},
  {"id":"q771","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"When monitoring detects a failed backup job at 2 a.m., a workflow automatically opens a tracked work item in the service desk, pre-filled and assigned, so the failure is owned and followed to closure by morning. Which of the following automation capabilities is this?","options":["escalation","guard rails","continuous integration","ticket creation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Ticket creation. Automatically opening tracked work items from events guarantees problems enter the queue with no human noticing required. The tell: ticket creation = the item gets opened automatically, escalation = it gets pushed up a tier when stuck, guard rails = limits on automation's actions, continuous integration = the dev-pipeline practice."},
  {"id":"q772","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"A service desk workflow watches every open item; anything unacknowledged past its SLA is automatically routed up to the next support tier with an alert to that tier's lead. Which of the following automation capabilities is this?","options":["ticket creation","guard rails","escalation","continuous integration"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Escalation. Automatically pushing stalled or overdue items to a higher tier is escalation, enforcing that nothing quietly rots in the queue. The tell: escalation = up the chain when stuck, ticket creation = into the queue at the start, guard rails = boundaries on automated actions, CI = code integration practice."},
  {"id":"q773","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"A DevOps team's deployment pipeline automatically rejects any infrastructure template that disables storage encryption or opens administrative ports to the internet, no matter who submits it. Which of the following automation use cases does this describe?","options":["Security groups","Continuous integration and testing","Guard rails","Escalation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Guard rails. Automated policy boundaries block dangerous actions before they deploy, keeping fast-moving teams inside policy without manual review; security groups lose because they are the network access rule sets themselves, not limits on what actions are allowed. The tell: guard rails = automated limits on actions, security groups = managed network access rules, continuous integration = automatic build and test of code changes, escalation = routing an item up when a threshold hits."},
  {"id":"q774","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"A workflow reacts to each DLP alert by opening a pre-populated case in the tracking system; a later rule moves any case untouched for four hours to a senior analyst queue. Which of the following use cases does the case-opening step demonstrate?","options":["Escalation","Ticket creation","Guard rails","User provisioning"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Ticket creation. The alert automatically becomes a tracked, pre-filled work item so nothing is dropped or forgotten; escalation loses because it is the second behavior, the routing upward after the four-hour threshold passes. The tell: ticket creation = auto-open a tracked work item, escalation = auto-route it to a higher tier when severity or time thresholds hit, guard rails = automated limits that block dangerous actions."},
  {"id":"q775","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"At 03:00 a SIEM flags logins to one account from two continents within minutes. Before any analyst responds, a workflow disables the account and terminates its active sessions. Which of the following automation use cases does this demonstrate?","options":["Continuous integration and testing","Ticket creation","Guard rails","Enabling and disabling services and access"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Enabling and disabling services and access. A scripted response action cuts off a likely compromised account at machine speed instead of waiting for a human; ticket creation loses because opening a work item tracks the event but does not stop it. The tell: enabling/disabling access = automated response that turns access off or on, ticket creation = auto-open a tracked item, guard rails = prevent dangerous configuration actions before they happen."},
  {"id":"q776","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"Every time a developer commits code, the pipeline automatically compiles the change and runs static and dynamic security tests before it is allowed to merge. Which of the following automation use cases is in place?","options":["Continuous integration and testing","Guard rails","Integrations and APIs","User provisioning"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Continuous integration and testing. Every code change is automatically built and security-tested, catching flaws consistently before they reach production; guard rails lose because they block dangerous infrastructure actions rather than build and test code changes. The tell: continuous integration = automatic build plus test on every commit, guard rails = policy limits on actions, integrations and APIs = tools invoking each other programmatically."},
  {"id":"q777","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"A security engineer needs 20 identical hardened web servers for a product launch. A template deploys all of them in minutes with the approved baseline already applied. Which of the following automation use cases does this illustrate?","options":["User provisioning","Resource provisioning","Security groups","Escalation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Resource provisioning. Infrastructure is deployed automatically from a template with security baked in, so every server arrives configured the same way; user provisioning loses because it automates account creation and removal, not servers. The tell: resource provisioning = automated deployment of infrastructure, user provisioning = automated account lifecycle, security groups = programmatic network access rules, escalation = routing work items upward."},
  {"id":"q778","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"A nightly script compares cloud firewall rule sets against policy, closes ports that were opened outside change control, and opens ports a newly approved service requires. Which of the following is being automated?","options":["Security groups","Guard rails","Ticket creation","Continuous integration and testing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Security groups. The script programmatically manages the cloud network access rule groupings, opening and closing ports per policy; guard rails lose because they prevent noncompliant actions up front rather than manage the rule sets themselves. The tell: security groups = automated management of network access rules, guard rails = automated limits that block dangerous actions, ticket creation = auto-open a tracked work item."},
  {"id":"q779","domain":"D4","family":"automation","question":"When the SIEM alerts on beaconing from a workstation, an automated SOAR playbook calls the EDR platform through a documented programmatic interface to isolate the host, with no analyst involved. Which of the following makes this cross-tool workflow possible?","options":["Escalation","Guard rails","Integrations and APIs","Continuous integration and testing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Integrations and APIs. The tools talk to each other programmatically, letting the SOAR playbook invoke the EDR isolation action directly; escalation loses because it routes a work item to a higher tier of humans rather than connecting tools to each other. The tell: integrations and APIs = the glue that lets one tool invoke another, escalation = auto-route up the chain, continuous integration = automatic build and test of code changes."},
  {"id":"q780","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"Confirming ransomware is spreading between file shares, the response team immediately disables the infected VLAN's uplinks and blocks the malware's ports at every switch - stopping movement while the malware itself remains on the infected hosts. Which of the following incident response phases is this?","options":["eradication","containment","recovery","preparation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Containment. Stopping the spread while the threat still exists inside is containment; removal comes next as eradication. The tell: containment = limit the damage radius, eradication = remove the threat, recovery = restore service, preparation = the readiness beforehand."},
  {"id":"q781","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"A new analyst is documenting the incident response lifecycle in the team's runbook. Which of the following lists the phases in the correct order?","options":["detection, preparation, containment, analysis, recovery, eradication, lessons learned","containment, detection, analysis, preparation, eradication, lessons learned, recovery","preparation, analysis, detection, eradication, containment, recovery, lessons learned","preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, lessons learned"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, lessons learned. Ready first, notice it, understand it, stop its spread, remove it, restore service, then learn. The tell: preparation can only come first and lessons learned only last; containment must precede eradication - you stop the bleeding before you operate."},
  {"id":"q782","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"An incident response team has just cut the infected hosts off from the network, stopping the malware's spread. According to the standard incident response lifecycle, which of the following phases comes NEXT?","options":["eradication","recovery","detection","lessons learned"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Eradication. Containment is done; next the threat itself is removed - malware deleted, persistence cleared, the entry vulnerability closed. The lifecycle: preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, lessons learned. The tell: containment = stop the spread, eradication = remove the cause, recovery = restore operations."},
  {"id":"q783","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"After an alert fires, responders confirm the event is a genuine incident, then scope it: how many systems, which accounts, what data. No action to stop it has been taken yet. Which of the following incident response phases does this describe?","options":["detection","analysis","containment","preparation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Analysis. Confirming the incident is real and determining its scope is analysis; detection was the alert itself, and containment has not begun. The tell: detection = noticing something, analysis = confirming and scoping it, containment = stopping the spread, preparation = everything done before any incident."},
  {"id":"q784","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"The malware is removed, persistence mechanisms are cleared, and the vulnerability that let it in is patched - systems are clean but not yet back in service. Per the incident response lifecycle, which of the following phases comes NEXT?","options":["containment","lessons learned","recovery","eradication"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Recovery. With eradication complete, recovery restores systems to production - rebuilding, restoring data, monitoring for recurrence. The tell: eradication = threat removed, recovery = business restored, lessons learned = the retrospective after normality returns."},
  {"id":"q785","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"Two weeks after operations returned to normal, the whole team meets to document what worked, what failed, and which playbooks and tools need improvement before the next incident. Which of the following phases does this describe?","options":["preparation","recovery","analysis","lessons learned"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Lessons learned. The post-incident retrospective converts the incident into better preparation, closing the lifecycle loop. The tell: lessons learned = the look-back that improves the process, preparation = where its outputs get applied, recovery = restoring service, analysis = scoping during the incident."},
  {"id":"q786","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"Long before any alert fires, a security team writes response playbooks, stocks a forensic jump kit, defines call trees, and trains staff in their roles. Which of the following incident response phases covers this work?","options":["detection","preparation","analysis","recovery"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Preparation. Everything done in advance - playbooks, tools, training - is the preparation phase, and it determines how well every later phase goes. The tell: preparation = before any incident, detection = the moment one is noticed, analysis = scoping it, recovery = restoring afterward."},
  {"id":"q787","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"At 3:40 a.m., a monitoring alert flags unusual outbound traffic from a database server - the first moment anyone realizes something might be wrong. Which of the following incident response phases does this alert represent?","options":["preparation","analysis","detection","containment"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Detection. The initial recognition that something may be wrong is detection; confirming and scoping it comes next as analysis. The tell: detection = noticing, analysis = confirming and sizing, containment = stopping the spread, preparation = the readiness built beforehand."},
  {"id":"q788","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"Every affected system has been fully restored, validated, and returned to normal production; the incident is operationally over. According to the incident response lifecycle, which of the following phases comes NEXT?","options":["lessons learned","containment","eradication","detection"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Lessons learned. After recovery completes, the retrospective closes the loop: what failed, what worked, what changes before next time. The tell: recovery = back to normal, lessons learned = the mandatory look-back that follows, containment/eradication = already behind you by then."},
  {"id":"q789","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"After service is restored, leadership asks what allowed the breach so it never recurs. The team traces past the phishing email to an unpatched VPN gateway with no MFA. Which of the following activities is this?","options":["Root cause analysis","Lessons-learned review","Threat hunting","Containment"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Root cause analysis. RCA identifies the underlying cause that made the incident possible - the unpatched, MFA-less VPN - rather than the phishing symptom, so the fix prevents recurrence. Lessons learned is close but is the broader retrospective on how the whole response went, not the technical causation finding. The tell: root cause analysis = why it was possible from the technical underlying cause; lessons learned = whole-response after-action review."},
  {"id":"q790","domain":"D4","family":"ir-process","question":"Fresh indicators of compromise for a new campaign arrive, and although no alert has fired, analysts proactively sweep months of historical logs and EDR telemetry for matches. Which of the following activities is this?","options":["Incident response","Threat hunting","Root cause analysis","Continuous monitoring"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Threat hunting. It is a proactive, hypothesis-driven search through existing telemetry for threats that evaded detection, triggered by intelligence rather than an alert. Incident response loses because that reacts to an already-detected event, whereas here nothing has alerted and analysts go looking. The tell: threat hunting = proactive hunt with no alert; incident response = reaction to a confirmed detection; monitoring = automated alerting on rules."},
  {"id":"q791","domain":"D4","family":"ir-testing","question":"No alerts have fired, but analysts spend the week proactively combing the environment - odd persistence mechanisms, unusual lateral connections - for an attacker who may have slipped past every detection tool. Which of the following BEST describes this activity?","options":["threat hunting","root cause analysis","tabletop","simulation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Threat hunting. Proactively searching for intrusions that generated no alerts assumes the tools missed something. The tell: threat hunting = look for what detection missed, root cause analysis = explain an incident you already found, tabletop/simulation = exercises testing the response plan."},
  {"id":"q792","domain":"D4","family":"ir-testing","question":"After restoring service from a breach-driven outage, the team works backward through logs and evidence to identify the single underlying failure - an unpatched gateway - that made the whole incident possible. Which of the following activities does this describe?","options":["threat hunting","simulation","root cause analysis","tabletop"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Root cause analysis. Tracing an incident back to the fundamental failure that enabled it is RCA, feeding fixes that prevent recurrence. The tell: root cause analysis = why did this happen at bottom, threat hunting = is something here we have not seen, tabletop/simulation = practicing the response."},
  {"id":"q793","domain":"D4","family":"ir-testing","question":"An incident response exercise consists entirely of discussion: participants around a table talk through their actions in a scripted scenario, and no live system is touched at any point. Which of the following exercise types is this?","options":["simulation","threat hunting","root cause analysis","tabletop"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tabletop. Discussion-only plan walkthroughs are tabletops: lowest cost, zero operational risk, great for exposing gaps in the plan. The tell: tabletop = talk through it, simulation = act it out against real or staged systems, threat hunting = searching for real intruders, RCA = explaining a real incident."},
  {"id":"q794","domain":"D4","family":"ir-testing","question":"To test true readiness, the team runs a controlled mock intrusion: injects realistic artifacts into a staging environment and has responders actually execute their playbook steps, not merely describe them. Which of the following exercise types is this?","options":["tabletop","root cause analysis","simulation","threat hunting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Simulation. Executing real response steps against a controlled scenario is a simulation, the hands-on step past the tabletop. The tell: simulation = do the steps, tabletop = say the steps, threat hunting = hunt real adversaries, root cause analysis = post-incident why."},
  {"id":"q795","domain":"D4","family":"ir-testing","question":"New SOC analysts walk through the incident response playbooks, tooling, and their assigned roles before they join the on-call rotation. Which of the following BEST describes this preparation activity?","options":["Tabletop exercise","Hands-on simulation exercise","Incident response training","Lessons-learned review"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Training. Teaching responders the plan, tools, and roles before an incident is IR training, part of preparation. A tabletop exercise loses because that gathers an already-trained team to talk through a scenario to test the plan, not to teach it for the first time. The tell: training = learn the plan and tools beforehand; tabletop = discuss a scenario to test readiness; simulation = act the scenario out."},
  {"id":"q796","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"Defense counsel discovers a four-hour window where nobody documented who possessed the seized drive between the field collection and the lab. The judge is asked to exclude the evidence entirely. Which of the following failures created this risk?","options":["a broken chain of custody","a missing legal hold","incomplete e-discovery","an unsigned report"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A broken chain of custody. Any undocumented gap in possession invites the claim that evidence was altered, and courts may toss it - the chain's value is its unbroken continuity. The tell: chain of custody = every handoff logged, no gaps, legal hold = the preservation order, e-discovery = production for litigation, reporting = the findings document."},
  {"id":"q797","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"Every time a seized laptop drive changes hands - collector to examiner to evidence locker - each person signs a log recording who took custody, when, and why, keeping the trail unbroken for court. Which of the following terms describes this record?","options":["legal hold","chain of custody","acquisition","e-discovery"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Chain of custody. The unbroken, signed record of who held the evidence and when is what keeps it admissible; a gap invites the tampering argument. The tell: chain of custody = the handling trail, legal hold = the do-not-delete order, acquisition = collecting the evidence, e-discovery = producing it for litigation."},
  {"id":"q798","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"Anticipating litigation, the legal team directs that all emails and files of certain employees must not be deleted or altered - even where normal retention schedules would purge them. Which of the following terms describes this directive?","options":["legal hold","preservation","chain of custody","acquisition"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Legal hold. The formal directive suspending deletion because litigation is anticipated is the legal hold, which overrides retention schedules. The tell: legal hold = the binding do-not-delete order, preservation = the general practice of protecting evidence, chain of custody = the handling record, acquisition = the collection step."},
  {"id":"q799","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"An examiner attaches a write blocker and creates a bit-for-bit forensic image of the suspect drive, hashing both to prove the copy is exact. Which of the following forensic steps does this describe?","options":["e-discovery","reporting","legal hold","acquisition"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Acquisition. Actually collecting the evidence - the verified bit-for-bit image - is acquisition. The tell: acquisition = gathering the evidence itself, legal hold = the order to preserve it, e-discovery = producing it in litigation, reporting = documenting the findings."},
  {"id":"q800","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"During active litigation, the organization must locate, collect, and hand over relevant electronic records - emails, chat logs, documents - to the opposing party's legal team. Which of the following forensic processes is this?","options":["legal hold","chain of custody","preservation","e-discovery"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"E-discovery. Identifying, collecting, and producing electronic records for litigation is electronic discovery. The tell: e-discovery = produce it for the lawsuit, legal hold = the earlier do-not-delete order, preservation = keeping evidence intact, chain of custody = the handling trail."},
  {"id":"q801","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"With the investigation complete, the forensic analyst compiles a formal document detailing the methods used, the evidence examined, and the conclusions reached, written to survive courtroom scrutiny. Which of the following forensic steps is this?","options":["reporting","acquisition","chain of custody","legal hold"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reporting. The formal write-up of methods, evidence, and conclusions is the reporting step that carries the investigation into court. The tell: reporting = the final document, acquisition = collecting, chain of custody = the custody log, legal hold = the preservation order."},
  {"id":"q802","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"A retention schedule will purge email older than three years this weekend. General counsel tells IT that a lawsuit from a former distributor is expected. Which of the following should be put in place FIRST?","options":["E-discovery","Legal hold","Chain of custody","Acquisition"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Legal hold. Anticipated litigation triggers a formal preservation order that suspends the normal retention schedule for relevant data, and letting the purge run risks spoliation sanctions; e-discovery loses because producing records comes later - the data must first be preserved from deletion. The tell: legal hold = preserve everything relevant when litigation is anticipated, overriding retention; e-discovery = locate and produce the records for the legal process; chain of custody = the handling log for collected evidence."},
  {"id":"q803","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"An incident responder reaches a powered-on server suspected of compromise and must begin collecting evidence. Which of the following should be captured FIRST?","options":["The contents of system memory","A bit-for-bit image of the disk drive","Archived backups of the server","Exported copies of retained log archives"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The contents of system memory. Order of volatility requires collecting the most short-lived evidence first, and RAM disappears the moment power is lost; the disk image loses because disk contents persist after shutdown and can be acquired later. The tell: order of volatility runs CPU state and RAM first, then disk, then backups and archives - capture memory before anything that survives a power-off."},
  {"id":"q804","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"A junior analyst boots a seized laptop, browses the file system, and copies the user's folders to a USB drive for review. Which of the following BEST describes the flaw in this evidence handling?","options":["A legal hold was never issued by counsel before the collection began","The laptop's transfers between handlers were never recorded in a custody log","The recovered files were never produced to opposing counsel through e-discovery","The original was altered because no write blocker or bit-for-bit image was used"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Acquisition was not forensically sound: booting the laptop and copying files changed timestamps and system state, where a write blocker and a hash-verified bit-for-bit image would have left the original untouched. The custody-log option loses because no undocumented transfer is described; the flaw shown is the alteration itself. The tell: sound acquisition = write blocker, bit-for-bit image, matching hashes; chain of custody = documenting who held the evidence; legal hold = a preservation order, not an imaging method."},
  {"id":"q805","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"A forensic analyst has finished examining a compromised server and is preparing the case deliverable for the legal team. Which of the following BEST describes what the reporting activity must accomplish?","options":["Documenting methods and findings so another examiner could reproduce them","Logging every transfer of evidence between handlers with times and signatures","Suspending deletion of data relevant to expected litigation","Producing electronic records to the opposing party's counsel"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reporting. The examiner documents methodology, evidence, and conclusions in a form a peer could reproduce and a court could scrutinize; the transfer log loses because that is chain of custody, which records who possessed evidence, not what the analysis found. The tell: reporting = reproducible documentation of findings, chain of custody = the who-held-it-when record, legal hold = the preservation order, e-discovery = producing records for the legal process."},
  {"id":"q806","domain":"D4","family":"forensics","question":"Litigation support staff search preserved mailboxes and chat exports for messages matching case terms, then deliver the responsive items to the opposing party in an agreed format. Which of the following processes is being performed?","options":["Chain of custody","E-discovery","Preservation","Reporting"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"E-discovery. Identifying, collecting, and producing electronically stored information for a legal proceeding is exactly this process, scoped by the legal hold; reporting loses because it documents an examiner's analytical findings rather than handing records to opposing counsel. The tell: e-discovery = locate and produce electronic records for litigation, preservation = keep collected evidence unaltered, chain of custody = document who handled evidence and when."},
  {"id":"q807","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"Investigating failed purchases, an analyst reads a log of stack traces, payment-gateway timeouts, and per-transaction error codes written by the e-commerce software itself. Which of the following log sources is this?","options":["OS security log","firewall log","application log","packet capture"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Application log. Events written by the application about its own operations - errors, transactions, timeouts - are application logs, invisible to OS and network logging. The tell: application log = the app narrating itself, OS security log = host logons and audit events, firewall log = boundary permit/deny, packet capture = raw traffic."},
  {"id":"q808","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"To reconstruct exactly what a compromised laptop did locally - which processes launched, what files were touched, which USB devices mounted - the analyst pulls the record generated by the device's own monitoring agent. Which of the following log sources is this?","options":["endpoint log","network log","metadata","firewall log"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Endpoint log. Host-local activity - processes, files, devices - lives in endpoint logs from the OS and EDR agent; no network source saw any of it. The tell: endpoint log = what happened ON the machine, network/firewall logs = what crossed the wire, metadata = descriptive data about communications."},
  {"id":"q809","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"To reconstruct exactly what data left the network during a breach, an analyst needs every raw byte of the traffic itself, not summaries of it. Which of the following sources provides this?","options":["metadata","packet capture","firewall log","network log"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Packet capture. Full packet capture preserves complete traffic contents, byte for byte - the only source that shows exactly what was transferred. The tell: packet capture = the whole conversation, metadata = data about the data, firewall/network logs = events and flows, not payloads."},
  {"id":"q810","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"An investigator examines an email's headers, timestamps, sender and recipient fields, and routing path - everything about the message except the message body itself. Which of the following sources is being used?","options":["packet capture","endpoint log","metadata","firewall log"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Metadata. Data describing the communication - headers, timestamps, participants - rather than its content is metadata, often enough to map an incident without reading a single body. The tell: metadata = about the message, packet capture = the message itself, endpoint/firewall logs = device and traffic events."},
  {"id":"q811","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"An analyst reconstructing an intrusion needs to see which connections were permitted or denied, by source address, destination, and port, at the network boundary. Which of the following log sources records this?","options":["endpoint log","firewall log","application log","metadata"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firewall log. Permit/deny decisions with addresses and ports are exactly what the firewall records at the boundary. The tell: firewall log = allow/deny by address and port, endpoint log = host activity, application log = app-level events, metadata = descriptive data about communications."},
  {"id":"q812","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"To trace an account compromise, an analyst pulls the record of successful and failed sign-ins, privilege use, and account changes from one server's operating system. Which of the following log sources is the analyst using?","options":["network log","firewall log","OS security log","packet capture"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"OS security log. Authentication and audit events on a specific host live in the operating system's security log (Windows Security log, Linux auth logs). The tell: OS security log = logons and account changes on the host, firewall log = boundary decisions, network log = traffic events, packet capture = raw contents."},
  {"id":"q813","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"Investigators need to determine which exploit was launched against a web server and whether the inline security appliance dropped the traffic or only raised an alert. Which of the following log sources answers BOTH questions?","options":["Firewall logs","Application logs","IPS/IDS logs","Network flow records"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"IPS/IDS logs. They record which signature fired and the action taken, showing both the attack technique and whether it was blocked or merely alerted on; firewall logs lose because they show connections permitted or denied by rule, not which exploit payload was detected. The tell: IPS/IDS logs = attack signature plus action taken, firewall logs = connection dispositions by rule, flow records = who talked to whom and how much, application logs = events inside the app."},
  {"id":"q814","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"During a post-incident review, the team finds the SIEM's weekly scheduled summary listed the attacker's account among anomalous logins ten days before detection, but nobody had opened it. Which of the following data sources contained the early warning?","options":["Dashboards","Automated reports","Packet captures","Vulnerability scan results"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Automated reports. The tool-generated periodic summary already held the indicator, so the failure was process, not visibility; dashboards lose because they are live displays watched in the moment, not scheduled documents that can sit unread. The tell: automated report = periodic generated document, dashboard = real-time visual status display, packet capture = raw traffic content, vulnerability scan = the state of known weaknesses."},
  {"id":"q815","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"A security analyst reviews firewall log entries: DENY TCP 198.51.100.7 to 10.0.0.5:21; DENY TCP 198.51.100.7 to 10.0.0.5:22; DENY TCP 198.51.100.7 to 10.0.0.5:23; DENY TCP 198.51.100.7 to 10.0.0.5:25. Which of the following is MOST likely occurring?","options":["A reconnaissance port scan","A distributed denial-of-service attack","Data exfiltration to an external host","A successful brute-force login"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A reconnaissance port scan. One external source probing sequential service ports on a single host is classic scanning to map open services, and every attempt was denied; the denial-of-service option loses because that pattern shows overwhelming traffic from many sources, not one source touching each port once. The tell: one source sweeping many ports = scanning, many sources hammering one service = distributed denial of service, large outbound transfers = exfiltration - and firewall logs record attempts with dispositions, so denies prove probing, not compromise."},
  {"id":"q816","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"A server's OS security log shows 40 failed logons for one service account between 02:01 and 02:13, a successful logon at 02:14, and creation of a new local administrator at 02:16. Which of the following MOST likely occurred?","options":["A password spraying attempt was blocked by lockout policy","An administrator completed scheduled overnight maintenance","A replay attack reused a captured authentication token","A brute-force attack succeeded and a persistence account was created"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A brute-force attack succeeded and a persistence account was created. Many failures against one account ending in a success, then an unexplained new administrator two minutes later, is the classic break-in timeline; password spraying loses because it tries a few passwords across many accounts, while this log shows many attempts against one. The tell: brute force = many passwords, one account; spraying = few passwords, many accounts; and the OS security log is the source that captures logons and account changes in one timeline."},
  {"id":"q817","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"An analyst reviews a suspicious message showing From: ceo@company.com, a Received: header naming mx1.bulk-sender.example [203.0.113.9], and Authentication-Results: spf=fail for that IP. Which of the following BEST explains the finding?","options":["The message is spoofed and came from an unauthorized server","The CEO's mailbox was compromised and used to send the message","The message was altered in transit after a legitimate send","The gateway corrupted the headers while scanning the message"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The message is spoofed and came from an unauthorized server. Header metadata records the true sending IP, and the SPF failure proves that server is not permitted to send for the domain; the compromised-mailbox option loses because mail from the real mailbox would leave the company's authorized servers and pass SPF. The tell: email headers are metadata - the routing path and authentication results reveal the actual origin regardless of the displayed From address, and spf=fail on the real sending IP points to spoofing, not tampering."},
  {"id":"q818","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"After a customer portal account is confirmed compromised, investigators must determine exactly which customer records the account viewed and exported through the portal. Which of the following log sources provides this?","options":["OS security logs","Firewall logs","Application logs","Endpoint logs"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Application logs. The portal software records in-app actions such as record views, searches, and exports tied to the account; firewall logs lose because they show connections to the server, not which records were touched inside the app. The tell: application logs = events inside the software, OS security logs = system logons and audit events, firewall logs = connection dispositions, endpoint logs = activity on the user's host."},
  {"id":"q819","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"A user reports opening a phishing attachment. Investigators need to confirm whether the file actually executed on the laptop and what child processes it spawned. Which of the following sources provides this evidence?","options":["Email metadata","IPS/IDS logs","Network logs","Endpoint logs"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Endpoint logs. EDR and host telemetry record process execution chains on the machine itself, showing whether the attachment ran and what it launched; email metadata loses because headers show where the message came from, not what happened after it was opened. The tell: endpoint logs = what ran on the host, email metadata = the message's origin and routing, IPS/IDS logs = attacks detected on the wire, network logs = who connected where."},
  {"id":"q820","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"An IDS alert names only the internal address 10.4.8.77 at 14:32. Investigators must identify which laptop held that address at that moment before any host analysis can start. Which of the following log sources resolves this?","options":["Endpoint logs","Application logs","Network logs","Metadata"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Network logs. DHCP lease records and switch logs map an IP address to a specific device and port at a point in time; endpoint logs lose because host telemetry cannot be pulled until the investigators know which host the address belonged to. The tell: network logs = DHCP, DNS, and flow records tying addresses to devices and lookups; endpoint logs = activity on an already-identified host; metadata = data about files and messages, not address leases."},
  {"id":"q821","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"During a breach investigation, the team wants to know whether the exploited flaw had already been identified on the compromised host and left unremediated before the attack. Which of the following data sources answers this?","options":["Packet captures","Vulnerability scan results","Dashboards","Automated reports"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Vulnerability scan results. Prior scan data records which weaknesses were flagged on the host at the time, showing whether the exploited flaw was known and unfixed, which feeds root cause analysis; automated reports lose because they are scheduled summaries of tool output, while the scan data itself is the record of the host's flaws. The tell: vulnerability scans = the state of weaknesses at a point in time, logs and captures = events as they happened, dashboards = live status, automated reports = periodic summaries."},
  {"id":"q822","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"During an active incident bridge, a SOC lead needs a continuously updating view of alert volume and affected segments to track spread, before assigning analysts to dig into raw logs. Which of the following BEST serves this need?","options":["Automated reports","Packet captures","Vulnerability scan results","Dashboards"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Dashboards. A live visual display gives moment-to-moment situational awareness during the incident and acts as the pivot point into deeper data; automated reports lose because a scheduled document cannot track a situation changing by the minute. The tell: dashboard = real-time glanceable status, automated report = periodic generated document, raw logs and packet captures = the detailed evidence analysts pivot into afterward."},
  {"id":"q823","domain":"D4","family":"log-sources","question":"Flow records prove 40 GB left the network toward an unknown host overnight, but regulators require proof of exactly which files and contents were transferred. Which of the following sources can provide it?","options":["Packet captures","Network flow records","Firewall logs","IPS/IDS logs"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Packet captures. Full raw traffic includes payloads, letting investigators reconstruct sessions and recover the actual transferred content; flow records lose because they summarize who talked to whom and how much, never the bytes themselves. The tell: packet capture = full content, flow records = volume and endpoints only, firewall logs = permit and deny decisions, IPS/IDS logs = which signatures fired - and the capture must already have been running to hold the evidence."},
  {"id":"q824","domain":"D4","family":"hardening-targets","question":"An OT engineer must harden programmable logic controllers that run a vendor real-time OS: no endpoint agents can be installed, and patches arrive only at annual maintenance windows. Which of the following actions BEST reduces the risk?","options":["Deploy endpoint detection and response agents to each controller","Apply operating system patches as soon as the vendor releases them","Isolate the controllers on a restricted network segment","Enroll the controllers in the mobile device management platform"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Segmentation with strict access rules. When ICS/SCADA gear cannot take agents or timely patches, compensating network controls shrink what can reach the flaw; agents and immediate patching both violate the stated constraints. The tell: match the hardening action to the device class: agents and rapid patching fit servers and workstations, while unpatchable ICS, embedded, and IoT gear gets segmentation and compensating controls."},
  {"id":"q825","domain":"D4","family":"hardening-targets","question":"A network technician is racking new access switches for a branch office. Before connecting them to the production network, which of the following hardening steps should the technician perform FIRST?","options":["Change the default administrative credentials","Disable all unused switch ports","Restrict management sessions to SSH only","Move management traffic to a dedicated VLAN"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Change the default credentials. Out-of-box passwords are published and actively scanned for, so eliminating them precedes every other step; disabling unused ports matters, but a default admin login undoes all of it instantly. The tell: all four are legitimate switch hardening moves, and default credentials come first because they are the fastest, best-known way in."},
  {"id":"q826","domain":"D4","family":"hardening-targets","question":"A security engineer inherits a cloud environment where the management console accepts password-only logins and a storage bucket is publicly readable. Which of the following BEST hardens this environment?","options":["Disable unused physical ports on the data center core switches","Install host-based firewalls on all employee workstations","Push signed firmware updates to the wireless access points","Require console MFA and remove public access from the bucket"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"MFA on the console plus closing the public bucket. Cloud hardening targets identity and exposed storage because the console and buckets are the internet-facing attack surface; the other actions harden switches, workstations, and access points, none of which the findings involve. The tell: match the hardening action to the device class: console MFA and bucket permissions are cloud infrastructure moves, while port and firmware work belongs to network gear."},
  {"id":"q827","domain":"D4","family":"hardening-targets","question":"An office installs smart TVs and connected thermostats that cannot run the corporate endpoint agent. Which of the following BEST limits the risk these devices introduce?","options":["Install the endpoint agent using a lightweight configuration","Change the default credentials and use a dedicated VLAN","Enable full-disk encryption and screen locks on each device","Require the devices to authenticate through the VPN concentrator"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Change defaults and isolate on a dedicated VLAN. IoT devices ship with well-known passwords and cannot host security software, so credential hygiene plus segmentation contains whatever they catch; any agent option contradicts the stated constraint. The tell: match the hardening action to the device class: where software cannot be installed, network isolation and default-credential changes carry the load."},
  {"id":"q828","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-connections","question":"Several hotel guests report stolen credentials after their laptops joined an attacker's access point broadcasting the same network name as the hotel's. Which of the following connection methods was exploited?","options":["Wi-Fi","Cellular","Bluetooth","NFC"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Wi-Fi. A rogue access point imitating a legitimate network name is an evil twin, an attack unique to Wi-Fi association; cellular's rogue equivalent is a fake base station, not a named access point. The tell: match the radio to its attack: evil twins and open hotspots are Wi-Fi, rogue base stations are cellular, unsolicited pairing is Bluetooth."},
  {"id":"q829","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-connections","question":"While waiting at an airport gate, a user's phone repeatedly displays pairing requests from an unknown nearby device. Which of the following settings changes BEST stops the unwanted requests?","options":["Disable automatic joining of open Wi-Fi networks","Turn off cellular data roaming","Set Bluetooth to non-discoverable mode","Remove all saved wireless network profiles"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Set Bluetooth to non-discoverable. Pairing requests arrive over Bluetooth, and hiding the device from discovery stops strangers from initiating them; the Wi-Fi and cellular options adjust radios that play no part in pairing. The tell: match the radio to its attack: unsolicited pairing while discoverable is Bluetooth, evil twins and open hotspots are Wi-Fi, rogue base stations are cellular."},
  {"id":"q830","domain":"D4","family":"mobile-connections","question":"A security manager reviewing egress controls realizes some smartphone traffic never passes through the corporate firewall or web filter, even when users are inside the building. Which of the following connection methods MOST likely explains this?","options":["Corporate Wi-Fi","Guest Wi-Fi","Bluetooth","Cellular"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Cellular. Traffic over the carrier's network goes straight to the internet and never touches the corporate perimeter, so on-premises controls cannot see or filter it; guest Wi-Fi is tempting but still rides equipment the company controls. The tell: cellular bypasses the corporate perimeter entirely, both Wi-Fi networks traverse company infrastructure, and Bluetooth is short-range pairing, not an internet path."},
  {"id":"q831","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-security","question":"A penetration tester captures a WPA2 four-way handshake at a small office and cracks the passphrase offline overnight. The office has no RADIUS server and must keep using a shared passphrase. Which of the following upgrades BEST prevents a repeat?","options":["WPA2-Enterprise","WPA3-Personal","A longer WPA2 passphrase","Disabling SSID broadcast"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"WPA3-Personal. SAE replaces the WPA2 four-way handshake, so a captured exchange cannot be brute-forced offline even with a modest passphrase; WPA2-Enterprise would also remove the shared secret but requires the RADIUS server the office lacks. The tell: SAE defeats offline dictionary attacks on captured handshakes, while longer passphrases only slow the same crack and hidden SSIDs stop nothing."},
  {"id":"q832","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-security","question":"A build checklist for a new enterprise WLAN has two blank fields: the cryptographic protocol and the authentication protocol. Which of the following correctly fills both fields?","options":["Cryptographic: WPA3, authentication: 802.1X with EAP","Cryptographic: 802.1X with EAP, authentication: WPA3","Cryptographic: RADIUS, authentication: AES","Cryptographic: SAE, authentication: WEP"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"WPA3 for encryption with 802.1X/EAP for authentication. WPA3 defines how traffic is encrypted while 802.1X carries EAP to prove who is connecting, so each blank gets the knob that does that job; the second option is tempting because it lists the right names with the roles swapped. The tell: the encryption choice and the identity-proving choice are two separate settings: WPA3/AES encrypts, 802.1X/EAP authenticates, RADIUS is the server behind 802.1X, and WEP is a broken legacy cipher."},
  {"id":"q833","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-security","question":"A hospital requires every clinician to join the wireless network with individual directory credentials checked by a central server, so a departing employee's access can be revoked without re-keying any devices. Which of the following should be deployed?","options":["WPA3-Personal","Enhanced Open","WPA3-Enterprise","WPA2-Personal"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"WPA3-Enterprise. It authenticates each user through 802.1X against a RADIUS server, so access is per-person and individually revocable; WPA3-Personal is tempting for the modern encryption but still shares one passphrase everyone would have to change. The tell: Personal modes share a passphrase, Enterprise modes authenticate individuals against a central server, and Enhanced Open encrypts open networks without authenticating anyone."},
  {"id":"q834","domain":"D4","family":"wireless-security","question":"A wireless engineer proposes replacing WPA2-Personal across the campus network. Which of the following BEST describes the security improvement WPA3's SAE handshake provides over the WPA2 pre-shared key exchange?","options":["It hides the network name from unauthorized clients","It requires each user to present individual directory credentials","It removes the need for any passphrase on the network","Captured handshakes cannot be cracked offline for the passphrase"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Offline cracking resistance. SAE's exchange never exposes material an attacker can grind through a dictionary, so capturing the handshake yields nothing, unlike WPA2's crackable four-way handshake; individual credentials describe Enterprise mode, a separate choice from SAE. The tell: SAE defeats offline dictionary attacks on captured handshakes but still uses a shared passphrase, so the passphrase-free and per-user options describe Enhanced Open and WPA3-Enterprise instead."},
  {"id":"q835","domain":"D4","family":"remediation-validation","question":"An analyst deployed the vendor patch for a critical finding across all web servers on Friday. Which of the following should the analyst do NEXT before closing the finding?","options":["Submit a formal exception for the finding","Run the vulnerability scanner against the servers again","Commission an independent audit of the patching process","Remove the servers from future scan scopes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Rescanning. A finding closes only when the scanner confirms it no longer appears, making the rescan the standard step between deployment and closure; an audit reviews whether the process was followed and comes later, if at all, not as the ticket-closure check. The tell: rescan = the tool re-check that gates closure, verification = hands-on confirmation the fix works, audit = independent review of the remediation process."},
  {"id":"q836","domain":"D4","family":"remediation-validation","question":"A scanner still flags a host after patching, so an engineer logs in, confirms the installed version, and safely re-runs the exploit to prove it now fails. Which of the following validation activities does this describe?","options":["Rescanning","Auditing","Patching","Verification"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Verification. Directly inspecting the configuration and re-testing the exploit is hands-on confirmation that the fix really works, which settles whether the lingering alert is a false positive; rescanning is tempting but is the automated tool check that already ran and disagreed. The tell: rescan = tool re-check, verification = direct human confirmation the fix holds, audit = independent review that the process was followed."},
  {"id":"q837","domain":"D4","family":"remediation-validation","question":"Each quarter, an independent reviewer samples closed vulnerability tickets to confirm every fix was approved, tested, and documented according to the remediation procedure. Which of the following validation activities is being performed?","options":["Rescanning","Verification","An audit","Reporting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An audit. Independent, structured review of whether the remediation process was followed - approvals, testing, documentation - is auditing rather than re-testing any host; verification is tempting but means hands-on confirmation that a specific fix works. The tell: audit = independent review of process compliance, verification = direct confirmation a fix works, rescan = running the scanner again, reporting = communicating posture metrics to stakeholders."},
  {"id":"q838","domain":"D4","family":"secure-protocols","question":"A network administrator's packet capture of a device login shows two cleartext lines, 'Username: admin' and 'Password: Sw1tch1!', both sent to TCP port 23. Which of the following BEST secures this management access?","options":["Replace Telnet with SSH and allow only TCP 22","Replace Telnet with FTPS and allow only TCP 990","Keep Telnet but restrict it to TCP 8023","Replace Telnet with LDAPS and allow only TCP 636"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"SSH. Telnet on TCP 23 carries credentials in cleartext, so its secure replacement is SSH, which encrypts the interactive session on TCP 22 and lets the old port be closed. FTPS loses because it secures file transfer, not an interactive device shell. The tell: Telnet 23 to SSH 22 for remote administration; FTPS and SFTP replace FTP file transfer, LDAPS replaces directory binds on 636."},
  {"id":"q839","domain":"D4","family":"secure-protocols","question":"A systems engineer must encrypt server-to-server file transfers and wants them to reuse the organization's existing SSH key infrastructure instead of deploying TLS certificates. Which of the following protocols BEST fits?","options":["FTPS","FTP over TCP 21","SFTP","HTTPS"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SFTP. It runs file transfer over SSH, so it reuses the existing SSH keys and listens on TCP 22, matching the stated constraint. FTPS loses because it secures FTP with TLS, requiring the certificate infrastructure the team wants to avoid. The tell: SFTP = file transfer over SSH on port 22; FTPS = file transfer over TLS with certificates; plain FTP has no encryption at all."},
  {"id":"q840","domain":"D4","family":"secure-protocols","question":"An auditor flags that domain controllers accept directory authentication binds over cleartext TCP port 389. Which of the following BEST remediates the finding?","options":["Migrate the binds to SSH on TCP 22","Migrate the binds to LDAPS on TCP 636","Migrate the binds to HTTPS on TCP 443","Migrate the binds to SNMPv3 on UDP 161"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"LDAPS. Cleartext LDAP on 389 exposes directory credentials, so wrapping the directory protocol in TLS as LDAPS on TCP 636 protects the binds. SSH loses because it secures interactive shells, not directory lookups. The tell: LDAP 389 to LDAPS 636; SSH secures shells on 22, HTTPS secures web on 443, SNMPv3 secures device polling on 161."},
  {"id":"q841","domain":"D4","family":"secure-protocols","question":"A monitoring server polls switches with SNMP, and a scan shows the community strings crossing the network in cleartext. Which of the following BEST protects the polling data in transit?","options":["SNMP v2c with a longer community string","SNMP v1 restricted to a management VLAN","SNMP v2c allowed only from the poller's IP","SNMPv3"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"SNMPv3. Only SNMPv3 adds authentication and encryption; v1 and v2c send community strings in cleartext no matter how complex the string or how the traffic is filtered. A restricted VLAN or source-IP rule narrows exposure but still transmits the strings in the clear, so those lose. The tell: SNMPv3 is the secure version on 161; a strong community string or an ACL is not encryption."},
  {"id":"q842","domain":"D4","family":"secure-protocols","question":"Attackers are feeding clients forged DNS responses that redirect them to malicious hosts. Which of the following BEST ensures the authenticity of DNS records, even though it does not encrypt the queries?","options":["DNS over TLS","HTTPS","DNSSEC","LDAPS"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DNSSEC. It signs DNS records so resolvers can validate that answers are authentic and unaltered, defeating forged responses; it provides integrity and authenticity, not confidentiality. DNS over TLS loses because it encrypts queries for privacy but does not sign the records to prove authenticity. The tell: DNSSEC = signed, authentic DNS answers for integrity; DNS over TLS or HTTPS = encrypted DNS for confidentiality."},
  {"id":"q843","domain":"D4","family":"secure-protocols","question":"After replacing HTTP with HTTPS on a public web server, a firewall administrator must add a rule permitting the encrypted web traffic. Which of the following ports should the new rule permit?","options":["TCP 443","TCP 80","TCP 22","TCP 389"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"TCP 443. HTTPS wraps HTTP in TLS and listens on port 443, so the rule must permit TCP 443 while the old TCP 80 is closed. Port 80 loses because it is the cleartext HTTP port being retired. The tell: HTTP 80 to HTTPS 443; SSH is 22 and LDAP is 389 - protocol choice and port choice are separate selection knobs."},
  {"id":"q844","domain":"D4","family":"automation-benefits","question":"After a security team automates its containment playbook, the time from malware alert to host isolation drops from three hours to under two minutes. Which of the following automation benefits does this BEST illustrate?","options":["Workforce multiplier","Enforcing baselines","Employee retention","Reaction time"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Reaction time. Automation responds to a single event at machine speed, shrinking the window an attacker has between alert and containment; workforce multiplier loses because the scenario measures speed on one event, not a small team producing big-team output. The tell: reaction time = machine-speed response to an event, workforce multiplier = small team achieving big-team output, enforcing baselines = drift auto-corrected over time."},
  {"id":"q845","domain":"D4","family":"automation-benefits","question":"A three-person security team uses automated patching, provisioning, and alert triage to manage 6,000 endpoints, work that comparable companies staff with teams five times larger. Which of the following benefits of automation does this show?","options":["Reaction time","Workforce multiplier","Enforcing baselines","Employee retention"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Workforce multiplier. Automation amplifies a small team into big-team output rather than replacing staff; reaction time loses because nothing here measures the speed of response to a single event, only sustained output per person. The tell: workforce multiplier = small team, big-team output; reaction time = machine-speed response to one event; employee retention = analysts freed from drudgery stay longer; enforcing baselines = drift auto-corrected."},
  {"id":"q846","domain":"D4","family":"automation-benefits","question":"A configuration job checks every server hourly against the approved build and automatically reverts any setting an administrator changed outside change control. Which of the following automation benefits does this BEST describe?","options":["Enforcing baselines","Standard infrastructure configurations","Scaling in a secure manner","Efficiency and time saving"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Enforcing baselines. Drift is detected and auto-corrected over the system's life so servers stay compliant long after deployment; standard infrastructure configurations lose because that benefit is about identical initial builds, and these servers were already built. The tell: enforcing baselines = drift auto-corrected over time, standard configurations = every new build identical, scaling securely = the 100th deployment as secure as the 1st."},
  {"id":"q847","domain":"D4","family":"automation-drawbacks","question":"An orchestration platform goes down for a day, and patch deployment, server provisioning, and incident response playbooks all stop across the company until it returns. Which of the following automation considerations does the outage expose?","options":["Technical debt","Complexity","Single point of failure","Cost"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Single point of failure. Everything the automation platform ran halts when the platform itself fails, and the manual alternatives have atrophied; complexity loses because the problem is not difficulty understanding the system but total dependence on one component. The tell: single point of failure = the platform outage stops everything it ran, technical debt = brittle accumulated shortcuts needing rework, complexity = hard to understand and debug."},
  {"id":"q848","domain":"D4","family":"automation-drawbacks","question":"A security team depends on dozens of quick one-off scripts written over five years, undocumented and interdependent; engineers refuse to modify them for fear of breaking unknown workflows. Which of the following considerations of automation does this represent?","options":["Single point of failure","Cost","Ongoing supportability","Technical debt"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Technical debt. Years of expedient shortcuts have accumulated into a brittle, undocumented tangle that now needs rework before anything can safely change; ongoing supportability loses because the authors are still on staff - the burden is the accumulated shortcuts themselves. The tell: technical debt = brittle accumulated shortcuts needing rework, supportability = the author leaves and nobody can maintain it, single point of failure = one platform outage stops everything."},
  {"id":"q849","domain":"D4","family":"automation-drawbacks","question":"The only engineer who understands the identity provisioning automation resigns. Within a month, onboarding errors pile up because nobody remaining can read or fix the code. Which of the following automation considerations was neglected?","options":["Cost","Single point of failure","Ongoing supportability","Complexity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ongoing supportability. Automations need maintenance forever, and without documentation and shared knowledge the system dies with its author; single point of failure loses because the platform still runs - the missing dependency is human expertise to maintain it. The tell: supportability = the author leaves and nobody can maintain it, single point of failure = the platform outage stops everything it ran, technical debt = brittle accumulated shortcuts."},
  {"id":"q850","domain":"D4","family":"automation-drawbacks","question":"A CISO proposes a SOAR deployment. Finance notes the licensing, integration engineering, and playbook development will consume most of this year's budget before a single incident is handled faster. Which of the following automation considerations is finance raising?","options":["Complexity","Cost","Technical debt","Single point of failure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cost. Automation demands significant up-front build, licensing, and maintenance investment before the payoff arrives; complexity loses because the objection is the price tag, not the difficulty of understanding or debugging the system. The tell: cost = up-front investment before payoff, complexity = hard to understand and debug subtle failures, technical debt = accumulated shortcuts needing rework, single point of failure = one outage stops everything."},
  {"id":"q851","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"A security document reads: \"Step 1 - open the admin console. Step 2 - navigate to Users. Step 3 - disable the account and record the ticket number.\" Which of the following governance document types is this?","options":["Policy","Standard","Procedure","Guideline"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Procedure. Numbered, exact, do-this-then-that steps are a procedure - the how; a standard loses because it states a required specification, not the steps to perform it. The tell: policy = the high-level what and why, standard = the mandatory specific requirement, procedure = the step-by-step how, guideline = the recommended-but-optional advice."},
  {"id":"q852","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"A governance framework includes one document type that offers recommended best practices staff are encouraged to follow - but compliance is not mandatory. Which of the following document types is this?","options":["Guideline","Policy","Standard","Procedure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Guideline. Recommended and optional is the guideline's defining trait; everything else in the hierarchy binds, so a standard loses the moment compliance is not mandatory. The tell: guideline = should, not must, policy = the mandatory intent, standard = the mandatory specifics, procedure = the mandatory steps."},
  {"id":"q853","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"Executives publish a one-page statement declaring that all company data must be protected and that security is every employee's responsibility. It names no technologies and gives no technical specifics. Which of the following document types is this?","options":["Standard","Policy","Guideline","Procedure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Policy. High-level management intent stating what must happen and why, without technical detail, is a policy; a standard loses because it pins down specific technical requirements. The tell: policy = broad mandatory intent from leadership, standard = the specific required settings beneath it, procedure = the steps, guideline = optional advice."},
  {"id":"q854","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"A mandatory rule requires stored passwords to use a defined bcrypt work factor and all disk encryption to use AES-256, supporting the broader security policy. Which of the following document types is this?","options":["Guideline","Procedure","Policy","Standard"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Standard. Specific mandatory technical requirements that implement a policy's intent are standards; a policy loses because it states the general mandate without naming algorithms. The tell: standard = exact required specifics (AES-256, work factors), policy = the general mandate above it, procedure = how to carry it out, guideline = optional recommendation."},
  {"id":"q855","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"A new analyst is handed a numbered document walking through exactly how to enroll a user's hardware token, step by step, so the task comes out identical every time regardless of who performs it. Which of the following document types is this?","options":["procedure","standard","guideline","policy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Procedure. Sequenced steps producing a repeatable outcome are a procedure. The tell: procedure = ordered how-to steps, standard = what settings are required, policy = why and what at a high level, guideline = advisory best practice."},
  {"id":"q856","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"In the governance hierarchy, which of the following documents is the high-level, mandatory statement of management intent - what the organization will do and why - deliberately free of specific settings or steps?","options":["guideline","procedure","policy","standard"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Policy. Leadership's binding statement of intent sits at the top; standards and procedures translate it into specifics and steps. The tell: policy = mandatory intent, standard = mandatory specifics, procedure = mandatory steps, guideline = optional advice."},
  {"id":"q857","domain":"D5","family":"governance-hierarchy","question":"An auditor compares two governance excerpts. Excerpt A reads: 'Staff are encouraged to consider using a password manager.' Excerpt B reads: 'All systems must enforce AES-256 for data at rest.' Which of the following document tiers does Excerpt A represent?","options":["Guideline","Standard","Policy","Procedure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Guideline. 'Encouraged to consider' is recommended, non-mandatory language, the defining mark of a guideline. Standard is tempting because both excerpts name a technical practice, but Excerpt B's mandatory 'must enforce' with an exact spec is the standard. The tell: guideline = recommended and optional, standard = mandatory specific spec, policy = high-level mandatory intent, procedure = ordered steps."},
  {"id":"q858","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"A hurricane floods corporate headquarters. One plan keeps order fulfillment, payroll, and customer support running from alternate locations for the weeks the building is unusable. Which of the following plans is this?","options":["Disaster recovery policy","Business continuity policy","Incident response policy","Acceptable use policy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Business continuity. Keeping the BUSINESS functions operating through disruption is continuity; rebuilding the IT systems is disaster recovery's slice of it, so DR loses when the focus is operations, not technology. The tell: business continuity = keep operations running, disaster recovery = restore the technology, incident response = handle the security event, acceptable use = rules for users."},
  {"id":"q859","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"After a fire destroys the server room, the team executes a documented plan: rebuild servers, restore databases from backup, bring IT systems back online in priority order. Which of the following plans is the team following?","options":["Business continuity policy","Change management policy","Disaster recovery policy","Incident response policy"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Disaster recovery. Restoring the technology stack after a catastrophe is DR, the IT-focused subset of continuity planning; business continuity loses because it keeps operations moving rather than rebuilding systems. The tell: disaster recovery = rebuild and restore systems, business continuity = keep the business functioning meanwhile, incident response = security-event handling, change management = controlled routine changes."},
  {"id":"q860","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"An employee is disciplined for running a personal side business from a company laptop. The policy cited defines what staff may and may not do with corporate laptops, email, and internet access. Which of the following policies is it?","options":["Acceptable use policy","Incident response policy","Business continuity policy","SDLC policy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acceptable use policy. The AUP is the rulebook for how employees may use company systems, usually signed at onboarding. The tell: AUP = user behavior on company resources, incident response = breach handling phases, business continuity = operating through disruption, SDLC = secure development practices."},
  {"id":"q861","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"A policy document defines the phases the security team follows for handling security incidents: preparation, detection, analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, and lessons learned. Which of the following policies is this?","options":["Business continuity policy","Disaster recovery policy","Acceptable use policy","Incident response policy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Incident response policy. The phased lifecycle for handling security events, ending in lessons learned, is the IR policy's content; disaster recovery loses because it restores technology after a catastrophe rather than defining event-handling phases. The tell: incident response = the breach-handling phases, disaster recovery = restoring systems after catastrophe, business continuity = keeping operations alive, acceptable use = user conduct rules."},
  {"id":"q862","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"Company policy requires every proposed modification to production systems to pass through approval, impact analysis, testing, and a documented backout plan before it may be applied. Which of the following policies governs this process?","options":["change management","SDLC","incident response","information security"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Change management. Approval, impact analysis, testing, and backout are the change management gate for production modifications. The tell: change management = controlled changes to live systems, SDLC = how software is built, incident response = breach handling, information security = the umbrella policy."},
  {"id":"q863","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"During onboarding, a new hire signs a document defining permitted use of company email, internet, and devices. Months later it is cited when she streams pirated media on her work laptop. Which of the following policies is this?","options":["Acceptable use policy","Information security policy","Change management policy","SDLC policy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acceptable use policy. The AUP defines acceptable and prohibited use of company systems and is signed at onboarding, so streaming pirated media on a work laptop violates it. Information security policy is broader, the umbrella protecting confidentiality, integrity, and availability, not the specific use rules. The tell: AUP = rules for using systems, information security policy = umbrella assigning roles and protecting the CIA triad, change management = controlling modifications, SDLC = secure development."},
  {"id":"q864","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"An auditor asks for the single umbrella policy set that assigns security roles and responsibilities and mandates protection of the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of all information assets. Which of the following BEST fits this description?","options":["Acceptable use policy","Business continuity policy","Information security policy","Incident response policy"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Information security policy. It is the overarching policy set that assigns roles and mandates protecting confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets. Acceptable use policy is narrower, governing only how individuals may use systems. The tell: information security policy = umbrella covering the CIA triad and roles, AUP = acceptable use of systems, business continuity = keep the business running, incident response = handle security incidents."},
  {"id":"q865","domain":"D5","family":"policies","question":"A governance document mandates secure-coding standards, peer code review, and security testing gates at each phase of building software. Which of the following policies does this describe?","options":["Change management policy","SDLC policy","Acceptable use policy","Disaster recovery policy"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SDLC policy. It mandates security activities such as secure coding, code review, and testing gates within each software development phase. Change management is tempting because both govern controlled processes, but change management controls modifications to production systems, not the build lifecycle. The tell: SDLC = security in software development phases, change management = approval and backout for production changes, AUP = acceptable use, disaster recovery = restore IT after a disaster."},
  {"id":"q866","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"The SOC maintains a document titled \"Phishing Report Response\" that scripts exactly what to do, in order, whenever a phishing report arrives - who verifies, who blocks the sender, who notifies. Which of the following BEST describes this document?","options":["Playbook","Onboarding procedure","Change management procedure","Password standard"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Playbook. A step-by-step response script for one specific scenario type is a playbook, the SOC's per-scenario procedure; a change management procedure loses because it governs approving system changes, not responding to events. The tell: playbook = scenario-specific response steps, onboarding/offboarding = personnel procedures, change management procedure = how changes get approved, password standard = required password specifics."},
  {"id":"q867","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"An employee resigns. HR's notification triggers the procedure that revokes all system access, disables accounts, and collects equipment on the person's last day. Which of the following BEST describes this process?","options":["Onboarding","Offboarding","Change management procedure","Access control standard"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Offboarding. The structured exit process - access revoked, accounts disabled, assets returned - is offboarding, and gaps in it create lingering-access risk; onboarding loses because it grants access at hire, the mirror image. The tell: offboarding = the exit process, onboarding = the entry process, change management = system changes, access control standard = the required access rules."},
  {"id":"q868","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"On a new hire's first day, IT runs the defined process: create accounts, add role group memberships, issue equipment, and grant day-one access. Which of the following describes this procedure?","options":["offboarding","onboarding","playbook","change management procedure"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Onboarding. The structured entry process granting a new person accounts and access is onboarding, offboarding's mirror. The tell: onboarding = access granted at entry, offboarding = access removed at exit, playbook = scenario response script, change management procedure = system change steps."},
  {"id":"q869","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"A security team publishes a binding document specifying the exact minimum password length, required character classes, and reuse limits every account in the company must meet. Which of the following document types is this?","options":["password policy","password guideline","password standard","encryption standard"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Password standard. A binding document mandating exact specifics - length, character classes, reuse limits - is a standard, and this one is scoped to passwords; a password policy loses because policies state high-level intent, not exact settings. The tell: standard = the required specifics, policy = the high-level mandate, guideline = optional recommendation, encryption standard = required specifics for a different topic."},
  {"id":"q870","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"A binding document requires least privilege, 90-day access reviews, and defined provisioning rules for every account in the company. Which of the following BEST classifies this document?","options":["Access control standard","Access control policy","Onboarding procedure","Information security policy"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Access control standard. It is a mandatory, specific requirement, with an exact review cadence and provisioning rules, that supports a higher-level policy. Access control policy is the tempting runner-up, but a policy states high-level intent without the precise 90-day spec. The tell: standard = mandatory specific spec, policy = high-level intent, procedure = ordered steps, information security policy = the umbrella document."},
  {"id":"q871","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"A mandatory specification states that a badge is required at every entrance, visitors are escorted at all times, and cameras cover every server-room door. Which of the following BEST classifies this document?","options":["Physical security policy","Physical security guideline","Access control vestibule","Physical security standard"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Physical security standard. It is a mandatory, specific set of physical requirements supporting a broader policy. Physical security policy is tempting, but a policy states intent while these exact required measures make it a standard; an access control vestibule is a physical control, not a governance document. The tell: standard = mandatory specific spec, policy = high-level intent, guideline = optional advice, access control vestibule = a control being specified, not the document itself."},
  {"id":"q872","domain":"D5","family":"standards-procedures","question":"A SOC maintains an overall incident response plan plus a separate scripted document giving the exact ordered steps to run whenever ransomware is detected. Which of the following BEST names the ransomware-specific document?","options":["Incident response plan","Standard operating procedure","Playbook","Change management procedure"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Playbook. A playbook is the scenario-specific set of ordered response steps for one situation, such as ransomware detection. The incident response plan is tempting, but that is the overall program document the playbook sits under, not the per-scenario script. The tell: playbook = one-scenario response script, incident response plan = overall program document, standard operating procedure = general repeatable steps, change management procedure = steps for controlling production changes."},
  {"id":"q873","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A retailer must comply with PCI DSS because it accepts card payments. The requirement is imposed and enforced by the payment card brands through contracts - no government wrote it. Which of the following external governance sources is this?","options":["Regulatory","Industry","Legal","National"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Industry. PCI DSS is the textbook industry requirement: binding through business relationships, not law; regulatory loses because no government agency imposes or enforces it. The tell: industry = imposed by the sector itself (PCI DSS), regulatory = imposed by a government agency with penalties, legal = statutes and liability, national/local = the jurisdiction level of the rule."},
  {"id":"q874","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A government agency issues enforceable cybersecurity rules for the sector it oversees and can levy fines on organizations that fail to comply. Which of the following external governance categories is this?","options":["Industry","Legal","Regulatory","Local"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Regulatory. Rules issued and enforced by a government agency with penalty power are regulatory requirements (HIPAA, GDPR enforcement); industry loses because sector-imposed standards bind through contracts, not agency fines. The tell: regulatory = agency-enforced rules and fines, industry = sector-imposed standards like PCI DSS, legal = the broader body of law and liability, local = the jurisdiction tier."},
  {"id":"q875","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"Counsel reminds the board that the company must honor terms written into signed contracts and comply with statutes, since breaching either invites lawsuits and liability. Which of the following categories of external governance does this describe?","options":["regulatory","industry","global","legal"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Legal. Obligations grounded in laws and contracts, enforced through courts and liability, are the legal category. The tell: legal = statutes, contracts, lawsuits, regulatory = agency rules and fines, industry = sector-imposed standards like PCI DSS, global = the worldwide jurisdiction scope."},
  {"id":"q876","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A multinational must satisfy the EU's GDPR for European residents' data while simultaneously meeting the differing rules of every other country it operates in, reconciling them all in one program. Which of the following describes governance at this worldwide scope?","options":["global","national","local/regional","industry"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Global. Cross-country, worldwide obligations define the global governance scope, the hardest tier to reconcile. The tell: global = across countries, national = one country's laws, local/regional = state or municipal rules, industry = sector-imposed requirements."},
  {"id":"q877","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"Counsel flags e-discovery duties and contractual obligations arising from signed customer agreements. Which of the following categories of external consideration is this?","options":["Regulatory","Legal","Industry","Local/regional"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Legal. Contracts, statutes, liability, and e-discovery duties are legal considerations. Regulatory is tempting, but that means enforceable rules issued by a government agency, whereas these obligations flow from contract and civil litigation. The tell: legal = laws and contracts, regulatory = government agency rules with penalties, industry = sector frameworks like PCI DSS, local/regional = a jurisdiction scope rather than a source."},
  {"id":"q878","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A compliance officer explains that the company's duty to follow PCI DSS comes from the payment card brands enforcing it through the merchant contract, with no legislature or regulator behind it. Which of the following categories of external consideration is this?","options":["Industry","Regulatory","Legal","National"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Industry. PCI DSS is a sector framework imposed and enforced by the card brands through contract, not written by any government. Regulatory is the classic trap, but a regulation is a government rule with statutory penalties; card-brand enforcement is contractual. The tell: industry = sector frameworks like PCI DSS enforced by industry bodies, regulatory = government rules with penalties, legal = statutes and lawsuits broadly, national = a country-wide jurisdiction scope."},
  {"id":"q879","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A state breach-notification statute applies only to organizations doing business within that single state. Which of the following BEST describes the scope of this external consideration?","options":["National","Global","Industry","Local/regional"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Local/regional. A single-state statute binds only organizations operating in that state, which is a regional jurisdiction scope. National is the trap, but national law would apply country-wide rather than to one state. The tell: local/regional = a state or provincial scope, national = whole-country scope, global = extraterritorial worldwide reach, industry = a sector framework rather than a jurisdiction."},
  {"id":"q880","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A healthcare privacy law applies uniformly to organizations throughout the entire country but has no force beyond its borders. Which of the following BEST describes the scope of this external consideration?","options":["Local/regional","Global","National","Regulatory"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"National. The law binds the whole country uniformly yet stops at the border, which is national scope, a HIPAA-style law. Global is the trap, but global considerations reach across borders to residents wherever they are. The tell: national = whole-country scope, local/regional = a single state or province, global = extraterritorial worldwide reach, regulatory = the government-rule source, not a scope."},
  {"id":"q881","domain":"D5","family":"external-governance","question":"A company with no offices in the EU must still comply with GDPR because it processes the personal data of EU residents. Which of the following BEST describes the scope of this external consideration?","options":["National","Global","Local/regional","Legal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Global. GDPR's extraterritorial reach binds an organization based on whose data it processes, regardless of where the company sits, which is a global consideration. National is the trap, but a national law would stop at its own border rather than follow EU residents' data abroad. The tell: global = extraterritorial worldwide reach, national = whole-country scope, local/regional = a state or province, legal = the source type rather than a scope."},
  {"id":"q882","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"A marketing company decides which customer data to collect and exactly what it will be used for, then hires an outside email vendor to execute the campaigns with that data. Which of the following BEST describes the marketing company under GDPR?","options":["Data processor","Data controller","Data custodian","Data owner"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data controller. Deciding the purposes and means of processing personal data makes it the controller, regardless of who does the hands-on work; processor loses because the vendor, not the company, acts on instructions. The tell: controller = decides why and how, processor = processes on the controller's instructions, custodian = the day-to-day technical caretaker, owner = the internal accountability role for a dataset."},
  {"id":"q883","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"The email vendor sends campaigns using only the customer data and instructions its client supplies, making zero decisions about why or how the personal data is used. Which of the following BEST describes the vendor under GDPR?","options":["Data processor","Data controller","Data owner","Data custodian"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Data processor. Handling personal data strictly on another party's instructions is processing; the client remains the controller, so controller loses because the vendor decides nothing about purpose or means. The tell: processor = acts only on instructions, controller = sets purpose and means, owner = internal accountable executive, custodian = the technical caretaker role."},
  {"id":"q884","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"One role delivers the hands-on technical protection of data every day - running backups, applying access controls, patching the storage systems - while never deciding the data's classification or purpose. Which of the following roles is this?","options":["Data owner","Data controller","Data custodian/steward","Data processor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Data custodian/steward. The custodian implements protection technically; owners and controllers make the classification and purpose decisions the custodian enforces, so data owner loses because ownership means accountability, not hands-on care. The tell: custodian = hands-on caretaker, owner = accountable decision-maker for the dataset, controller = decides purpose under privacy law, processor = external party processing on instructions."},
  {"id":"q885","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"A senior vice president is formally accountable for the customer dataset: she sets its classification and approves who may access it, while technical staff execute the controls she mandates. Which of the following BEST describes her role?","options":["data controller","data owner","data processor","data custodian"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data owner. Internal accountability for a dataset - setting classification and access rules - is the owner role; the custodians do the hands-on enforcement. The tell: owner = accountable decision-maker inside the org, custodian = technical caretaker, controller/processor = the GDPR pairing about purpose and instructions."},
  {"id":"q886","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"A payroll bureau hosts and backs up client employee records on its own servers but calculates paychecks strictly as each client instructs, never deciding why the personal data is used. Under GDPR, which of the following BEST describes the bureau?","options":["data controller","data custodian","data owner","data processor"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Data processor. Under GDPR, a vendor handling personal data solely on a controller's instructions is the processor, even when it hosts and backs up the data itself; data custodian loses because custodian is an internal caretaker role, not the GDPR term for an external party acting on instructions. The tell: processor = executes the controller's instructions, controller = decides purpose and means, custodian = internal hands-on caretaker, owner = internal accountable executive."},
  {"id":"q887","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"A cloud analytics vendor is breached while running computations on patient records for a hospital. Under GDPR, which of the following parties determined the purposes and means of the processing and remains accountable for the data?","options":["the data processor","the data custodian","the data controller","the data owner"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The data controller. The hospital decided why and how the patient data would be processed, so it stays accountable under GDPR even when the breach occurs at its vendor; the data processor loses because the vendor only computes on the hospital's instructions. The tell: controller = decides purpose and means and keeps accountability, processor = acts on instructions, owner/custodian = internal accountability and caretaking roles, not the GDPR party names."},
  {"id":"q888","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"A senior executive is accountable for a customer data set, sets its classification, and accepts its risk; hiring a vendor to process the data does not transfer that accountability. Which of the following roles does the executive hold?","options":["Data custodian","Data processor","Data owner","Data controller"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Data owner. The owner is the accountable senior person who classifies the data and accepts risk, and accountability cannot be outsourced to a processor. Data controller is tempting in a privacy context, but the controller decides the purposes and means of processing, whereas ownership is the internal accountability role. The tell: owner = accountable, classifies, accepts risk; custodian = hands-on technical care; processor = acts on the controller's instructions; controller = decides why and how personal data is processed."},
  {"id":"q889","domain":"D5","family":"data-roles","question":"A data-governance chart shows a role that executes the technical controls the data owner mandates, such as provisioning access, encrypting the database, and restoring from backup, while holding no say over classification. Which of the following roles is this?","options":["Data custodian","Data owner","Data controller","Data processor"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Data custodian. The custodian delivers hands-on technical protection, including encryption, backups, and permissions, exactly as the owner's rules dictate, without setting classification. Data owner is the trap, but the owner defines the rules and is accountable, while the custodian merely executes them. The tell: custodian = hands-on technical care, owner = accountable and sets classification, controller = decides why and how personal data is processed, processor = acts for the controller."},
  {"id":"q890","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"Automated tooling watches the environment around the clock, recalculating risk scores in real time as configurations drift and new vulnerabilities appear. Which of the following risk assessment types is this?","options":["Ad hoc","Recurring","Continuous","One-time"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Continuous. Real-time, always-on automated assessment is continuous, catching risk changes the moment they happen; recurring loses because it runs on a fixed schedule, not around the clock. The tell: continuous = constant automated monitoring, recurring = on a fixed schedule (annual, quarterly), ad hoc = triggered by an event, one-time = a single point-in-time exercise."},
  {"id":"q891","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"The morning after a direct competitor discloses a major breach, the CISO orders an immediate, unplanned risk assessment prompted specifically by that news. Which of the following assessment types is this?","options":["Ad hoc","Recurring","Continuous","One-time"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Ad hoc. An unscheduled assessment triggered by a specific event or concern is ad hoc; one-time loses because it describes a planned single exercise, not an event-driven reaction. The tell: ad hoc = event-driven, unplanned, recurring = calendar-driven, continuous = automated and constant, one-time = single occasion, such as before an acquisition."},
  {"id":"q892","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"Policy requires a full enterprise risk assessment every October, same scope, same date, regardless of whether anything notable happened that year. Which of the following assessment types is this?","options":["recurring","ad hoc","one-time","continuous"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Recurring. Fixed-schedule, calendar-driven assessments are recurring. The tell: recurring = on the calendar, ad hoc = triggered by events, continuous = automated and always-on, one-time = a single non-repeating exercise."},
  {"id":"q893","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"Before launching an entirely new product line, the firm commissions a single risk assessment scoped to that launch alone, with no intention of repeating it. Which of the following assessment types is this?","options":["recurring","continuous","ad hoc","one-time"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"One-time. A single assessment for a specific occasion - a launch, merger, or migration - with no repeat cycle is one-time. The tell: one-time = single planned occasion, ad hoc = unplanned reaction to an event, recurring = scheduled repetition, continuous = constant automated monitoring."},
  {"id":"q894","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"A widely publicized zero-day is disclosed that hits the organization's exact technology stack, prompting the CISO to launch an immediate, unplanned assessment that same day. Which of the following risk assessment types is this?","options":["One-time","Recurring","Ad hoc","Continuous"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Ad hoc. An assessment triggered reactively by a surprise event and run on the spot is ad hoc. One-time is the trap, but a one-time assessment is planned in advance for a defined single purpose, whereas this one was forced by an unexpected trigger. The tell: ad hoc = triggered and reactive, one-time = planned single-scope engagement, recurring = fixed schedule, continuous = always-on automated."},
  {"id":"q895","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"For a pending acquisition, a firm commissions a due-diligence risk assessment scoped solely to that deal, planned in advance and never intended to repeat. Which of the following risk assessment types is this?","options":["One-time","Ad hoc","Recurring","Continuous"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"One-time. A planned, single-scope assessment tied to one project and not meant to repeat is one-time. Ad hoc is the trap, but ad hoc assessments are reactive responses to surprise triggers, while this one was deliberately planned for the acquisition. The tell: one-time = planned single-scope engagement, ad hoc = triggered and reactive, recurring = fixed schedule, continuous = always-on automated."},
  {"id":"q896","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"To satisfy PCI DSS, a merchant runs the same-scope risk assessment on a fixed calendar every 12 months. Which of the following risk assessment types is this?","options":["Continuous","Recurring","Ad hoc","One-time"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Recurring. A compliance-driven assessment on a fixed, repeating schedule is recurring. Continuous is the trap, but continuous assessment is always-on automated evaluation, whereas this runs as discrete annual snapshots. The tell: recurring = fixed schedule and cadence, continuous = always-on automated, ad hoc = triggered, one-time = planned single-scope."},
  {"id":"q897","domain":"D5","family":"risk-assessment-types","question":"A platform updates each risk's score the instant an asset changes or a new weakness is disclosed, so leadership sees current risk rather than a periodic snapshot. Which of the following risk assessment types is this?","options":["Recurring","Ad hoc","One-time","Continuous"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Continuous. Automated, always-on evaluation that re-scores risk in near real time is continuous. Recurring is the trap, but recurring assessments capture discrete scheduled snapshots rather than updating the moment anything changes. The tell: continuous = always-on automated, recurring = fixed-schedule snapshots, ad hoc = triggered, one-time = planned single-scope."},
  {"id":"q898","domain":"D5","family":"qual-quant","question":"An analyst calculates that a server failure costs $40,000 per event and strikes about twice a year, producing an $80,000 expected annual loss to weigh against the cost of controls. Which of the following risk analysis approaches is this?","options":["Qualitative","Quantitative","Semi-quantitative","Hybrid"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Quantitative. Hard dollar values and frequency math (SLE x ARO = ALE) are quantitative analysis, built for cost-benefit decisions; semi-quantitative loses because it uses numeric scales, not true currency calculations. The tell: quantitative = numbers and currency, qualitative = High/Medium/Low judgment, semi-quantitative = numeric scales or scores with no true currency, hybrid = a deliberate mix of qualitative and quantitative methods."},
  {"id":"q899","domain":"D5","family":"qual-quant","question":"A workshop team rates each risk High, Medium, or Low on a likelihood-versus-impact matrix using expert judgment alone - not one monetary figure appears anywhere. Which of the following approaches is this?","options":["Qualitative","Quantitative","Hybrid","Semi-quantitative"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Qualitative. Subjective ratings on a matrix, no dollars, is qualitative analysis: faster and easier, less precise; quantitative loses the instant no monetary figure appears. The tell: qualitative = judgment categories, quantitative = calculated currency values, semi-quantitative = numeric scales or scores with no true currency, hybrid = a deliberate mix of qualitative and quantitative methods."},
  {"id":"q900","domain":"D5","family":"qual-quant","question":"Which of the following risk analysis methods expresses risk purely as descriptive categories such as High, Medium, or Low based on expert judgment, using neither numeric scores nor monetary values?","options":["Quantitative","Semi-quantitative","Hybrid","Qualitative"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Qualitative. Purely descriptive categories from expert judgment, with no numbers of any kind, define qualitative analysis; semi-quantitative loses because it assigns numeric scale scores even though it stops short of real currency. The tell: qualitative = descriptive labels only, semi-quantitative = numeric scales without true currency, quantitative = calculated monetary values, hybrid = a deliberate mix of methods."},
  {"id":"q901","domain":"D5","family":"qual-quant","question":"An analyst multiplies asset value by an exposure factor, then by an annualized rate of occurrence, arriving at a precise expected yearly loss in dollars to justify a control purchase. Which of the following risk analysis approaches is this?","options":["qualitative","quantitative","semi-quantitative","hybrid rating"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Quantitative. The SLE/ARO/ALE arithmetic producing currency figures is quantitative analysis, purpose-built for cost-benefit arguments. The tell: quantitative = formulas and dollars, qualitative = expert judgment in categories, semi-quantitative = numeric scales or scores with no true currency, hybrid = a deliberate mix of qualitative and quantitative methods."},
  {"id":"q902","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A production database server is valued at $200,000. Fire modeling says a single fire would destroy 25 percent of its value. Which of the following is the single loss expectancy (SLE)?","options":["$100,000","$25,000","$200,000","$50,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"$50,000. SLE = asset value x exposure factor = $200,000 x 0.25; $200,000 loses because that is the full asset value, not the per-event loss. The tell: SLE = cost of ONE event (AV x EF), ALE = cost per YEAR (SLE x ARO), EF = the percentage lost, ARO = events per year."},
  {"id":"q903","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A warehouse is valued at $500,000. A flood would destroy 40 percent of it, and floods are expected once every 2 years. Which of the following is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?","options":["$200,000","$250,000","$1,000,000","$100,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"$100,000. SLE = $500,000 x 0.40 = $200,000; ARO = once per 2 years = 0.5; ALE = $200,000 x 0.5; $200,000 loses because stopping at the SLE skips the annualization step. The tell: ALE = SLE x ARO, and an every-other-year event has ARO 0.5, not 2."},
  {"id":"q904","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A risk register lists a server's single loss expectancy as $40,000 and its annualized loss expectancy as $10,000. Which of the following is the expected frequency of the loss event?","options":["Once every 4 years","Four times per year","Once every 10 years","Twice per year"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Once every 4 years. ARO = ALE / SLE = 10,000 / 40,000 = 0.25 events per year, i.e., one event per 4 years; four times per year loses because it inverts the division. The tell: the ALE formula runs both ways - divide ALE by SLE to recover the frequency."},
  {"id":"q905","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"Hydrology data says a data center flood should occur once every 5 years. Which of the following is the annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)?","options":["0.2","5","1","0.5"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"0.2. ARO expresses events per single year: 1 event / 5 years = 0.2; 5 loses because it counts the years between events, not events per year. The tell: ARO = yearly frequency, so rarer-than-annual events have AROs below 1 - never write 5 for a once-in-5-years event."},
  {"id":"q906","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A facility is valued at $1,200,000. Each occurrence of a particular event destroys 15 percent of its value, and the event strikes about 3 times per year. Which of the following is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?","options":["$180,000","$540,000","$360,000","$60,000"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"$540,000. SLE = $1,200,000 x 0.15 = $180,000; ALE = SLE x ARO = $180,000 x 3; $180,000 loses because it is only the single-event cost. The tell: two multiplications, in order - value x EF gives one event's cost, then x ARO annualizes it."},
  {"id":"q907","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"Company laptops worth $60,000 in total keep walking out the door: each theft incident wipes out 30 percent of that value, and history shows about two incidents per year. Which of the following annualized loss expectancy (ALE) values should the risk register show?","options":["$18,000","$72,000","$36,000","$9,000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"$36,000. SLE = $60,000 x 0.30 = $18,000; ALE = $18,000 x 2. The tell: $18,000 is the SLE trap answer - stopping one multiplication early is the classic mistake this question punishes."},
  {"id":"q908","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A database server is valued at $80,000, and a ransomware event is expected to destroy 45 percent of its value in one occurrence. Which of the following is the single loss expectancy (SLE)?","options":["$18,000","$36,000","$44,000","$80,000"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"$36,000. SLE = asset value x exposure factor = $80,000 x 0.45. The tell: SLE is one event's damage - value times the loss percentage, nothing about frequency yet."},
  {"id":"q909","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A storage array is valued at $250,000. A power surge destroys 20 percent of its value per occurrence and is expected once every 4 years. Which of the following is the annualized loss expectancy (ALE)?","options":["$50,000","$62,500","$200,000","$12,500"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"$12,500. SLE = $250,000 x 0.20 = $50,000; ARO = 1/4 = 0.25; ALE = $50,000 x 0.25. The tell: $50,000 is the SLE trap - remember to multiply by the fractional ARO for events rarer than yearly."},
  {"id":"q910","domain":"D5","family":"risk-math","question":"A risk report lists a single loss expectancy of $30,000 and an annualized loss expectancy of $90,000. Which of the following is the annualized rate of occurrence (ARO)?","options":["0.33","2","3","1.5"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"3. ARO = ALE / SLE = 90,000 / 30,000 = 3 events per year. The tell: when ALE exceeds SLE, the event strikes more than once a year - divide to recover the frequency."},
  {"id":"q911","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"A risk analysis concludes that IF a breach occurs, the resulting harm - lawsuits, regulatory fines, customer exodus - would be severe. Which of the following risk dimensions does this describe?","options":["Probability","Likelihood","Exposure factor","Impact"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Impact. How bad the consequences would be is impact; likelihood loses because it rates the chance something happens, not the severity when it does. The tell: impact = severity of the harm, probability = the computed statistical chance, likelihood = the qualitative chance rating, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost per event."},
  {"id":"q912","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"In a quantitative risk model, one variable expresses the percentage of an asset's value that would be lost in a single event. Which of the following terms names this variable?","options":["Probability","Likelihood","Exposure factor","Impact"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Exposure factor. The per-event loss percentage is the EF, multiplied by asset value to produce SLE; impact loses because it names the overall severity concept, not a percentage variable. The tell: exposure factor = percent of value lost, impact = severity of the harm, probability = computed statistical chance, likelihood = qualitative chance rating."},
  {"id":"q913","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"A workshop rates the chance of a successful phishing attack this year as \"high,\" based on the panel's expert judgment rather than any computed statistic. Which of the following terms describes this qualitative expression of chance?","options":["probability","likelihood","impact","exposure factor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Likelihood. In SY0-701 usage, likelihood is the qualitative judgment of chance; probability is its statistically computed counterpart. The tell: likelihood = judged chance (high/medium/low), probability = calculated chance (a number), impact = how bad, exposure factor = percent of value lost."},
  {"id":"q914","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"In a quantitative model, which of the following terms names the numerically computed, statistical chance that a given event will occur?","options":["probability","likelihood","exposure factor","impact"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Probability. The computed numeric chance is probability; the qualitative judged version is likelihood. The tell: probability = the statistic, likelihood = the judgment, impact = severity of consequences, exposure factor = per-event loss percentage."},
  {"id":"q915","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"An actuarial model uses decades of historical data to compute a 12 percent annual chance of a flood at a facility. Which of the following BEST names this numerically computed chance?","options":["Likelihood","Impact","Probability","Exposure factor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Probability. A statistical, numerically computed chance of an event is probability. Likelihood is the trap, but likelihood is the qualitative rating such as high, medium, or low, whereas this is a computed percentage. The tell: probability = computed statistical chance, likelihood = qualitative chance rating, impact = magnitude of harm if it occurs, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost per event."},
  {"id":"q916","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"A risk committee places each threat on a five-point scale from rare to almost certain based purely on members' collective judgment, with no computed percentage behind the rating. Which of the following BEST names this qualitative expression of chance?","options":["Likelihood","Probability","Impact","Exposure factor"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Likelihood. A qualitative, judgment-based rating of how probable an event is, here a rare-to-almost-certain scale, is likelihood. Probability is the trap, but probability is a computed statistical number, not a judgment label. The tell: likelihood = qualitative chance rating, probability = computed statistical chance, impact = magnitude of harm if it occurs, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost."},
  {"id":"q917","domain":"D5","family":"probability-impact","question":"An analysis concludes that a particular event is very unlikely to occur but would be catastrophic to the business if it ever did. Which of the following risk dimensions does 'catastrophic' measure?","options":["Likelihood","Impact","Probability","Exposure factor"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Impact. Impact is the magnitude of harm if the event occurs, so 'catastrophic' describes impact regardless of how unlikely the event is. Likelihood is the trap, but likelihood measures the chance of occurrence, which the stem already called very low. The tell: impact = magnitude of harm if it happens, likelihood = qualitative chance rating, probability = computed chance, exposure factor = percent of asset value lost."},
  {"id":"q918","domain":"D5","family":"risk-register","question":"The security team tracks \"number of overdue critical patches\" because when that number climbs, it signals a specific risk is growing before any incident occurs. Which of the following risk register elements BEST describes this early-warning metric?","options":["Key risk indicator (KRI)","Risk owner","Risk threshold","Risk appetite"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Key risk indicator. A measurable metric that warns a risk is rising is a KRI, the register's smoke detector; risk threshold loses because it is the preset level that triggers action, not the metric being watched. The tell: KRI = the early-warning metric, risk owner = the accountable person, risk threshold = the trigger line that demands action, risk appetite = how much risk the org wants overall."},
  {"id":"q919","domain":"D5","family":"risk-register","question":"Every entry in the risk register names exactly one accountable manager responsible for monitoring that risk and driving its treatment. Which of the following BEST describes that named individual?","options":["Key risk indicator (KRI)","Risk threshold","Risk appetite","Risk owner"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Risk owner. Each risk gets one accountable person; without an owner, treatment stalls. The tell: risk owner = the accountable name on the line, KRI = the warning metric, risk threshold = the action trigger, risk appetite = the organization's overall willingness to take risk."},
  {"id":"q920","domain":"D5","family":"risk-register","question":"The register specifies that once unpatched critical systems exceed ten, the risk becomes unacceptable and treatment must begin immediately. Which of the following terms describes this preset level that forces action?","options":["key risk indicator","risk owner","risk threshold","risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Risk threshold. The defined line that, once crossed, mandates action is the threshold; the metric being watched is the KRI. The tell: risk threshold = the trigger line, key risk indicator = the metric approaching it, risk owner = the accountable person, risk tolerance = acceptable deviation on the risk."},
  {"id":"q921","domain":"D5","family":"risk-register","question":"A new security manager needs the single living document that lists each identified risk alongside its accountable owner, key risk indicators, severity, and treatment status. Which of the following should the manager consult?","options":["risk assessment report","business impact analysis","risk matrix","risk register"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Risk register. The register is the ongoing tracking document pairing every identified risk with its owner, key risk indicators, and treatment status; a risk assessment report loses because it captures one point-in-time analysis, not a continuously maintained record. The tell: risk register = the living tracking document, risk matrix = the likelihood-impact grid used to rate risks, business impact analysis = ranks processes by criticality, risk assessment report = a single assessment's findings."},
  {"id":"q922","domain":"D5","family":"risk-register","question":"An analyst must distinguish the person accountable for one specific entry in the risk register and its treatment from the executive accountable for classifying an entire data set. Which of the following names the first role?","options":["Data owner","Risk threshold","Key risk indicator","Risk owner"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Risk owner. The risk owner is accountable for a single register entry and for driving its treatment, distinct from the data owner who is accountable for a data set. Data owner is the trap, but that role owns a data set's classification, not one register line. The tell: risk owner = accountable for one register entry, data owner = accountable for a data set, risk threshold = the action-trigger level, key risk indicator = the early-warning metric."},
  {"id":"q923","domain":"D5","family":"risk-register","question":"A risk register row reads - Risk: data exfiltration via email; Owner: R. Cole; Key risk indicator: blocked DLP events per day; Action rule: escalate to treatment when blocked events exceed 500 in a day. Which risk concept does the action rule define?","options":["Risk tolerance","Risk appetite","Key risk indicator","Risk threshold"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Risk threshold. The preset level at which a specific tracked risk becomes unacceptable and treatment must begin is the threshold; the 'exceed 500' rule is that action trigger. Risk tolerance is tempting but names the organization's acceptable variance around risk generally, not one register entry's trigger line. The tell: threshold = the action-trigger level for one tracked risk; tolerance = acceptable variance around a specific risk; appetite = overall willingness to take risk; KRI = the early-warning metric being measured (here, blocked DLP events)."},
  {"id":"q924","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A fintech startup's board explicitly embraces significant risk - launching fast, entering unproven markets - because capturing market share quickly matters more than avoiding losses. Which of the following BEST describes this risk appetite?","options":["Expansionary","Conservative","Neutral","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Expansionary. Willingly taking on substantial risk in pursuit of growth is the expansionary appetite. The tell: expansionary = risk-hungry for reward, conservative = risk-averse, neutral = balanced middle, risk tolerance = the acceptable deviation on one specific risk, not the overall posture."},
  {"id":"q925","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A governance course asks for the term describing the broad, overall amount of risk an organization is willing to pursue or retain in exchange for reward. Which of the following terms is being described?","options":["Risk tolerance","Risk appetite","Conservative","Neutral"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Risk appetite. Appetite is the organization-wide, strategic quantity of risk leadership wants to take. The tell: risk appetite = the overall willingness, risk tolerance = the per-risk acceptable deviation around it, conservative/neutral/expansionary = the postures appetite can take."},
  {"id":"q926","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A bank publishes its overall appetite, then adds: no single trading desk may deviate more than 2 percent from its assigned risk limit before mandatory action. Which of the following does that 2 percent boundary represent?","options":["Risk tolerance","Expansionary posture","Risk appetite","Conservative posture"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Risk tolerance. The permissible deviation around a specific limit is tolerance - the operational fine print under the broad appetite. The tell: risk tolerance = acceptable variance on one risk/limit, risk appetite = the overall strategic willingness, postures = the flavor of that appetite."},
  {"id":"q927","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A regulated pension fund's charter minimizes risk-taking: it accepts only investments with near-certain outcomes, sacrificing returns to protect beneficiaries. Which of the following BEST describes this risk appetite posture?","options":["expansionary","conservative","neutral","risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Conservative. Deliberately minimizing risk even at the cost of reward is the conservative posture. The tell: conservative = risk-averse, expansionary = risk-hungry, neutral = balanced, risk tolerance = the acceptable variance on one specific risk, not a posture."},
  {"id":"q928","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A mid-size firm's strategy balances growth against caution: measured risks where the numbers justify them, no strong lean toward aggression or restraint. Which of the following risk appetite postures fits BEST?","options":["expansionary","risk tolerance","neutral","conservative"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Neutral. A balanced middle posture, taking justified risks without favoring either extreme, is neutral appetite. The tell: neutral = the balanced middle, expansionary = growth-hungry, conservative = protective, risk tolerance = per-risk variance, not the overall posture."},
  {"id":"q929","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A governance quiz distinguishes two terms: one is the organization's overall willingness to take risk; the other is the acceptable variance around a SPECIFIC risk before action is required. Which of the following is the second term?","options":["risk appetite","risk tolerance","expansionary appetite","neutral appetite"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Risk tolerance. Tolerance operates per-risk: how far one risk may drift before action. Appetite is the broad strategic willingness. The tell: appetite = the overall meal, tolerance = the wiggle room on each dish."},
  {"id":"q930","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A venture-backed gaming company's leadership deliberately pursues high-risk bets - shipping unproven features fast and outspending rivals - accepting large potential losses to capture users first. Which risk appetite posture BEST describes this?","options":["Conservative","Neutral","Expansionary","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Expansionary. An expansionary risk appetite deliberately accepts more risk in pursuit of growth and reward, exactly this aggressive market-grab stance; it is a chosen strategy, not recklessness. Conservative is its opposite and neutral sits between them. The tell: expansionary = high appetite for reward; conservative = low, minimizes risk; neutral = balanced; risk tolerance = the acceptable variance around a specific risk, not an overall posture."},
  {"id":"q931","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A regional water utility's board rejects any initiative carrying meaningful downside and approves only projects with near-certain outcomes, accepting slower growth to guarantee reliability. Which risk appetite posture is this?","options":["Conservative","Expansionary","Neutral","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Conservative. A conservative risk appetite minimizes risk-taking, favoring predictable, near-certain outcomes even at the cost of growth, which is this utility's stance. Neutral would accept measured risk when justified, and expansionary would embrace it for reward. The tell: conservative = low appetite, avoids downside; expansionary = high appetite for reward; neutral = balanced; risk tolerance = the acceptable variance around a specific risk before acting."},
  {"id":"q932","domain":"D5","family":"risk-appetite","question":"A logistics company evaluates each expansion on its own merits, taking risk when a solid business case supports it and declining when it does not, with no built-in lean toward boldness or caution. Which risk appetite posture fits?","options":["Expansionary","Neutral","Conservative","Risk tolerance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Neutral. A neutral risk appetite takes balanced, measured risks justified by analysis, leaning neither aggressive nor risk-averse, which is this case-by-case stance. Conservative would refuse meaningful downside outright; expansionary would chase reward aggressively. The tell: neutral = balanced and justified; expansionary = high appetite for reward; conservative = low appetite, avoids downside; risk tolerance = acceptable variance around one specific risk."},
  {"id":"q933","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A company purchases a cyber-insurance policy so that if a data breach lands, the payout comes from the insurer rather than the business's own balance sheet. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["Accept","Mitigate","Transfer","Avoid"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Transfer. Shifting the financial consequences to a third party - insurance being the classic vehicle - is risk transference. The tell: transfer = someone else absorbs the loss, mitigate = reduce it with controls, accept = live with it knowingly, avoid = stop doing the risky thing."},
  {"id":"q934","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A regional bank concludes that its aging public wire-transfer portal cannot be secured at a cost leadership will approve, so it permanently decommissions the service and retires the exposure with it. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["Transfer","Avoid","Mitigate","Accept"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Avoid. Shutting down the risky service eliminates the risk at its source because the activity that created it no longer exists. Mitigate loses because nothing keeps running behind controls - the service is gone. The tell: avoid = the activity stops, mitigate = continue with safeguards, transfer = shift the loss elsewhere, accept = continue and absorb the risk."},
  {"id":"q935","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"To cut ransomware risk, a firm deploys EDR, segments its network, and keeps offline immutable backups - and continues operating normally with the risk reduced but not gone. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["Accept","Avoid","Mitigate","Transfer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Mitigate. Applying controls to shrink probability or impact while continuing the activity is mitigation, the most common strategy. The tell: mitigate = reduce with safeguards, avoid = stop entirely, transfer = shift the loss, accept = take it as-is."},
  {"id":"q936","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A low-impact reporting bug would cost $50,000 to fix but could never cause more than $5,000 in harm. Leadership documents the analysis, signs off, and leaves it as-is. Which of the following risk strategies does this represent?","options":["Mitigate","Accept","Transfer","Avoid"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Accept. A conscious, documented decision to absorb a risk because treatment costs more than the exposure is acceptance - rational when the math says so. The tell: accept = informed sign-off to live with it, mitigate = spend to reduce it, transfer = pay someone else to hold it, avoid = drop the activity."},
  {"id":"q937","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A retailer keeps accepting card payments in every store but hands processing to a third party whose contract makes the processor liable for losses from any card-data breach on its platform. Which of the following risk strategies does this represent?","options":["Avoid","Accept","Mitigate","Transfer"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Transfer. Contractually shifting breach liability to the processor moves the loss to a third party while the retailer keeps taking cards. Avoid loses because the activity continues - avoidance would mean no longer accepting cards at all. The tell: transfer = liability moves by contract or insurance while the activity continues, avoid = the activity stops, mitigate = harden your own handling, accept = keep the liability knowingly."},
  {"id":"q938","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A study guide defines one strategy as reducing a risk's probability or impact by applying safeguards while the organization continues the activity itself. Which of the following strategies is being defined?","options":["Accept","Transfer","Avoid","Mitigate"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Mitigate. Keep doing it, but with controls that shrink the risk - the definition of mitigation. The tell: mitigate = continue with safeguards, avoid = discontinue, transfer = shift the loss, accept = continue with no treatment at all."},
  {"id":"q939","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"Unlike mitigation, which spends effort lowering a risk with controls, which of the following strategies involves consciously deciding to apply no treatment and simply absorb any loss that comes?","options":["Accept","Avoid","Transfer","Mitigate"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Accept. Acceptance is a deliberate, informed decision to carry the risk untreated - legitimate when exposure is small or treatment uneconomical, dangerous when it is just neglect. The tell: accept = knowing inaction, mitigate = active reduction, transfer = shifted loss, avoid = ended activity."},
  {"id":"q940","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"To shrink the damage from stolen laptops, a firm enables full-disk encryption and remote wipe on every device - staff keep working normally, but a theft now costs hardware instead of data. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["mitigate","avoid","accept","transfer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Mitigate. Controls that reduce the impact while the activity continues are mitigation. The tell: mitigate = reduce with safeguards, avoid = stop issuing laptops, transfer = insure the loss, accept = live with unencrypted risk."},
  {"id":"q941","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"After repeated security findings, a company permanently retires its legacy customer file-upload portal, ending the service so the exposure it created no longer exists. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["transfer","mitigate","accept","avoid"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Avoid. Shutting the risky service down entirely removes the risk at its source; mitigation would keep the portal running behind added controls, which is not what happened. The tell: avoid = stop the activity so the risk is gone, mitigate = keep it running with safeguards, transfer = shift the loss to another party, accept = keep it running and absorb the risk."},
  {"id":"q942","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A small firm negotiates its cloud contract so the provider is contractually liable for losses caused by platform outages. The firm keeps using the platform; the loss just lands elsewhere. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["transfer","mitigate","accept","avoid"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Transfer. Moving the financial consequences to a third party by contract is transference. The tell: transfer = the loss is someone else's bill, mitigate = reduce the loss, accept = pay it yourself, avoid = end the dependency."},
  {"id":"q943","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A retail chain's risk committee reviews the exposure of leaving a remote self-service kiosk unstaffed after hours, judges the worst-case loss trivial, and formally documents its decision to take no action. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["mitigate","transfer","accept","avoid"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Accept. A documented, informed decision to live with a trivial residual risk is acceptance - the record is what separates it from negligence. Mitigation loses because no control is added; the committee deliberately does nothing. The tell: accept = deliberate inaction on record, mitigate = act to reduce, transfer = shift the cost, avoid = end the activity."},
  {"id":"q944","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"An insurance broker describes a risk strategy that keeps the activity running while a third party - an insurer or outsourcer - absorbs the financial consequences of any loss. Which of the following strategies is being described?","options":["accept","transfer","mitigate","avoid"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Transfer. Activity continues, loss lands on the third party: transference via insurance or contract. The tell: transfer = the payout comes from elsewhere, accept = from your own budget, mitigate = made smaller by controls, avoid = never incurred."},
  {"id":"q945","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"A vulnerable public service cannot be retired, so rather than leaving it exposed or shutting it down, the team fronts it with a web application firewall and tighter access rules. The service keeps running with less exposure. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["mitigate","accept","avoid","transfer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Mitigate. Adding controls to lower exposure while continuing the service is mitigation, the everyday middle path. The tell: mitigate = keep running, better protected, avoid = shut it down, accept = keep running unprotected, transfer = make the loss someone else's."},
  {"id":"q946","domain":"D5","family":"risk-strategies","question":"Actuarial review shows a rare risk whose worst case costs less than either the insurance premium or the fix, so the company simply budgets a reserve to absorb any loss itself. Which of the following risk strategies is this?","options":["transfer","mitigate","avoid","accept"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Accept. Consciously self-funding a small risk because treatment costs more than exposure is textbook acceptance. The tell: accept = budget for the loss yourself, transfer = pay premiums so another pays the loss, mitigate = pay to shrink it, avoid = walk away from it."},
  {"id":"q947","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"The business impact analysis states that after an outage, the payment system must be back online within 2 hours before harm becomes unacceptable. Which of the following metrics is that 2-hour target?","options":["RPO","RTO","MTTR","MTBF"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"RTO. The recovery time objective is the maximum tolerable downtime - how fast service must return. The tell: RTO = how long you can be DOWN, RPO = how much DATA you can lose, MTTR = average repair time observed, MTBF = average time between failures."},
  {"id":"q948","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"A database is backed up every 4 hours because the business declared it can tolerate losing at most 4 hours of transactions in a disaster. Which of the following metrics defines that acceptable data-loss window?","options":["RTO","MTBF","RPO","MTTR"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"RPO. The recovery point objective is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, and it directly dictates backup frequency. The tell: RPO = data-loss window, RTO = downtime window, MTBF = reliability between failures, MTTR = repair duration."},
  {"id":"q949","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"A reliability report tracks how long, on average, technicians take to repair a failed component and return it to service. Which of the following metrics is this?","options":["MTTR","MTBF","RTO","RPO"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MTTR. Mean time to repair is the observed average fix-and-restore duration. The tell: MTTR = average repair time, MTBF = average uptime between failures, RTO = the downtime target you set, RPO = the data-loss target you set. Objectives are goals; MTTR/MTBF are measurements."},
  {"id":"q950","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"For a repairable server, which of the following metrics captures the average operating time between one failure and the next, serving as the core indicator of its reliability?","options":["MTTR","RPO","RTO","MTBF"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"MTBF. Mean time between failures measures how long the device typically runs before breaking again - bigger is better. The tell: MTBF = time between failures, MTTR = time to fix each one, RTO/RPO = recovery objectives the business sets, not measurements."},
  {"id":"q951","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"An e-commerce company engineers its backup pipeline so that after ANY incident, no more than 15 minutes of order data can ever be lost. Which of the following metrics does the 15-minute limit define?","options":["RTO","MTTR","MTBF","RPO"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"RPO. Maximum tolerable data loss expressed as time is the recovery point objective, and it dictates backup/replication frequency. The tell: RPO = how much data you may lose, RTO = how long you may be down, MTTR = observed repair time, MTBF = observed time between failures."},
  {"id":"q952","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"A reliability engineer cites a server model's MTBF in a hardware selection meeting. Which of the following does that value actually describe?","options":["average operating time between failures","target time to restore service","maximum tolerable data loss","average time to repair a failure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MTBF = average run time between failures, the core reliability statistic - higher is better. The tell: MTBF = uptime between breaks, MTTR = time to fix each break, RTO = the restore target you set, RPO = the data-loss target you set."},
  {"id":"q953","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"A vendor's datasheet boasts that its appliances average four hours from the moment of failure to being repaired and back in service. Which of the following metrics is that four-hour figure?","options":["MTBF","RTO","MTTR","RPO"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"MTTR. Average failure-to-fixed duration is mean time to repair, a measured property of the product and support process. The tell: MTTR = the observed repair average, RTO = the recovery target the business demands, MTBF = time between failures, RPO = data-loss tolerance."},
  {"id":"q954","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"Before drafting any recovery plan, a continuity team ranks which business functions would cause unacceptable harm if they went down and maps the systems each one depends on. Which analysis are they performing?","options":["Disaster recovery plan","Risk assessment","Business continuity plan","Business impact analysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Business impact analysis. A BIA identifies critical business functions, quantifies the harm of their disruption over time, and maps dependencies, producing the inputs recovery planning needs. A disaster recovery plan is tempting but is the later technical plan to restore IT, not the analysis that prioritizes functions. The tell: BIA = find and rank critical functions and dependencies; DRP = restore IT systems after a disaster; BCP = keep the business running during disruption; risk assessment = score threats to assets."},
  {"id":"q955","domain":"D5","family":"bia","question":"An airline's BIA sets two targets for its booking platform: it must be restored within 2 hours of any outage, and no more than 15 minutes of reservation data may be lost. The 2-hour target represents which metric?","options":["Recovery point objective","Recovery time objective","Mean time to repair","Mean time between failures"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Recovery time objective. The RTO is the maximum tolerable time to restore service after an outage, here 2 hours; the 15-minute data-loss limit is the RPO. Recovery point objective is the trap because both numbers sit in the same sentence. The tell: RTO = how fast you restore service (time forward); RPO = how much data you can lose (time backward to last backup); MTTR = average actual repair time; MTBF = average uptime between failures."},
  {"id":"q956","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"Before signing with a payment vendor, a bank insists the contract include a provision letting the bank inspect the vendor's security controls and records on demand throughout the relationship. Which of the following contract elements is this?","options":["Independent assessment","Right-to-audit clause","Supply chain analysis","Due diligence"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Right-to-audit clause. The contractual right to inspect the vendor yourself, on demand, is the right-to-audit provision. The tell: right-to-audit = you may inspect them, independent assessment = a neutral third party inspects them, supply chain analysis = examining their upstream suppliers, due diligence = the pre-signing investigation."},
  {"id":"q957","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"A manufacturer maps risk beyond its direct vendor, evaluating the smaller upstream firms that supply that vendor's chips and firmware for hidden compromise or fragility. Which of the following vendor-assessment activities is this?","options":["Supply chain analysis","Right-to-audit clause","Conflict of interest","Penetration testing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Supply chain analysis. Looking past the direct vendor to the suppliers behind it is supply chain analysis - risk inherits upstream. The tell: supply chain analysis = the vendors behind your vendor, right-to-audit = your contractual inspection right, conflict of interest = competing loyalties, penetration testing = technical attack simulation."},
  {"id":"q958","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"A bank asks a prospective vendor for a report in which an unaffiliated assessment firm - chosen for having no stake in the outcome - objectively reviewed the vendor's security controls. Which of the following vendor-assessment elements is this?","options":["right-to-audit clause","penetration testing","independent assessment","supply chain analysis"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Independent assessment. A neutral third party's objective review (a SOC 2 report, for example) is the independent assessment. The tell: independent assessment = an unaffiliated firm reviews them, right-to-audit = YOU may inspect them by contract, supply chain analysis = reviewing their upstream suppliers, penetration testing = simulated attack."},
  {"id":"q959","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"During vendor selection, a reviewer discloses that her spouse holds shares in one of the bidding firms - a personal interest that could bias her scoring. Which of the following concerns does this represent?","options":["due diligence","conflict of interest","independent assessment","right-to-audit clause"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Conflict of interest. A personal stake that could compromise professional judgment is a conflict of interest, and disclosure is the required first step. The tell: conflict of interest = competing loyalties, due diligence = the investigation itself, independent assessment = neutral review, right-to-audit = the inspection clause."},
  {"id":"q960","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"A retailer's contract with its cloud platform vendor authorizes the retailer to hire an external team to actively attack and attempt to exploit the vendor's hosted systems, validating the vendor's security claims. Which vendor-assessment method is this?","options":["Right-to-audit clause","Evidence of internal audits","Penetration testing","Independent assessment"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Penetration testing. Contractually authorized active exploitation of the vendor's systems to prove real-world security is penetration testing. A right-to-audit clause is tempting but only grants inspection of controls and records, not active attack. The tell: penetration testing = authorized simulated attack to prove exploitability; right-to-audit = contractual right to inspect records and controls; evidence of internal audits = the vendor's own self-reported audit results; independent assessment = a neutral third party's review."},
  {"id":"q961","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"A hospital negotiates a clause letting its own compliance staff inspect the electronic-health-record vendor's controls, logs, and facilities whenever the hospital chooses throughout the contract. Which vendor-assessment element does this clause provide?","options":["Independent assessment","Penetration testing","Supply chain analysis","Right-to-audit clause"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Right-to-audit clause. This contractual provision lets the customer inspect the vendor's controls and records on demand; the customer's own staff perform the review. Independent assessment is the trap - it looks similar but is performed by a neutral third party, not the customer. The tell: right-to-audit = the customer inspects; independent assessment = an unaffiliated firm reviews; penetration testing = active attack to prove exploitability; supply chain analysis = evaluating the vendor's own suppliers."},
  {"id":"q962","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"Asked to prove its security, a vendor hands over the results of its OWN internal audit team's reviews rather than any outside report. A risk analyst notes this is the weakest form of assurance offered. Which vendor-assessment element is it?","options":["Evidence of internal audits","Independent assessment","Right-to-audit clause","Penetration testing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Evidence of internal audits. Supplying the vendor's own internal audit results is self-reported proof, the weakest assurance tier because the vendor grades itself. Independent assessment is the contrast: a neutral third party with no stake performs it, which is why it carries more weight. The tell: evidence of internal audits = the vendor's self-reported audit findings; independent assessment = unaffiliated third-party review; right-to-audit = the customer's contractual right to inspect; penetration testing = authorized active attack."},
  {"id":"q963","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"Before investing, a venture firm demands that a SaaS startup provide a SOC 2 report produced by an outside auditing firm with no stake in the outcome, valuing it precisely for that objectivity. Which vendor-assessment element is this?","options":["Right-to-audit clause","Evidence of internal audits","Independent assessment","Penetration testing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Independent assessment. A report from an unaffiliated firm with no stake (SOC 2 style) is prized for objectivity because no self-interest colors the findings. Evidence of internal audits is the tempting near-miss, but those are self-reported and far weaker. The tell: independent assessment = neutral third-party review; evidence of internal audits = the vendor's self-reported findings; right-to-audit = the customer inspects directly; penetration testing = authorized active attack."},
  {"id":"q964","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"A defense contractor evaluates not only its direct software vendor but the third-party code libraries and offshore subcontractors that vendor depends on, hunting for weaknesses upstream. Which vendor-assessment activity is this?","options":["Independent assessment","Right-to-audit clause","Due diligence","Supply chain analysis"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Supply chain analysis. Assessing risk beyond the direct vendor - its own suppliers, subcontractors, and dependencies (fourth-party risk) - is supply chain analysis. Due diligence is tempting but describes investigating the direct vendor before signing, not tracing the chain behind it. The tell: supply chain analysis = evaluate the vendor's own suppliers; due diligence = investigate the vendor before signing; independent assessment = a neutral third party's review; right-to-audit = the customer's right to inspect the vendor."},
  {"id":"q965","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-assessment","question":"During a vendor selection, an evaluator scoring the proposals also earns money moonlighting as a paid consultant for one of the bidding companies. Which concern does this raise, remedied by disclosure and recusal?","options":["Due diligence","Conflict of interest","Right-to-audit clause","Supply chain analysis"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Conflict of interest. A personal or financial stake that could bias the evaluator's scoring is a conflict of interest, handled by disclosing it and recusing from the decision. Due diligence is a real vendor-selection step but names the pre-signing investigation, not the bias itself. The tell: conflict of interest = a personal stake that could bias a decision (fix: disclose and recuse); due diligence = investigate the vendor before signing; right-to-audit = contractual inspection right; supply chain analysis = assessing the vendor's suppliers."},
  {"id":"q966","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"Before granting a contractor access to proprietary source code, a firm requires a signed document legally barring the contractor from disclosing any confidential information encountered. Which of the following agreements is this?","options":["NDA","MOU","SLA","MOA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"NDA. The non-disclosure agreement's single job is enforceable confidentiality. The tell: NDA = secrecy obligations only, MOU = non-binding cooperation intent, SLA = service metrics, MOA = binding roles and responsibilities."},
  {"id":"q967","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"An IT director is asked to review the SLA in a new cloud contract before signature. Which of the following does that document primarily establish?","options":["A non-binding statement of mutual intent to cooperate","Overarching master terms that future projects reference","Measurable service targets and penalties for missing them","The contribution and profit terms of a business partnership"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"SLA = targets plus consequences. Uptime percentages, response times, and the credits owed when missed. The tell: SLA = the numbers and the penalties, MOU = intent, MSA = the master framework, BPA = partnership economics."},
  {"id":"q968","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"Unlike an MOA, which binds each party to defined roles and responsibilities, which of the following documents records only a general, non-binding statement of mutual intent to cooperate?","options":["MOU","SOW","MOA","MSA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MOU. Understanding versus agreement: the MOU expresses intent without enforceable obligations, while the MOA commits parties to defined duties. The tell: MOU = handshake on paper, MOA = binding responsibilities, SOW = project specifics, MSA = master terms."},
  {"id":"q969","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"Before entering an acquisition due-diligence data room, each investor must sign a document legally forbidding disclosure of anything confidential seen inside. Which of the following agreements is this?","options":["BPA","MOA","SLA","NDA"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"NDA. Enforceable confidentiality is the non-disclosure agreement's single purpose. The tell: NDA = keep it secret, BPA = partnership terms, MOA = binding cooperation roles, SLA = service commitments."},
  {"id":"q970","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"A contracts primer contrasts two documents: one sets measurable targets backed by penalties; the other merely puts shared goodwill on paper with nothing enforceable behind it. Which of the following is the second document?","options":["SLA","MSA","MOU","SOW"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"MOU. Recorded goodwill with no enforceable obligations is the memorandum of understanding; the metrics-with-penalties document it is contrasted against is the SLA. The tell: MOU = intent without teeth, SLA = numbers with teeth, MSA = the master framework, SOW = one project's scope."},
  {"id":"q971","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"In a vendor contract stack, which of the following documents is the master framework that individual statements of work attach to, rather than the scope of any one specific job?","options":["SOW","MSA","SLA","BPA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MSA. The master service agreement is the standing framework; each SOW plugs a specific project into it. The tell: MSA = the trunk, SOW = the branches, SLA = performance metrics within either, BPA = partnership structure."},
  {"id":"q972","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"Before a scheduled security test, both parties sign a document stating: in-scope IP ranges 10.4.0.0/16; testing permitted 6 p.m. to 6 a.m. only; no denial-of-service techniques; emergency stop contact is the on-call NOC. Which document is this?","options":["Statement of work","Service-level agreement","Rules of engagement","Non-disclosure agreement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Rules of engagement. The document fixing the operational boundaries and conduct of a security test - scope, permitted hours, banned techniques, and a stop contact - is the rules of engagement, signed before testing begins. A statement of work is the trap, but it defines commercial scope, deliverables, and schedule, not testing conduct. The tell: rules of engagement = what testers may and may not do, and when; SOW = the specific job's deliverables and timeline; SLA = measurable service targets with penalties; NDA = confidentiality only."},
  {"id":"q973","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"Two independent firms agree to jointly run a new venture and sign a document setting out each partner's capital contribution, profit-sharing split, and decision-making authority. Which of the following BEST describes this document?","options":["MOA","MSA","BPA","MOU"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"BPA. A business partnership agreement fixes the financial and governance terms between business partners, such as ownership and profit split. A MOA loses because it defines roles for a shared effort, not the economics of a partnership. The tell: BPA = terms of a business partnership, MOA = binding roles and responsibilities, MSA = master framework other contracts hang off, MOU = non-binding statement of intent."},
  {"id":"q974","domain":"D5","family":"agreements","question":"A company already has a master services agreement with a consultancy. For one specific website redesign, it now needs a document that spells out the exact deliverables, milestones, and timeline for that single project. Which of the following BEST fits?","options":["MSA","SOW","SLA","BPA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SOW. A statement of work defines the scope, deliverables, and schedule for one specific engagement that hangs off a master agreement. An MSA loses because it is the overarching framework, not the per-project detail. The tell: SOW/WO = the specific job's scope and timeline, MSA = the master framework it hangs off, SLA = measurable service targets with penalties, BPA = terms of a business partnership."},
  {"id":"q975","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"After years of repeated health-data violations, the regulator revokes a hospital lab's authorization to operate at all - the business is legally barred from continuing. Which of the following compliance consequences is this?","options":["Fines","Loss of license","Reputational damage","Sanctions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Loss of license. Losing the legal authorization to operate is the terminal compliance consequence, beyond any fine. The tell: loss of license = you may no longer operate, fines = monetary penalties, sanctions = imposed restrictions short of closure, reputational damage = the market's punishment."},
  {"id":"q976","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"Following a breach of customer records, the company is legally required to notify the data-protection regulator within 72 hours and inform affected individuals. Which of the following is this obligation an example of?","options":["Contractual impacts","Sanctions","External reporting","Internal reporting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"External reporting. Mandatory breach notification to regulators and the public is external reporting - disclosure outside the organization required by law. The tell: external reporting = telling regulators/public, internal reporting = informing your own leadership, sanctions = penalties imposed on you, contractual impacts = consequences under agreements."},
  {"id":"q977","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"After failing its payment-card standard audit, a merchant is ordered to pay a monetary penalty to the card brands and faces higher processing fees. Which of the following compliance consequences is the monetary penalty?","options":["fines","sanctions","reputational damage","loss of license"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Fines. Direct monetary penalties for non-compliance are fines, whether levied by regulators or industry bodies. The tell: fines = pay money, sanctions = imposed restrictions on activity, reputational damage = the market punishes you, loss of license = you may no longer operate."},
  {"id":"q978","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"Weeks of news coverage about a company's badly handled breach send customers to competitors and crater its brand-trust scores, though no regulator has acted. Which of the following consequences is this?","options":["fines","loss of license","reputational damage","contractual impacts"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Reputational damage. The market's verdict - lost customers and trust - is reputational damage, often costlier than any fine and imposed by no authority. The tell: reputational damage = the public's punishment, fines = a regulator's bill, loss of license = forced shutdown, contractual impacts = consequences under agreements."},
  {"id":"q979","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"Each quarter a company's compliance team delivers a control-status dashboard to the audit committee and department heads to drive internal remediation. No regulator or outside party receives it. Which type of compliance reporting is this?","options":["Internal compliance reporting","External compliance reporting","Risk reporting","Attestation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Internal compliance reporting. Reporting compliance status to management, the board, or an audit committee to drive remediation inside the org is internal reporting; the audience is internal. External reporting, by contrast, goes to regulators or the public. The tell: internal reporting = to management and board; external reporting = to regulators, auditors, or the public; risk reporting = communicating risk posture, not compliance status; attestation = a formal signed statement that controls are in place."},
  {"id":"q980","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"A bank submits its mandated annual compliance report to the federal banking regulator and files the required disclosures with the securities authority, as the law demands. Which type of compliance reporting is this?","options":["Internal compliance reporting","External compliance reporting","Self-assessment","Risk reporting"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"External compliance reporting. Legally mandated reporting to regulators and authorities outside the organization is external reporting; the audience sits outside the company. Internal reporting is the contrast: it goes to management and the board to drive remediation. The tell: external reporting = to regulators or the public, often legally required; internal reporting = to management and board; self-assessment = a team grading its own controls; risk reporting = communicating risk posture to stakeholders."},
  {"id":"q981","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"A company's own compliance function continuously tracks the status of its security controls against policy and flags gaps for fixing, with no regulator or outside auditor involved in the watching. Which activity is this?","options":["External compliance monitoring","Independent assessment","Regulatory examination","Internal compliance monitoring"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Internal compliance monitoring. When the organization's own compliance function tracks control status and flags gaps, the monitoring is internal because it happens inside the org. External monitoring is the sibling, performed by regulators, auditors, or partners from outside. The tell: internal monitoring = the org watches its own controls; external monitoring = an outside party (regulator, auditor, partner) watches; independent assessment = a neutral third party's formal review; regulatory examination = an authority's official inspection."},
  {"id":"q982","domain":"D5","family":"compliance","question":"A GRC platform automatically pulls control evidence from systems every night, flags any configuration drift, and generates the compliance status report with no analyst effort. Which element of compliance monitoring does this illustrate?","options":["Attestation","Self-assessment","Automation","Independent assessment"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Automation. A platform that continuously collects control evidence, detects drift, and generates reports on its own is automation of compliance monitoring; it scales oversight and cuts manual error. Self-assessment is tempting but is a manual checklist a team fills in itself. The tell: automation = tooling continuously gathers evidence and reports; self-assessment = a team manually rates its own controls; attestation = a formal signed statement of compliance; independent assessment = a neutral third party's review."},
  {"id":"q983","domain":"D5","family":"due-diligence-care","question":"Months after signing a SaaS vendor that has now suffered a breach, a review finds nobody examined the vendor's audit reports, financials, or breach history before the contract. Which of the following did the firm fail to perform?","options":["Due care","Attestation","Due diligence","Acknowledgement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Due diligence. The pre-signing investigation into what the firm was getting into is due diligence, and skipping it left the risk unexamined. Due care loses because it covers the ongoing responsible actions after engagement, not the upfront homework. The tell: due diligence = look before you leap, due care = act responsibly on an ongoing basis afterward, attestation = formally affirming something is true, acknowledgement = confirming receipt or awareness."},
  {"id":"q984","domain":"D5","family":"due-diligence-care","question":"After engaging the vendor, the firm keeps doing the responsible things - maintaining access controls, monitoring, patching - to protect the shared data day after day. Which of the following BEST describes these ongoing reasonable actions?","options":["Due care","Due diligence","Attestation","Acknowledgement"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Due care. Continuously acting as a reasonable, prudent organization would is due care - the ongoing conduct, where due diligence was the upfront homework. The tell: due care = ongoing responsible action, due diligence = the prior investigation, attestation = signed affirmation, acknowledgement = confirmed awareness."},
  {"id":"q985","domain":"D5","family":"due-diligence-care","question":"A compliance package includes a signed statement in which an executive formally affirms that a specific control is implemented and operating as described. Which of the following terms names this formal affirmation?","options":["Due diligence","Due care","Acknowledgement","Attestation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Attestation. Formally affirming, usually in writing, that a condition or control is true is attestation - it carries accountability for the claim. The tell: attestation = signed affirmation of truth, acknowledgement = confirming you received/are aware, due diligence = investigating first, due care = behaving responsibly ongoing."},
  {"id":"q986","domain":"D5","family":"due-diligence-care","question":"A board refuses to vote on a proposed acquisition until the target's compliance history, audit findings, and open litigation have been fully investigated. Which of the following terms describes this pre-decision investigation?","options":["due care","attestation","acknowledgement","due diligence"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Due diligence. Investigating before committing to the decision is due diligence - the homework phase. Due care loses because it is the ongoing prudent conduct after an engagement begins, not the upfront research. The tell: due diligence = investigate before the decision, due care = act responsibly after it, attestation = formally affirming a fact is true, acknowledgement = confirming receipt or awareness."},
  {"id":"q987","domain":"D5","family":"due-diligence-care","question":"After the annual security policy update, every employee signs a form confirming they received and understood the new policy. Which of the following terms BEST describes this act?","options":["acknowledgement","attestation","due care","due diligence"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Acknowledgement. Confirming receipt and awareness is acknowledgement; attesting would mean affirming that some control or condition is actually true. The tell: acknowledgement = I received and understood it, attestation = I affirm it is true, due diligence/due care = investigation and ongoing prudence."},
  {"id":"q988","domain":"D5","family":"due-diligence-care","question":"A liability seminar contrasts two duties: the upfront investigation before an engagement, and the ongoing reasonable actions taken to protect assets after it begins. Which of the following terms names the ongoing duty?","options":["due diligence","attestation","due care","acknowledgement"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Due care. The continuing duty to act as a reasonable, prudent organization is due care; due diligence was the investigation that preceded it. The tell: diligence = before (investigate), care = after (act responsibly, continuously)."},
  {"id":"q989","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A customer emails a formal request demanding the company erase every piece of personal data it holds about her, as privacy law entitles her to do. Which of the following privacy rights is she exercising?","options":["Data inventory","Right to be forgotten","Data retention","Data subject"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Right to be forgotten. The enforceable demand for erasure of one's personal data is the right to be forgotten (right to erasure). The tell: right to be forgotten = delete my data, data subject = the person the data is about, data inventory = the org's map of what it holds, data retention = how long data is kept."},
  {"id":"q990","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"In privacy law, which of the following terms names the individual human being that a set of personal data actually describes?","options":["Data controller","Data subject","Data processor","Data custodian"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data subject. The person the data is about is the data subject - the one privacy rights belong to. The tell: data subject = the individual described, data controller = decides purpose and means, data processor = handles data on instructions, data custodian = the technical caretaker."},
  {"id":"q991","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"To comply with privacy law, a company builds a catalog of exactly what personal data it holds, which systems store it, and the purpose for each collection. Which of the following practices is this?","options":["data inventory","right to be forgotten","retention","data subject"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Data inventory. The catalog of what personal data exists, where, and why is the data inventory - the prerequisite for honoring any privacy right. The tell: data inventory = the map of held data, right to be forgotten = the erasure demand, retention = how long data is kept, data subject = the person described."},
  {"id":"q992","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A privacy policy commits to keeping customer records for seven years and deleting them afterward. Which of the following concepts does the seven-year period define?","options":["data inventory","retention","right to be forgotten","ownership"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Retention. The defined keep-then-delete period is the retention schedule, balancing legal requirements against data-minimization. The tell: retention = how long data lives, data inventory = what data exists, right to be forgotten = deletion on the subject's demand, ownership = accountability for the data."},
  {"id":"q993","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A single US state's consumer-privacy act grants data-deletion rights only to residents of that state and applies only to companies doing business there. Which legal scope does this privacy law have?","options":["Local or regional","National","Global","Industry-specific"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Local or regional. A privacy law that binds only within one state or province and protects only that jurisdiction's residents has local/regional scope. National is the trap, but that would apply across the whole country, not a single state. The tell: local/regional = one state or province; national = the entire country but stops at the border; global = extraterritorial reach across countries (GDPR-style); industry-specific = tied to a sector rather than a jurisdiction."},
  {"id":"q994","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A country enacts a privacy statute covering all of its citizens' data anywhere within its borders, but the law has no force once data crosses into another country. Which legal scope does this law have?","options":["Global","Local or regional","National","Industry-specific"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"National. A law that applies uniformly across an entire country but stops at its border has national scope. Global is the trap, but global scope reaches beyond borders to a country's residents wherever their data goes. The tell: national = the whole country but stops at the border; local/regional = a single state or province; global = extraterritorial reach across countries; industry-specific = tied to a sector, not a jurisdiction."},
  {"id":"q995","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A cloud firm based outside the EU processes EU residents' data, so GDPR still binds it, yet a data-localization law in another market forbids exactly the cross-border transfer GDPR would allow. Which privacy legal implication does this clash illustrate?","options":["National","Global","Local or regional","Industry-specific"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Global. Privacy duties that reach across borders - GDPR binding a non-EU firm while a foreign data-localization law pulls the other way - are a global legal implication, the multinational compliance pain point. National is tempting, but a national law stops at one border; the conflict here spans jurisdictions. The tell: global = extraterritorial reach and conflicting jurisdictions; national = one whole country only; local/regional = a single state or province; industry-specific = tied to a sector, not geography."},
  {"id":"q996","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A retailer stores purchase and contact records describing its customer Priya. In privacy terms, Priya - the identifiable person the records are about - is which party?","options":["Data owner","Data subject","Data controller","Data custodian"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Data subject. In privacy law the data subject is the identifiable natural person the personal data describes, here Priya. Data owner is the trap: it is an internal accountability role for a data set, not the person the data is about. The tell: data subject = the person the data describes; data owner = the internal role accountable for the data set; data controller = decides why and how data is processed; data custodian = provides hands-on technical care."},
  {"id":"q997","domain":"D5","family":"privacy","question":"A company's policy is to keep customer data for five years and then delete it. Separately, a customer submits a formal demand to erase her data now. Her demand invokes which right, as distinct from the company's disposal schedule?","options":["Right to be forgotten","Data retention","Data inventory","Data minimization"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Right to be forgotten. A subject-initiated demand to erase personal data exercises the right to be forgotten; the company must locate and delete it. Data retention is the trap, but that is the organization's own scheduled keep-then-dispose policy, not a right the subject exercises. The tell: right to be forgotten = the subject's erasure right; data retention = how long the org keeps data by schedule; data inventory = knowing what personal data you hold; data minimization = collecting only what you need."},
  {"id":"q998","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"A department scores its own controls against a checklist and reports results up to its own manager - no reviewer from outside the team is involved. Which of the following audit types is this?","options":["Independent third-party audit","External audit","Regulatory examination","Self-assessment"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Self-assessment. Reviewing your own controls yourself is a self-assessment: cheap, fast, and least convincing to outsiders. The tell: self-assessment = graded your own homework, internal audit = the org's audit function reviews, external/third-party = unaffiliated outsiders review, regulatory examination = the regulator itself inspects."},
  {"id":"q999","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"To satisfy investors who distrust in-house reviews, a company engages an unaffiliated auditing firm with no financial stake in the outcome to examine its controls objectively. Which of the following audit types is this?","options":["Self-assessment","Internal audit","Attestation","Independent third-party audit"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Independent third-party audit. An unaffiliated outside firm with no stake delivers the objectivity internal reviews cannot. The tell: independent third-party = unaffiliated outsiders, internal audit = the company's own audit staff, self-assessment = the team itself, attestation = the signed conclusion an audit may produce."},
  {"id":"q1000","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"A company's audit committee has in-house staff review control effectiveness across departments and report the findings to the board - no outside firm is engaged. Which of the following audit types is this?","options":["external audit","independent third-party","attestation","internal audit"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Internal audit. The organization's own audit function reviewing controls and reporting to the board is an internal audit - more rigorous than a self-assessment, less independent than an outside review. The tell: internal audit = your audit staff, self-assessment = the team grading itself, external/third-party = outsiders, attestation = the resulting signed affirmation."},
  {"id":"q1001","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"A banking regulator's examiners arrive to formally inspect a bank's controls and records, confirming compliance with the law the regulator enforces. Which of the following audit types is this?","options":["self-assessment","internal audit","external audit","attestation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"External audit. An outside authority - here the regulator itself - examining the organization is an external audit (a regulatory examination). The tell: external = conducted from outside the organization, internal = your own audit staff, self-assessment = the team itself, attestation = the signed conclusion."},
  {"id":"q1002","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"After completing its fieldwork, an audit firm issues a signed letter stating: 'We affirm that the controls operated effectively throughout the period.' The signed sign-off itself, as opposed to the evidence-gathering, is which type of engagement output?","options":["Assessment","Attestation","Regulatory examination","Self-assessment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Attestation. The formal signed opinion affirming that controls operated effectively is an attestation, the sign-off distinct from the audit fieldwork that gathered the evidence. An assessment is the trap, but an assessment is an advisory gap-finding evaluation, not a formal signed opinion. The tell: attestation = the formal signed statement or opinion of compliance; assessment = advisory evaluation to find gaps; regulatory examination = an authority's official inspection; self-assessment = a team rating its own controls."},
  {"id":"q1003","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"A manufacturer's internal audit team - employees who report independently to the audit committee rather than to the departments they examine - verifies that each unit follows company policy and applicable regulations. Which of the following BEST describes this activity?","options":["Self-assessment","Internal compliance audit","Independent third-party audit","Regulatory examination"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Internal (compliance) audit. The org's own staff, kept independent of the units they review, check adherence to policy and regulations. Self-assessment loses because that is a team grading its OWN controls with no independent reviewer. The tell: internal audit = in-house but independent function reviewing others; self-assessment = the team rates itself; independent third-party = an unaffiliated outside firm; regulatory examination = a government authority inspects under legal power."},
  {"id":"q1004","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"A board-level subcommittee reviews the internal audit team's findings, safeguards that team's independence, and selects and engages the external auditor, yet it never performs any testing itself. Which of the following is described?","options":["Audit committee","Internal audit function","Self-assessment team","Independent third-party auditor"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Audit committee. It provides board-level oversight of the audit program - receiving findings, protecting auditor independence, and hiring the external auditor - without running tests. Internal audit function loses because that is the group that actually performs the testing the committee oversees. The tell: audit committee = oversight and governance of audits; internal audit = executes the reviews; self-assessment = a team rates itself; independent third-party auditor = the outside firm engaged."},
  {"id":"q1005","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"Ahead of an external audit, each business unit completes a control checklist rating its own compliance and submits the scores to its unit leadership, with no independent reviewer involved. Which of the following assessment types is this?","options":["Internal compliance audit","Regulatory examination","Self-assessment","Independent third-party audit"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Self-assessment. The unit evaluates its OWN controls and reports to its own leadership - the cheapest and least independent form of review. Internal compliance audit loses because that requires a separate in-house audit function, independent of the unit, to do the review. The tell: self-assessment = team grades itself; internal audit = independent in-house function; independent third-party = unaffiliated outside firm; regulatory examination = authority-mandated inspection."},
  {"id":"q1006","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"Insurance regulators with statutory authority arrive to formally inspect a firm's records and controls for compliance with state insurance law, and the firm cannot lawfully decline the inspection. Which of the following BEST describes this?","options":["Independent third-party audit","Internal compliance audit","Self-assessment","Regulatory examination"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Regulatory examination. A government authority both requires and conducts a formal, legally backed inspection the firm cannot refuse. Independent third-party audit loses because an unaffiliated firm merely performs a review and has no legal power to compel it. The tell: regulatory examination = authority requires and performs it; independent third-party audit = outside firm performs, no legal authority; internal audit and self-assessment = conducted inside the organization."},
  {"id":"q1007","domain":"D5","family":"audits","question":"A company hires an outside consultancy to evaluate its security program against a framework, identify gaps, and recommend improvements, producing guidance rather than a pass-or-fail opinion. Which of the following BEST describes this engagement?","options":["Independent third-party audit","Assessment","Regulatory examination","Attestation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Assessment. It is an advisory evaluation aimed at finding gaps and recommending improvements, with no formal pass-or-fail verdict. Independent third-party audit loses because an audit is a formal, evidence-based verification against a standard that issues findings or an opinion. The tell: assessment = advisory gap-finding; audit = formal pass/fail verification; regulatory examination = authority-mandated inspection; attestation = the signed formal opinion on an audit."},
  {"id":"q1008","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"A penetration tester is hired with zero inside help: no documentation, no credentials, no network diagrams. Everything must be discovered exactly as a real external attacker would. Which of the following engagement types is this?","options":["Known environment","Partially known environment","Unknown environment","Integrated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unknown environment. No prior knowledge is the unknown-environment test (the SY0-701 term for the retired color-box naming), maximizing realism at the cost of time. The tell: unknown = no information, known = full documentation and access, partially known = some but not all, integrated = combined testing approach."},
  {"id":"q1009","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"In early reconnaissance, an analyst mines public records, search engines, DNS registries, and social media - and never sends a single packet toward the target's systems. Which of the following activities is this?","options":["Active reconnaissance","Known environment","Defensive testing","Passive reconnaissance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Passive reconnaissance. Gathering intelligence without touching the target is passive; the moment you probe their systems - scans, pings - it becomes active. The tell: passive = they cannot detect you looking, active = your packets hit their infrastructure, known environment = an engagement type, not a recon method."},
  {"id":"q1010","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"A penetration tester begins the engagement holding full network diagrams, application source code, and administrative credentials - total inside knowledge from day one. Which of the following engagement types is this?","options":["unknown environment","partially known environment","passive reconnaissance","known environment"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Known environment. Full documentation and access is the known-environment test: maximum coverage per hour, minimum realism. The tell: known = everything shared, unknown = nothing shared, partially known = something shared, passive reconnaissance = a recon technique, not an engagement type."},
  {"id":"q1011","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"An analyst runs port scans and fires crafted packets directly at the target to map live services - actions the target's sensors could log and alert on. Which of the following reconnaissance types is this?","options":["passive reconnaissance","active reconnaissance","unknown environment","known environment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Active reconnaissance. Probing the target directly - scans, crafted packets - is active recon, detectable by the defender. The tell: active = your traffic touches their systems, passive = you only consult public sources, known/unknown environment = engagement scoping, not recon methods."},
  {"id":"q1012","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"Wanting the deepest possible test in a short window, a bank gives the testers full architecture diagrams, application source code, and administrator accounts before work begins. Which of the following engagement types is this?","options":["Partially known environment","Known environment","Unknown environment","Active reconnaissance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Known environment. The testers start with complete inside knowledge - diagrams, source, and admin credentials - so they can probe deeply and fast. Partially known loses because that provides only some information, such as a user account and a diagram but no source or admin access. The tell: known = full information; partially known = some information; unknown = no inside information; active reconnaissance = a probing method, not a knowledge level."},
  {"id":"q1013","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"To simulate an internet-based attacker as realistically as possible, a retailer tells the testing firm only its company name and lets the testers discover everything else themselves. Which of the following engagement types is this?","options":["Unknown environment","Partially known environment","Known environment","Passive reconnaissance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Unknown environment. The testers get no inside information and must discover the entire attack surface from outside, like a real external attacker. Partially known loses because that would hand over some detail, such as a standard user account or a network diagram. The tell: unknown = no inside information; partially known = some information; known = full information; passive reconnaissance = a non-touching recon method, not a knowledge level."},
  {"id":"q1014","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"Before any scanning, a tester profiles the target using WHOIS records, employee profiles on professional networks, and public job postings that reveal its technologies, never touching the company's own systems. Which of the following is this?","options":["Active reconnaissance","Known environment","Unknown environment","Passive reconnaissance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Passive reconnaissance. Information is gathered entirely from third-party and public sources, with no packets sent to the target, so the activity is essentially undetectable. Active reconnaissance loses because it directly probes the target and can be logged. The tell: passive = no interaction with the target; active = direct probing that can be detected; known and unknown environment describe the tester's starting knowledge, a different axis."},
  {"id":"q1015","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-types","question":"A SOC analyst reviews an IDS log:\n15:02 203.0.113.9 -> 10.0.0.5 SYN port 22\n15:02 203.0.113.9 -> 10.0.0.5 SYN port 80\n15:02 203.0.113.9 -> 10.0.0.5 SYN port 443\n15:02 203.0.113.9 -> 10.0.0.5 SYN port 3389\nWhich of the following is the external host MOST likely performing?","options":["Passive reconnaissance","Unknown environment","Active reconnaissance","Known environment"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Active reconnaissance. One external host is sequentially probing multiple ports (22, 80, 443, 3389) on a target and generating traffic the IDS can log - direct interaction with the target. Passive reconnaissance loses because passive methods never send packets at the target and leave no such logs. The tell: active = probing and scanning the target that can be detected; passive = gathering public information with zero interaction; known and unknown environment describe tester knowledge, not the activity."},
  {"id":"q1016","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"Each quarter, the security team sends employees realistic simulated lure emails that impersonate trusted vendors, tracks who clicks and who reports, and tailors follow-up training to the results. Which of the following BEST describes this exercise?","options":["Recurring reporting","Risky behavior","Initial reporting","Phishing campaign"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Phishing campaign. Simulated phishing to measure and improve user response is an internal phishing campaign, the awareness program's live-fire drill. Recurring reporting loses because it names the cadence of program metrics, not the simulation itself. The tell: phishing campaign = the simulation exercise, recurring/initial reporting = how program results get reported, risky behavior = a category the campaign measures."},
  {"id":"q1017","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"An employee accidentally emails a spreadsheet of customer records to the wrong recipient - pure mistake, no ill intent, promptly self-reported. Which of the following categories of anomalous behavior is this?","options":["Risky behavior","Unexpected behavior","Unintentional behavior","Recurring reporting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unintentional behavior. An honest mistake without intent is unintentional - the answer is training and guardrails, not discipline. The tell: unintentional = accident, risky = knowingly cutting corners (password sharing, policy bypass), unexpected = out-of-pattern activity that needs investigating, recurring reporting = a program metric."},
  {"id":"q1018","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"An employee knowingly disables the endpoint protection agent to install an unapproved application, fully aware this violates policy. Which of the following categories of anomalous behavior is this?","options":["risky behavior","unexpected behavior","unintentional behavior","recurring reporting"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Risky behavior. Knowingly bypassing controls or policy is risky behavior - deliberate corner-cutting, distinct from an honest mistake. The tell: risky = knew better and did it anyway, unintentional = genuine accident, unexpected = out-of-pattern activity needing investigation, recurring reporting = a program metric."},
  {"id":"q1019","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"A security awareness program sends staff refreshers every quarter and publishes its metrics to leadership on the same fixed cadence all year. Which of the following reporting types is this?","options":["initial reporting","recurring reporting","risky behavior","phishing campaign"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Recurring reporting. Fixed-cadence, repeated reporting of awareness activity and metrics is recurring reporting; the program's kickoff numbers were initial reporting. The tell: recurring = on a schedule forever, initial = the first baseline report, phishing campaign = the simulation exercise, risky behavior = a category being reported on."},
  {"id":"q1020","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"To measure human risk, a company periodically emails staff benign look-alike scam messages, delivers just-in-time coaching to anyone who interacts with them, and watches whether the reporting rate climbs each cycle. Which of the following practices is this?","options":["Phishing simulation campaign","Disciplinary enforcement program","Insider threat awareness program","Security policy acknowledgement"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Phishing simulation campaign. The organization sends its own fake phishing to measure click and report rates and trains those who fall for it. Disciplinary enforcement program loses because the correct response to a simulation click is immediate training, not punishment. The tell: a phishing campaign trains and measures; disciplinary action punishes; insider threat awareness teaches reporting of risky colleagues; policy acknowledgement is signing that you read the rules."},
  {"id":"q1021","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"When a new awareness program launches, the team runs one first phishing test purely to capture the organization's starting click and report rates before any training begins. Which of the following reporting types is this?","options":["Recurring reporting","Initial reporting","Ad hoc reporting","External reporting"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Initial reporting. This first measurement establishes the baseline metrics against which later results are compared. Recurring reporting loses because that is the ongoing, repeated cadence of measurement, not the one-time baseline at launch. The tell: initial = the first baseline; recurring = the repeating cadence; ad hoc = triggered by an event; external = reported to outside parties."},
  {"id":"q1022","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"An employee clicks the simulated phishing test for the third quarter running, and the team's ongoing quarterly metrics flag the repeat pattern to leadership and trigger extended training. Which of the following reporting types is at work?","options":["One-time assessment","Initial reporting","Recurring reporting","Ad hoc reporting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Recurring reporting. The repeating quarterly cadence is what surfaces trends and repeat offenders and drives escalated training. Initial reporting loses because that is only the first baseline, which cannot reveal a repeat pattern across quarters. The tell: recurring = ongoing repeated cadence catching trends; initial = the first baseline; ad hoc = event-triggered; one-time assessment = a single planned engagement."},
  {"id":"q1023","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"A newly hired awareness manager defines target audiences, selects topics from last year's incident data, drafts the training materials, and sets success metrics, but has delivered nothing yet. Which of the following program phases is this?","options":["Development","Execution","Monitoring","Initial reporting"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Development. This is the build phase - defining audiences, choosing topics, creating materials, and setting metrics - all before any delivery. Execution loses because execution is the delivery phase: running sessions, launching campaigns, and enforcing completion. The tell: development = design and build before delivery; execution = deliver and run; monitoring = track results over time; initial reporting = capture the first baseline."},
  {"id":"q1024","domain":"D5","family":"awareness","question":"The following quarter, the same manager schedules the training sessions, launches the first phishing campaign, enforces completion deadlines, and begins collecting metrics. Which of the following program phases is this?","options":["Development","Monitoring","Execution","Initial reporting"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Execution. Delivering the sessions, launching the campaign, enforcing completion, and collecting metrics is running the program. Development loses because development is the earlier build phase - defining audiences and drafting materials - before anything is delivered. The tell: execution = deliver and run; development = build it first; monitoring = track results over time; initial reporting = the first baseline measurement."},
  {"id":"q1025","domain":"D5","family":"policy-lifecycle","question":"After a breach, a security committee's lessons-learned review exposes a gap, so the committee updates the incident response policy and re-publishes it with a new scheduled review date. Which of the following governance practices is this?","options":["Initial policy development","Policy monitoring and revision","Policy publication and distribution","Policy exception handling"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Policy monitoring and revision. Governance documents live on a review cycle, and an incident that exposes a gap is a classic trigger to revise and re-publish. Initial development is tempting, but the policy already existed; this is updating it, not creating it. The tell: monitoring and revision = updating a document on a trigger or review cycle, initial development = creating it the first time, publication = distributing it, exception handling = approving deviations from it."},
  {"id":"q1026","domain":"D5","family":"policy-lifecycle","question":"An audit finds a security policy that still references retired technology and has accumulated a stack of exception requests filed against it. Which of the following should the governance team do NEXT?","options":["Grant a standing exemption for the retired technology","Archive the policy and stop enforcing it","Escalate each exception request to legal counsel","Schedule a review and revise the policy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Schedule a review and revise the policy. Outdated technology references and repeated exception requests are textbook signs a policy needs review and revision, since publishing is never the end state. Granting a standing exemption is tempting, but that only patches one gap while leaving the stale policy in force. The tell: revision is triggered by incidents, new regulations, review dates, or repeated exceptions; exemptions and archiving dodge fixing the document rather than updating it."},
  {"id":"q1027","domain":"D5","family":"governance-structures","question":"A panel of senior executives accountable to shareholders sets the organization's high-level mandate to reduce cyber risk and delegates the detailed design work to others. Which of the following governance structures is this panel?","options":["Board of directors","Security steering committee","Government entity","Centralized governance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Board of directors. The board sets strategic direction and holds top accountability, then delegates the details. A security steering committee is the trap, but that is the subject-matter-expert subset that works the details rather than setting the top mandate. The tell: board = strategic direction and accountability, committee = SME subset working the details, government entity = external oversight body, centralized governance = one internal decision body for the whole org."},
  {"id":"q1028","domain":"D5","family":"governance-structures","question":"A security steering committee receives the board's direction and performs the detailed work of selecting the control framework and reviewing the program. Which of the following governance structures is described?","options":["Board of directors","Government entity","Security steering committee","Decentralized governance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Security steering committee. A committee is the subject-matter-expert subset that takes board direction and works the details, such as choosing the framework. The board is the trap, but the board sets direction and accountability rather than doing the hands-on framework selection. The tell: committee = SME subset working the details, board = strategic direction and accountability, government entity = external oversight, decentralized governance = business units deciding independently."},
  {"id":"q1029","domain":"D5","family":"governance-structures","question":"A government-sponsored body issues binding cybersecurity rules for a critical-infrastructure sector and oversees the compliance of the firms operating in it. Which of the following governance structures is this body?","options":["Board of directors","Government entity","Security steering committee","Centralized governance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Government entity. A government-sponsored body that imposes rules and oversees a sector is an external government entity, relevant even to private firms in that sector. A board is the trap, but a board governs a single organization internally rather than imposing external sector rules. The tell: government entity = external oversight imposing rules, board = internal strategic direction, committee = internal SME subset, centralized governance = one internal decision body."},
  {"id":"q1030","domain":"D5","family":"governance-structures","question":"Within a single corporation, each regional business unit writes its own security procedures independently, as long as they stay within the overarching corporate policy. Which of the following BEST classifies this governance model?","options":["Centralized governance","Board of directors","Government entity","Decentralized governance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Decentralized governance. Letting each business unit make its own security decisions is decentralized: faster and closer to the work, but prone to inconsistency. Centralized governance is the trap, but that concentrates decisions in one body for consistency across the whole org. The tell: decentralized = business units decide (faster, inconsistent), centralized = one decision body (consistent, slower), board = strategic direction, government entity = external oversight."},
  {"id":"q1031","domain":"D5","family":"risk-process","question":"A newly hired CISO's first workshop catalogs every asset and what could go wrong with each, deliberately assigning no ratings or dollar figures yet. Which of the following risk management steps is this?","options":["Risk assessment","Risk identification","Risk reporting","Risk treatment"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Risk identification. The first step discovers and catalogs risks without scoring them, exactly as described. Risk assessment is the trap, but assessment scores or analyzes the risks, and here no ratings exist yet. The tell: identification = catalog risks with no scoring, assessment = score and analyze them, reporting = communicate posture to stakeholders, treatment = act on the risks."},
  {"id":"q1032","domain":"D5","family":"risk-process","question":"Each quarter the security team delivers the board a deck summarizing the top risks, key risk indicator trends, and a treatment-status heat map. Which of the following risk management activities is this?","options":["Risk assessment","Risk register","Risk identification","Risk reporting"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Risk reporting. Communicating risk posture to stakeholders through dashboards and heat maps is reporting. The risk register is the trap, but that is the underlying log of risks and treatments, not the act of communicating posture to the board. The tell: reporting = communicate posture to stakeholders, register = the log or document of risks, assessment = score the risks, identification = catalog them."},
  {"id":"q1033","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-monitoring","question":"A year into a contract, a company holds quarterly reviews of its vendor's SLA performance metrics and runs a full security reassessment each year to confirm nothing has slipped. Which third-party risk activity is this?","options":["Vendor monitoring","Due diligence","Independent assessment","Security questionnaire"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Vendor monitoring. Ongoing oversight after signing - periodic SLA reviews, reassessments, and scorecards - is vendor monitoring. Due diligence is the trap, but it is the homework done before signing; the timing gives it away. The tell: due diligence = investigate before signing; vendor monitoring = ongoing oversight after signing; independent assessment = a neutral third party's one-time review; security questionnaire = cheap self-attested input at either stage."},
  {"id":"q1034","domain":"D5","family":"vendor-monitoring","question":"Ahead of a contract renewal, procurement emails the vendor a standard 200-item control questionnaire for the vendor to fill in itself. A reviewer flags that these answers are self-reported, not verified. Which assurance method is this?","options":["Independent assessment","Right-to-audit clause","Penetration testing","Security questionnaire"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Security questionnaire. A standardized set of control questions the vendor answers itself is cheap but self-attested, useful input rather than verified assurance. Independent assessment is the contrast the reviewer wants: a neutral third party verifies the controls. The tell: security questionnaire = self-reported vendor answers; independent assessment = neutral third-party verification; right-to-audit = the customer inspects directly; penetration testing = authorized active attack to prove exploitability."},
  {"id":"q1035","domain":"D5","family":"noncompliance-consequences","question":"A data-protection authority orders a company to pay a monetary penalty equal to 4 percent of its global annual turnover for mishandling personal data. Which consequence of non-compliance is this penalty?","options":["Sanctions","Fines","Loss of license","Contractual impacts"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fines. A monetary penalty imposed by a regulator, here up to 4 percent of global turnover, is a fine; the defining feature is that it is money owed. Sanctions are broader imposed restrictions rather than a straight monetary penalty. The tell: fines = money owed; sanctions = imposed restrictions or exclusions short of shutdown; loss of license = authorization to operate revoked entirely; contractual impacts = private-party consequences like termination."},
  {"id":"q1036","domain":"D5","family":"noncompliance-consequences","question":"After compliance failures, a regulator bars a firm from bidding on new government contracts for two years, but the firm keeps running its existing business. Which consequence of non-compliance is this restriction?","options":["Loss of license","Fines","Reputational damage","Sanctions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Sanctions. An imposed restriction or exclusion - here a ban on new government contracts - that stops short of shutting the business down is a sanction. Loss of license is the trap, but that would revoke the firm's authorization to operate at all; this firm keeps running. The tell: sanctions = imposed restrictions or exclusions short of shutdown; loss of license = authorization to operate revoked entirely; fines = money owed; reputational damage = brand and customer-trust harm."},
  {"id":"q1037","domain":"D5","family":"noncompliance-consequences","question":"Weeks after a widely reported privacy failure, a company's app-store ratings crater, its social channels fill with angry customers, and its brand-trust scores plunge - all before any fine or lawsuit is filed. Which consequence of non-compliance is this?","options":["Reputational damage","Fines","Sanctions","Loss of license"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Reputational damage. Erosion of brand trust and customer loyalty from a publicized failure, occurring even with no regulatory action, is reputational damage. Fines and sanctions require an authority to act; here none has. The tell: reputational damage = brand and customer-trust harm; fines = money owed to a regulator; sanctions = imposed restrictions short of shutdown; loss of license = authorization to operate revoked entirely."},
  {"id":"q1038","domain":"D5","family":"noncompliance-consequences","question":"A national aviation regulator pulls a charter airline's operating certificate after safety-compliance failures, so the airline may no longer fly passengers or run any operations. Which consequence of non-compliance is this?","options":["Sanctions","Fines","Loss of license","Contractual impacts"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Loss of license. Pulling the operating certificate strips the airline's authorization to operate at all, which is loss of license, the most severe regulatory consequence. Sanctions is the near-miss, but sanctions impose lesser restrictions while the entity keeps operating. The tell: loss of license = authorization to operate revoked entirely; sanctions = imposed restrictions short of shutdown; fines = money owed; contractual impacts = private-party consequences like termination."},
  {"id":"q1039","domain":"D5","family":"noncompliance-consequences","question":"A merchant fails its PCI DSS assessment, and its acquiring bank - not any government - invokes the contract to strip the merchant of its ability to process card payments. Which consequence of non-compliance is this?","options":["Sanctions","Loss of license","Fines","Contractual impacts"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Contractual impacts. Losing card-processing ability because a private acquiring bank enforces a contract term is a contractual consequence, not a government action. Sanctions is the trap, but sanctions are imposed by a regulator or authority; PCI DSS is industry-contractual, enforced by the card brands and banks. The tell: contractual impacts = private-party consequences (termination, PCI DSS card-processing loss); sanctions = government or regulator restrictions; fines = money owed; loss of license = authorization to operate revoked entirely."},
  {"id":"q1040","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-approaches","question":"A hired team attempts to clone employee badges and bypass door locks to reach the server room and plant a rogue device. Which of the following BEST describes this penetration test?","options":["Physical penetration test","Offensive penetration test","Defensive penetration test","Integrated penetration test"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Physical penetration test. The engagement targets tangible controls - badges, locks, and physical entry to a room. Offensive penetration test is tempting because attacking is involved, but offensive testing exploits systems and applications, not doors and locks. The tell: physical = defeat badges/locks/entry; offensive = attack-side exploitation of systems; defensive = test detection and response; integrated = attackers and defenders sharing findings in real time."},
  {"id":"q1041","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-approaches","question":"The goal of an engagement is to actively exploit weaknesses and prove real access, mimicking the actions of a genuine external adversary. Which of the following penetration testing approaches is this?","options":["Defensive penetration test","Offensive penetration test","Integrated penetration test","Physical penetration test"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Offensive penetration test. The team plays the attacker, exploiting weaknesses to demonstrate access. Defensive testing loses because it measures the defenders' detection and response rather than proving exploitation. The tell: offensive = attack-side exploitation; defensive = test how fast the SOC detects and responds; integrated = offense and defense working together; physical = defeat physical entry controls."},
  {"id":"q1042","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-approaches","question":"An exercise injects simulated attack activity specifically to measure how quickly the security operations center detects and responds to it. Which of the following penetration testing approaches does this describe?","options":["Offensive penetration test","Integrated penetration test","Defensive penetration test","Physical penetration test"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Defensive penetration test. The exercise evaluates the defenders' detection and response speed, not the attacker's exploitation. Offensive testing loses because its aim is to breach and prove access, whereas here success is measured by how fast the SOC reacts. The tell: defensive = test detection and response; offensive = attack-side exploitation; integrated = offense and defense sharing findings live; physical = defeat physical entry controls."},
  {"id":"q1043","domain":"D5","family":"pentest-approaches","question":"Attackers and defenders work side by side throughout the engagement, sharing findings in real time so the defenders tune controls the moment the attackers uncover a gap. Which of the following approaches is this?","options":["Physical penetration test","Defensive penetration test","Offensive penetration test","Integrated penetration test"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Integrated penetration test. Offense and defense collaborate continuously, tuning controls as gaps surface - the SY0-701 term for this cooperative model. Offensive or defensive alone loses because each names only one side; integration is both working together. The tell: integrated = attackers and defenders sharing findings in real time; offensive = attack only; defensive = defense only; physical = defeating physical entry controls."},
  {"id":"q1044","domain":"D5","family":"phishing-response","question":"An employee receives this email:\nFrom: IT Support <accounts-update@m1crosoft-verify.com>\nSubject: Your password expires in 2 hours - act now\nWhich of the following cues MOST strongly indicates this is a phishing attempt?","options":["The message sets a two-hour deadline to pressure a fast reaction","The message contains a link the reader is told to click","The message uses a generic greeting instead of the employee's name","The sender domain m1crosoft-verify.com is a look-alike of the real one"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The look-alike sender domain. The display name says IT Support, but the real sending domain m1crosoft-verify.com is not the organization's or the vendor's domain - a definitive spoofing indicator. Urgency, links, and generic greetings are weaker because legitimate mail has them too. The tell: a mismatched or look-alike sender domain is the decisive recognition cue; deadline pressure, action links, and impersonal greetings are supporting signals, not proof."},
  {"id":"q1045","domain":"D5","family":"phishing-response","question":"An employee forwards a suspicious invoice email to the security team's reporting alias, and analysis confirms it is malicious. Which of the following should the team do NEXT?","options":["Search every mailbox for the same message and purge or block all copies","Delete the reported copy and take no further action","Reply to the sender and ask them to stop emailing staff","Forward the message to all employees as a warning"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Search all mailboxes and purge or block copies. After confirming the report, the team hunts for identical messages across the organization and removes or blocks them, then closes the loop with the reporter. Delete-and-ignore loses because other recipients would still be exposed. The tell: responding to a report means triage, org-wide hunt, purge or block, and reporter feedback - never a silent delete; recognizing is only the user-side spotting of cues."},
  {"id":"q1046","domain":"D5","family":"anomalous-behavior","question":"A developer, fully aware the security policy forbids it, shares his single sign-on password with an offshore contractor so she can meet a deadline. Which of the following BEST categorizes this behavior?","options":["Unintentional behavior","Risky behavior","Unexpected behavior","Malicious insider behavior"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Risky behavior. He knows the rule and violates it anyway for convenience, with no intent to harm the company. Unintentional loses because that requires an honest mistake, whereas he acted knowingly. Malicious insider loses because he means no harm. The tell: risky = knew the rule and did it anyway; unintentional = honest mistake; unexpected = deviation from the user's baseline; malicious insider = deliberate intent to harm."},
  {"id":"q1047","domain":"D5","family":"anomalous-behavior","question":"A bookkeeper who normally works 9 to 5 suddenly shows account activity at 3 a.m. and downloads large volumes of files she has never accessed before. Nothing yet proves intent. Which of the following BEST categorizes this?","options":["Risky behavior","Unintentional behavior","Unexpected behavior","Malicious insider behavior"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Unexpected behavior. The activity is a sharp deviation from the user's established baseline, which may signal a compromised account regardless of intent. Risky and unintentional both lose because they hinge on the user's own intent, which is not established here. The tell: unexpected = deviation from the normal baseline (possible compromise); risky = knowing violation; unintentional = honest mistake; malicious insider = proven intent to harm."},
  {"id":"q1048","domain":"D5","family":"anomalous-behavior","question":"While troubleshooting, an employee mistypes a firewall rule and briefly exposes an internal server to the internet before noticing and reverting it. There was no intent to weaken security. Which of the following BEST categorizes this?","options":["Risky behavior","Unexpected behavior","Malicious insider behavior","Unintentional behavior"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Unintentional behavior. It is an honest mistake made during legitimate work, with no awareness of doing anything wrong. Risky behavior loses because that requires a knowing violation, whereas this was accidental. The tell: unintentional = honest mistake, no intent; risky = knew the rule and broke it; unexpected = deviation from baseline; malicious insider = deliberate intent to harm."},
  {"id":"q1049","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"During onboarding, new hires are walked through the employee security handbook and acceptable use policy and sign an acknowledgement that they have read and understood the rules. Which of the following training topics is this?","options":["Situational awareness training","Policy and handbook training","Insider threat awareness","Operational security training"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Policy and handbook training. It is anchored to the official governing documents - the handbook and acceptable use policy - with a signed acknowledgement. Situational awareness loses because that teaches noticing your surroundings, not learning the written rules. The tell: policy and handbooks = know the documented rules; situational awareness = what you notice around you; insider threat = report risky colleagues; operational security = control what you reveal."},
  {"id":"q1050","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"A module teaches employees to stay alert to who is nearby, to challenge unfamiliar people in restricted areas, and to be aware of what is visible on their screen in public. Which of the following training topics is this?","options":["Situational awareness training","Operational security training","Insider threat awareness","Hybrid and remote work guidance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Situational awareness training. It trains staff to notice the security context around them - people nearby, strangers in restricted areas, and exposed screens. Operational security is the trap because it is the mirror concept: OPSEC is what you REVEAL, situational awareness is what you NOTICE. The tell: situational awareness = what you notice; operational security = what you reveal; insider threat = reporting colleagues; hybrid and remote = securing off-site work."},
  {"id":"q1051","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"A session teaches staff to recognize and report warning signs in coworkers, such as hoarding sensitive data or probing systems outside their role, through a confidential channel. Which of the following training topics is this?","options":["Situational awareness training","Operational security training","Insider threat awareness","Policy and handbook training"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Insider threat awareness. As a training topic it teaches employees to spot indicators in colleagues and report them safely, rather than describing a threat actor. Operational security loses because that is about limiting what you personally reveal, not watching coworkers. The tell: insider threat awareness = spot and report risky colleagues; situational awareness = notice your surroundings; operational security = control what you reveal; policy and handbooks = know the rules."},
  {"id":"q1052","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"A training topic instructs employees to use the company password manager, choose a unique password for every site, enroll in multifactor authentication, and never share credentials. Which of the following topics is this?","options":["Policy and handbook training","Operational security training","Removable media and cable guidance","Password management guidance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Password management guidance. It covers password managers, uniqueness, multifactor enrollment, and not sharing credentials, aligned to the password standard. Policy and handbook training loses because that teaches the rules broadly, not these specific credential practices. The tell: password management = manager, uniqueness, MFA, no sharing; policy and handbooks = the documented rules; operational security = what you reveal; removable media = untrusted drives and cables."},
  {"id":"q1053","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"A training topic warns staff never to plug in USB drives found in the parking lot or to use untrusted charging cables, and to report any found devices to security. Which of the following topics is this?","options":["Situational awareness training","Removable media and cable guidance","Operational security training","Hybrid and remote work guidance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Removable media and cable guidance. The objective explicitly covers cables and chargers as well as drives, and this training targets exactly those - untrusted USB drives and cables. Situational awareness loses because that is about noticing your surroundings, not handling found hardware. The tell: removable media and cables = do not trust found drives or unknown cables and chargers; situational awareness = what you notice; operational security = what you reveal; hybrid and remote = off-site work."},
  {"id":"q1054","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"Training warns that a lab selfie posted online can expose a network diagram on the whiteboard behind the poster and that overly detailed out-of-office replies reveal travel plans. Which of the following topics is this?","options":["Insider threat awareness","Situational awareness training","Operational security training","Password management guidance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Operational security training. OPSEC is about controlling what you inadvertently REVEAL through everyday behavior - social posts, whiteboards in photos, and out-of-office details. Situational awareness is the trap because it is the mirror: it is what you NOTICE, not what you reveal. The tell: operational security = what you reveal; situational awareness = what you notice; insider threat = report colleagues; password management = credential practices."},
  {"id":"q1055","domain":"D5","family":"user-training-topics","question":"A training topic covers using the VPN on untrusted hotel or home Wi-Fi, applying a privacy screen, locking the laptop when stepping away, and keeping household members off company devices. Which of the following topics is this?","options":["Situational awareness training","Operational security training","Removable media and cable guidance","Hybrid and remote work guidance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Hybrid and remote work guidance. It addresses the specific risks of working off-site - VPN on untrusted networks, screen privacy, device locking, and keeping family off work equipment. Operational security loses because that is about not revealing sensitive information, not securing a remote setup. The tell: hybrid and remote = secure off-site work; operational security = what you reveal; situational awareness = what you notice; removable media = untrusted drives and cables."}
];
